/** CRS key-points review: approved batch 1, four syllabus-ordered lessons. */
window.CRS_REVIEW_LESSONS=[{"id":"creation-covenants","revised":true,"new":{"title":"Creation, human dignity and God's covenants","body":"CREATION, HUMAN RESPONSIBILITY, THE FALL AND GOD'S COVENANTS — COMPLETE LESSON\n\nINTRODUCTION\nThe opening chapters of Genesis answer important questions in the biblical account: Who created the world? Why do human beings have dignity? Why did people disobey God? How did God continue to relate to humanity? Read each story in its own setting rather than mixing up events.\n\n1. THE CREATION ACCOUNTS (Genesis 1–2)\nCreation means God's bringing the world into existence. Genesis 1 describes a structured sequence:\nDay 1 — light and the separation of day from night.\nDay 2 — the expanse separating waters.\nDay 3 — dry land, seas and vegetation.\nDay 4 — sun, moon and stars.\nDay 5 — creatures of the sea and birds.\nDay 6 — land animals and human beings.\nDay 7 — God rested, blessing and making the seventh day holy.\nGenesis 2 focuses on the human setting: the man, the garden of Eden, the command concerning the tree of the knowledge of good and evil, the animals and the formation of the woman. These chapters have different emphases; do not treat the order of every detail as a single identical list.\n\n2. HUMAN DIGNITY AND RESPONSIBILITY (Genesis 1:26–28; 2:15–24)\nBeing made in God's image gives human beings a special standing and responsibility; it does not mean that people are gods. Humans are instructed to be fruitful, exercise stewardship over creation and care for the garden. Genesis 2 describes the woman as a suitable partner, and the union of husband and wife. JAMB may ask about the responsibilities rather than only the creation day.\n\n3. DISOBEDIENCE AND ITS CONSEQUENCES (Genesis 3)\nThe serpent questioned God's command. The woman and man ate from the prohibited tree. They became ashamed, hid and shifted blame. The narrative describes consequences involving pain, difficult work, broken relationships and expulsion from Eden. The serpent is cursed; the ground is cursed because of the man. Distinguish what the text says about each person.\n\n4. CAIN, ABEL AND THE FLOOD (Genesis 4; 6–9)\nCain and Abel offered gifts to God. Cain killed Abel after becoming angry and jealous; God questioned Cain and judged him, yet placed a protective mark on him. In Noah's time, widespread wickedness led to the flood. Noah obeyed God's instructions to build the ark. After the flood, God made a covenant that a flood would not again destroy all flesh. The rainbow is its sign.\n\n5. ABRAHAM AND THE COVENANT (Genesis 12; 15; 17; 22)\nGod called Abram to leave his homeland, promising land, descendants and blessing to the nations. His trust was tested repeatedly. Circumcision was the sign of the covenant in Genesis 17. Abraham's willingness to offer Isaac in Genesis 22 illustrates obedience and faith; God provided a ram instead. Isaac is the covenant heir, while Ishmael also receives a promise of descendants.\n\n6. COMPARE THE COVENANTS\nNoah — promise concerning the earth and living creatures; sign: rainbow (Genesis 9).\nAbraham — descendants, land and blessing; sign: circumcision (Genesis 17).\nIsrael at Sinai — covenant relationship with commands and obligations (Exodus 19–20).\nDo not swap the signs or assume all three covenants were made with the same people.\n\n\nDEEPER EXPLANATION — CREATION AND HUMAN RESPONSIBILITY\nThe first creation account (Genesis 1:1–2:3) gives an ordered description of creation and repeatedly says that what God made was good. Human beings, male and female, are created in God's image (Genesis 1:26–27). In this lesson, “image of God” points to human dignity and responsibility; it does not mean that God must have a human body. “Dominion” is authority to care responsibly for creation, not permission to destroy it.\nGenesis 2 concentrates on life in Eden. The man is formed from dust and placed in the garden to work and keep it. The command about the tree shows that human freedom includes responsibility. The woman is described as a suitable helper or partner. Do not confuse the different emphases of Genesis 1 and Genesis 2.\n\nTHE FALL — CAUSE, ACTION AND CONSEQUENCES (Genesis 3)\nThe serpent questions God's instruction; the woman and the man eat the forbidden fruit. Their disobedience is followed by shame, hiding, blame, hardship and exclusion from Eden. “The Fall” refers to humanity's movement from obedient fellowship with God into sin and its consequences. The text does not identify the fruit as an apple; a question claiming that it was an apple adds information not supplied by Genesis.\n\nWHAT A COVENANT MEANS\nA covenant is a binding relationship involving promises and responsibilities. God's covenant with Noah (Genesis 9) includes a promise not to destroy all life again by a flood; the rainbow is its sign. The call of Abram in Genesis 12 includes land, descendants and blessing for other families of the earth. In Genesis 17 circumcision is the covenant sign associated with Abraham and his descendants. A covenant sign is not the same as the covenant promise.\nAbraham's name was changed from Abram; Sarai's name became Sarah. Isaac is the promised son born to Sarah, whereas Ishmael is Abraham's son through Hagar. Isaac's sons are Esau and Jacob; Jacob is later named Israel. Learn this family sequence because JAMB questions often test relationships rather than the main story alone.\n\nGUIDED WALKTHROUGH — HOW TO SOLVE A COVENANT QUESTION\nStep 1: Identify the person named in the question: Noah, Abraham or Moses.\nStep 2: Identify whether the question asks for a promise, a sign or a human responsibility.\nStep 3: Match the passage: rainbow—Noah; circumcision—Abraham; law at Sinai—Israel through Moses.\nStep 4: Reject an answer that belongs to a different covenant even if it is a true biblical statement.\n\nADDITIONAL WORKED EXAMPLES\nExample (iv): What was the sign of God's covenant with Noah?\nSolution: Genesis 9:12–17 names the rainbow as the sign. Answer: The rainbow.\nExample (v): What does Genesis 1 teach about the value of human beings?\nSolution: Genesis 1:26–27 says that humans were created in God's image. Answer: Human beings possess God-given dignity and responsibility.\nExample (vi): Was the forbidden fruit specifically an apple?\nSolution: Genesis 3 speaks of the fruit of the forbidden tree but does not name its type. Answer: No; the Bible does not specify an apple.\n\nEXAM TRAPS AND REMINDERS\nDo not mix the rainbow and circumcision. Do not identify the forbidden fruit by an unsupported name. Distinguish creation in God's image from the later disobedience in Eden. When asked for a consequence of sin, name the consequence in the passage rather than a general moral lesson.\n\nWORKED JAMB-STYLE EXAMPLES\nExample (i): On which day were humans created in Genesis 1?\nSolution: Genesis 1:26–31 places humans on the sixth day. The seventh day is God's rest. Answer: Sixth day.\n\nExample (ii): What was the sign of God's covenant with Noah?\nSolution: Genesis 9:12–17 identifies the rainbow. Circumcision belongs to the Abrahamic covenant. Answer: Rainbow.\n\nExample (iii): What does God's image in human beings imply?\nSolution: The passage gives humanity dignity and responsibility for creation. It does not claim that humans have divine powers. Answer: Dignity and responsible stewardship.\n\n\nDETAILED BIBLE STUDY — NOAH'S FLOOD (GENESIS 6–9)\nWhy the flood happened: Genesis 6:5–13 describes widespread human wickedness and violence. Noah found favour with God and was described as righteous in his generation. God told Noah to build an ark of gopher wood, coat it inside and outside with pitch, and make rooms in it. Its dimensions were 300 cubits long, 50 cubits wide and 30 cubits high (Genesis 6:14–16). It had three decks, a door in its side and an opening near the roof.\n\nWho entered the ark? Noah, his wife, his three sons—Shem, Ham and Japheth—and their wives: eight people (Genesis 7:7, 13; 1 Peter 3:20). Animals entered according to God's directions. Genesis 6:19–20 describes pairs of living creatures; Genesis 7:2–3 gives additional instructions for clean animals and birds. Do not assume that the same number of every kind of animal is given in both passages.\n\nTHE FLOOD TIMELINE — LEARN EACH NUMBER\n• Noah was 600 years old when the flood came (Genesis 7:6).\n• God gave a warning of seven more days before the rain (Genesis 7:4, 10).\n• The rain fell for 40 days and 40 nights (Genesis 7:12).\n• The waters prevailed upon the earth for 150 days (Genesis 7:24). This does NOT mean that rain fell for 150 days.\n• The ark came to rest on the mountains of Ararat in the seventh month, on the seventeenth day (Genesis 8:4).\n• After the mountaintops became visible, Noah waited 40 days before opening the ark's window and sending out a raven (Genesis 8:5–7).\n• Noah then sent out a dove. It returned because it found no resting place. After seven more days, the dove returned with a freshly plucked olive leaf; after another seven days, it did not return (Genesis 8:8–12).\n• Noah left the ark when the earth was dry, after God instructed him to come out (Genesis 8:13–19). From the stated calendar dates, the narrative spans approximately a year, not merely 40 days.\nThe raven and dove are different birds with different outcomes. The olive leaf indicated that the waters had receded enough for vegetation to be accessible.\n\nWHAT NOAH DID AFTER THE FLOOD\nNoah built an altar and offered burnt offerings (Genesis 8:20). God promised that the regular cycles of seedtime and harvest, cold and heat, summer and winter, day and night would continue while the earth remains (Genesis 8:22). In Genesis 9 God established his covenant with Noah, his descendants and living creatures: never again would a flood destroy all flesh. The rainbow was the sign. The command concerning blood and respect for human life also appears in Genesis 9. The rainbow is a sign, not the ark's construction material.\n\nABRAHAM — GOD'S EXACT CALL AND PROMISES\nGenesis 12:1–3: God told Abram to leave his country, his kindred and his father's house for a land God would show him. God promised to make him a great nation, bless him, make his name great, make him a blessing, bless those who blessed him, curse the one who dishonoured him, and bless all the families of the earth through him. Abram was 75 years old when he departed from Haran (Genesis 12:4). His wife was Sarai (later Sarah), and Lot travelled with him.\nGenesis 15: Abram wondered how he could have an heir because he remained childless. God told him to look at the stars and said his descendants would be similarly numerous. Abram believed God. God also foretold that his descendants would be strangers in another land and would experience oppression for 400 years (Genesis 15:13). Distinguish this prophecy from the later description of Israel's stay in Egypt.\nGenesis 17: God changed Abram's name to Abraham and Sarai's to Sarah. He promised that Sarah would bear Isaac. Abraham was 99 when this covenant was reaffirmed; Sarah was 90 when Isaac was to be born, and Abraham was 100 when Isaac was born (Genesis 17:1, 17; 21:5). Circumcision was to be performed on male infants on the eighth day as the covenant sign. Ishmael was blessed with descendants, but the covenant was to be established through Isaac (Genesis 17:18–21).\nGenesis 18: Three visitors came to Abraham near the oaks of Mamre. Abraham showed hospitality, and Sarah heard that she would have a son. Sarah laughed at the promise; the child's name Isaac is connected with laughter. Abraham interceded for Sodom, asking whether it would be spared for decreasing numbers of righteous people, down to ten.\nGenesis 22: God tested Abraham by commanding him to offer Isaac in the land of Moriah. Abraham went with Isaac; at the decisive moment an angel stopped him, and a ram caught in a thicket was offered instead. The story tests obedience and trust; it does not say Isaac was killed.\n\nOTHER EARLY GENESIS DETAILS JAMB MAY TEST\nCain worked the ground; Abel kept sheep (Genesis 4:2). Abel's offering was from the firstborn of his flock. After Cain killed Abel, God asked, “Where is Abel your brother?” Cain replied with the question about being his brother's keeper. God marked Cain so that others would not kill him (Genesis 4:15).\nAt Babel (Genesis 11), people sought to build a city and tower to make a name for themselves. God confused their language and scattered them. This episode occurs before the call of Abram in the Genesis narrative.\n\nWORKED JAMB-STYLE QUESTIONS — FACTS AND REASONING\nExample (vii): For how many days and nights did rain fall during Noah's flood?\nSolution: Genesis 7:12 specifically says 40 days and 40 nights. The 150 days in Genesis 7:24 refers to how long the waters prevailed. Answer: 40 days and 40 nights.\nExample (viii): How long did the floodwaters prevail?\nSolution: Genesis 7:24 gives 150 days. Answer: 150 days.\nExample (ix): What did God tell Abram to leave in Genesis 12?\nSolution: The command names his country, kindred and father's house, and directs him towards a land God would show him. Answer: His country, relatives and father's household.\nExample (x): At what age did Abraham leave Haran?\nSolution: Genesis 12:4 records that Abram was 75 years old. Answer: 75 years.\nExample (xi): What was Abraham's age when Isaac was born?\nSolution: Genesis 21:5 states that Abraham was 100. Answer: 100 years.\nExample (xii): What did the dove bring back to Noah?\nSolution: Genesis 8:11 identifies a freshly plucked olive leaf. Answer: An olive leaf.\nExample (xiii): What was the sign of God's covenant with Abraham?\nSolution: Genesis 17:11 identifies circumcision. The rainbow belongs to the covenant with Noah. Answer: Circumcision.\n\nCHECK YOUR UNDERSTANDING — DO NOT JUST MEMORISE\nExplain the difference between 40 days of rain and 150 days of prevailing water. Name Noah's three sons. State three promises God made to Abram. Distinguish Genesis 12, Genesis 15, Genesis 17 and Genesis 22. Explain why the rainbow and circumcision cannot be interchanged.\n\nQUICK UNDERSTANDING CHECK\nExplain why the sixth and seventh days should not be confused. Compare Noah's and Abraham's covenant signs. Describe two consequences of disobedience in Genesis 3.\n\nPRACTICE QUESTIONS\nUse the Practice Questions button below to attempt mapped JAMB questions, then explain why the distractors are wrong."},"old":{"title":"Creation, human dignity and God's covenants","body":"Creation means God's bringing everything into existence. Genesis 1 presents an ordered creation, including land animals and human beings on the sixth day and God's rest on the seventh. Human beings are made in God's image: they reflect and represent him and receive responsibility for the earth. This is not a claim that people are gods or that God has the same physical shape as a person. It supports respect for every person's dignity, including people of a different tribe, social position or ability.\n\nGenesis 2 concentrates on the garden and human relationships. The river divides into Pishon, Gihon, Tigris (Hiddekel) and Euphrates. After the statement that the man needs a suitable helper, the narrative describes the animals being brought to him; the formation of the woman follows. The account ends with husband and wife becoming one flesh. Eve's name is linked with being the mother of all living. Do not confuse this with the earlier words “bone of my bones.”\n\nA covenant is a binding relationship involving commitments. God's covenant with Noah includes living creatures and the promise that a flood will not again destroy all flesh. Its sign is the rainbow. God's promises to Abraham include descendants, land and blessing reaching other nations. Circumcision is its sign. Abraham's obedience in leaving his familiar home is a major example of faith in Hebrews 11. God's promise concerning Ishmael recognises him as Abraham's offspring, while Isaac carries the particular covenant line.\n\nCommon traps:\n• Noah: rainbow; Abraham: circumcision; Sinai: law and covenant obedience.\n• The Sabbath ends the first creation sequence; marriage closes the second account.\n• Being made in God's image is likeness and responsibility, not physical appearance or human divinity.\n\nGenesis places the firmament, separating waters, on the second day and the sun, moon and stars on the fourth. Genesis 9 connects the prohibition of bloodshed with God's image in humanity. Noah obeyed God's instructions and entered the ark with his household under God's covenant. Genesis 11 names Terah, Abram, Sarai and Lot as leaving Ur; Haran was their stopping place. At Moriah Abraham named the place “The LORD will provide.” At Bethel Jacob promised a tenth of what God gave him. Keep these places, persons and covenant responses distinct.\n\nGenesis 6:3 names one hundred and twenty years; interpretations differ over whether this is a lifespan limit or a period before judgement. Genesis 1 calls the gathered waters seas and the dry land earth.\n\nGenesis 2 assigns garden cultivation and care. Genesis 3 explicitly curses the serpent and ground; the humans receive painful consequences and expulsion. Noah's ark floats as the waters rise.\n\nGenesis calls Noah's material gopher wood; the precise tree identification remains uncertain."}},{"id":"moses-exodus","revised":true,"new":{"title":"Moses, deliverance, the covenant and entry into Canaan","body":"MOSES, THE EXODUS AND GOD'S COVENANT WITH ISRAEL — COMPLETE LESSON\n\nINTRODUCTION\nThe Exodus tells how the Israelites were delivered from slavery in Egypt and formed into a covenant community. Follow the events in order: oppression, Moses' call, confrontation with Pharaoh, Passover, crossing the sea, wilderness and Sinai.\n\n1. MOSES' EARLY LIFE AND CALL (Exodus 1–4)\nPharaoh ordered the killing of Hebrew male infants. Moses' mother hid him, then placed him in a basket on the Nile; Pharaoh's daughter found him. After Moses fled Egypt to Midian, God called him from the burning bush at Horeb. God revealed the divine name and commissioned him to lead Israel. Moses objected that the people might not believe him and that he was not an effective speaker. God gave signs and appointed Aaron to speak with him.\n\n2. PHARAOH AND THE PLAGUES (Exodus 5–12)\nPharaoh resisted Moses' demand to let the Israelites go. The plagues demonstrated God's power and challenged Pharaoh's refusal. Remember the final plague: the death of the firstborn. The Passover instructions involved a lamb, blood on the doorposts and a meal eaten in readiness to depart. The blood marked Israelite households in the narrative. Passover commemorates deliverance; it is not the same event as the crossing of the sea.\n\n3. CROSSING THE SEA AND WILDERNESS PROVISION (Exodus 13–17)\nGod guided the people by a pillar of cloud by day and fire by night. At the sea, Moses stretched out his hand and the waters divided. After escaping Egypt, the Israelites faced shortages and complained. God provided manna and quail, and water in the wilderness. These stories test whether students can distinguish a crisis, the people's response and God's provision.\n\n4. THE SINAI COVENANT (Exodus 19–20; 24)\nA covenant is a binding relationship involving promises and responsibilities. At Sinai, God called Israel to be a treasured people and a holy nation if they kept the covenant. The Ten Commandments describe duties towards God and other people. Moses received the law; the people were called to obey. The Sabbath command concerns keeping a day holy, not the sign of the Noahic covenant.\n\n5. THE GOLDEN CALF (Exodus 32–34)\nWhile Moses was on the mountain, Aaron made a golden calf at the people's request. They worshipped it, violating the covenant. Moses interceded for them, and the narrative includes judgment and renewal. The central lesson is that covenant membership does not remove the obligation to obey God.\n\n6. LEADERSHIP, SPIES AND ENTRY INTO CANAAN (Numbers 13–14; Deuteronomy 34; Joshua 1–6)\nTwelve spies explored Canaan. Joshua and Caleb urged trust, while the others spread fear. The generation's refusal had consequences during the wilderness years. Moses saw the promised land but did not lead the people into it. Joshua succeeded Moses, led the crossing of the Jordan and directed the conquest of Jericho. Distinguish Moses' crossing of the sea from Joshua's crossing of the Jordan.\n\n\nDEEPER EXPLANATION — FROM OPPRESSION TO DELIVERANCE\nThe Israelites were oppressed in Egypt after the period of Joseph. Pharaoh ordered harsh labour and measures against Hebrew male children. Moses' mother hid him and later placed him in a basket among the reeds; Pharaoh's daughter found him (Exodus 2). Moses fled to Midian after killing an Egyptian who was beating a Hebrew. At the burning bush on Horeb, God called Moses to lead Israel out of Egypt (Exodus 3). Moses' reluctance is important: he questioned his ability and speech, while God promised help and appointed Aaron to speak with him.\n\nTHE PLAGUES AND PASSOVER\nThe plagues challenged Pharaoh's refusal to release Israel. The final plague involved the death of the firstborn. Before it, Israelite households were instructed to mark their doorposts with lamb's blood and eat the Passover meal (Exodus 12). Passover remembers God's deliverance. The crossing of the sea came after the departure from Egypt; it was not the same event as Passover.\nSequence to remember: oppression → Moses' call → confrontation with Pharaoh → plagues → Passover → departure → crossing of the sea → wilderness journey → Sinai.\n\nWILDERNESS PROVISION AND COMPLAINTS\nThe people faced thirst and hunger. God provided manna and quail, and water was supplied in the wilderness. Manna was food provided during the journey; it was not one of the Egyptian plagues. When the people complained, Moses had to lead a community that was still learning trust and obedience.\n\nTHE SINAI COVENANT AND TEN COMMANDMENTS\nAt Mount Sinai, God entered a covenant relationship with Israel (Exodus 19–20). The commandments address loyalty to God, proper worship, family responsibility and conduct towards neighbours. The first commandment concerns having no other gods; the command against making idols concerns images for worship. Do not merge them simply because both concern worship.\nThe golden calf incident (Exodus 32) happened after the people had received God's instructions; it shows a breach of covenant. Aaron was involved in making the calf, while Moses interceded for the people.\n\nGUIDED WALKTHROUGH — SEQUENCE QUESTIONS\nFirst underline the event in the question. Next ask whether it occurred in Egypt, during departure, in the wilderness or at Sinai. Only then select the person or location. A correct event placed in the wrong part of the journey is still a wrong answer.\n\nADDITIONAL WORKED EXAMPLES\nExample (iv): Where did Moses encounter God in the burning bush?\nSolution: Exodus 3 places the encounter at Horeb, the mountain of God. Answer: Horeb.\nExample (v): Which event immediately prepared Israelite families for the final plague?\nSolution: Exodus 12 describes the Passover instructions, including the lamb's blood on the doorposts. Answer: The first Passover.\nExample (vi): Why was the golden calf a serious offence?\nSolution: Exodus 32 describes the people making and worshipping an image while bound to worship the LORD. Answer: It violated their covenant obligation to worship God alone.\n\nEXAM TRAPS AND REMINDERS\nAaron was Moses' brother, not his father. The burning bush preceded the plagues. Passover preceded the sea crossing. Sinai is connected with covenant law, not the original call at the burning bush alone.\n\nWORKED JAMB-STYLE EXAMPLES\nExample (i): Why was Aaron assigned to assist Moses?\nSolution: Moses objected that he was not an effective speaker (Exodus 4:10–16). God appointed Aaron as his spokesman. Answer: To speak on Moses' behalf.\n\nExample (ii): Which event immediately preceded Israel's departure from Egypt?\nSolution: The final plague was the death of the firstborn, and the Passover instructions accompanied it (Exodus 12). Answer: The final plague and Passover.\n\nExample (iii): Who succeeded Moses as Israel's leader?\nSolution: Deuteronomy 34 and Joshua 1 identify Joshua. Aaron was Moses' brother and priest, not his successor as national leader. Answer: Joshua.\n\n\nDETAILED BIBLE STUDY — MOSES' FAMILY AND THE CALL\nMoses belonged to the tribe of Levi. His father was Amram and his mother Jochebed (Exodus 6:20); Aaron was his elder brother and Miriam his sister. His mother hid him for three months before placing him in a papyrus basket coated with bitumen and pitch among the reeds beside the Nile (Exodus 2:1–4). Pharaoh's daughter discovered him; Miriam arranged for the child's own mother to nurse him. The name Moses is connected in Exodus 2:10 with being drawn out of the water.\nAfter fleeing to Midian, Moses helped the daughters of the priest of Midian at a well and married Zipporah. Their son Gershom's name recalls Moses' life as a foreigner (Exodus 2:15–22). At Horeb God appeared in a flame from a bush that burned without being consumed. Moses was told to remove his sandals because he stood on holy ground. God identified himself as the God of Abraham, Isaac and Jacob and heard Israel's suffering. In Exodus 3:14 God said, “I AM WHO I AM.” Moses was to tell Pharaoh to release Israel to worship God. Moses' objections included his unworthiness, uncertainty about God's name, fear of unbelief and difficulty speaking. God supplied signs involving the staff, his hand and water, and appointed Aaron as spokesman (Exodus 4).\n\nTHE TEN PLAGUES — IN ORDER (EXODUS 7–12)\n1. Nile water turned to blood.\n2. Frogs.\n3. Gnats or lice (translation varies).\n4. Flies.\n5. Disease or pestilence on livestock.\n6. Boils.\n7. Hail.\n8. Locusts.\n9. Darkness for three days.\n10. Death of the firstborn.\nThe first nine plagues were not the Passover. The tenth plague was connected with the Passover instructions. Pharaoh repeatedly resisted, and the narrative emphasises the hardening of his heart. Do not confuse darkness, the ninth plague, with the final plague.\n\nPASSOVER — WHAT THE ISRAELITES WERE TOLD (EXODUS 12)\nEach household was to take a lamb without blemish; it was selected on the tenth day of the first month and slaughtered on the fourteenth day at twilight. Its blood was placed on the two doorposts and lintel. The meat was roasted and eaten with unleavened bread and bitter herbs. They were to eat ready for departure, with belt fastened, sandals on and staff in hand. No leaven was to be present during the associated Feast of Unleavened Bread. The blood marked the houses to be passed over during the judgement. Passover means God's deliverance, not that Moses himself passed through the sea.\n\nTHE SEA CROSSING AND THE JOURNEY\nGod guided Israel by cloud during the day and fire at night (Exodus 13:21–22). At the sea, Moses stretched out his hand and God drove back the waters; the Israelites crossed on dry ground (Exodus 14). Miriam led women with tambourines in celebration after the crossing (Exodus 15:20–21).\nAt Marah the water was bitter and was made drinkable (Exodus 15:23–25). At Elim there were twelve springs and seventy palm trees (Exodus 15:27). Manna appeared with the morning dew; people were told to gather enough for each day, but a double portion before the Sabbath (Exodus 16). At Rephidim water came from a rock when Moses struck it as instructed (Exodus 17:1–7). These are different locations and episodes.\n\nSINAI — GOD'S INSTRUCTIONS AND ISRAEL'S RESPONSE\nAt Sinai, God told Israel that if they obeyed his voice and kept his covenant, they would be his treasured possession, a kingdom of priests and a holy nation (Exodus 19:5–6). Moses prepared the people to meet God. The Ten Commandments (Exodus 20) include worship of God alone, no idols, proper use of God's name, keeping the Sabbath, honouring parents, and prohibitions of murder, adultery, stealing, false witness and coveting.\nIn Exodus 24 Moses read the Book of the Covenant; the people promised obedience. Moses used blood in the covenant ceremony. During Moses' absence on the mountain, the people demanded gods to go before them. Aaron collected gold earrings and fashioned a calf (Exodus 32). Moses broke the tablets when he saw the idolatry; later the covenant was renewed (Exodus 34).\n\nSPIES, WILDERNESS YEARS AND SUCCESSION\nNumbers 13 names twelve spies, one from each tribe, sent to inspect Canaan for 40 days. Ten brought a fearful report, while Joshua and Caleb urged confidence in God's promise (Numbers 13–14). The people's refusal led to the judgement of 40 years of wandering, corresponding to the 40 days of spying. Joshua and Caleb were the faithful exceptions among the adult generation. Moses later saw the land from Mount Nebo but died before entry (Deuteronomy 34). Joshua led the people across the Jordan, while priests carrying the ark stood in the riverbed (Joshua 3). At Jericho the people marched around the city once each day for six days and seven times on the seventh day before the walls fell (Joshua 6).\n\nWORKED JAMB-STYLE QUESTIONS — IMPORTANT NUMBERS\nExample (vii): Which plague immediately preceded the death of the firstborn?\nSolution: The ninth plague was darkness; the tenth was the death of the firstborn. Answer: Darkness.\nExample (viii): For how many days did the spies explore Canaan?\nSolution: Numbers 13:25 records 40 days. Answer: 40 days.\nExample (ix): What was the consequence of the people's refusal to enter Canaan?\nSolution: Numbers 14 connects their unbelief with 40 years of wandering. Answer: Forty years in the wilderness.\nExample (x): What did God instruct Moses to remove at the burning bush?\nSolution: Exodus 3:5 tells him to remove his sandals because the ground was holy. Answer: His sandals.\nExample (xi): Who led the Israelites across the River Jordan?\nSolution: Joshua 3 describes Joshua's leadership after Moses' death. Answer: Joshua.\nExample (xii): How many times did Israel march around Jericho on the seventh day?\nSolution: Joshua 6:15 states seven times. Answer: Seven times.\n\nCHECK YOUR UNDERSTANDING\nName all ten plagues in order. Describe the Passover meal and its purpose. Distinguish Marah, Elim and Rephidim. Explain why the 40 days of spying and 40 years of wandering are different. Compare Moses' sea crossing with Joshua's Jordan crossing.\n\nQUICK UNDERSTANDING CHECK\nExplain the purpose of Passover. List two responsibilities under the Sinai covenant. Explain why the crossing of the Red Sea and the crossing of the Jordan must not be confused.\n\nPRACTICE QUESTIONS\nUse the Practice Questions button to test the sequence of events and their biblical references."},"old":{"title":"Moses, deliverance, the covenant and entry into Canaan","body":"The Exodus is the account of God's deliverance of Israel from slavery in Egypt. At Moses' call, God gave signs because Moses feared the Israelites would not believe he had been sent. When the rod first became a snake, Moses fled; he took it by the tail only after God's instruction. At the sea, the Egyptians acknowledged that the LORD fought for Israel when their chariots were impeded. God avoided the shorter Philistine route lest seeing war make the people return to Egypt.\n\nIn the wilderness, food and water tested trust and obedience. Israel's complaints about food were heard, and God promised meat in the evening and bread in the morning. The people were to gather manna as commanded, rather than store extra in distrust. The special instructions before the Sabbath also required obedience. The Sabbath command concerns keeping the seventh day holy; its numerical position differs between Christian traditions, although the biblical command remains the same. Common Protestant lists number it fourth; Catholic and Lutheran lists usually number it third.\n\nAt Sinai, the people were consecrated and washed their garments. They were not told to touch the mountain: boundaries protected the holy meeting. In Exodus 24 they promised to do God's words; Moses recorded them, arranged sacrifices and confirmed the covenant with blood. After the golden calf, Moses interceded by appealing to God's promises to the patriarchs.\n\nKeep the two rock incidents distinct. Exodus 17 records a command to strike the rock at Horeb. Numbers 20 records a command to speak to the rock in the wilderness of Zin; Moses struck it instead. A familiar action can therefore be wrong in a different setting.\n\nBefore entry into Canaan, the spies assessed the land and its inhabitants.\n\nCommon trap: identify the particular episode and sequence before choosing a familiar name or action.\n\nThe Hebrew midwives spared boys because they feared God. Moses encountered God in a bush that burned without being consumed. Exodus 4 gives three signs: rod becoming a snake, diseased hand restored, and river water becoming blood; these are not the numbered plagues. Exodus 24 names oxen as the peace offerings. After the golden calf came a plague. On the Sabbath, disobedient gatherers found no manna; overnight hoarding on ordinary days produced worms. Numbers 20 excludes Moses from leading Israel into the land. \n\nMoses acts as mediator between God and the people. Pharaoh seeks his life after the Egyptian's killing, so he flees. The first manna comes in the wilderness of Sin, distinct from Zin. Shiphrah and Puah receive families because they fear God.\n\nA cloud guides by day and fire by night. Acts 7 connects Moses' defence of his Hebrew kin with attempted deliverance, without endorsing violence."}},{"id":"samuel-saul-david","revised":true,"new":{"title":"Samuel, Saul and David: leadership and obedience","body":"SAMUEL, SAUL AND DAVID — LEADERSHIP, FAITH AND OBEDIENCE\n\nINTRODUCTION\nIsrael moved from the period of judges to kingship. This lesson explains why the people requested a king, how Saul became king, why his leadership failed and how David rose to prominence. JAMB often tests names, actions and consequences.\n\n1. SAMUEL'S CHILDHOOD AND CALL (1 Samuel 1–3)\nHannah prayed for a child and promised to dedicate him to God. Samuel served under Eli at Shiloh. When God called Samuel at night, he initially thought Eli was speaking. Eli taught him how to respond. Samuel's call shows attentiveness and obedience. Eli's sons were Hophni and Phinehas; Samuel's sons were Joel and Abijah. Do not mix them up.\n\n2. ISRAEL REQUESTS A KING (1 Samuel 8)\nThe elders complained that Samuel's sons acted dishonestly and requested a king like the surrounding nations. Samuel warned them about the demands a king would make, including military service, labour and taxation. The issue was not simply that kingship itself was always forbidden; the account also stresses Israel's rejection of God's kingship.\n\n3. SAUL'S APPOINTMENT AND EARLY LEADERSHIP (1 Samuel 9–11)\nSaul, son of Kish from Benjamin, was anointed by Samuel and publicly chosen. He gained support after delivering Jabesh-gilead from the Ammonites. Distinguish private anointing from public selection.\n\n4. SAUL'S DISOBEDIENCE (1 Samuel 13; 15)\nSaul offered sacrifice when Samuel had not arrived as expected. Later he failed to carry out the command concerning Amalek fully, sparing Agag and valuable livestock. Samuel taught that obedience is better than sacrifice. Saul's downfall is associated with disobedience, not merely his military weakness.\n\n5. DAVID'S ANOINTING AND GOLIATH (1 Samuel 16–17)\nGod sent Samuel to Jesse's family at Bethlehem. David, the youngest son, was chosen. The lesson stresses that God looks at the heart, not outward appearance. David confronted Goliath with a sling and stones, trusting God rather than relying on Saul's armour.\n\n6. DAVID, JONATHAN AND SAUL (1 Samuel 18–31)\nJonathan and David developed a close covenant friendship. Saul became jealous of David's popularity and repeatedly sought to kill him. David spared Saul when he had opportunities to harm him. This demonstrates restraint and respect for the king's position.\n\n7. DAVID AS KING: ACHIEVEMENTS AND FAILURES (2 Samuel 5–12)\nDavid united the tribes and made Jerusalem his capital. He brought the ark to Jerusalem. He also committed adultery with Bathsheba and arranged Uriah's death. Nathan confronted him with a parable; David acknowledged his sin and faced consequences. A leader's achievements do not excuse wrongdoing.\n\n\nDEEPER EXPLANATION — THE TRANSITION TO KINGSHIP\nA judge in ancient Israel was a leader raised up to deliver or guide the people; this role was not identical to a modern courtroom judge. Samuel served as prophet, judge and leader. He heard God's call while serving under Eli (1 Samuel 3). Eli's sons behaved wickedly, and the people's later request for a king was influenced by Samuel's sons' misconduct and their wish to resemble surrounding nations (1 Samuel 8). Samuel warned that kings could demand labour, property and service from the people.\n\nSAUL'S APPOINTMENT AND FAILURES\nSaul, from the tribe of Benjamin, became Israel's first king. He was anointed by Samuel. In 1 Samuel 13, Saul offered a sacrifice when he should have waited for Samuel. In 1 Samuel 15, Saul failed to carry out the command concerning Amalek fully and then tried to justify his conduct. Samuel taught that obedience was better than sacrifice. These are two separate incidents; identify which chapter a question refers to.\n\nDAVID'S RISE AND LEADERSHIP\nDavid, son of Jesse of Bethlehem, was anointed by Samuel while Saul was still king (1 Samuel 16). David's victory over Goliath showed confidence in God rather than reliance on military equipment. Jonathan, Saul's son, formed a close friendship with David and protected him. David repeatedly refused to kill Saul when he had opportunities; this shows respect for the office of the LORD's anointed.\nAfter Saul's death, David first ruled Judah from Hebron and later ruled a united kingdom from Jerusalem. He brought the ark to Jerusalem. He wanted to build a temple, but the task was left to Solomon.\n\nDAVID'S SIN, REBUKE AND REPENTANCE\nDavid committed adultery with Bathsheba and arranged circumstances that led to Uriah's death (2 Samuel 11). Nathan confronted him through a parable about a rich man taking a poor man's lamb (2 Samuel 12). David admitted his wrongdoing. Repentance does not mean that the consequences of an action disappear automatically.\n\nGUIDED WALKTHROUGH — WHO DID WHAT?\nSamuel: called in Eli's household, anointed Saul and David.\nSaul: first king, disobeyed concerning sacrifice and Amalek.\nDavid: defeated Goliath, ruled from Hebron and Jerusalem, repented after Nathan's rebuke.\nJonathan: Saul's son and David's loyal friend.\nNathan: prophet who confronted David over Bathsheba and Uriah.\n\nADDITIONAL WORKED EXAMPLES\nExample (iv): Why did Israel ask Samuel for a king?\nSolution: In 1 Samuel 8 the elders cited Samuel's ageing, his sons' misconduct and their desire for a king like other nations. Answer: To have a king who would govern them like the surrounding nations.\nExample (v): What did Samuel mean by “obedience is better than sacrifice”?\nSolution: In 1 Samuel 15 Saul tried to excuse disobedience by referring to animals kept for sacrifice. Answer: Carrying out God's instruction matters more than using religious offerings to excuse wrongdoing.\nExample (vi): Which prophet confronted David after the Bathsheba incident?\nSolution: 2 Samuel 12 identifies Nathan. Answer: Nathan.\n\nEXAM TRAPS AND REMINDERS\nSaul was Israel's first king; David was not. Samuel anointed David; Nathan later rebuked David. Jonathan was Saul's son, not David's son. Distinguish Saul's two major disobedience accounts.\n\nWORKED JAMB-STYLE EXAMPLES\nExample (i): Why did Israel's elders request a king?\nSolution: Samuel's sons did not follow his example and accepted bribes; the elders wanted a king to judge and lead them like other nations (1 Samuel 8). Answer: Corrupt judges and the desire for a king like other nations.\n\nExample (ii): What did Samuel mean by obedience being better than sacrifice?\nSolution: Saul attempted to justify keeping livestock after disobeying God's instruction (1 Samuel 15). Religious offerings could not replace obedience. Answer: God's command must be obeyed rather than excused by ritual.\n\nExample (iii): Who confronted David over Bathsheba and Uriah?\nSolution: The prophet Nathan used a parable about a rich man taking a poor man's lamb (2 Samuel 12). Answer: Nathan.\n\n\nDETAILED BIBLE STUDY — HANNAH AND SAMUEL (1 SAMUEL 1–3)\nElkanah had two wives, Hannah and Peninnah. Hannah was distressed because she had no child, while Peninnah provoked her. At Shiloh Hannah prayed silently and promised that if God gave her a son, she would dedicate him to the LORD for his whole life (1 Samuel 1:11). Eli initially thought she was drunk, but later blessed her. She named the child Samuel and brought him to serve at the sanctuary after he was weaned. Her song in 1 Samuel 2 praises God's power to reverse human circumstances.\nEli's sons Hophni and Phinehas misused their priestly position and treated offerings with contempt. God announced judgement on Eli's household. Samuel heard his name called at night and ran to Eli three times before Eli recognised that the LORD was calling him. Eli taught him to say, “Speak, LORD, for your servant hears” (1 Samuel 3). The message concerned judgement on Eli's household. Samuel became recognised as a prophet throughout Israel.\n\nTHE ARK AND ISRAEL'S CRISIS (1 SAMUEL 4–7)\nIsrael took the ark into battle against the Philistines as though its presence guaranteed victory. Israel was defeated, Hophni and Phinehas died, and the ark was captured. Eli died after hearing the news. The Philistines placed the ark in the temple of Dagon; their idol fell before it (1 Samuel 5). The ark later returned to Israel. Samuel called the people to put away foreign gods and return to the LORD. At Mizpah the people confessed their sins; Samuel interceded, and Israel defeated the Philistines (1 Samuel 7). He set up a stone called Ebenezer, meaning a stone of help.\n\nWHY ISRAEL WANTED A KING (1 SAMUEL 8)\nSamuel's sons Joel and Abijah were judges at Beersheba but accepted bribes and perverted justice. The elders asked for a king to judge them like other nations. Samuel warned that a king would take sons for military service, daughters for royal work, a tenth of produce and servants for his service. The people still insisted. God told Samuel to heed their demand while warning them about the consequences.\n\nSAUL'S FAMILY, ANOINTING AND REJECTION\nSaul was the son of Kish of the tribe of Benjamin (1 Samuel 9). He met Samuel while searching for his father's lost donkeys. Samuel privately anointed him (1 Samuel 10); he was later chosen publicly at Mizpah. Saul's rescue of Jabesh-gilead from Nahash the Ammonite strengthened his position (1 Samuel 11).\nIn 1 Samuel 13 Saul failed to wait for Samuel at Gilgal and offered the burnt offering. In 1 Samuel 15 Saul was commanded to destroy Amalek's possessions but spared King Agag and the best animals. He claimed the animals were kept for sacrifice. Samuel replied that obedience was better than sacrifice and rebellion was like divination. Samuel then killed Agag (1 Samuel 15:33). These were distinct offences; JAMB may ask which incident led to which rebuke.\n\nDAVID — ANOINTING, GOLIATH AND FRIENDSHIP\nSamuel went to Jesse's home in Bethlehem. Seven older sons were presented, but God chose David, who was tending sheep (1 Samuel 16). David later faced Goliath of Gath, a Philistine champion. He rejected Saul's armour, took his staff, sling and five smooth stones, and struck Goliath on the forehead with one stone (1 Samuel 17). David then used Goliath's sword to kill him. Five stones were selected; only one struck Goliath in the account.\nJonathan, Saul's son, made a covenant with David and gave him his robe, armour, sword, bow and belt (1 Samuel 18:1–4). Saul grew jealous when women sang that Saul had killed thousands but David tens of thousands. David spared Saul in the cave at En-gedi (1 Samuel 24) and again in the wilderness of Ziph (1 Samuel 26). In one episode he cut a corner of Saul's robe; in the other he took Saul's spear and water jar. These are separate events.\n\nDAVID'S KINGSHIP AND THE NATHAN INCIDENT\nDavid first reigned over Judah in Hebron for seven years and six months, then ruled over all Israel from Jerusalem for 33 years, making a total of 40 years (2 Samuel 5:4–5). He captured Jerusalem and brought the ark there (2 Samuel 6). God's promise concerning David's dynasty appears in 2 Samuel 7.\nDavid saw Bathsheba bathing, committed adultery with her, and arranged for Uriah to be placed in dangerous battle where he died (2 Samuel 11). Nathan used a parable about a rich man taking a poor man's only ewe lamb to expose David's sin (2 Samuel 12). David confessed. The child born from that union died; later Bathsheba bore Solomon. Do not confuse Nathan's rebuke with Samuel's rebuke of Saul.\n\nWORKED JAMB-STYLE QUESTIONS — PEOPLE AND DETAILS\nExample (vii): What was Hannah's promise if she had a son?\nSolution: In 1 Samuel 1:11 she promised to give him to the LORD all his life. Answer: To dedicate her son to God's service.\nExample (viii): What was Saul searching for when he first met Samuel?\nSolution: 1 Samuel 9 says he was searching for his father's missing donkeys. Answer: Lost donkeys.\nExample (ix): How many stones did David select before confronting Goliath?\nSolution: 1 Samuel 17:40 says five smooth stones. Answer: Five.\nExample (x): What did David take from Saul during the second occasion when he spared him?\nSolution: In 1 Samuel 26 he took Saul's spear and water jar. Answer: His spear and water jar.\nExample (xi): How long did David reign in total?\nSolution: 2 Samuel 5:4–5 gives seven years and six months in Hebron plus 33 years in Jerusalem, conventionally stated as 40 years. Answer: Forty years.\nExample (xii): Which prophet used the parable of the poor man's lamb to rebuke David?\nSolution: 2 Samuel 12 identifies Nathan. Answer: Nathan.\n\nCHECK YOUR UNDERSTANDING\nDistinguish Eli's sons from Samuel's sons. Explain why the ark's presence did not guarantee victory. Compare Saul's offences in 1 Samuel 13 and 15. Distinguish the robe episode from the spear-and-water-jar episode. Identify who anointed Saul and David and who rebuked David.\n\nQUICK UNDERSTANDING CHECK\nName Eli's sons and Samuel's sons separately. Give two reasons Saul lost divine approval. Explain how David responded to Nathan's rebuke.\n\nPRACTICE QUESTIONS\nUse the Practice Questions button to practise names, events, causes and consequences."},"old":{"title":"Samuel, Saul and David: leadership and obedience","body":"Samuel served God under Eli. Hannah had dedicated him in fulfilment of her vow; Eli later prayed that she would have more children. Eli's sons were Hophni and Phinehas. Samuel's sons were Joel and Abijah, who became judges but took bribes. When Samuel reported judgement on Eli's household, Eli acknowledged the LORD's authority to do what seemed good to him.\n\nIsrael's demand for a king marked a change from the earlier leadership associated with judges. Theocracy means rule under God's authority; it does not mean Israel had no human leaders. Saul's rescue of Jabesh-gilead brought public support. He refused revenge on earlier opponents because the LORD had brought deliverance. In the Amalekite campaign he spared what he was commanded to destroy and afterwards set up a monument to himself. The Kenites were warned to leave because they had shown Israel kindness.\n\nDavid first came to Saul's court to serve as a musician; fighting Goliath belongs later. Saul's promised reward for defeating Goliath included his daughter in marriage. Seven of Jesse's sons passed before Samuel before David was brought from tending sheep. David's hiding places include Adullam and Hachilah; the Ziphites reported his whereabouts to Saul.\n\nDavid refused Abishai's proposal to kill Saul. He would not incur guilt by striking the LORD's anointed and trusted God to determine Saul's end. At Ziklag, he sought God's direction before pursuing the Amalekites. His later kingship faced Absalom's revolt, which forced him to flee Jerusalem.\n\nSamuel's sons differ from Eli's; David's first summons concerns music. Trust never excuses dishonesty, pride or abuse.\n\nAn unnamed man of God warned Eli before Samuel received his message. Saul later sought a medium when threatened by the Philistines. David's spies confirmed Saul's arrival at Ziph. Israel pursued the Philistines after Goliath's fall. At Ziklag David consulted God through the priest and ephod. His servants feared he might harm himself after his child's death. David's repentance after confronting his own wrongdoing contrasts with Saul's self-justification. During Absalom's revolt a soldier respected David's order to spare his son; the victorious troops entered quietly because David mourned. The story does not excuse David's abuse of Uriah.\n\nAt Ashdod, the Philistines suffered tumours after bringing in the ark; possessing a sacred object did not guarantee blessing.\n\nSaul says he feared the people and obeyed their voice. Before consulting the medium, he receives no answer through dreams, Urim or prophets. Their wrongdoing, not merely Samuel's age, explains the complaint.\n\nEli warns his sons about sinning against God, but fails to restrain them. God announces judgement to Samuel over that failure. Saul's servant suggests consulting a seer about the lost donkeys. Amalek's attack after the Exodus explains the later command."}},{"id":"kings-division","revised":true,"new":{"title":"Solomon, the divided kingdom and Josiah's reforms","body":"SOLOMON, THE DIVIDED KINGDOM AND RELIGIOUS REFORMS\n\nINTRODUCTION\nAfter David, Solomon's reign brought great achievements but also problems. His successor Rehoboam faced a divided kingdom. Later kings and prophets challenged idolatry and injustice. Students should distinguish Israel (north) from Judah (south).\n\n1. SOLOMON'S WISDOM (1 Kings 3–4)\nAt Gibeon, Solomon asked God for wisdom to govern the people rather than for wealth or long life. The judgment between two women claiming one child illustrated his discernment. His wisdom became widely known, including to the queen of Sheba.\n\n2. THE TEMPLE (1 Kings 5–8)\nSolomon built the Temple in Jerusalem, with help from Hiram of Tyre, who supplied timber and craftsmen. The Temple became a centre of worship. At its dedication, Solomon prayed that God would hear the prayers of his people. David had prepared for the project, but Solomon built it.\n\n3. SOLOMON'S FAILURES (1 Kings 11)\nSolomon's many foreign wives turned his heart towards other gods. The narrative also describes substantial building and labour demands. His wealth and wisdom did not prevent spiritual compromise.\n\n4. WHY THE KINGDOM DIVIDED (1 Kings 12)\nAfter Solomon's death, the people asked Rehoboam to reduce the heavy burden imposed under his father. Older advisers recommended a gentle answer; younger advisers recommended a harsher response. Rehoboam followed the harsh advice. Ten northern tribes followed Jeroboam; Judah remained under Rehoboam, with Benjamin associated with the southern kingdom. The split also reflects earlier tensions and prophetic judgment.\n\n5. NORTH AND SOUTH: DO NOT CONFUSE THEM\nNorthern kingdom: Israel; first king Jeroboam I; important later capital Samaria.\nSouthern kingdom: Judah; first king Rehoboam; capital Jerusalem.\nJeroboam established worship centres with golden calves at Bethel and Dan to discourage travel to Jerusalem. Rehoboam was Solomon's son; Jeroboam was not.\n\n6. PROPHETS AND REFORM (1 Kings 17–19; 21; 2 Kings 22–23)\nElijah challenged Baal worship during Ahab's reign, including the contest at Mount Carmel. He condemned Ahab's injustice in the seizure of Naboth's vineyard. Much later, Josiah of Judah initiated reforms after the Book of the Law was found in the Temple during repairs. He removed idolatrous practices and renewed covenant commitment. Do not attribute Josiah's reform to Solomon or Jeroboam.\n\n\nDEEPER EXPLANATION — SOLOMON'S ACHIEVEMENTS AND WEAKNESSES\nSolomon succeeded David and asked God for an understanding heart to judge the people (1 Kings 3). He became associated with wisdom, wealth, administration and the building of the Temple in Jerusalem (1 Kings 6–8). The Temple was a central place of worship, while the palace was the king's residence; they were not the same building. Solomon's many foreign marriages and involvement with other gods are presented as causes of religious unfaithfulness in 1 Kings 11.\n\nWHY THE KINGDOM DIVIDED (1 Kings 12)\nHeavy burdens under Solomon contributed to discontent. After Solomon died, the people asked Rehoboam to reduce their labour and taxes. The older advisers recommended a gentle response; younger advisers recommended a harsher one. Rehoboam chose the harsh answer. Ten northern tribes followed Jeroboam, while Rehoboam retained the southern kingdom centred on Judah. Jeroboam and Rehoboam are different men: Jeroboam became king of the north; Rehoboam was Solomon's son.\n\nNORTH AND SOUTH — A CLEAR COMPARISON\nNorthern kingdom: Israel; first king after division—Jeroboam; important later capital—Samaria.\nSouthern kingdom: Judah; first king after division—Rehoboam; capital—Jerusalem.\nJeroboam set up golden calves at Bethel and Dan to discourage travel to Jerusalem for worship. Bethel and Dan were northern worship centres, not two capitals of the southern kingdom.\n\nREFORMS AND THE BOOK OF THE LAW\nHezekiah of Judah is associated with religious reforms and trust in God during the Assyrian threat (2 Kings 18–20). Josiah's reforms followed the discovery of the Book of the Law during Temple repairs (2 Kings 22–23). Josiah renewed covenant commitment and removed idolatrous practices. Both kings were reformers, but the event involving the discovered Book of the Law points particularly to Josiah.\n\nGUIDED WALKTHROUGH — A DIVIDED-KINGDOM QUESTION\nAsk three questions: (1) Is this before or after Solomon's death? (2) Is the question about Israel in the north or Judah in the south? (3) Does it name the harsh response, golden calves or a later reform? These clues distinguish Rehoboam, Jeroboam and Josiah.\n\nADDITIONAL WORKED EXAMPLES\nExample (iv): Who established golden calves at Bethel and Dan?\nSolution: 1 Kings 12:28–30 attributes this action to Jeroboam. Answer: Jeroboam.\nExample (v): Who succeeded Solomon as king?\nSolution: Rehoboam, Solomon's son, succeeded him before the kingdom split (1 Kings 12). Answer: Rehoboam.\nExample (vi): Which ruler's reforms followed the discovery of the Book of the Law?\nSolution: The account appears in 2 Kings 22–23. Answer: Josiah.\n\nEXAM TRAPS AND REMINDERS\nJeroboam was not Solomon's son. Rehoboam's harsh reply helped trigger division; Jeroboam's calves encouraged false worship afterward. Solomon built the Temple; Josiah repaired and reformed worship in a later period.\n\nWORKED JAMB-STYLE EXAMPLES\nExample (i): What did Solomon request at Gibeon?\nSolution: He asked for an understanding heart to judge God's people (1 Kings 3). Answer: Wisdom to govern.\n\nExample (ii): What immediate decision helped cause the division under Rehoboam?\nSolution: He rejected the elders' advice and threatened heavier burdens (1 Kings 12). Answer: His harsh response to the people's request.\n\nExample (iii): Which king reformed worship after the Book of the Law was found?\nSolution: 2 Kings 22–23 names Josiah. The book was found during Temple repairs. Answer: Josiah.\n\n\nDETAILED BIBLE STUDY — SOLOMON'S REIGN\nSolomon was David's son by Bathsheba. He succeeded David after a succession dispute involving Adonijah (1 Kings 1–2). At Gibeon God appeared to Solomon in a dream and invited him to ask for what he wanted. Solomon requested an understanding mind to govern and discern good from evil (1 Kings 3:5–12). Because he did not ask for long life, riches or the life of his enemies, God granted wisdom and also promised riches and honour. His judgement between two women who each claimed a living baby demonstrated his wisdom: he proposed dividing the child, and the true mother begged that the baby be spared.\nSolomon began building the Temple in the fourth year of his reign and completed it in seven years (1 Kings 6:1, 38). His palace complex took thirteen years (1 Kings 7:1). Hiram king of Tyre supplied cedar and cypress timber and skilled assistance (1 Kings 5). At the Temple dedication, the ark was brought into the Most Holy Place, and Solomon prayed for God's attention to the prayers of his people (1 Kings 8).\nThe queen of Sheba visited Solomon to test his wisdom with hard questions (1 Kings 10). Solomon's wealth, trade and building projects were impressive, but 1 Kings 11 reports that his foreign wives turned his heart towards other gods. The account gives 700 wives of royal birth and 300 concubines. His political and religious failures contributed to the later crisis.\n\nTHE DIVISION — PEOPLE, PLACES AND CAUSES\nThe prophet Ahijah of Shiloh met Jeroboam and tore a new garment into twelve pieces, telling Jeroboam to take ten as a sign of the northern tribes that would be given to him (1 Kings 11:29–31). Jeroboam had been an official under Solomon; he was not Solomon's son. After Solomon's death, Rehoboam went to Shechem, where the people asked him to lighten Solomon's heavy yoke. The elders advised him to serve the people and answer gently. The young advisers recommended a threatening reply. Rehoboam said he would make the yoke heavier. The northern tribes rejected his rule (1 Kings 12).\nJeroboam became ruler of the northern kingdom, Israel. Rehoboam remained ruler of Judah in the south, with Jerusalem as its capital. The north's later capital, Samaria, was established by Omri (1 Kings 16:24), not by Jeroboam at the moment of division.\n\nJEROBOAM'S GOLDEN CALVES\nJeroboam feared that if northern Israelites continued to worship in Jerusalem, their loyalty might return to Rehoboam. He therefore made two golden calves and placed one at Bethel and the other at Dan (1 Kings 12:26–30). He appointed priests outside the usual Levitical arrangement and established a festival. This is religious compromise intended to strengthen political control. The golden calves of Jeroboam are not the same event as Aaron's golden calf during Moses' lifetime.\n\nAHAB, JEZEBEL AND LATER KINGS\nAhab ruled the northern kingdom and married Jezebel, daughter of Ethbaal, king of the Sidonians (1 Kings 16:31). Their reign is associated with promotion of Baal worship and the injustice against Naboth. Elijah's contest on Carmel occurred during Ahab's reign. Omri, Ahab's father, purchased the hill on which Samaria was built (1 Kings 16:23–24).\nHezekiah was a reforming king of Judah who removed high places and trusted God during an Assyrian threat (2 Kings 18–19). Josiah was also a king of Judah. During repairs to the Temple, Hilkiah the high priest found the Book of the Law; Shaphan the secretary read it to Josiah (2 Kings 22). Josiah tore his clothes, consulted the prophetess Huldah and led a covenant renewal. He removed idolatrous objects and celebrated the Passover (2 Kings 23). The discovered Book of the Law and consultation with Huldah are distinctive clues to Josiah.\n\nWORKED JAMB-STYLE QUESTIONS — SPECIFIC FACTS\nExample (vii): What did Solomon ask God for at Gibeon?\nSolution: In 1 Kings 3 he asked for an understanding heart to judge the people. Answer: Wisdom or an understanding heart.\nExample (viii): How long did Solomon take to build the Temple?\nSolution: 1 Kings 6:38 states seven years. Answer: Seven years.\nExample (ix): Which prophet tore a garment into twelve pieces?\nSolution: 1 Kings 11 identifies Ahijah of Shiloh. Answer: Ahijah.\nExample (x): Where did Rehoboam meet the people who asked for lighter burdens?\nSolution: 1 Kings 12:1 places the meeting at Shechem. Answer: Shechem.\nExample (xi): Who established Samaria as the northern kingdom's capital?\nSolution: 1 Kings 16:24 identifies Omri. Answer: Omri.\nExample (xii): Who discovered the Book of the Law during Josiah's reign?\nSolution: 2 Kings 22:8 names Hilkiah the high priest. Answer: Hilkiah.\nExample (xiii): Which prophetess was consulted during Josiah's reform?\nSolution: 2 Kings 22:14 identifies Huldah. Answer: Huldah.\n\nCHECK YOUR UNDERSTANDING\nDistinguish Solomon, Rehoboam and Jeroboam. Explain why Rehoboam's reply caused division. Name the two locations of Jeroboam's calves. Explain how Ahijah's torn garment represented the split. Distinguish the reforms of Hezekiah and Josiah.\n\nQUICK UNDERSTANDING CHECK\nCompare Jeroboam and Rehoboam. Explain why Solomon's reign is remembered for both achievement and failure. Name two actions associated with Josiah's reform.\n\nPRACTICE QUESTIONS\nUse the Practice Questions button to practise the divided kingdom, key kings and religious reforms.\n\nSYLLABUS DEEP DIVE — ASA'S REFORMS AND THE RESPONSIBILITY OF LEADERS (1 KINGS 15:9–24; 2 CHRONICLES 14–16)\nAsa succeeded Abijam (Abijah) as king of Judah. First Kings 15:11 says Asa did what was right in the sight of the LORD, as David his ancestor had done. He removed male cult prostitutes and idols, and even removed his grandmother Maacah from her position as queen mother because she had made an abominable image for Asherah; Asa destroyed the image at the brook Kidron (1 Kings 15:12–13). This is an important examination detail: loyalty to God took priority over family influence. The biblical record also qualifies his achievements: the high places were not removed according to 1 Kings 15:14, though Asa's heart was described as wholly true to the LORD. Second Chronicles 14 describes further reforms and a victory over Zerah the Ethiopian/Cushite; chapter 15 records covenant renewal after the prophet Azariah's encouragement. Later, Asa relied on an alliance with Ben-hadad of Syria against Baasha of Israel instead of relying on God, and the seer Hanani rebuked him (2 Chronicles 16:1–10). When diseased in his feet, the Chronicler says he sought help from physicians rather than seeking the LORD (16:12); the lesson is about the king's misplaced reliance, not a general prohibition of medical care. Distinguish Asa's earlier faithfulness from his later failures.\nExample (additional): Why did Asa depose Maacah? \nSolution: First Kings 15:13 says she had made an image for Asherah. Asa removed her from the office of queen mother and destroyed the image. Answer: Her idolatry, not merely a political disagreement.\nQUICK UNDERSTANDING CHECK: State two of Asa's reforms, identify Maacah's offence, and explain why Hanani rebuked Asa.\n"},"old":{"title":"Solomon, the divided kingdom and Josiah's reforms","body":"Solomon succeeded David and built the Temple; David's friend Hiram of Tyre provided materials. Solomon's organisation also involved heavy labour demands. When the people later asked Rehoboam to lighten their burden, they referred to this harsh service. Burdensome policies differ from religious offences.\n\nSolomon's turning towards other gods brought the announcement that much of the kingdom would be torn away. Division awaits his son's reign, retaining territory for David's sake. Rehoboam rejected the elders' advice to answer the people kindly and adopted the younger men's harsh counsel. First Kings 12:15 explains this as a turn of affairs fulfilling God's earlier word. Solomon's son Rehoboam differs from northern king Jeroboam.\n\nJeroboam established golden calves at Bethel and Dan. In later generations, royal misconduct affected the whole nation. Manasseh's shedding of innocent blood is specifically connected with judgement on Judah in 2 Kings 24. The judgement concerns injustice as well as false worship; religion never excuses violence against innocents.\n\nJosiah responded to the discovery of the law book by tearing his clothes and seeking the LORD. Huldah's reply says that his tender heart, humility and tears were the reason the announced disaster would not come during his lifetime. Do not substitute the general fact of his righteous reign for this particular reason.\n\nHis reforms included defiling Topheth to stop the sacrifice of children to Molech. At Bethel, he demolished the altar and high place and destroyed the idolatrous installation. Descriptions of breaking, burning and reducing to dust belong to the wider action; short multiple-choice options can oversimplify it.\n\nJosiah understood the book before seeking guidance; illiteracy was not the issue. Ceremonies never cancel justice.\n\nAsa removed ancestral idols. During Solomon's lifetime, Hadad became an adversary; the tearing away of the kingdom was assigned to his son's reign. Solomon's attempt to kill Jeroboam followed the threat to royal succession. At the Temple dedication Solomon recalled the promise of Davidic continuity with its condition of faithful obedience. Josiah deposed Judah's idolatrous priests; distinguish this from killing priests at the northern shrines. His reforms included Passover observance and sparing the tomb of the earlier prophet who foretold them. Jehoiachin surrendered when Babylon besieged Jerusalem.\n\nAt Gibeon, after a thousand offerings, God appears in a dream. Solomon's threatened division of a baby reveals maternal compassion. Josiah's workmen need no accounting because they act honestly. Asa burns Maacah's Asherah image at Kidron.\n\nJosiah dies fighting Neco at Megiddo. Solomon receives wisdom from God; at Gibeon, long life is conditional on obedience. Josiah renews the covenant after the book's discovery.\n\nSecond Kings 23 describes an exceptionally complete Passover, not proof that none had occurred since Judges. Ahijah foretells the division during Solomon's reign. Hilkiah finds the law book."}},{"id":"elijah-elisha","revised":true,"new":{"title":"Elijah: faithful witness against false worship","body":"ELIJAH AND ELISHA — GOD'S POWER, TRUE WORSHIP AND RESPONSIBLE LEADERSHIP\n\nINTRODUCTION\nA prophet is someone called to communicate God's message. Elijah and Elisha served in the northern kingdom of Israel, but their ministries were not identical. Elijah is especially remembered for confronting idolatry and injustice; Elisha continued the prophetic ministry through teaching, miracles and intervention in community life.\n\n1. WHY ELIJAH CONFRONTED AHAB (1 Kings 16–18)\nKing Ahab and Queen Jezebel promoted Baal worship. Baal was treated by his worshippers as a god associated with fertility and rain. Elijah's announcement of drought challenged the claim that Baal controlled the rain. At Mount Carmel, the prophets of Baal called without receiving an answer; Elijah prayed and the LORD answered with fire. The people were called to choose whom they would worship.\n\n2. DISTINGUISH THE LOCATIONS\nBrook Cherith — Elijah was fed by ravens during the drought (1 Kings 17).\nZarephath — a widow shared food with Elijah; her flour and oil did not run out, and her son was restored to life.\nMount Carmel — the contest concerning true worship took place (1 Kings 18).\nBrook Kishon — the captured prophets of Baal were taken there (1 Kings 18:40).\nUnder a broom tree — Elijah, fleeing Jezebel, expressed despair (1 Kings 19).\nMount Horeb — Elijah encountered God's presence and received renewed direction (1 Kings 19).\nThese are different events. Do not answer every Elijah location question with “Mount Carmel”.\n\n3. NABOTH'S VINEYARD: JUSTICE AND POWER (1 Kings 21)\nNaboth refused to surrender his inherited vineyard. Jezebel arranged false accusations against him; he was killed, and Ahab took possession. Elijah announced God's judgement. The lesson concerns abuse of authority, false testimony, greed and respect for another person's rights.\n\n4. ELIJAH AND ELISHA (2 Kings 2)\nElisha asked for a double portion of Elijah's spirit. This expression is linked to the inheritance of a firstborn son; it is not simply a request for twice as many miracles. Elijah was taken up in a whirlwind, and Elisha continued the prophetic work. Elisha's ministry includes the healing of Naaman (2 Kings 5), the widow's oil (2 Kings 4), and the healing of Jericho's water (2 Kings 2).\n\n5. HOW TO ANSWER QUESTIONS ON THESE PROPHETS\nIdentify the person, the exact event, and the passage. For example, Naaman's healing is associated with Elisha, while the contest with Baal's prophets is associated with Elijah. Never use the general statement “both performed miracles” to ignore the event named in the question.\n\n\nDEEPER TEACHING — WHY THE PROPHETS MATTER\nA prophet did not merely predict the future. Prophets taught the covenant, challenged rulers and helped people recognise God's authority. Elijah's message confronted both false worship and unjust government. Elisha's ministry showed God's care for ordinary families, foreigners and communities as well as kings.\n\nELIJAH'S FEAR AND RENEWED CALL (1 Kings 19)\nAfter the victory at Carmel, Elijah was threatened by Jezebel. His discouragement is not a contradiction of his earlier courage. God provided food and rest and later addressed him at Horeb. The narrative describes wind, earthquake and fire, followed by a quiet sound. The teaching point is that God's guidance is not restricted to spectacular displays. Elijah received further responsibilities rather than being abandoned.\n\nELISHA'S MIRACLES — UNDERSTAND THE NEED AND RESPONSE\nThe widow's oil (2 Kings 4:1–7) addressed a family's debt crisis: borrowed vessels were filled with oil, which could be sold to pay the debt. Naaman, an Aramean commander, was healed of a skin disease after following Elisha's instruction to wash seven times in the Jordan (2 Kings 5). Gehazi's dishonesty in the same chapter contrasts with Naaman's eventual obedience. In the story of the Shunammite woman (2 Kings 4), Elisha's ministry concerns hospitality, the birth of a son and the restoration of the child to life.\nWhen solving a miracle question, identify the need, the prophet, the instruction and the result.\n\nGUIDED WALKTHROUGH — A PROPHET IDENTIFICATION QUESTION\nStep 1: Underline the distinctive event: ravens, Carmel, widow's oil, Naaman or Naboth.\nStep 2: Ask whether the question concerns Elijah's confrontation or Elisha's continuing ministry.\nStep 3: Check the location and the biblical book before selecting an answer.\n\nADDITIONAL WORKED EXAMPLES\nExample (iv): What was Naaman instructed to do to be healed?\nSolution: In 2 Kings 5, Elisha directed him to wash seven times in the River Jordan. Answer: Wash seven times in the Jordan.\nExample (v): What did the widow do with the oil provided through Elisha?\nSolution: In 2 Kings 4:7 she was told to sell the oil, pay her debt and live on what remained. Answer: Sell it to pay her debt and support her family.\nExample (vi): What happened to Elijah after Jezebel threatened him?\nSolution: He fled, became discouraged, received provision and travelled to Horeb (1 Kings 19). Answer: He fled and later received renewed direction from God.\n\nEXAM TRAPS\nDo not assign Naaman's healing to Elijah. Do not confuse Elijah's stay at Cherith with his flight after Carmel. The contest occurred on Carmel; the captured Baal prophets were taken to Kishon.\n\nWORKED JAMB-STYLE EXAMPLES\nExample (i): Where did Elijah challenge the prophets of Baal?\nSolution: Read 1 Kings 18. The contest was held at Mount Carmel. Answer: Mount Carmel.\n\nExample (ii): Why did Elijah condemn Ahab over Naboth's vineyard?\nSolution: Naboth was unjustly killed through a plot so that Ahab could take his inheritance (1 Kings 21). Answer: The abuse of royal power and injustice against Naboth.\n\nExample (iii): Which prophet instructed Naaman to wash in the Jordan?\nSolution: The account is in 2 Kings 5 and concerns Elisha. Answer: Elisha.\n\n\nDETAILED BIBLE STUDY — ELIJAH'S PROPHECY AND THE DROUGHT (1 KINGS 17)\nElijah the Tishbite came from Tishbe in Gilead. He announced to Ahab that there would be neither dew nor rain except by his word (1 Kings 17:1). God told him to hide by the brook Cherith, east of the Jordan. Ravens brought bread and meat in the morning and evening, and he drank from the brook. When the brook dried up, God directed him to Zarephath in Sidon. There a widow was gathering sticks to prepare a last meal for herself and her son. Elijah asked for water and bread, and promised that her jar of flour and jug of oil would not run out until rain returned. Later her son became ill and died; Elijah prayed and the boy revived (1 Kings 17:17–24). Cherith and Zarephath are different places, and the widow's flour-and-oil miracle belongs to Elijah, not Elisha's widow's-oil miracle in 2 Kings 4.\n\nMOUNT CARMEL — THE EXACT ORDER (1 KINGS 18)\nIn the third year of the drought, God instructed Elijah to appear before Ahab and promised rain (1 Kings 18:1). Obadiah, an official in Ahab's palace who feared God, had hidden 100 prophets in two caves, 50 in each, and supplied them with food and water (1 Kings 18:4). Elijah asked Ahab to assemble Israel and the prophets of Baal on Mount Carmel. The text names 450 prophets of Baal and 400 prophets of Asherah in the summons (1 Kings 18:19); the contest itself specifically describes the 450 prophets of Baal.\nTwo bulls were prepared on separate altars without lighting a fire. The prophets of Baal cried out from morning until midday and continued until the time of the evening offering, but received no answer. Elijah repaired the LORD's altar using 12 stones, representing Israel's tribes. He dug a trench and ordered water poured over the sacrifice three times, with four jars on each pouring. Fire from the LORD consumed the offering, wood, stones, dust and water. The people acknowledged the LORD as God. Elijah then had the prophets of Baal seized and taken to the brook Kishon, where they were killed (1 Kings 18:40). He prayed for rain: his servant looked toward the sea seven times and finally saw a small cloud like a man's hand; heavy rain followed (1 Kings 18:41–45).\nImportant distinctions: Mount Carmel = contest; Kishon = execution of Baal's prophets; seven times = servant's observation for rain; 12 stones = tribes; three pourings = water over the sacrifice.\n\nELIJAH AT HOREB AND THE CALL OF ELISHA (1 KINGS 19)\nJezebel threatened Elijah after Carmel. He fled to Beersheba and then into the wilderness, where he prayed to die. An angel provided food and water twice. Strengthened by the food, he travelled 40 days and 40 nights to Horeb. There came a great wind, an earthquake and fire, but the LORD was not described as being in these; a low whisper followed. God instructed Elijah to anoint Hazael over Syria, Jehu over Israel and Elisha as prophet in his place. Elijah found Elisha ploughing with 12 yoke of oxen and threw his mantle over him (1 Kings 19:19). Elisha said farewell to his parents, sacrificed his oxen and followed Elijah.\n\nNABOTH'S VINEYARD — THE LEGAL WRONG (1 KINGS 21)\nAhab wanted Naboth's vineyard near his palace in Jezreel, offering money or another vineyard. Naboth refused because it was his ancestral inheritance. Jezebel wrote letters in Ahab's name, arranged a fast and seated Naboth prominently. Two worthless men falsely accused Naboth of cursing God and the king; he was stoned to death. Ahab then went to possess the vineyard. Elijah pronounced judgement on Ahab's house and Jezebel. When Ahab humbled himself, the announced disaster on his house was postponed beyond his lifetime (1 Kings 21:27–29). The offence was not merely Ahab's desire for land: it involved false testimony, murder and unlawful seizure.\n\nELISHA'S SUCCESSION AND MIRACLES (2 KINGS 2–6)\nElijah and Elisha travelled from Gilgal to Bethel, Jericho and the Jordan (2 Kings 2). Elijah struck the Jordan with his mantle and the water parted. Elisha asked for a double portion of Elijah's spirit; Elijah was taken up in a whirlwind, accompanied by a chariot and horses of fire. Elisha picked up the fallen mantle and parted the Jordan again. Fifty men of the sons of the prophets searched for Elijah for three days but did not find him. Elisha healed Jericho's water using salt in a new bowl (2 Kings 2:19–22).\nIn 2 Kings 4:1–7, a prophet's widow feared that a creditor would take her two sons as slaves. Elisha told her to borrow many empty vessels, shut the door and pour her small supply of oil into them. The oil stopped only when there were no more vessels; she sold it to pay her debts. In Shunem, a hospitable woman prepared a room for Elisha; her son later died and was restored to life (2 Kings 4:8–37).\nNaaman, commander of the Syrian army, suffered from a skin disease. A captive Israelite girl told his household about the prophet in Samaria. Elisha sent instructions for Naaman to wash in the Jordan seven times. At first Naaman was angry, mentioning the rivers Abana and Pharpar of Damascus, but his servants persuaded him to obey. He was healed. Elisha refused payment; Gehazi secretly obtained gifts and was punished with Naaman's disease (2 Kings 5). In 2 Kings 6, Elisha caused a borrowed iron axe-head to float.\n\nWORKED JAMB-STYLE EXAMPLES — EVENTS, NUMBERS AND PLACES\nExample (vii): How many stones did Elijah use to repair the altar on Carmel?\nSolution: 1 Kings 18:31 says 12, representing the tribes of Israel. Answer: Twelve.\nExample (viii): How many times did Elijah's servant look toward the sea before reporting a cloud?\nSolution: 1 Kings 18:43–44 records seven observations. Answer: Seven.\nExample (ix): Who hid 100 prophets in two caves?\nSolution: 1 Kings 18:4 identifies Obadiah, Ahab's palace official. Answer: Obadiah.\nExample (x): Where did Elijah travel for 40 days and nights?\nSolution: 1 Kings 19:8 says Horeb, the mountain of God. Answer: Horeb.\nExample (xi): What did Elisha ask Elijah for?\nSolution: 2 Kings 2:9 records a double portion of his spirit. Answer: A double portion of Elijah's spirit.\nExample (xii): Why was Gehazi punished?\nSolution: He lied to obtain payment from Naaman after Elisha had refused it (2 Kings 5:20–27). Answer: Dishonesty and greed.\nExample (xiii): How many times was Naaman told to wash in the Jordan?\nSolution: 2 Kings 5:10 specifies seven. Answer: Seven.\n\nTEST YOUR UNDERSTANDING\nRetell the Carmel contest in order, including the 12 stones and the water. Distinguish the 40-day journey to Horeb from the seven observations for rain. Explain the injustice to Naboth. Compare Elijah's widow at Zarephath with Elisha's widow who borrowed vessels. Name the rivers Naaman preferred to the Jordan.\n\nQUICK UNDERSTANDING CHECK\nExplain why the drought challenged Baal worship. Distinguish Mount Carmel from Brook Kishon. Give one example of injustice and one example of Elisha's ministry.\n\nPRACTICE QUESTIONS\nUse the lesson's Practice JAMB Past Questions button to test the events and their biblical settings.\n\nADDITIONAL BIBLICAL CONTEXT AND EXAM DISTINCTIONS\nElijah confronted the worship of Baal promoted under Ahab and Jezebel. The drought announced in 1 Kings 17 forms part of that confrontation. Elijah later explained that Israel's trouble came from abandoning the LORD's commands and following the Baals. God's provision through ravens sustained the prophet; it was not the reason he announced drought.\n\nAt Mount Carmel, Elijah challenged the prophets of Baal and called the people to decide whom they would serve. The LORD answered with fire. Afterwards Elijah brought the captured prophets down to the brook Kishon. Remember the two locations: Carmel was the contest site; Kishon was the place of their killing. A question asking for one event's location should not automatically receive the most famous place in the story.\n\nJezebel then threatened Elijah's life. He fled into the wilderness and prayed beneath a broom tree that he might die. This episode is different from his earlier stay at the brook Cherith. God met the exhausted prophet with provision and further direction. The account recognises fear and discouragement even in a person who had acted courageously.\n\nNaboth's vineyard shows the relationship between false worship, royal power and injustice. Naboth refused to surrender his ancestral inheritance. Jezebel arranged false testimony and his death, allowing Ahab to take possession. Elijah announced judgement on Ahab's house, including dogs licking Ahab's blood. When Ahab humbled himself in sackcloth and fasting, the disaster on his house was postponed until his son's days. The story does not say that Ahab returned the vineyard.\n\nAhab's dead in the country are associated with birds; those in the city with dogs. Prophetic power never excuses exploitation.\n\nObadiah's reverence for God included hiding and feeding a hundred prophets during Jezebel's persecution, not hiding Elijah. When Elijah asked how long the people would waver between two opinions, they initially answered nothing. Naboth's accusers falsely charged him with cursing God and the king. Distinguish an accusation used to secure a conviction from the person's actual conduct. Jezebel's threat first made Elijah flee for his life; his day's journey and stay in a cave were subsequent events. Sequence matters when an examination asks for the immediate response.\n\nAfter Naboth refuses the exchange, Ahab first returns home vexed and sullen; Jezebel subsequently arranges the killing.\n\nFirst Kings 18 summons 450 Baal prophets and 400 Asherah prophets; it reports the Baal prophets' execution, not execution of all 400 Asherah prophets."},"old":{"title":"Elijah: faithful witness against false worship","body":"Elijah confronted the worship of Baal promoted under Ahab and Jezebel. The drought announced in 1 Kings 17 forms part of that confrontation. Elijah later explained that Israel's trouble came from abandoning the LORD's commands and following the Baals. God's provision through ravens sustained the prophet; it was not the reason he announced drought.\n\nAt Mount Carmel, Elijah challenged the prophets of Baal and called the people to decide whom they would serve. The LORD answered with fire. Afterwards Elijah brought the captured prophets down to the brook Kishon. Remember the two locations: Carmel was the contest site; Kishon was the place of their killing. A question asking for one event's location should not automatically receive the most famous place in the story.\n\nJezebel then threatened Elijah's life. He fled into the wilderness and prayed beneath a broom tree that he might die. This episode is different from his earlier stay at the brook Cherith. God met the exhausted prophet with provision and further direction. The account recognises fear and discouragement even in a person who had acted courageously.\n\nNaboth's vineyard shows the relationship between false worship, royal power and injustice. Naboth refused to surrender his ancestral inheritance. Jezebel arranged false testimony and his death, allowing Ahab to take possession. Elijah announced judgement on Ahab's house, including dogs licking Ahab's blood. When Ahab humbled himself in sackcloth and fasting, the disaster on his house was postponed until his son's days. The story does not say that Ahab returned the vineyard.\n\nAhab's dead in the country are associated with birds; those in the city with dogs. Prophetic power never excuses exploitation.\n\nObadiah's reverence for God included hiding and feeding a hundred prophets during Jezebel's persecution, not hiding Elijah. When Elijah asked how long the people would waver between two opinions, they initially answered nothing. Naboth's accusers falsely charged him with cursing God and the king. Distinguish an accusation used to secure a conviction from the person's actual conduct. Jezebel's threat first made Elijah flee for his life; his day's journey and stay in a cave were subsequent events. Sequence matters when an examination asks for the immediate response.\n\nAfter Naboth refuses the exchange, Ahab first returns home vexed and sullen; Jezebel subsequently arranges the killing.\n\nFirst Kings 18 summons 450 Baal prophets and 400 Asherah prophets; it reports the Baal prophets' execution, not execution of all 400 Asherah prophets."}},{"id":"prophetic-justice","revised":true,"new":{"title":"Prophets, true worship and social justice","body":"PROPHETS, TRUE WORSHIP AND SOCIAL JUSTICE — COMPLETE LESSON\n\nINTRODUCTION\nBiblical prophets were messengers of God. Their work included warning, teaching, correcting rulers and calling people to live faithfully. Social justice means treating people fairly, especially those who are vulnerable to exploitation. In the prophetic books, religious ceremonies without justice and obedience are strongly criticised.\n\n1. AMOS: JUSTICE MUST ACCOMPANY WORSHIP\nAmos preached against exploitation, dishonest trade, corrupt courts and comfortable people who ignored the suffering of the poor. In Amos 5:24, justice and righteousness are compared to flowing water. The image teaches that fairness should be continuous, not an occasional performance.\nAmos 2:6 — the righteous are sold for silver and the needy for a pair of sandals.\nAmos 4:1 — the “cows of Bashan” describe wealthy women in Samaria who oppress the poor.\nAmos 5:11 — people who trample the poor are warned about houses they will not enjoy.\nAmos 8:4–6 — dishonest trading practices and exploitation are condemned.\nAmos 8:11 — a famine of hearing the LORD's words is announced. This is not an ordinary shortage of food.\n\n2. HOSEA: COVENANT LOVE AND FAITHFULNESS\nHosea used marriage imagery to explain Israel's unfaithfulness and God's continuing call to return. Covenant love includes loyalty and mercy. Hosea 6:6 places steadfast love and knowledge of God above empty ritual. It does not mean worship is always wrong; it means ritual cannot replace faithful conduct.\n\n3. ISAIAH AND MICAH: RIGHT LIVING\nIsaiah 1:16–17 calls people to stop doing evil, seek justice, correct oppression and defend the vulnerable. Micah 6:8 summarises what God requires: do justice, love kindness and walk humbly with God. Learn the specific prophet and passage rather than treating every statement about justice as interchangeable.\n\n4. TRUE AND FALSE PROPHECY\nA prophet's message should not be judged merely by how pleasing it sounds. Jeremiah challenged false assurances of peace while wrongdoing continued. Students should distinguish a genuine warning that calls people to obedience from a flattering message that ignores injustice.\n\n5. EXAM TRAPS\nThe “cows of Bashan” are not the cattle offered in sacrifice; the phrase criticises oppressive luxury (Amos 4:1).\nThe famine in Amos 8:11 is a famine of hearing God's word.\nAmos, Hosea, Isaiah and Micah all address wrongdoing, but the wording and setting of each passage matter.\n\n\nDEEPER TEACHING — WHAT MAKES WORSHIP GENUINE?\nWorship includes prayer, sacrifice and religious gatherings, but the prophets insist that outward actions must agree with daily behaviour. Someone who gives offerings while cheating customers or denying justice has not understood the covenant's moral demands. This does not mean that Amos or Isaiah rejected all worship; they rejected worship used to hide injustice.\n\nAMOS: IDENTIFY THE SPECIFIC WRONG\nDishonest scales and measures cheat buyers. Selling people for debts treats human beings as objects. Corrupt courts deny fair treatment to the poor. The “cows of Bashan” image condemns privileged people who benefit from oppression. These examples help explain why Amos demands justice that flows continuously rather than occasional acts of charity.\nA question about Amos 8:11 asks about a famine of hearing God's word. A question about Amos 8:4–6 asks about exploitation in trade. The chapter number alone is not enough; read the verse.\n\nHOSEA, ISAIAH AND MICAH — SIMILAR THEMES, DIFFERENT WORDS\nHosea's marriage imagery represents covenant unfaithfulness and continuing divine love. Isaiah 1 urges the people to learn to do good, seek justice and defend the vulnerable. Micah 6:8 summarises justice, mercy and humility. All three concern faithful living, but JAMB may ask which prophet used a particular expression.\n\nGUIDED WALKTHROUGH — A QUOTATION QUESTION\nStep 1: Read the key phrase rather than only the broad theme.\nStep 2: Match it to its prophet: “cows of Bashan”—Amos; “do justice, love kindness”—Micah; “learn to do good; seek justice”—Isaiah.\nStep 3: Check the action condemned or required.\nStep 4: Explain why a similar but differently worded option is less accurate.\n\nADDITIONAL WORKED EXAMPLES\nExample (iv): Why did Amos condemn dishonest trading?\nSolution: It exploited vulnerable people and violated the demand for justice (Amos 8:4–6). Answer: It was unfair and oppressive.\nExample (v): Which prophet taught that people should do justice, love kindness and walk humbly with God?\nSolution: This wording is in Micah 6:8. Answer: Micah.\nExample (vi): Does Isaiah 1 teach that sacrifices alone guarantee God's approval?\nSolution: No. Isaiah calls people to stop wrongdoing and defend the oppressed. Answer: No; worship must be joined to just conduct.\n\nEXAM TRAPS\nSocial justice is not simply giving money away; it includes fairness in courts, business and leadership. Do not mix Amos 8's dishonest trade with the famine of hearing God's word.\n\nWORKED JAMB-STYLE EXAMPLES\nExample (i): What is the famine described in Amos 8:11?\nSolution: The verse identifies a famine of hearing the words of the LORD, not a lack of bread or water. Answer: A shortage of hearing God's word.\n\nExample (ii): What does Micah 6:8 require?\nSolution: The passage names justice, kindness and humble walking with God. Answer: Just conduct, mercy and humility before God.\n\nExample (iii): Who are the “cows of Bashan” in Amos 4:1?\nSolution: The prophet addresses privileged women of Samaria who oppress the poor. Answer: Wealthy, oppressive women of Samaria.\n\n\nDETAILED BIBLE STUDY — AMOS: WHO HE WAS AND WHY HE SPOKE\nAmos was from Tekoa in Judah, but his messages concerned especially the northern kingdom of Israel. Amos 1:1 places his ministry in the days of Uzziah king of Judah and Jeroboam son of Joash king of Israel, two years before an earthquake. In Amos 7:14–15 he said he was neither a prophet nor a prophet's son by profession: he was a herdsman and dresser of sycamore figs, and the LORD took him from following the flock. At Bethel, Amaziah the priest opposed him and told him to return to Judah (Amos 7:10–17). This is important because Amos was not simply an official court prophet delivering messages that pleased the king.\n\nAMOS' CHARGES AGAINST ISRAEL (AMOS 2, 4, 5 AND 8)\nAmos condemned selling the righteous for silver and the needy for a pair of sandals (2:6), mistreatment of the poor and abuse of vulnerable people (2:7), and exploitation through unfair taxes and corrupt justice (5:10–12). Amos 4:1 addresses the “cows of Bashan” on Mount Samaria: privileged women benefiting from oppression and demanding luxury. In Amos 8:4–6 merchants eagerly await the end of religious observances so they can sell grain dishonestly: they make the ephah small (giving less grain), the shekel great (charging more), and use false balances. Explain how each device cheats a buyer rather than merely memorising “dishonest scales.”\nAmos 5:21–24 says that God rejects festivals, assemblies and offerings when justice is absent. The positive demand is that justice should roll down like waters and righteousness like an ever-flowing stream. Amos 8:11 announces a famine not of bread or thirst for water, but of hearing the words of the LORD. These two water-related images refer to different ideas: flowing justice and a famine of God's word.\n\nAMOS' VISIONS — WHAT EACH SYMBOL MEANS\nAmos 7:1–3: locusts threatened the land; Amos interceded and the LORD relented.\nAmos 7:4–6: judgement by fire; Amos again interceded and the LORD relented.\nAmos 7:7–9: a plumb line showed that Israel was being measured against God's standard and judgement would not simply be withdrawn.\nAmos 8:1–3: a basket of summer fruit signified that Israel's end was near. The Hebrew wordplay links ripe fruit with the end.\nAmos 9:1–4: a vision of the LORD beside the altar announced unavoidable judgement.\nA plumb line is a builder's tool for checking whether a wall is straight. It is not a measuring device for the amount of rain.\n\nHOSEA'S MARRIAGE IMAGE (HOSEA 1–3)\nHosea was instructed to marry Gomer, daughter of Diblaim. The marriage imagery represents the covenant relationship between God and an unfaithful Israel. The children are named Jezreel, Lo-ruhamah (“not pitied” or “no mercy”) and Lo-ammi (“not my people”). Their names convey messages of judgement; later passages offer hope of renewed relationship. Hosea 6:6 stresses steadfast love and knowledge of God over sacrifices offered without faithfulness. Hosea 11 depicts God's care for Israel using the image of a parent teaching a child to walk. Do not confuse Hosea's symbolic marriage with Jeremiah's new covenant.\n\nISAIAH, MICAH AND JEREMIAH — DISTINCTIVE TEACHINGS\nIsaiah 1:10–17 condemns worship that is accompanied by bloodshed and injustice, telling the people to wash, make themselves clean, learn to do good, seek justice, rescue the oppressed, defend the orphan and plead for the widow. Isaiah 6 describes his call: he saw the LORD enthroned, seraphim cried “Holy, holy, holy,” and a seraph touched his lips with a burning coal. Isaiah responded, “Here am I; send me.” The coal belongs to Isaiah's call, not Ezekiel's.\nMicah 6:6–8 asks whether offerings alone can please God, then answers that God requires justice, kindness and humble walking with him. Micah 3 condemns rulers who distort justice for gain. Jeremiah 7 warns that merely trusting the Temple while oppressing others is false security. In Jeremiah 26, opposition arose because of his warning concerning the Temple.\n\nWORKED JAMB-STYLE EXAMPLES — QUOTATIONS AND SYMBOLS\nExample (vii): What occupation did Amos identify before his call?\nSolution: Amos 7:14–15 describes him as a herdsman and dresser of sycamore figs. Answer: Herdsman and dresser of sycamore figs.\nExample (viii): Which priest opposed Amos at Bethel?\nSolution: Amos 7:10 names Amaziah. Answer: Amaziah.\nExample (ix): What did the plumb line in Amos 7 signify?\nSolution: It represented God's assessment of Israel and the coming judgement. Answer: God's standard of judgement.\nExample (x): What was Amos' fourth vision?\nSolution: Amos 8:1–3 describes a basket of summer fruit. Answer: A basket of summer fruit.\nExample (xi): Who was Hosea's wife?\nSolution: Hosea 1:3 names Gomer, daughter of Diblaim. Answer: Gomer.\nExample (xii): Which prophet's lips were touched with a burning coal?\nSolution: Isaiah 6:6–7 describes this event. Answer: Isaiah.\nExample (xiii): What did Amos mean by making the ephah small and shekel great?\nSolution: Traders reduced the quantity delivered while increasing the amount charged (Amos 8:5). Answer: Cheating buyers in trade.\n\nTEST YOUR UNDERSTANDING\nName Amos' five visions in order and explain the plumb line. Explain two ways dishonest traders exploited customers. Identify Hosea's three symbolic children. Distinguish Amos 5:24 from Amos 8:11. Explain why Isaiah rejected worship without justice.\n\nQUICK UNDERSTANDING CHECK\nExplain social justice in your own words. Name two practices condemned by Amos. Distinguish outward worship from covenant faithfulness.\n\nPRACTICE QUESTIONS\nUse the linked JAMB questions to practise identifying each prophet and the correct passage.\n\nADDITIONAL BIBLICAL CONTEXT AND EXAM DISTINCTIONS\nA biblical prophet speaks God's message to the people. Prediction can be part of that work, but the role is not merely forecasting future events. Prophets call people back to covenant faithfulness and challenge wrongdoing in public and private life. Amos is commonly described in school accounts as an early writing prophet; that description should not be treated as a precise, undisputed date for every prophetic book.\n\nAmos rebukes wealth obtained by exploitation. The “cows of Bashan” in Amos 4:1 are wealthy women of Samaria who oppress the poor. The image draws on well-fed cattle, exposing comfort supported by another person's suffering. In Amos 5:11, people who trample on the poor build stone houses but will not enjoy them. A comparable Nigerian classroom example is a powerful person taking traders' goods unfairly and then displaying the proceeds as success.\n\nRead accusations in their particular passages. Selling the righteous for silver occurs in Amos 2:6. Turning justice into poison and boasting of strength occur near the threat of an invading nation in Amos 6:12–14. All are genuine charges, but a question about a specific threat should not casually mix their contexts. Amos 8 announces a famine of hearing the LORD's word, not merely a shortage of food or water.\n\nIsaiah's vision is introduced as concerning Judah and Jerusalem. He tells the people to cease evil, learn good, seek justice, defend the fatherless and plead for the widow. Sacrifices without justice do not answer that demand. Religious activity must not become a cover for mistreating vulnerable people.\n\nHosea uses marriage imagery to portray Israel's covenant unfaithfulness. References to harlotry express pursuit of other gods and misplaced loyalties. In Hosea 4:10, eating without satisfaction is one announced punishment. Interpret the image within the prophet's message rather than using it to make a general rule about innocent children.\n\nCommon trap: several options may state true biblical ideas, but only some belong to the passage being tested.\n\nAmos 2:7 joins trampling the poor with a man and his father going to the same woman, profaning God's holy name. An option that only names economic abuse does not reproduce every detail of that charge. Amaziah accused Amos of conspiracy before Jeroboam. Jeremiah contrasts faithless Israel with treacherous or false Judah; Judah's outward return was not wholehearted. Isaiah dates his throne vision to the year King Uzziah died. The commission warns of unresponsive hearing and devastation, so it must not be reduced to a promise of immediate national improvement.\n\nJeremiah first sees an almond rod, then a boiling pot. Hosea names Uzziah, Jotham, Ahaz and Hezekiah as Judah's kings during his ministry. Amos's blight and mildew did not produce repentance."},"old":{"title":"Prophets, true worship and social justice","body":"A biblical prophet speaks God's message to the people. Prediction can be part of that work, but the role is not merely forecasting future events. Prophets call people back to covenant faithfulness and challenge wrongdoing in public and private life. Amos is commonly described in school accounts as an early writing prophet; that description should not be treated as a precise, undisputed date for every prophetic book.\n\nAmos rebukes wealth obtained by exploitation. The “cows of Bashan” in Amos 4:1 are wealthy women of Samaria who oppress the poor. The image draws on well-fed cattle, exposing comfort supported by another person's suffering. In Amos 5:11, people who trample on the poor build stone houses but will not enjoy them. A comparable Nigerian classroom example is a powerful person taking traders' goods unfairly and then displaying the proceeds as success.\n\nRead accusations in their particular passages. Selling the righteous for silver occurs in Amos 2:6. Turning justice into poison and boasting of strength occur near the threat of an invading nation in Amos 6:12–14. All are genuine charges, but a question about a specific threat should not casually mix their contexts. Amos 8 announces a famine of hearing the LORD's word, not merely a shortage of food or water.\n\nIsaiah's vision is introduced as concerning Judah and Jerusalem. He tells the people to cease evil, learn good, seek justice, defend the fatherless and plead for the widow. Sacrifices without justice do not answer that demand. Religious activity must not become a cover for mistreating vulnerable people.\n\nHosea uses marriage imagery to portray Israel's covenant unfaithfulness. References to harlotry express pursuit of other gods and misplaced loyalties. In Hosea 4:10, eating without satisfaction is one announced punishment. Interpret the image within the prophet's message rather than using it to make a general rule about innocent children.\n\nCommon trap: several options may state true biblical ideas, but only some belong to the passage being tested.\n\nAmos 2:7 joins trampling the poor with a man and his father going to the same woman, profaning God's holy name. An option that only names economic abuse does not reproduce every detail of that charge. Amaziah accused Amos of conspiracy before Jeroboam. Jeremiah contrasts faithless Israel with treacherous or false Judah; Judah's outward return was not wholehearted. Isaiah dates his throne vision to the year King Uzziah died. The commission warns of unresponsive hearing and devastation, so it must not be reduced to a promise of immediate national improvement.\n\nJeremiah first sees an almond rod, then a boiling pot. Hosea names Uzziah, Jotham, Ahaz and Hezekiah as Judah's kings during his ministry. Amos's blight and mildew did not produce repentance."}},{"id":"repentance-restoration","revised":true,"new":{"title":"Repentance, personal responsibility and restoration","body":"REPENTANCE, PERSONAL RESPONSIBILITY AND RESTORATION — COMPLETE LESSON\n\nINTRODUCTION\nRepentance means turning away from wrongdoing and returning to God in thought and action. It is more than feeling sorry. Restoration means rebuilding a damaged relationship or community. The prophets taught that God's judgement is serious, but they also proclaimed opportunities for genuine change.\n\n1. HOSEA: RETURN TO THE LORD (Hosea 14)\nHosea calls Israel to confess sin, stop trusting foreign powers as saviours and abandon idols. His teaching joins honest words to a changed life. Hosea 14:9 explains that the LORD's ways are right: the upright walk in them, while rebels stumble. The difference is the response of the people, not a change in God's ways.\n\n2. JEREMIAH: CHANGE OF HEART AND A NEW COVENANT\nJeremiah criticised empty confidence in the Temple when people's behaviour contradicted their worship (Jeremiah 7). Jeremiah 31:31–34 promises a new covenant: God's law written on hearts, knowledge of God and forgiveness. The promise concerns inner transformation, not merely replacing one building or ruler with another.\nJeremiah 3:15 describes shepherds who lead with knowledge and understanding. In this passage “shepherds” refers to leaders, not only people caring for animals.\n\n3. EZEKIEL: PERSONAL RESPONSIBILITY (Ezekiel 18)\nEzekiel rejects the idea that an individual must automatically bear guilt for another person's wrongdoing. Each person is responsible for their own conduct. The wicked person who turns away from wrongdoing may live; a righteous person who turns to evil cannot rely on past good behaviour as an excuse.\nThe proverb about fathers eating sour grapes and children's teeth being set on edge expresses the attitude Ezekiel corrects.\n\n4. JONAH: REPENTANCE AT NINEVEH (Jonah 3–4)\nJonah announced judgement against Nineveh. Its people responded with fasting and turning from evil, and God did not bring the threatened destruction. Jonah was displeased by God's mercy. The account teaches both the seriousness of repentance and the breadth of divine compassion.\n\n5. HOW TO DISTINGUISH THE PASSAGES\nHosea 14 — return, confession and rejection of idols.\nJeremiah 31 — the new covenant and inward law.\nEzekiel 18 — individual responsibility and the possibility of change.\nJonah 3 — Nineveh's repentance.\nThese themes overlap, but the passages answer different examination questions.\n\n\nDEEPER TEACHING — REPENTANCE IS A PROCESS\nRecognising wrongdoing is the beginning, not the end. Genuine repentance involves admitting the wrong, turning from it and choosing different conduct. Restoration describes the repair that follows: renewed trust, forgiveness and sometimes rebuilding damaged community life. A person may be forgiven and still need to repair harm caused to others.\n\nHOSEA: WHY TURNING BACK MATTERS\nHosea 14 asks the people to return to the LORD, speak honestly about their sin and abandon dependence on idols and military alliances. The chapter describes God's healing and renewed blessing. The message combines divine mercy with a real change in allegiance; it is not a promise that saying words without changing behaviour is enough.\n\nJEREMIAH'S NEW COVENANT — WHAT IS NEW?\nThe old covenant relationship had been repeatedly broken. Jeremiah 31:31–34 promises a transformed relationship in which God's law is written on the heart. The passage emphasises inner knowledge of God and forgiveness. The new covenant is not merely a new political ruler, another Temple building or a different set of ceremonial objects.\n\nEZEKIEL 18 — FOLLOW THE ARGUMENT\nThe people quoted a proverb about parents eating sour grapes and children suffering the taste. Ezekiel challenges using inherited guilt as an excuse. He describes a righteous person, a wicked son and a righteous grandson to show that each person's conduct matters. A wicked person who turns away from wrongdoing may live; a person who abandons righteousness cannot rely on past good actions. The emphasis is responsibility and the possibility of genuine change.\n\nJONAH — REPENTANCE BEYOND ISRAEL\nNineveh was a foreign city. Its people responded to Jonah's warning, and the threatened destruction did not take place. Jonah's anger highlights a question about mercy: should compassion be limited to the prophet's own people? The book presents God's concern for people beyond Israel.\n\nGUIDED WALKTHROUGH — IDENTIFY THE PROPHET\nIf the question names the sour-grapes proverb, think Ezekiel 18. If it names law written on hearts, think Jeremiah 31. If it names Nineveh and Jonah's displeasure, think Jonah 3–4. If it asks for Israel to return and renounce idols, think Hosea 14.\n\nADDITIONAL WORKED EXAMPLES\nExample (iv): What did Ezekiel reject in the sour-grapes proverb?\nSolution: Ezekiel 18 challenges the idea that a person is automatically punished for another's wrongdoing regardless of their own actions. Answer: The denial of individual moral responsibility.\nExample (v): What does the law written on the heart signify?\nSolution: Jeremiah 31 describes inward knowledge and obedience rather than merely outward conformity. Answer: An inwardly transformed relationship with God.\nExample (vi): Why was Jonah unhappy after Nineveh repented?\nSolution: Jonah 4 shows his displeasure at God's mercy toward the city. Answer: He resented God's decision to spare Nineveh.\n\nEXAM TRAPS\nRepentance is not identical to regret. Individual responsibility does not mean that people's actions have no effects on others. Jeremiah's new covenant and Ezekiel's personal responsibility are related but distinct teachings.\n\nWORKED JAMB-STYLE EXAMPLES\nExample (i): What is central to the new covenant in Jeremiah 31?\nSolution: Jeremiah says God's law will be written on people's hearts and that God will forgive their sins. Answer: Inward obedience and forgiveness.\n\nExample (ii): According to Ezekiel 18, can a wicked person change their outcome?\nSolution: Yes. The chapter says that a wicked person who turns away from evil and does what is right may live. Answer: Yes, through genuine repentance.\n\nExample (iii): Why was Nineveh spared after Jonah's warning?\nSolution: Jonah 3 describes the people's response to the warning and their turning from evil. Answer: They repented.\n\n\nDETAILED BIBLE STUDY — WHAT REPENTANCE REQUIRES\nRepentance involves recognition of wrongdoing, confession and a changed direction. Biblical prophets do not present it as merely feeling bad. In Hosea 14:1–3, Israel is told to return to the LORD, bring words of repentance, renounce reliance on Assyria and war horses, and stop calling handmade objects gods. Hosea 14:4–8 describes God's healing of their unfaithfulness and renewed growth through images of dew, lily, cedar and olive tree. The promise of restoration follows a call to turn away from false sources of security.\n\nJEREMIAH'S CALL AND TEMPLE SERMON\nJeremiah 1:4–10 says God knew Jeremiah before his birth and appointed him a prophet to the nations. Jeremiah protested that he was only a youth and did not know how to speak. God touched his mouth and promised to put words there. He was appointed to uproot and tear down, destroy and overthrow, build and plant. In Jeremiah 7:1–15, he stood at the gate of the LORD's house and challenged people who trusted the repeated phrase “the temple of the LORD” while stealing, murdering, committing adultery and worshipping other gods. The warning was not against the Temple itself but against using it as a substitute for justice and obedience.\n\nTHE NEW COVENANT IN JEREMIAH 31:31–34\nGod promised a new covenant with the house of Israel and the house of Judah, unlike the covenant broken after the Exodus. Its distinguishing features are: God's law written within the people and on their hearts; the relationship “I will be their God, and they shall be my people”; knowledge of God; and forgiveness of sin. This is not the rainbow covenant with Noah or the sign of circumcision with Abraham. Jeremiah 18 uses a potter and clay: the potter reshapes a spoiled vessel, illustrating God's authority and the importance of a nation's response to warning.\n\nEZEKIEL'S CALL AND SYMBOLIC ACTIONS\nEzekiel was among the exiles by the river Chebar when he received visions of God's glory (Ezekiel 1). In Ezekiel 2–3 he was called to speak to a rebellious house and was given a scroll to eat; it tasted sweet as honey. He was appointed a watchman responsible for warning people (Ezekiel 3:16–21; 33:1–9). The watchman image concerns the duty to deliver God's warning; it does not mean that the prophet can force people to obey.\nEzekiel 18 challenges the proverb “The fathers have eaten sour grapes, and the children's teeth are set on edge.” The chapter gives examples of a righteous father, a wicked son and a righteous grandson. Its central teaching is that each person is accountable for their own conduct, and someone who turns away from wrongdoing can live. Ezekiel 36:26 promises a new heart and a new spirit. Ezekiel 37:1–14 presents a valley of dry bones: bones come together, receive flesh and breath, and become living people. God explains that the vision signifies the restoration of the house of Israel from hopelessness. It is not merely a story about a cemetery.\n\nJONAH — THE ENTIRE SEQUENCE (JONAH 1–4)\nGod told Jonah to go to Nineveh and speak against its wickedness. Jonah instead went down to Joppa and boarded a ship bound for Tarshish. A great storm arose; the sailors cast lots and the lot fell on Jonah. At his request they threw him into the sea, and the sea became calm. A great fish swallowed Jonah, and he was inside it for three days and three nights (Jonah 1:17). He prayed, was released onto dry land and received the command again. Jonah proclaimed that Nineveh would be overthrown in 40 days (Jonah 3:4). From the king downwards, the people fasted, wore sackcloth and turned from evil; God did not bring the threatened destruction.\nJonah was angry at God's mercy. God appointed a plant to shade him, then a worm that attacked it, and a scorching east wind. Jonah cared deeply about the plant, and God used the incident to challenge his lack of compassion for Nineveh's inhabitants (Jonah 4). The plant was not the reason Nineveh repented.\n\nWORKED JAMB-STYLE EXAMPLES — PROPHETS AND IMAGES\nExample (vii): What objection did Jeremiah raise when called?\nSolution: Jeremiah 1:6 says he did not know how to speak because he was young. Answer: His youth and inability to speak.\nExample (viii): What did Ezekiel eat in his call vision?\nSolution: Ezekiel 3:1–3 describes a scroll that tasted sweet as honey. Answer: A scroll.\nExample (ix): What did the dry bones signify?\nSolution: Ezekiel 37 explains them as the house of Israel and God's promise to restore them. Answer: Israel's restoration from hopelessness.\nExample (x): Where did Jonah board a ship while fleeing his commission?\nSolution: Jonah 1:3 says Joppa. Answer: Joppa.\nExample (xi): How long was Jonah inside the great fish?\nSolution: Jonah 1:17 says three days and three nights. Answer: Three days and three nights.\nExample (xii): How many days did Jonah announce before Nineveh's destruction?\nSolution: Jonah 3:4 gives 40 days. Answer: Forty days.\nExample (xiii): What was Jeremiah's new covenant to be written on?\nSolution: Jeremiah 31:33 says the people's hearts. Answer: Their hearts.\n\nTEST YOUR UNDERSTANDING\nCompare Hosea's return to God, Jeremiah's inward covenant and Ezekiel's individual responsibility. Explain the potter-and-clay image and the dry-bones vision. Retell Jonah's journey from Joppa to Nineveh, including the numbers three and 40. Explain the lesson of Jonah's plant.\n\nQUICK UNDERSTANDING CHECK\nDefine repentance and restoration. Explain personal responsibility in Ezekiel 18. Compare Jeremiah's new covenant with Hosea's call to return.\n\nPRACTICE QUESTIONS\nPractise the passages using the linked JAMB questions.\n\nADDITIONAL BIBLICAL CONTEXT AND EXAM DISTINCTIONS\nRepentance turns from wrongdoing towards God, changing loyalties and conduct rather than merely expressing regret. Hosea 14 calls Israel to return with words, ask God to take away iniquity and renounce reliance on Assyria, military power and handmade gods. “All iniquity” covers wrongdoing generally; the following mention of idols explains why a question may focus on idolatry in that setting.\n\nHosea's concluding verse says that the LORD's ways are right. The upright walk in them, but transgressors stumble in them. The difference lies in people's response, not in one set of ways being right for some people and wrong for others. The prophet's marriage imagery also expresses persistent covenant love, often described by the Hebrew term hesed: steadfast loyalty, mercy and faithfulness.\n\nJeremiah promises faithful shepherds who will feed the people with knowledge and understanding. Leaders are not merely to gather followers or seek status. Jeremiah 4 joins return to God with truthful, just and righteous swearing, so that other nations may bless themselves in God. His new covenant promises inward law, knowledge of God and forgiveness. Remember that the short formula “my people … their God” appears in more than one prophetic book.\n\nJonah is commissioned to cry against Nineveh's wickedness. He flees, but the storm, his rescue and renewed commission bring him back to the task. The sailors fear the LORD and make vows after the sea becomes calm. Jonah's own prayer comes from the fish's belly. Nineveh's response shows the place of repentance and divine mercy beyond Israel.\n\nCommon trap: distinguish forgiveness and changed conduct from religious language that leaves injustice or idolatry untouched.\n\nEzekiel explicitly identifies the dry bones with the whole house of Israel. The mariners cast lots concerning Jonah; Nineveh's king orders sackcloth and repentance.\n\nIsaiah 61:9 promises descendants recognised among nations; Isaiah 9:6–7 promises enduring Davidic rule. An unspecified restoration question may refer to either.\n\nThe dry bones of Ezekiel 37 represent the whole house of Israel in despair. God's restoration brings hope; Ezekiel answers the question about their life by acknowledging God's knowledge rather than claiming independent power. Jonah explains his reluctance by recognising that God is gracious, merciful, slow to anger and willing to relent from announced disaster. “Repent of evil” in older translations means relent from calamity, not confess divine wrongdoing. Isaiah's “there is no other God” declares unique sovereignty. Second Peter says the Lord desires repentance rather than people perishing.\n\nHope is not exclusive to one prophet: Jeremiah promises restoration, Amos rebuilding and planting, Hosea healing of unfaithfulness, and Obadiah deliverance on Zion with restored possession for Jacob's house. Broad questions need a distinguishing quotation. Sovereignty means God's supreme authority over nations and events.\n\nJeremiah 3:12 addresses Israel northward."},"old":{"title":"Repentance, personal responsibility and restoration","body":"Repentance turns from wrongdoing towards God, changing loyalties and conduct rather than merely expressing regret. Hosea 14 calls Israel to return with words, ask God to take away iniquity and renounce reliance on Assyria, military power and handmade gods. “All iniquity” covers wrongdoing generally; the following mention of idols explains why a question may focus on idolatry in that setting.\n\nHosea's concluding verse says that the LORD's ways are right. The upright walk in them, but transgressors stumble in them. The difference lies in people's response, not in one set of ways being right for some people and wrong for others. The prophet's marriage imagery also expresses persistent covenant love, often described by the Hebrew term hesed: steadfast loyalty, mercy and faithfulness.\n\nJeremiah promises faithful shepherds who will feed the people with knowledge and understanding. Leaders are not merely to gather followers or seek status. Jeremiah 4 joins return to God with truthful, just and righteous swearing, so that other nations may bless themselves in God. His new covenant promises inward law, knowledge of God and forgiveness. Remember that the short formula “my people … their God” appears in more than one prophetic book.\n\nJonah is commissioned to cry against Nineveh's wickedness. He flees, but the storm, his rescue and renewed commission bring him back to the task. The sailors fear the LORD and make vows after the sea becomes calm. Jonah's own prayer comes from the fish's belly. Nineveh's response shows the place of repentance and divine mercy beyond Israel.\n\nCommon trap: distinguish forgiveness and changed conduct from religious language that leaves injustice or idolatry untouched.\n\nEzekiel explicitly identifies the dry bones with the whole house of Israel. The mariners cast lots concerning Jonah; Nineveh's king orders sackcloth and repentance.\n\nIsaiah 61:9 promises descendants recognised among nations; Isaiah 9:6–7 promises enduring Davidic rule. An unspecified restoration question may refer to either.\n\nThe dry bones of Ezekiel 37 represent the whole house of Israel in despair. God's restoration brings hope; Ezekiel answers the question about their life by acknowledging God's knowledge rather than claiming independent power. Jonah explains his reluctance by recognising that God is gracious, merciful, slow to anger and willing to relent from announced disaster. “Repent of evil” in older translations means relent from calamity, not confess divine wrongdoing. Isaiah's “there is no other God” declares unique sovereignty. Second Peter says the Lord desires repentance rather than people perishing.\n\nHope is not exclusive to one prophet: Jeremiah promises restoration, Amos rebuilding and planting, Hosea healing of unfaithfulness, and Obadiah deliverance on Zion with restored possession for Jacob's house. Broad questions need a distinguishing quotation. Sovereignty means God's supreme authority over nations and events.\n\nJeremiah 3:12 addresses Israel northward."}},{"id":"exile-return","revised":true,"new":{"title":"Exile, faithful witness and rebuilding","body":"EXILE, FAITHFULNESS AND THE RETURN TO JERUSALEM — COMPLETE LESSON\n\nINTRODUCTION\nExile is the forced removal of people from their homeland. Judah's exile followed the Babylonian conquest of Jerusalem. Biblical writers connect the disaster with persistent disobedience and injustice. The return from exile later involved rebuilding community life and worship.\n\n1. THE FALL OF JERUSALEM (2 Kings 25; Jeremiah 39)\nBabylon captured Jerusalem during the reign of Zedekiah. He fled, was caught in the plains of Jericho, and was taken to Riblah for judgement. His sons were killed before him, and his eyes were put out. Remember the distinction: he was captured near Jericho but judged at Riblah. Jerusalem and its Temple were destroyed, and many inhabitants were deported; some poorer people remained to work the land.\n\n2. JEREMIAH AND EZEKIEL: DIFFERENT CALLS\nJeremiah 1 describes God's call of Jeremiah before his birth, his objection that he was young, and God's promise to be with him. Ezekiel 2–3 describes a commission to speak to a rebellious people, whether or not they listen. The prophets both warned God's people, but their calling stories must not be mixed up.\n\n3. DANIEL: FAITHFULNESS IN A FOREIGN LAND\nDaniel and his companions faced pressure to compromise their worship.\nDaniel 3 — Nebuchadnezzar ordered worship of a golden image. Shadrach, Meshach and Abednego refused and survived the fiery furnace.\nDaniel 6 — under Darius, Daniel's habit of praying became the basis of an accusation. He was thrown into a den of lions and survived.\nThe furnace and lions' den involved different rulers, accusations and people.\n\n4. THE RETURN AND REBUILDING\nAfter Babylon fell to Persia, Cyrus permitted exiles to return and rebuild the Temple (Ezra 1). Zerubbabel is associated with the rebuilt Temple; Ezra with teaching the law and reform; Nehemiah with rebuilding Jerusalem's walls and organising the community. The Temple and the city walls are not the same project.\nOpposition and discouragement occurred during rebuilding, but the community was called to perseverance and covenant obedience.\n\n5. WHAT THE EVENTS TEACH\nFaithfulness is possible even under pressure. Courage does not always mean the absence of danger. Rebuilding after disaster requires leadership, cooperation, worship and just conduct.\n\n\nDEEPER TEACHING — THE HISTORICAL SEQUENCE\nJudah was conquered by Babylon, and Jerusalem's Temple was destroyed. Exile meant loss of home, political independence and familiar worship arrangements. After Persia conquered Babylon, Cyrus allowed some exiles to return. Not everyone returned at once; rebuilding happened in stages and involved opposition and setbacks.\nSequence: Jerusalem's fall → Babylonian exile → Persian rule → return under Cyrus' permission → rebuilding the Temple → later reforms and rebuilding of Jerusalem's walls.\n\nDANIEL AND HIS COMPANIONS — THREE DIFFERENT TESTS\nDaniel 1: Daniel and his companions declined the royal food and wine because they wished to avoid defilement. They were granted permission for a test involving vegetables and water; the story says they appeared healthy.\nDaniel 3: Shadrach, Meshach and Abednego refused to bow to Nebuchadnezzar's golden image and survived the fiery furnace.\nDaniel 6: Daniel continued praying despite a decree under Darius and survived the lions' den.\nEach event has a different pressure, ruler or response. Do not answer a furnace question with the lions' den story.\n\nTHE REBUILDERS AND THEIR TASKS\nZerubbabel was associated with the return and rebuilding of the Temple (Ezra 3–6). Ezra was a priest and scribe who taught the law and led religious reform (Ezra 7–10). Nehemiah, who served the Persian king as cupbearer, led the rebuilding of Jerusalem's walls (Nehemiah 1–6). Temple rebuilding and wall rebuilding are separate tasks. Haggai and Zechariah encouraged the rebuilding of the Temple.\n\nFAITHFULNESS AND RESPONSIBLE LEADERSHIP\nDaniel's stories emphasise loyalty under pressure. The return narratives show that restoring a community involves more than construction: people must cooperate, learn the law and act justly. Nehemiah organised workers and responded to threats while the wall was being rebuilt.\n\nGUIDED WALKTHROUGH — PEOPLE AND PROJECTS\nWhen the question names the Temple, consider Zerubbabel and the prophetic encouragement of Haggai and Zechariah. When it names teaching the law, consider Ezra. When it names Jerusalem's walls, consider Nehemiah. When it names the fiery furnace, consider Nebuchadnezzar and Daniel's three companions.\n\nADDITIONAL WORKED EXAMPLES\nExample (iv): Which Persian ruler permitted exiles to return and rebuild the Temple?\nSolution: Ezra 1 names Cyrus. Answer: Cyrus.\nExample (v): Which leader organised the rebuilding of Jerusalem's walls?\nSolution: Nehemiah 2–6 records his leadership of the work. Answer: Nehemiah.\nExample (vi): What distinguished Daniel's test in Daniel 1 from the fiery furnace?\nSolution: Daniel 1 concerns food and defilement; Daniel 3 concerns refusal to worship an image. Answer: They involved different acts of faithfulness and different challenges.\n\nEXAM TRAPS\nZedekiah was captured near Jericho but judged at Riblah. The three companions were thrown into the furnace; Daniel was thrown into the lions' den. Do not confuse Cyrus with Nebuchadnezzar or Darius.\n\nWORKED JAMB-STYLE EXAMPLES\nExample (i): Where was Zedekiah captured, and where was he judged?\nSolution: Jeremiah 39 and 2 Kings 25 distinguish the plains of Jericho from Riblah. Answer: Captured near Jericho; judged at Riblah.\n\nExample (ii): Which king ordered the worship of the golden image in Daniel 3?\nSolution: Daniel 3 identifies Nebuchadnezzar. Answer: Nebuchadnezzar.\n\nExample (iii): Who is especially associated with rebuilding Jerusalem's walls?\nSolution: Nehemiah led the wall-building project described in the book bearing his name. Answer: Nehemiah.\n\n\nDETAILED BIBLE STUDY — JERUSALEM'S FALL AND THE EXILE\nBabylon's King Nebuchadnezzar attacked Judah. An earlier deportation included King Jehoiachin, who surrendered and was taken to Babylon with leading citizens and craftsmen (2 Kings 24:10–17). Nebuchadnezzar installed Mattaniah, renamed Zedekiah, as king. Zedekiah rebelled. In the ninth year of his reign Babylon besieged Jerusalem; the siege lasted into his eleventh year (2 Kings 25:1–3). When the city wall was breached, Zedekiah fled but was captured in the plains of Jericho and taken to Riblah, where his sons were killed before his eyes and he was blinded (2 Kings 25:4–7).\nNebuzaradan, captain of the guard, burned the Temple, royal palace and houses, broke down Jerusalem's walls and carried people into exile (2 Kings 25:8–12). Some of the poorest people remained to work vineyards and fields. Gedaliah was appointed governor over those who remained but was later assassinated (2 Kings 25:22–26). Distinguish Nebuchadnezzar the king from Nebuzaradan the official who supervised destruction.\n\nDANIEL 1 — NAMES AND THE TEN-DAY TEST\nDaniel, Hananiah, Mishael and Azariah were among the youths chosen for royal training in Babylon. Their Babylonian names were Belteshazzar, Shadrach, Meshach and Abednego respectively (Daniel 1:6–7). Daniel asked not to defile himself with royal food and wine. The official feared for his safety, so Daniel proposed a test of vegetables and water for ten days. At the end they appeared healthier than those eating the royal food. God gave them knowledge and skill; Daniel received understanding of visions and dreams. Ten days is the food test, not the duration of the exile.\n\nDANIEL 2 AND 3 — THE KING'S DREAM AND GOLDEN IMAGE\nNebuchadnezzar dreamed of a great statue with a head of gold, chest and arms of silver, belly and thighs of bronze, legs of iron, and feet partly of iron and clay. A stone struck the statue and destroyed it; Daniel explained the dream as a succession of kingdoms and God's enduring kingdom (Daniel 2). Daniel credited God with revealing the mystery.\nIn Daniel 3 Nebuchadnezzar set up a golden image 60 cubits high and six cubits wide on the plain of Dura. People were ordered to bow when musical instruments sounded. Shadrach, Meshach and Abednego refused. The furnace was heated seven times hotter than usual; the men were bound and thrown in. The king saw four figures walking unharmed in the fire. The three emerged without the smell of fire on them. Daniel himself is not named among those thrown into the furnace.\n\nDANIEL 5 AND 6 — BELSHAZZAR AND DARIUS\nAt Belshazzar's feast, vessels taken from the Jerusalem Temple were used while guests praised idols. A hand wrote on the wall; Daniel interpreted the writing “Mene, Mene, Tekel, Parsin” as judgement on Belshazzar's kingdom (Daniel 5). That night Belshazzar was killed, and Darius the Mede received the kingdom in the narrative.\nIn Daniel 6 officials persuaded Darius to issue a decree that petitions must be addressed only to the king for 30 days. Daniel continued praying three times daily with his windows open toward Jerusalem. He was thrown into the lions' den, but God sent an angel to shut the lions' mouths. Darius was distressed by the decree and later honoured Daniel's God. Thirty days is the decree's duration; three times daily is Daniel's prayer practice.\n\nCYRUS, ZERUBBABEL, EZRA AND NEHEMIAH — DO NOT CONFUSE THEIR TASKS\nEzra 1:1–4 records Cyrus king of Persia permitting the return and the rebuilding of the LORD's house in Jerusalem. Cyrus returned Temple vessels taken by Nebuchadnezzar. Sheshbazzar is named in Ezra 1:8–11 in connection with receiving the vessels; Zerubbabel led the rebuilding community in the accounts of Ezra 3–6. The altar was rebuilt and the Temple foundation laid, but opposition delayed work. Haggai and Zechariah encouraged the people; the Temple was completed in the sixth year of Darius (Ezra 6:15).\nEzra was a priest and scribe skilled in the law of Moses (Ezra 7:6). Under Artaxerxes he travelled to Jerusalem to teach God's law and address covenant unfaithfulness. Nehemiah served Artaxerxes as cupbearer (Nehemiah 1–2). Hearing that Jerusalem's walls were broken, he prayed and sought permission to rebuild. Sanballat and Tobiah opposed the work, later joined by Geshem. Workers repaired sections of the wall and guarded against attack. Nehemiah 6:15 says the wall was completed in 52 days. The completion of the wall is not the same as the completion of the Temple.\n\nWORKED JAMB-STYLE EXAMPLES — CHRONOLOGY AND NUMBERS\nExample (vii): Who blinded Zedekiah after his capture?\nSolution: 2 Kings 25 describes the judgement carried out under the Babylonian king after Zedekiah was brought to Riblah. Answer: Nebuchadnezzar king of Babylon.\nExample (viii): How many days did Daniel's food test last?\nSolution: Daniel 1:12 says ten days. Answer: Ten days.\nExample (ix): What were Daniel's three companions called in Babylon?\nSolution: Daniel 1:7 names Shadrach, Meshach and Abednego. Answer: Shadrach, Meshach and Abednego.\nExample (x): How often did Daniel pray despite the decree?\nSolution: Daniel 6:10 says three times daily. Answer: Three times a day.\nExample (xi): For how long did the decree against prayer last?\nSolution: Daniel 6:7 specifies 30 days. Answer: Thirty days.\nExample (xii): In how many days was Jerusalem's wall completed under Nehemiah?\nSolution: Nehemiah 6:15 records 52 days. Answer: Fifty-two days.\nExample (xiii): Which prophets encouraged rebuilding the Temple?\nSolution: Ezra 5:1 names Haggai and Zechariah. Answer: Haggai and Zechariah.\n\nTEST YOUR UNDERSTANDING\nExplain the differences between Jehoiachin and Zedekiah. Distinguish Nebuchadnezzar from Nebuzaradan. Memorise the Hebrew and Babylonian names of Daniel's companions. Compare the ten-day food test, 30-day decree and 52-day wall rebuilding. Explain the separate tasks of Cyrus, Zerubbabel, Ezra and Nehemiah.\n\nQUICK UNDERSTANDING CHECK\nDistinguish Daniel 3 from Daniel 6. Explain the difference between Temple rebuilding and wall rebuilding. State one lesson from the exile.\n\nPRACTICE QUESTIONS\nUse the linked past questions to practise people, places and the sequence of events.\n\nADDITIONAL BIBLICAL CONTEXT AND EXAM DISTINCTIONS\nExile means enforced removal from one's homeland. Biblical accounts connect Judah's fall with persistent covenant unfaithfulness and injustice, not merely a weaker army. When Jerusalem fell, Zedekiah fled but was captured in the plains of Jericho. He was then taken to Riblah, where his sons were killed before him and his eyes put out. Capture and judgement occurred in different places. Some of the poorest people remained in the land as agricultural workers.\n\nThe prophets' commissions explain their tasks. Jeremiah was known before birth and appointed a prophet to the nations. His call includes his objection that he is young and God's promise to be with him. Ezekiel is sent to a rebellious people who may refuse to listen. These are distinct opening accounts, not interchangeable statements addressed to any prophet.\n\nDaniel portrays faithfulness under foreign rule. Nebuchadnezzar assembled officials to dedicate the golden image. The soldiers who threw Shadrach, Meshach and Abednego into the furnace were killed by the heat, while the three survived. Later, under Darius, officials exploited Daniel's regular prayer to arrange the decree leading to the lions' den. The furnace and lions' den involve different kings and different charges.\n\nIn Daniel 12, Michael is named as the great prince standing for the people. Gabriel appears elsewhere as a messenger. Questions quoting an angel's description require the particular name supplied in that passage.\n\nCommon traps:\n• Jericho: Zedekiah's capture; Riblah: his judgement.\n• Nebuchadnezzar: image and furnace; Darius: decree and lions' den.\n• Cupbearer was Nehemiah's court office, not a title meaning priest or prophet.\n\nDaniel's excellent spirit distinguished him among officials. In the furnace story Nebuchadnezzar saw a fourth figure resembling a divine being; the KJV renders this “the Son of God,” while other translations use “a son of the gods.” The king later speaks of God's angel. His astonishment concerned men loose and unharmed; his proclamation acknowledged God's power to deliver, without saying everyone abandoned all other gods. Temple rebuilding under Ezra met opposition from the people of the land. Distinguish this earlier rebuilding from Nehemiah's later work on Jerusalem's wall.\n\nAhab imprisoned Micaiah for the unwelcome prophecy, ordering meagre bread and water rather than an absolute denial of food. Darius later praised Daniel's God after the rescue from lions. Daniel requests vegetables and water from the steward; the image-worship decree names musical instruments as its signal.\n\nDaniel and his friends avoid the royal food and wine because of defilement, not because it tastes unpleasant. God gives the four learning and skill in letters and wisdom; Daniel's special understanding of visions is separately stated."},"old":{"title":"Exile, faithful witness and rebuilding","body":"Exile means enforced removal from one's homeland. Biblical accounts connect Judah's fall with persistent covenant unfaithfulness and injustice, not merely a weaker army. When Jerusalem fell, Zedekiah fled but was captured in the plains of Jericho. He was then taken to Riblah, where his sons were killed before him and his eyes put out. Capture and judgement occurred in different places. Some of the poorest people remained in the land as agricultural workers.\n\nThe prophets' commissions explain their tasks. Jeremiah was known before birth and appointed a prophet to the nations. His call includes his objection that he is young and God's promise to be with him. Ezekiel is sent to a rebellious people who may refuse to listen. These are distinct opening accounts, not interchangeable statements addressed to any prophet.\n\nDaniel portrays faithfulness under foreign rule. Nebuchadnezzar assembled officials to dedicate the golden image. The soldiers who threw Shadrach, Meshach and Abednego into the furnace were killed by the heat, while the three survived. Later, under Darius, officials exploited Daniel's regular prayer to arrange the decree leading to the lions' den. The furnace and lions' den involve different kings and different charges.\n\nIn Daniel 12, Michael is named as the great prince standing for the people. Gabriel appears elsewhere as a messenger. Questions quoting an angel's description require the particular name supplied in that passage.\n\nCommon traps:\n• Jericho: Zedekiah's capture; Riblah: his judgement.\n• Nebuchadnezzar: image and furnace; Darius: decree and lions' den.\n• Cupbearer was Nehemiah's court office, not a title meaning priest or prophet.\n\nDaniel's excellent spirit distinguished him among officials. In the furnace story Nebuchadnezzar saw a fourth figure resembling a divine being; the KJV renders this “the Son of God,” while other translations use “a son of the gods.” The king later speaks of God's angel. His astonishment concerned men loose and unharmed; his proclamation acknowledged God's power to deliver, without saying everyone abandoned all other gods. Temple rebuilding under Ezra met opposition from the people of the land. Distinguish this earlier rebuilding from Nehemiah's later work on Jerusalem's wall.\n\nAhab imprisoned Micaiah for the unwelcome prophecy, ordering meagre bread and water rather than an absolute denial of food. Darius later praised Daniel's God after the rescue from lions. Daniel requests vegetables and water from the steward; the image-worship decree names musical instruments as its signal.\n\nDaniel and his friends avoid the royal food and wine because of defilement, not because it tastes unpleasant. God gives the four learning and skill in letters and wisdom; Daniel's special understanding of visions is separately stated."}},{"id":"jesus-identity","revised":true,"new":{"title":"Jesus' identity, birth and teaching in John","body":"JESUS' IDENTITY, BIRTH AND TEACHING — DETAILED CRS LESSON\n\nINTRODUCTION\nThe four Gospels—Matthew, Mark, Luke and John—tell about Jesus, but they select and arrange events differently. Matthew and Luke describe his birth. Mark begins with John the Baptist and Jesus' public ministry. John opens by describing Jesus as the eternal Word. A JAMB question may ask for an event, a person, a quotation, a location or which Gospel reports a detail. Learn the story and its source, not just the broad message.\n\n1. THE BIRTH ANNOUNCEMENTS (LUKE 1)\nThe angel Gabriel appeared to Zechariah, a priest married to Elizabeth. Both were advanced in years and had no child. Gabriel announced that Elizabeth would bear John, who would prepare people for the Lord. Zechariah doubted and was unable to speak until the child's naming (Luke 1:5–25, 57–64). Elizabeth was in her sixth month when Gabriel visited Mary in Nazareth (Luke 1:26–38). Mary was a virgin betrothed to Joseph, of David's house. Gabriel told her she would bear a son called Jesus; he would be called Son of the Most High. Mary asked how this could happen, and Gabriel spoke of the Holy Spirit's action. Mary accepted God's message. Elizabeth greeted Mary, and Mary's song of praise is often called the Magnificat (Luke 1:39–56). John was named on the eighth day when he was circumcised; Zechariah wrote that his name was John.\n\n2. JESUS' BIRTH AND CHILDHOOD (MATTHEW 1–2; LUKE 2)\nMatthew 1 records Joseph's concern when Mary was found pregnant. An angel told him in a dream not to fear taking Mary as wife because the child was conceived through the Holy Spirit. The name Jesus was linked with saving his people from their sins; Matthew also cites Emmanuel, meaning God with us. Luke 2 explains that Joseph and Mary travelled from Nazareth to Bethlehem because of a registration associated with Caesar Augustus and Quirinius. Jesus was born in Bethlehem and laid in a manger because there was no room in the lodging place. Angels announced the birth to shepherds, who went to see the child. Luke reports Jesus' circumcision on the eighth day, presentation at the Temple, and recognition by Simeon and Anna. At age 12 Jesus stayed behind in Jerusalem during Passover and was found in the Temple among teachers after his parents searched for him.\nMatthew 2 tells of wise men from the east who followed a star and brought gold, frankincense and myrrh. Matthew does not give their number or call them kings. Herod asked about the child's birthplace; the chief priests and scribes named Bethlehem. Joseph was warned in a dream to flee with Mary and Jesus to Egypt. Herod ordered the killing of boys aged two years and under in Bethlehem and its surroundings. After Herod's death the family returned and settled in Nazareth. Do not put the wise men into Luke's shepherd scene as though both accounts describe the same visitors at the same moment.\n\n3. JOHN THE BAPTIST AND JESUS' BAPTISM (MATTHEW 3; MARK 1; LUKE 3; JOHN 1)\nJohn preached repentance and baptised in the Jordan region. His clothing was camel's hair with a leather belt, and his food was locusts and wild honey (Matthew 3:4). He warned that ancestry alone was not enough: repentance should produce fruit. Jesus came to be baptised by John. In Matthew, John hesitated, but Jesus said it was fitting to fulfil all righteousness. The Spirit descended like a dove, and a heavenly voice declared Jesus God's beloved Son. John's Gospel reports the Baptist's witness that he saw the Spirit descend and remain on Jesus; it does not narrate the baptism in exactly the same way as the Synoptic Gospels. John called Jesus the Lamb of God (John 1:29).\n\n4. THE TEMPTATIONS — EXACT ORDER MATTERS (MATTHEW 4; LUKE 4)\nAfter fasting for 40 days and 40 nights, Jesus was hungry. The devil challenged him to turn stones into bread; Jesus answered that people do not live by bread alone. Matthew's second temptation takes place at the Temple pinnacle, where the devil urges him to throw himself down; Jesus refuses to test God. Matthew's third involves the kingdoms of the world and an offer of power in exchange for worship; Jesus insists that only God must be worshipped. Luke places the kingdom temptation second and the Temple temptation third. Both accounts show Jesus resisting misuse of power and Scripture. Mark mentions temptation in the wilderness without the same detailed three-part sequence.\n\n5. THE FIRST DISCIPLES AND PETER'S CONFESSION\nMatthew 4:18–22 and Mark 1:16–20 describe Jesus calling Simon Peter and Andrew, then James and John, sons of Zebedee, from fishing work beside the Sea of Galilee. They left their nets to follow him. In John 1:35–42, two of John the Baptist's disciples followed Jesus; Andrew brought his brother Simon, whom Jesus called Cephas (Peter). The accounts emphasise different scenes, so a question naming a particular Gospel needs its own details.\nAt Caesarea Philippi Jesus asked who people said he was. Peter confessed that Jesus was the Christ or Messiah (Mark 8:27–30; Matthew 16:13–20). Matthew includes “the Son of the living God,” the blessing on Peter, and the language of keys. Jesus then taught about suffering, death and resurrection. Peter's confession is not the same event as his later denial.\n\n6. THE TRANSFIGURATION (MATTHEW 17; MARK 9; LUKE 9)\nJesus took Peter, James and John up a mountain. His appearance changed, and Moses and Elijah appeared speaking with him. Peter suggested three shelters or booths. A cloud overshadowed them, and a voice identified Jesus as the beloved Son and commanded the disciples to listen to him. Luke specifically says Jesus went up to pray and that Moses and Elijah spoke about his departure. Matthew says the disciples fell on their faces in fear after the voice. Moses and Elijah are commonly understood as representing the Law and the Prophets. The transfiguration occurred before the crucifixion and resurrection.\n\n7. JOHN'S PROLOGUE AND JESUS' IDENTITY (JOHN 1)\nJohn 1:1–18 calls Jesus the Word (Logos): the Word was with God, the Word was God, and all things came into being through him. “The Word became flesh” (John 1:14) expresses the Christian teaching of the Incarnation: the eternal Son truly entered human life. John the Baptist was sent to bear witness to the light; he was not himself the light. John 1:12 says those who received the Word and believed in his name were given power to become children of God. John 1:17 contrasts the law given through Moses with grace and truth coming through Jesus Christ.\n\n8. IMPORTANT SAYINGS AND THEIR SETTINGS IN JOHN\nJohn 3: Nicodemus, a Pharisee and ruler of the Jews, came to Jesus by night. Jesus spoke of being born from above or again, of water and Spirit, and of God's love for the world (3:16). John 3:19–21 connects judgement with people's preference for darkness because their deeds are evil.\nJohn 4: Jesus spoke with a Samaritan woman at Jacob's well near Sychar. He offered “living water,” revealed knowledge of her life, and taught that true worshippers worship the Father in spirit and truth. The woman told her town about him. Do not confuse this woman with Mary Magdalene.\nJohn 5: At the pool of Bethesda, Jesus healed a man who had been ill for 38 years and told him to carry his mat. Religious leaders objected because the healing took place on the Sabbath; opposition intensified because Jesus called God his Father, making himself equal with God (5:18).\nJohn 6: After feeding about 5,000 men with five barley loaves and two fish, Jesus described himself as the bread of life. Twelve baskets of fragments were gathered. John 6:29 says the work God requires is believing in the one he sent.\nJohn 8: Jesus said, “I am the light of the world.” John 9: He healed a man born blind, using mud and telling him to wash in the pool of Siloam. John 10: He called himself the door of the sheep and the good shepherd who lays down his life for the sheep. John 11: He raised Lazarus of Bethany, brother of Mary and Martha, after Lazarus had been in the tomb four days; Jesus declared himself the resurrection and the life. John 14: Jesus spoke of himself as the way, truth and life, and promised another Helper or Advocate—the Holy Spirit. John 15: Jesus described himself as the true vine, his Father as the vine-grower, and disciples as branches called to abide in him and bear fruit. John 16: The Spirit would guide disciples into truth and glorify Jesus.\n\n9. COMMON EXAM DISTINCTIONS\nBethlehem = place of birth; Nazareth = place of upbringing. Gabriel announced John's birth to Zechariah and Jesus' birth to Mary. The shepherds appear in Luke's birth account; the wise men appear in Matthew. The Baptist's purpose was to prepare the way and bear witness; he was not the Messiah. Nicodemus came by night; the Samaritan woman met Jesus at a well. The man at Bethesda had been ill for 38 years; Lazarus had been in the tomb four days. Jesus' wilderness fast lasted 40 days; the number 40 must not be confused with the 38 years of illness.\n\nWORKED JAMB-STYLE EXAMPLES\nExample (i): Who announced the birth of Jesus to Mary?\nSolution: Luke 1:26–31 identifies the angel Gabriel. Answer: Gabriel.\nExample (ii): What gifts did the visitors from the east bring?\nSolution: Matthew 2:11 lists gold, frankincense and myrrh. Answer: Gold, frankincense and myrrh.\nExample (iii): Which temptation comes last in Luke's account?\nSolution: Luke 4:9–12 ends with the challenge at the Temple. Answer: Throwing himself down from the Temple.\nExample (iv): Who appeared with Jesus at the transfiguration?\nSolution: Matthew 17:3 names Moses and Elijah. Answer: Moses and Elijah.\nExample (v): What did Jesus mean by “the Word became flesh”?\nSolution: John 1:14 teaches that the divine Word entered genuine human life. Answer: The Incarnation.\nExample (vi): For how many years had the man at Bethesda been ill?\nSolution: John 5:5 states 38 years. Answer: Thirty-eight years.\nExample (vii): Who came to Jesus by night?\nSolution: John 3:1–2 names Nicodemus. Answer: Nicodemus.\nExample (viii): Who is called the Advocate or Helper in John 14?\nSolution: John 14:16–17, 26 refers to the Holy Spirit. Answer: The Holy Spirit.\n\nQUICK UNDERSTANDING CHECK\nExplain the difference between the birth announcements to Zechariah and Mary. Retell Matthew's and Luke's temptation orders. Name the three disciples at the transfiguration. Identify the settings of John 3, 4, 5, 6, 10 and 11. Explain what the vine-and-branches teaching expects of disciples.\n\nPRACTICE QUESTIONS\nUse the Practice Questions button to attempt JAMB questions linked to this lesson. Always check the Gospel and chapter named in the question.\n\nSYLLABUS DEEP DIVE — THE MISSIONS OF THE TWELVE AND THE SEVENTY/TWO (MATTHEW 10:1–42; MARK 6:7–13; LUKE 9:1–6; 10:1–24)\nJesus commissioned the Twelve and gave them authority over unclean spirits and diseases. Matthew 10 records instructions to go first to the lost sheep of Israel, proclaim that the kingdom of heaven is at hand, heal the sick and give freely because they had received freely. Mark 6 says they were sent out two by two; Mark permits a staff whereas Matthew's travel instructions are worded differently. A question that specifies a Gospel must be answered from that Gospel's account rather than forcing all details into one version. They were to rely on hospitality and shake the dust off their feet where they were not welcomed. Jesus warned them about opposition and urged faithfulness.\nLuke 10 records a further mission: Jesus appointed seventy or seventy-two others, depending on the manuscript and Bible translation, and sent them two by two ahead of him into towns he intended to visit. They were to pray for labourers because the harvest was plentiful, announce peace, accept hospitality, heal the sick and proclaim the nearness of God's kingdom. They returned rejoicing that demons submitted to them in Jesus' name. Jesus told them to rejoice above all that their names were written in heaven (Luke 10:17–20). Do not confuse the Twelve with this larger group, and do not treat the manuscript variation of seventy/seventy-two as a moral contradiction.\nExample (additional): According to Luke 10, why should the returning messengers rejoice most? \nSolution: Jesus said they should rejoice that their names were written in heaven, rather than focus chiefly on power over spirits (Luke 10:20). Answer: Their standing with God.\nQUICK UNDERSTANDING CHECK: Compare the number commissioned, their purpose, their instructions and the lessons from their return.\n"},"old":{"title":"Jesus' identity, birth and teaching in John","body":"The Gospels present Jesus' identity through titles, actions and teaching.\n\nAt the transfiguration Moses and Elijah appear with Jesus, commonly understood as representing the Law and the Prophets. Peter's confession identifies Jesus as the Christ; at that stage Jesus tells the disciples not to publicise the identification. The eventual resurrection commission belongs to a later setting.\n\nJohn supplies important sayings and signs. Jesus calls himself the bread of life, the door of the sheep and the good shepherd. He identifies the work God requires as believing in the one God sent. In the vine discourse, disciples are clean because of his word and are told to abide in him. The Paraclete is the Holy Spirit, described as Helper, Advocate or Comforter.\n\nJohn 3 links unbelief with judgement and explains avoidance of the light by evil deeds. John 5 distinguishes opposition to a Sabbath healing from the intensified objection to Jesus calling God his Father. Close reading matters: “all the more” points to the second stage, not simply the earlier offence.\n\nChristian teaching calls God's Son taking human nature the Incarnation. In school CRS, explain this within Christian belief without saying that Jesus only appeared human or that all human beings thereby become divine.\n\nCommon trap: a true title or event is not automatically the answer to a quotation from a different Gospel passage.\n\nJohn the Baptist preached repentance and baptism for forgiveness, preparing the Lord's way. Matthew and Luke order the temptations differently: the Temple challenge is Luke's third but Matthew's second. Luke says Jesus was praying at the transfiguration; Matthew says the disciples fell on their faces when they heard the cloud's voice. The voice affirms Jesus' sonship. Matthew and Mark first name Simon, Andrew, James and John, fishermen by Galilee's sea. John says believers in the light become sons of light. Details from one Gospel should not be silently imposed on another.\n\nIn John's vine picture, the Father tends the vine: fruitful branches are pruned, unfruitful ones removed.\n\nThe good shepherd lays down his life for the sheep and takes it up again; the Father's love is linked with that willing act. The hireling runs away at danger because the sheep are not his. John the Baptist is a witness to the light, not the light himself. In the vine image, a branch that refuses to abide is cast out and withers. After Peter's confession Jesus predicts rejection and suffering; public guesses about him as a prophet are not Peter's confession.\n\nAndrew follows John the Baptist before following Jesus. Thomas is also called Didymus, meaning Twin. Rabbi means “my teacher”; Rabboni is likewise addressed as Teacher, not a disciple or priest. Kingdom keys follow Peter's confession."}},{"id":"miracles-signs","revised":true,"new":{"title":"Jesus' healings and signs: faith and careful reading","body":"JESUS' MIRACLES AND SIGNS — DETAILED CRS LESSON\n\nINTRODUCTION\nA miracle in the Gospel narratives is an extraordinary act revealing God's power, compassion or Jesus' identity. John's Gospel often calls such works “signs” because they point beyond the event to who Jesus is. JAMB tests the people, places, instructions, numbers, reactions and exact Gospel. Do not combine similar healings into one event.\n\n1. WATER INTO WINE AT CANA (JOHN 2:1–11)\nJesus attended a wedding at Cana in Galilee with his mother and disciples. When the wine ran out, Mary told Jesus. He instructed servants to fill six stone water jars, used for Jewish purification rites, with water. Each jar held two or three measures. The servants filled them to the brim, and Jesus told them to draw some and take it to the master of the feast. The master tasted wine and praised the bridegroom for keeping the good wine until then, not knowing where it came from. John calls this Jesus' first sign; it manifested his glory, and his disciples believed in him. Six jars is the key number. The master of the feast did not perform the miracle.\n\n2. HEALING THE OFFICIAL'S SON (JOHN 4:46–54)\nJesus returned to Cana. A royal official from Capernaum begged him to come and heal his dying son. Jesus told him to go because his son would live. The official believed Jesus' word and left. His servants met him with news of recovery, which had occurred at the seventh hour, the time Jesus spoke. John calls it the second sign performed after Jesus came from Judea to Galilee. The boy was healed at a distance; Jesus did not travel to the home to touch him.\n\n3. HEALING AT BETHESDA (JOHN 5:1–18)\nAt a pool in Jerusalem called Bethesda, near the Sheep Gate, lay many sick people. A man had been ill for 38 years. Jesus asked whether he wanted to be healed. The man complained that nobody helped him into the pool. Jesus told him to rise, take up his mat and walk. He was healed immediately. It happened on the Sabbath, and opponents challenged his carrying the mat. Jesus later told him not to sin again. John 5:18 says the hostility grew because Jesus not only acted on the Sabbath but also called God his Father. The question may ask for either stage of opposition.\n\n4. FEEDING THE FIVE THOUSAND (MATTHEW 14; MARK 6; LUKE 9; JOHN 6)\nA large crowd followed Jesus. In John 6, Jesus asked Philip where bread could be bought to feed them. Andrew identified a boy with five barley loaves and two fish. Jesus gave thanks and distributed food; everyone ate. Twelve baskets of leftover fragments were collected. John says there were about 5,000 men. The miracle shows provision and leads into the bread-of-life teaching. Do not confuse it with feeding the four thousand (Matthew 15; Mark 8), which used seven loaves and a few small fish and left seven baskets.\n\n5. WALKING ON THE WATER (MATTHEW 14; MARK 6; JOHN 6)\nAfter the feeding, the disciples travelled by boat. Jesus came towards them walking on the sea. They were frightened. In Matthew 14, Peter asked to come to Jesus on the water, walked briefly, became afraid and began to sink; Jesus caught him. John's account describes Jesus approaching the boat but does not include Peter walking on water. In Mark 6, the disciples' lack of understanding is linked to the loaves. Identify the Gospel before selecting details.\n\n6. HEALING A MAN BORN BLIND (JOHN 9)\nJesus saw a man blind from birth. The disciples asked whose sin had caused the blindness. Jesus rejected the assumption that it was caused by either the man's or his parents' sin. He made mud with saliva, applied it to the man's eyes and told him to wash in the pool of Siloam, meaning Sent. The man washed and returned seeing. Pharisees questioned him repeatedly because the healing occurred on a Sabbath. His parents confirmed that he was born blind but avoided further answers out of fear. The healed man defended Jesus and was expelled; Jesus later spoke to him about spiritual sight. The place is Siloam, not Bethesda. Bethesda concerns the 38-year illness.\n\n7. RAISING LAZARUS (JOHN 11)\nLazarus lived in Bethany with his sisters Martha and Mary. Jesus heard he was sick but remained where he was for two more days before travelling. When he arrived, Lazarus had already been in the tomb four days. Martha said she believed her brother would rise in the resurrection at the last day. Jesus said he was the resurrection and the life. At the tomb, Jesus wept; he ordered the stone removed, prayed, and called Lazarus out. Lazarus emerged with burial cloths. Some witnesses believed; others reported the event to the authorities. Do not confuse Lazarus with the widow's son at Nain (Luke 7) or Jairus' daughter (Mark 5; Luke 8).\n\n8. JAIRUS' DAUGHTER AND THE WOMAN WITH BLEEDING (MARK 5; LUKE 8; MATTHEW 9)\nJairus, a synagogue leader, asked Jesus to help his dying daughter, about 12 years old (Mark 5:42). On the way, a woman who had suffered bleeding for 12 years touched Jesus' garment and was healed. Jesus told her that her faith had made her well. Messengers then reported that Jairus' daughter had died. Jesus encouraged Jairus not to fear but believe. He entered with Peter, James, John and the child's parents, took her by the hand and told her to rise. Mark preserves the Aramaic words “Talitha cumi.” The two 12-year details belong to different people.\n\n9. THE CENTURION'S SERVANT (MATTHEW 8; LUKE 7)\nA centurion sought healing for his servant. In Matthew he addresses Jesus directly; Luke describes Jewish elders and friends conveying his request. The centurion said he was not worthy to have Jesus under his roof and trusted that Jesus could heal with a word, just as a commander gives orders. Jesus praised such faith, and the servant recovered. The story teaches authority and faith without requiring physical contact.\n\n10. THE TEN LEPERS (LUKE 17:11–19)\nTen people with leprosy met Jesus at a distance and asked for mercy. Jesus told them to show themselves to the priests. They were cleansed while going. Only one returned to thank Jesus, and he was a Samaritan. Jesus asked where the other nine were. This story combines obedience, healing and gratitude. It is different from the individual leper cleansed in Mark 1.\n\n11. CALMING THE STORM AND OTHER SIGNS\nIn Mark 4:35–41, a storm threatened the boat while Jesus slept. The disciples woke him; he rebuked the wind and said to the sea, “Peace! Be still!” The wind ceased. Jesus questioned their fear and lack of faith. In Luke 7:11–17, Jesus met a funeral procession at Nain and raised a widow's only son. In Mark 7:31–37 Jesus healed a man who was deaf and had difficulty speaking; the word “Ephphatha” means “Be opened.” In Mark 10:46–52 blind Bartimaeus, son of Timaeus, called out to Jesus near Jericho and received his sight. In Luke 19, Zacchaeus was a tax collector at Jericho; his encounter with Jesus was a conversion narrative, not a physical healing.\n\n12. COMPARISON TABLE IN WORDS\nCana: water to wine; six jars; John 2. Official's son: healed at a distance; seventh hour; John 4. Bethesda: ill 38 years; Sabbath; John 5. Feeding 5,000: five loaves, two fish, 12 baskets. Feeding 4,000: seven loaves, seven baskets. Siloam: man born blind; mud and washing; John 9. Bethany: Lazarus; four days in tomb; John 11. Ten lepers: one grateful Samaritan; Luke 17. Jairus' daughter: about 12 years old; woman ill 12 years; Mark 5.\n\nWORKED JAMB-STYLE EXAMPLES\nExample (i): How many stone jars were used at Cana?\nSolution: John 2:6 names six stone jars. Answer: Six.\nExample (ii): At what hour did the royal official's son recover?\nSolution: John 4:52 records the seventh hour. Answer: The seventh hour.\nExample (iii): How many baskets remained after feeding the five thousand?\nSolution: John 6:13 states twelve baskets. Answer: Twelve.\nExample (iv): Where did Jesus send the man born blind to wash?\nSolution: John 9:7 identifies the pool of Siloam. Answer: Siloam.\nExample (v): How many days had Lazarus been in the tomb?\nSolution: John 11:17 gives four days. Answer: Four days.\nExample (vi): Who returned to thank Jesus after the ten lepers were healed?\nSolution: Luke 17:16 identifies the grateful man as a Samaritan. Answer: A Samaritan.\nExample (vii): What words did Jesus say to Jairus' daughter in Mark?\nSolution: Mark 5:41 records “Talitha cumi.” Answer: Talitha cumi.\nExample (viii): Which feeding left seven baskets?\nSolution: Matthew 15:37 describes seven baskets after feeding the four thousand. Answer: The feeding of the four thousand.\n\nQUICK UNDERSTANDING CHECK\nExplain how John's signs reveal more than physical power. Distinguish Bethesda and Siloam. Compare the two feeding miracles using their numbers. Explain why the centurion's faith was praised. Name three resurrection miracles and the people involved.\n\nPRACTICE QUESTIONS\nUse the Practice Questions button to attempt mapped JAMB questions. Match the numbers and locations to the correct Gospel event."},"old":{"title":"Jesus' healings and signs: faith and careful reading","body":"The Gospels describe Jesus' miracles as acts of compassion and signs of divine authority. In John, turning water into wine at Cana is the first sign. When Mary reports the lack of wine, Jesus first answers about his hour. Mary then tells the servants to obey him. Do not attribute Mary's instruction to Jesus or move his later order to fill the jars into the first reply.\n\nJesus reassures the frightened disciples while walking on the sea. Peter initially walks towards him, then becomes afraid when he sees the wind. The immediate trigger for fear and the later rebuke of doubt are related but different answers. Likewise, the woman with the flow of blood acts because she trusts that touching Jesus' garment will make her well; her earlier suffering is background.\n\nIn John 9, Jesus speaks of doing the sender's works before healing the man born blind. A paper saying “after” reverses that sequence. His light-of-the-world saying likewise precedes the healing.\n\nJesus' delay before visiting Lazarus is connected with God's glory and the disciples' belief, not indifference. Many studies of John's seven signs place Lazarus' raising as the climax; that numbering is a teaching arrangement, not a number stated beside the miracle in the Gospel.\n\nBefore raising Lazarus, Jesus prays so onlookers may believe the Father sent him. Cana likewise manifests his glory, and the disciples believe in him. Mary's appeal expresses confidence without making her presence essential to every miracle.\n\nCalming the storm is a nature miracle, demonstrating authority over wind and water. The disciples marvel that these obey him. In the Gerasene episode Jesus delivers a man from demons; the local people ask him to leave. Their response is different from the disciples' belief at Cana.\n\nA sign points beyond the unusual event to Jesus' identity and work. Nature miracles, healings and raisings can express related authority without making their circumstances interchangeable. The common examination trap is to select a true detail from a different episode. For example, naming a bereaved widow does not answer a question about Lazarus, and calling a storm a healing confuses categories."}},{"id":"parables-discipleship","revised":true,"new":{"title":"Parables, mercy and the call to discipleship","body":"PARABLES, MERCY AND DISCIPLESHIP — DETAILED CRS LESSON\n\nINTRODUCTION\nA parable is a story or comparison used to teach a spiritual or moral truth. Jesus often used familiar experiences—farming, family life, money and travel—to challenge listeners. The lesson is not learned by memorising a title alone: JAMB may ask who acted, what was lost, what amount was given, what Jesus said at the end and what the story teaches.\n\n1. THE SOWER (MATTHEW 13; MARK 4; LUKE 8)\nA sower scattered seed on four kinds of ground. Seed on the path was eaten by birds; on rocky ground it sprouted quickly but withered because it had no deep root; among thorns it was choked; on good soil it grew and produced a harvest. Mark 4:8 mentions yields of thirty, sixty and a hundredfold. Jesus explained that the seed represents the word. The path describes those from whom the word is taken away; rocky ground describes those who receive it joyfully but fall away under trouble; thorny ground represents people distracted by cares, riches and desires; good soil represents those who receive and bear fruit. The central issue is the response to God's word, not a lesson on agricultural fertiliser.\n\n2. THE GOOD SAMARITAN (LUKE 10:25–37)\nA lawyer asked what he must do to inherit eternal life. Jesus directed him to the law: love God and neighbour. Wanting to justify himself, the lawyer asked, “Who is my neighbour?” Jesus told of a man travelling from Jerusalem to Jericho who was attacked by robbers, stripped, beaten and left half dead. A priest saw him and passed by; a Levite also passed by. A Samaritan was moved with compassion, treated his wounds with oil and wine, placed him on his animal and took him to an inn. The next day he gave the innkeeper two denarii and promised to repay extra costs. Jesus asked which traveller proved to be a neighbour; the lawyer answered, the one who showed mercy. Jesus told him to go and do likewise. The lesson is active compassion across social and religious boundaries.\n\n3. THE PRODIGAL OR LOST SON (LUKE 15:11–32)\nA younger son asked for his share of the inheritance, left home and wasted it in reckless living. When famine struck, he fed pigs and longed to eat their food. He came to his senses, decided to return and confess, and planned to ask to be treated as a hired worker. His father saw him while he was still far away, ran to him, embraced him, gave him the best robe, a ring and sandals, and ordered the fattened calf killed for celebration. The older brother became angry and refused to join; he complained that he had served faithfully without such a feast. The father explained that they should rejoice because the lost brother had returned. The story teaches repentance, forgiveness and the danger of self-righteous resentment. It does not say the father travelled abroad to find the son.\n\n4. THE LOST SHEEP AND LOST COIN (LUKE 15:1–10)\nA shepherd with 100 sheep lost one, left the 99 and searched until he found it. He rejoiced and called others to rejoice. A woman with ten silver coins lost one, lit a lamp, swept the house and searched carefully until she found it. Both stories describe joy over recovery, and Jesus connects them with joy in heaven over one sinner who repents. Remember the numbers: 100 sheep, one lost; ten coins, one lost.\n\n5. THE RICH FOOL (LUKE 12:13–21)\nA rich man's land produced a plentiful harvest. Instead of considering others or his relationship with God, he planned to pull down his barns and build larger ones, then relax and enjoy his wealth. God called him a fool because his life would be demanded that night. Jesus warned against storing treasures for oneself without being rich towards God. The problem is not productive farming itself; it is greed and false confidence that possessions guarantee life.\n\n6. THE PHARISEE AND TAX COLLECTOR (LUKE 18:9–14)\nTwo men went to the Temple to pray. The Pharisee listed his fasting and tithing and compared himself favourably with others. The tax collector stood at a distance, would not lift his eyes and prayed for mercy as a sinner. Jesus said the tax collector went home justified rather than the Pharisee. The contrast is humility and repentance versus self-righteousness, not a claim that prayer or giving is wrong.\n\n7. THE UNFORGIVING SERVANT (MATTHEW 18:21–35)\nPeter asked whether he should forgive up to seven times. Jesus answered with a much greater standard and told a parable about a servant owing a king ten thousand talents, an enormous debt. The king forgave it when the servant pleaded. That servant then seized a fellow servant who owed him a hundred denarii and had him imprisoned. The king condemned his refusal to show mercy after receiving mercy himself. The main teaching is that those forgiven should forgive others. Ten thousand talents and one hundred denarii are deliberately unequal amounts.\n\n8. THE TEN VIRGINS (MATTHEW 25:1–13)\nTen virgins waited for a bridegroom: five were wise and brought extra oil, while five were foolish and brought no reserve. The bridegroom was delayed; all became drowsy and slept. At midnight the call came. The foolish went to buy oil, and the door was shut before they returned. Jesus ended with a warning to keep watch because the day and hour are unknown. The parable teaches readiness; it does not teach that sleep alone made the five foolish.\n\n9. THE TALENTS AND THE MINAS — DIFFERENT PARABLES\nIn Matthew 25:14–30 a master entrusted five talents, two talents and one talent to three servants according to ability. The first two doubled their sums; the third buried his one talent. The master praised the faithful servants and condemned the one who did nothing. In Luke 19:11–27 the parable of the minas has a different setting and distribution. Do not treat every money parable as the same story.\n\n10. THE RICH MAN AND LAZARUS (LUKE 16:19–31)\nA rich man lived in luxury while a poor man named Lazarus lay at his gate, covered with sores and longing for scraps. Both died; Lazarus was carried to Abraham's side, while the rich man was in torment. The rich man asked for relief and for Lazarus to warn his brothers, but Abraham pointed to Moses and the prophets. This Lazarus is not the Lazarus whom Jesus raised in John 11. The story challenges indifference to suffering and refusal to heed God's message.\n\n11. THE COST OF DISCIPLESHIP (LUKE 9; 14; MARK 8)\nA disciple is a learner and follower of Jesus. Jesus taught that anyone who would follow him must deny themselves, take up their cross and follow him (Mark 8:34). In Luke 9:57–62, he challenged would-be followers about comfort, divided priorities and looking back. In Luke 14:25–33 he used examples of a builder calculating the cost of a tower and a king considering battle to explain serious commitment. In John 13:34–35 Jesus gave the command to love one another as he loved them; love is the mark by which disciples are recognised. In John 15, abiding in Jesus as branches abide in the vine is linked to fruitfulness.\n\n12. THE LORD'S PRAYER AND FORGIVENESS (MATTHEW 6; LUKE 11)\nIn Matthew 6:9–13 Jesus taught prayer addressed to “Our Father in heaven,” including honour for God's name, God's kingdom and will, daily bread, forgiveness, and deliverance from temptation and evil. He warned against praying merely to impress others and against empty repetition. Matthew 6:14–15 connects receiving forgiveness with forgiving others. Luke 11 also records teaching on prayer, but its wording and setting differ.\n\nWORKED JAMB-STYLE EXAMPLES\nExample (i): How much did the Samaritan give the innkeeper?\nSolution: Luke 10:35 states two denarii. Answer: Two denarii.\nExample (ii): How many sheep remained when the shepherd sought the lost one?\nSolution: Luke 15:4 says he left 99 of the 100. Answer: Ninety-nine.\nExample (iii): What did the younger son do when he returned?\nSolution: Luke 15:18–21 shows that he confessed his wrongdoing. Answer: He admitted his sin and sought his father's mercy.\nExample (iv): Which man went home justified in the Temple prayer parable?\nSolution: Luke 18:14 identifies the humble tax collector. Answer: The tax collector.\nExample (v): How many of the ten virgins were wise?\nSolution: Matthew 25:2 says five. Answer: Five.\nExample (vi): What did the third servant do with his talent?\nSolution: Matthew 25:18 says he buried it in the ground. Answer: He buried it.\nExample (vii): What is the main teaching of the unforgiving servant?\nSolution: Matthew 18:21–35 calls for extending mercy after receiving forgiveness. Answer: Forgive others as God has forgiven you.\nExample (viii): What does taking up one's cross mean in discipleship?\nSolution: Mark 8:34 links following Jesus with self-denial and costly commitment. Answer: Willingness to sacrifice for faithful obedience.\n\nQUICK UNDERSTANDING CHECK\nExplain the four soils and their meanings. Retell the Good Samaritan's actions in order. Compare the lost sheep, coin and son. Distinguish the talents from the minas. Explain how the rich fool differs from the rich man and Lazarus.\n\nPRACTICE QUESTIONS\nUse the Practice Questions button to test the stories, numbers, quotations and meanings of Jesus' parables.\n\nSYLLABUS DEEP DIVE — THE WEDDING FEAST AND THE DRAGNET (MATTHEW 22:1–14; 13:47–50)\nTHE WEDDING FEAST AND GARMENT: Jesus compared the kingdom of heaven to a king who prepared a wedding banquet for his son. Those first invited refused to come; some ignored the invitation and others mistreated or killed the king's servants. The king judged the murderers and sent servants to invite people from the roads, both bad and good, so that the hall was filled. When he inspected the guests, he found a man without wedding clothes. The man was speechless when questioned and was expelled. Jesus concluded, 'Many are called, but few are chosen.' The parable teaches that an invitation to the kingdom calls for an appropriate response; it does not say that merely being present at the feast is enough. The text does not explicitly say who supplied the garment, so do not invent that detail as a Bible fact.\nTHE DRAGNET: In Matthew 13:47–50 a net cast into the sea gathered fish of every kind. When full, the fishermen drew it ashore, kept the good fish and threw away the bad. Jesus explained that at the close of the age angels would separate the evil from the righteous. The point is final judgment, not advice about commercial fishing. Compare the Dragnet with the Weeds (Matthew 13:24–30, 36–43): both warn against assuming that everyone gathered together has the same final outcome.\nExample (additional): Who separates the evil from the righteous in Jesus' explanation of the Dragnet? \nSolution: Matthew 13:49 identifies the angels at the end of the age. Answer: The angels.\nExample (additional): What happened to the guest without a wedding garment? \nSolution: The king ordered him bound and cast into the outer darkness (Matthew 22:11–13). Answer: He was expelled from the banquet.\nQUICK UNDERSTANDING CHECK: Retell the invitations and the garment incident in order. Explain how the Dragnet differs from the Parable of the Sower.\n"},"old":{"title":"Parables, mercy and the call to discipleship","body":"A parable uses a familiar situation to teach a spiritual or moral lesson. Follow Jesus' explanation rather than inventing meanings. In the weeds, harvest means the age's close, reapers are angels, and the righteous shine like the sun in their Father's kingdom.\n\nMark's seed-growing-secretly parable describes seed developing through blade, ear and full grain while the farmer does not understand how it grows. This is different from the sower, which emphasises soils and responses to the word, and from the mustard seed, which contrasts small beginnings with growth. The presence of seed in several parables does not make their lessons identical.\n\nLuke 15 celebrates repentance through the lost sheep, lost coin and lost son. The saying about joy before God's angels follows the coin. The returning son is welcomed by a father who runs, embraces and celebrates. These stories express restoration; they do not portray wrongdoing as harmless or repentance as unnecessary.\n\nThe Good Samaritan shows compassion and mercy through practical help to a wounded traveller. It challenges an attempt to restrict neighbour-love to a favoured group. A Nigerian classroom example is helping an injured person without first demanding that the person belong to one's church, tribe or social circle.\n\nThe rich fool stores wealth as if it guarantees many years, but his life is required that night. In the rich man and Lazarus, the fixed chasm prevents crossing after death. Distinguish the rich man's past luxury from the direct reason Lazarus cannot cross to bring water.\n\nDiscipleship means learning from Jesus and practising his teaching. The Twelve are sent to preach God's kingdom and heal; the later Great Commission extends disciple-making to all nations.\n\nCommon trap: recall the particular parable's explanation instead of answering from a general religious idea.\n\nThe lost sheep's recovery leads to shared rejoicing; the prodigal son's father acts from compassion. The seventy are sent to heal and announce God's kingdom, like lambs among wolves: opposition is expected, not literal residence with animals. The sower concerns God's kingdom and responses to its message, not merely agriculture.\n\nBearing the cross means costly commitment to following Jesus, not literally carrying timber or deliberately seeking death. “Leave the dead to bury their dead” stresses the urgency of discipleship, not a universal ban on attending funerals. Immediately following the call requires faith rather than delaying until every competing demand disappears. The weeds also concern final judgement and the kingdom's demands.\n\nIn the sower, the road loses the word to the evil one, rocky ground fails under trouble, and thorns choke it with cares and wealth; good soil bears fruit.\n\nLuke says the lawyer asks about eternal life to test Jesus; the Good Samaritan answers the ensuing question about one's neighbour."}},{"id":"passion-resurrection","revised":true,"new":{"title":"The last days, crucifixion and resurrection of Jesus","body":"JESUS' LAST DAYS, CRUCIFIXION AND RESURRECTION — DETAILED CRS LESSON\n\nINTRODUCTION\nThe Passion refers to Jesus' suffering and death. The Gospels narrate his entry into Jerusalem, final teaching, meal with disciples, arrest, trials, crucifixion, burial and resurrection. The sequence matters. Matthew, Mark, Luke and John sometimes emphasise different details, especially in the resurrection appearances. A correct answer must match the passage named.\n\n1. ENTRY INTO JERUSALEM (MATTHEW 21; MARK 11; LUKE 19; JOHN 12)\nJesus entered Jerusalem riding on a young donkey, fulfilling prophetic imagery of a humble king. Crowds spread cloaks and branches and shouted words of praise including “Hosanna.” Matthew reports two animals in the fulfilment description, while Mark and Luke focus on the colt. In Luke 19:41–44 Jesus wept over Jerusalem and warned about its coming destruction. John 12 mentions palm branches. Do not confuse the triumphal entry with Jesus' earlier baptism in the Jordan.\n\n2. CLEANSING THE TEMPLE AND CONTROVERSIES\nIn the Synoptic Gospels, Jesus entered the Temple area and drove out those buying and selling, overturning money-changers' tables. He referred to God's house as a house of prayer and condemned its misuse as a den of robbers (Mark 11:15–17). John's Gospel places a Temple-cleansing account early in Jesus' ministry (John 2:13–22). In the final days Jesus debated questions about authority, taxes to Caesar, resurrection and the greatest commandment. The chief priests and other leaders sought ways to arrest him.\n\n3. THE ANOINTING AT BETHANY AND JUDAS' BETRAYAL\nMatthew 26:6–13 and Mark 14:3–9 describe a woman anointing Jesus with costly perfume at Bethany in the house of Simon the leper. Some objected that the perfume could have been sold for the poor. Jesus defended her action as preparation for burial. John 12:1–8 names Mary, sister of Martha and Lazarus, as the one who anointed Jesus' feet and wiped them with her hair; Judas objected. These Gospel details should not be silently merged.\nJudas Iscariot, one of the Twelve, agreed to betray Jesus. Matthew 26:15 mentions thirty pieces of silver. At the arrest he identified Jesus with a kiss in the Synoptic accounts. Judas' action is different from Peter's later denial.\n\n4. THE LAST SUPPER (MATTHEW 26; MARK 14; LUKE 22; JOHN 13)\nJesus ate a final meal with his disciples before his arrest. In the Synoptic accounts he took bread, gave thanks or blessed it, broke it and connected it with his body. He gave the cup and connected it with his blood of the covenant. Christians remember this meal in Holy Communion or the Lord's Supper. Luke 22:19 includes the command to do this in remembrance of him.\nJohn 13 instead gives extended attention to Jesus washing the disciples' feet. Peter initially resisted, but Jesus explained that his disciples should imitate his humble service. Jesus also predicted betrayal. John 13:34–35 records the new commandment to love one another. The washing of feet teaches service, not that Jesus abandoned his authority.\n\n5. GETHSEMANE AND THE ARREST (MATTHEW 26; MARK 14; LUKE 22)\nAfter the meal, Jesus went to the Mount of Olives and prayed at Gethsemane. He took Peter, James and John farther with him and asked them to keep watch. He was deeply distressed and prayed that the cup might pass, yet submitted to God's will. The disciples repeatedly fell asleep. Judas arrived with an armed crowd and Jesus was arrested. One disciple cut off the ear of the high priest's servant; John 18:10 names Peter as the disciple and Malchus as the servant. Luke 22:51 says Jesus healed the ear. The disciples fled.\n\n6. PETER'S DENIAL AND THE TRIALS\nJesus had predicted that Peter would deny him three times before the cock crowed (Matthew 26:34; Luke 22:34). Peter denied knowing Jesus, and after the cock crowed he remembered the prediction and wept bitterly. Mark's account specifies the cock crowing twice (Mark 14:30, 72), a detail important for source-specific questions.\nJesus was questioned before Jewish authorities, including the high priest, and taken to Pontius Pilate, the Roman governor. Luke 23 describes Pilate sending Jesus to Herod Antipas, who sent him back. The crowd demanded the release of Barabbas rather than Jesus. Pilate sentenced Jesus to crucifixion. The inscription over the cross identified Jesus as King of the Jews, with wording varying among the Gospels.\n\n7. CRUCIFIXION AT GOLGOTHA\nJesus was led to Golgotha, meaning Place of a Skull. Simon of Cyrene was compelled to carry his cross in the Synoptic accounts (Mark 15:21). Jesus was crucified between two criminals. Soldiers divided his garments. Mark 15:25 gives the third hour for the crucifixion; Mark 15:33 describes darkness from the sixth to the ninth hour. Jesus cried out, and the curtain of the Temple was torn in two. A Roman centurion responded to Jesus' death with a confession; Mark 15:39 says, “Truly this man was the Son of God.”\nLuke 23 includes Jesus' prayer for forgiveness, the promise to the repentant criminal, and the words “Father, into your hands I commit my spirit.” John 19 records Jesus' care for his mother, his words “I thirst” and “It is finished,” and the piercing of his side. These sayings are not all narrated in every Gospel.\n\n8. BURIAL BY JOSEPH OF ARIMATHEA\nJoseph of Arimathea, a respected council member and disciple, requested Jesus' body from Pilate. He wrapped it in linen and placed it in a tomb cut from rock, with a stone rolled to the entrance (Mark 15:42–47; Matthew 27:57–61). John 19:39–42 adds Nicodemus bringing myrrh and aloes for burial. The women observed where Jesus was laid so they could return after the Sabbath.\n\n9. THE EMPTY TOMB — READ EACH GOSPEL CAREFULLY\nEarly on the first day of the week, women went to the tomb. Mark 16:1 names Mary Magdalene, Mary the mother of James, and Salome bringing spices. Matthew 28:1 names Mary Magdalene and the other Mary; Luke 24:10 lists Mary Magdalene, Joanna, Mary the mother of James and other women. They found the stone moved and received the message that Jesus had risen. Matthew mentions an angel; Luke mentions two men in dazzling clothes; John focuses on Mary Magdalene's discovery and her encounter with Jesus. These are different narrative emphases, so answer from the Gospel specified.\nIn John 20, Mary Magdalene initially thought Jesus was the gardener. She recognised him when he called her name. Peter and the disciple Jesus loved also visited the tomb. Jesus later appeared to the disciples, showing his wounds.\n\n10. THOMAS, EMMAUS AND THE COMMISSION\nLuke 24 describes two disciples travelling to Emmaus; one is named Cleopas. Jesus joined them unrecognised, explained the Scriptures and was recognised in the breaking of bread. In John 20 Thomas was absent from Jesus' first appearance to the gathered disciples and said he needed to see the wounds. Eight days later Jesus appeared again; Thomas responded, “My Lord and my God!” Jesus blessed those who believe without seeing.\nMatthew 28:18–20 gives the Great Commission to make disciples of all nations, baptising and teaching them. Luke 24 and Acts 1 emphasise the disciples waiting for power from the Holy Spirit. Acts 1:3 says Jesus appeared to the apostles over 40 days after his suffering; Acts 1:9–11 describes his ascension and the promise of his return.\n\n11. EXAM FACTS TO KEEP SEPARATE\nThirty pieces of silver = Matthew's account of Judas' betrayal payment. Three denials = Peter's denial. Golgotha = Place of a Skull. Simon of Cyrene = compelled to carry the cross. Joseph of Arimathea = requested and buried the body. Nicodemus = helped with burial in John. Emmaus = road where Jesus appeared to two disciples. Thomas = confession after seeing the risen Jesus. Forty days = post-resurrection appearances in Acts 1, not the period Jesus spent in the tomb.\n\nWORKED JAMB-STYLE EXAMPLES\nExample (i): How much did Judas receive in Matthew for betraying Jesus?\nSolution: Matthew 26:15 records thirty pieces of silver. Answer: Thirty pieces of silver.\nExample (ii): Who was compelled to carry Jesus' cross?\nSolution: Mark 15:21 names Simon of Cyrene. Answer: Simon of Cyrene.\nExample (iii): What does Golgotha mean?\nSolution: Mark 15:22 explains the name as Place of a Skull. Answer: Place of a Skull.\nExample (iv): Who requested Jesus' body from Pilate?\nSolution: Mark 15:43 identifies Joseph of Arimathea. Answer: Joseph of Arimathea.\nExample (v): Who said, “My Lord and my God”?\nSolution: John 20:28 records Thomas' response. Answer: Thomas.\nExample (vi): Which disciple was named on the road to Emmaus?\nSolution: Luke 24:18 names Cleopas. Answer: Cleopas.\nExample (vii): How long did Jesus appear to the apostles after his suffering, according to Acts?\nSolution: Acts 1:3 states 40 days. Answer: Forty days.\nExample (viii): Which Gospel names Malchus as the servant whose ear was cut off?\nSolution: John 18:10 identifies him. Answer: John.\n\nQUICK UNDERSTANDING CHECK\nRetell the Passion events from Jerusalem entry to burial. Distinguish Judas' betrayal from Peter's denial. Identify the distinctive resurrection details in Mark, Luke and John. Explain the significance of the Last Supper and foot-washing. Distinguish the forty days of appearances from the time in the tomb.\n\nPRACTICE QUESTIONS\nUse the Practice Questions button to practise the events, persons, locations, quotations and source-specific details."},"old":{"title":"The last days, crucifixion and resurrection of Jesus","body":"The triumphal entry includes the crowd's acclaim of the coming kingdom of David. It is distinct from the transfiguration and from the later Passover meal. At the Last Supper, the material emblems are bread and the cup, described as the fruit of the vine. In the Passover setting the bread is unleavened. Body and blood are what the emblems signify, not alternative material objects placed on the table.\n\nThe Synoptic Gospels present the Last Supper as a Passover meal. John uses a different chronological presentation around the preparation for Passover. Do not combine the accounts into an inaccurate date statement or treat different Jewish day reckonings as if they were simply modern midnight-to-midnight dates. A question should identify which Gospel account it is testing.\n\nIn John 20, Mary reports the missing body to Peter and the other disciple, who then head towards the tomb. Her encounter with angels follows later. The Emmaus disciples recount Jesus' death and reports of the empty tomb before recognising their companion as the risen Jesus. Peter's Pentecost sermon proclaims resurrection as an event to which the apostles bear witness.\n\nCommon traps:\n• Keep sayings attached to their Gospel accounts.\n• “Immediately after” requires the next event, not any later event in the chapter.\n• Distinguish a report of resurrection from the moment a character personally recognises the risen Jesus.\n\nThe empty tomb is discovered on the first day, not Passover day. “It is finished” announces completed redemption; the torn curtain signifies access to God through Christ.\n\nAt the triumphal entry Jesus rides a colt and is acclaimed as king, fulfilling the humble-king imagery of Zechariah. Palm Sunday commemorates that entry. When challenged to silence the disciples, Jesus says the stones would cry out. Matthew names an angel rolling back the tombstone. Mark's young man tells the women that Jesus goes before the disciples to Galilee. The Emmaus companions, including Cleopas, later recall their hearts burning as he explains the Scriptures. Jesus' words to Thomas bless those who believe without seeing him physically. These details explain different encounters rather than one simultaneous scene.\n\nMatthew describes bribery of guards to spread the claim that disciples stole the body. John's risen Jesus greets the gathered disciples with peace. Mary calls him “Rabboni,” explained as Teacher. Distinguish reports, lies and narration.\n\nAt the Temple, Jesus drives out the traders before citing the house of prayer and den of robbers sayings.\n\nMatthew says the guards tremble at the angel and become like dead men; this is distinct from the women's response."}},{"id":"early-church","revised":true,"old":{"title":"The Holy Spirit, the apostles and the Jerusalem Council","body":"Acts continues Luke's earlier account and addresses Theophilus. It describes the spread of the Christian message from Jerusalem onwards. At Pentecost the Holy Spirit fills the disciples and they speak in other tongues. Peter explains the event by proclaiming the crucified Jesus as risen and exalted, both Lord and Christ. Christians often describe Pentecost as the Church's birth; this is a teaching description of the event's significance.\n\nJudas' replacement had to accompany the group from John's baptism to the ascension and witness the resurrection. Mere Galilean origin or following John was insufficient: sustained participation in Jesus' ministry mattered.\n\nAfter the lame man's healing, the council acknowledges the sign but forbids Peter and John to speak in Jesus' name. A later apostolic arrest arises from the high priest's party's jealousy. Gamaliel counsels restraint: human work fails, but God's cannot be overthrown. Distinguish these hearings.\n\nAnanias and Sapphira's deaths bring great fear upon the Church. Peter's words make clear that the property and its proceeds had been under their control; the offence was deceit, not failure to surrender every possession. Stephen is the first Christian martyr described in Acts. He was one of the Seven, not one of the Twelve. James son of Zebedee is the first of the Twelve whose martyrdom Acts records.\n\nPersecution following Stephen's death scatters believers, while the apostles remain in Jerusalem. Gentile conversion later raises the dispute over circumcision. At the Jerusalem Council, salvation is not made dependent on circumcision. The letter names abstention from idol-related food, sexual immorality, strangled meat and blood.\n\nCommon trap: distinguish a divine initiative, a church's human action, and the particular group named in each episode.\n\nPeter quotes Joel to explain Pentecost. Acts 6 describes the Seven as reputable men full of the Spirit and wisdom. After Samaria receives Philip's preaching, the apostles send Peter and John, who pray and lay hands on believers. Peter's vision prepares him to receive Gentiles; Cornelius's household then receives the Spirit. After his angelic release from prison, Peter visits Mary, the mother of John Mark. Acts 4 names Peter and John as detained after the healing at the Temple. Each episode has its own people and purpose.\n\nJames proposes the Jerusalem Council's concluding judgement. Philip explains Isaiah to the Ethiopian eunuch. Matthias replaces Judas. The first believers devote themselves to apostolic teaching and fellowship.\n\nThe Hellenists complain that their widows are neglected in daily distribution, prompting the Seven's appointment. An angel frees the apostles and sends them to teach in the Temple. Simon the magician is acclaimed in Samaria. Cornelius is a centurion whose prayers and alms are remembered before God. Peter says God cleansed Gentile hearts by faith. Peter's question to Sapphira tests her truthfulness."},"new":{"title":"The Holy Spirit, the apostles and the Jerusalem Council","body":"THE HOLY SPIRIT, THE APOSTLES AND THE JERUSALEM COUNCIL — DETAILED LESSON\n\nINTRODUCTION\nActs of the Apostles is the second volume associated with Luke and is addressed to Theophilus (Acts 1:1). It follows the spread of the message about Jesus from Jerusalem to Judea, Samaria and beyond (Acts 1:8). A student must know the sequence, the people involved, why each problem arose and what the apostles decided.\n\n1. THE ASCENSION AND REPLACEMENT OF JUDAS (ACTS 1)\nAfter his resurrection, Jesus appeared to the apostles over 40 days, spoke about God's kingdom and told them to wait in Jerusalem for the promised Holy Spirit. When they asked about restoring Israel's kingdom, Jesus said that the times were the Father's concern; they were to be his witnesses. He was lifted up, and a cloud took him from their sight. Two men in white said he would return in the manner he had gone.\nThe disciples returned from the Mount of Olives to Jerusalem. About 120 believers were gathered (Acts 1:15). Peter explained that someone must replace Judas Iscariot. The replacement had to have accompanied Jesus' followers from John's baptism to the ascension and be a witness to the resurrection. Joseph called Barsabbas (surnamed Justus) and Matthias were proposed. After prayer, lots were cast, and Matthias was chosen (Acts 1:23–26). Paul was not the apostle selected to replace Judas.\n\n2. PENTECOST — WHAT HAPPENED (ACTS 2)\nPentecost was a Jewish festival. The believers were together when a sound like a rushing mighty wind filled the house. Divided tongues as of fire appeared and rested on them. They were filled with the Holy Spirit and began speaking in other tongues as the Spirit enabled them. Jews from many lands heard the mighty works of God in their own languages. Some were amazed; others accused the speakers of being drunk.\nPeter explained that the event fulfilled Joel's prophecy about the outpouring of God's Spirit. He proclaimed Jesus' death, resurrection and exaltation, and concluded that God had made the crucified Jesus both Lord and Christ. When listeners asked what to do, Peter told them to repent and be baptised in Jesus Christ's name for forgiveness and the gift of the Holy Spirit (Acts 2:37–38). About 3,000 people were added that day (Acts 2:41). The new believers devoted themselves to the apostles' teaching, fellowship, breaking of bread and prayers. They shared possessions to meet needs. Do not confuse the approximately 120 gathered before Pentecost with the approximately 3,000 added after Peter's sermon.\n\n3. THE LAME MAN AT THE BEAUTIFUL GATE (ACTS 3)\nPeter and John went to the Temple at the ninth hour, the hour of prayer (about 3 p.m.). A man lame from birth was carried daily to the gate called Beautiful to beg. He asked them for money. Peter said he had no silver or gold, but commanded him in Jesus Christ's name to rise and walk. Peter took his right hand, and the man's feet and ankles became strong. He walked, leaped and praised God. The crowd gathered at Solomon's Portico, where Peter explained that the healing came through faith in Jesus, not the apostles' own power. The man was more than 40 years old (Acts 4:22); this is his age, not the number of years after Pentecost.\n\n4. PETER AND JOHN BEFORE THE COUNCIL (ACTS 4)\nPriests, the captain of the Temple and Sadducees arrested Peter and John because they taught the resurrection in Jesus. The rulers, elders and scribes questioned by what power the healing occurred. Peter, filled with the Holy Spirit, said it was through Jesus Christ of Nazareth, whom they crucified and God raised. He called Jesus the stone rejected by builders that became the cornerstone. The council could not deny the healed man's presence, but ordered them not to speak or teach in Jesus' name. Peter and John answered that they must obey God rather than human orders. They were threatened and released. A later arrest of apostles in Acts 5 must not be mistaken for this first hearing.\n\n5. ANANIAS AND SAPPHIRA (ACTS 4:36–5:11)\nBarnabas sold a field and brought the proceeds for the community (Acts 4:36–37). Ananias and his wife Sapphira also sold property but secretly kept part of the money while presenting the gift as though it were the whole amount. Peter said Ananias had lied to the Holy Spirit; Ananias fell and died. About three hours later Sapphira arrived, repeated the deception and also died. The problem was deliberate dishonesty, not that Christians were forbidden to own property or required to donate every sale in full.\n\n6. GAMALIEL'S ADVICE (ACTS 5)\nThe high priest and his associates, identified with the Sadducees, arrested the apostles because of jealousy. An angel opened the prison doors, and the apostles returned to teaching. When brought before the council, Peter said, “We must obey God rather than men.” Gamaliel, a Pharisee and respected teacher of the law, urged caution. He cited Theudas and Judas the Galilean, whose movements collapsed, and warned that a human undertaking would fail but a work of God could not be overthrown. The apostles were beaten, warned and released. Gamaliel's speech belongs to Acts 5, not the first hearing in Acts 4.\n\n7. THE SEVEN AND STEPHEN (ACTS 6–7)\nGreek-speaking Jewish believers complained that their widows were neglected in the daily food distribution. The Twelve asked the community to select seven reputable men full of the Spirit and wisdom, allowing the apostles to focus on prayer and ministry of the word. The seven were Stephen, Philip, Prochorus, Nicanor, Timon, Parmenas and Nicolaus (a proselyte of Antioch). Stephen performed signs and debated opponents. False witnesses accused him of speaking against the Temple and law. In his speech he reviewed Israel's history and accused his hearers of resisting the Holy Spirit. As he was stoned, he saw Jesus standing at God's right hand and prayed for his killers. Saul approved of Stephen's death (Acts 8:1). Stephen is commonly described as the first Christian martyr.\n\n8. PHILIP IN SAMARIA AND THE ETHIOPIAN OFFICIAL (ACTS 8)\nPersecution scattered believers. Philip preached in Samaria; many believed, and Simon, who had practised magic, also professed belief. Peter and John later visited and prayed for believers to receive the Holy Spirit. Simon offered money for the power to give the Spirit; Peter rebuked him. The attempt to buy spiritual power is associated with the term simony.\nAn angel directed Philip toward the desert road from Jerusalem to Gaza. He met an Ethiopian eunuch, an official responsible for the treasury of Candace, queen of the Ethiopians. The official was reading Isaiah 53 but needed help understanding it. Philip explained the good news about Jesus. At water the official requested baptism; Philip baptised him. The official went on rejoicing. Do not confuse Philip the evangelist with Philip the apostle simply because they share a name.\n\n9. CORNELIUS AND THE GENTILES (ACTS 10–11)\nCornelius was a centurion of the Italian cohort in Caesarea, described as devout and generous. An angel told him to send for Simon Peter in Joppa. Peter, praying on a rooftop, saw a sheet containing various animals and heard a voice telling him to kill and eat. He protested that he had never eaten anything common or unclean. The vision was repeated three times. Peter learned that he should not call any person common or unclean. He went to Cornelius' house, preached about Jesus, and the Holy Spirit fell on the Gentile listeners. Peter ordered them baptised. Later he explained these events to believers in Jerusalem. The issue was the acceptance of Gentiles, not merely changing Peter's diet.\n\n10. THE JERUSALEM COUNCIL (ACTS 15)\nSome believers from Judea insisted that Gentile converts must be circumcised according to Moses' custom to be saved. Paul and Barnabas disagreed and travelled to Jerusalem to discuss the matter with apostles and elders. Peter recalled that God had given the Holy Spirit to Gentiles and cleansed their hearts by faith. Paul and Barnabas reported signs among Gentiles. James referred to Scripture and proposed not placing the full burden of the Mosaic law on Gentile converts. The council's letter asked them to abstain from food polluted by idols, sexual immorality, what had been strangled and blood (Acts 15:19–29). Judas Barsabbas and Silas accompanied Paul and Barnabas with the letter to Antioch. The decision did not require Gentiles to become Jews through circumcision before belonging to Christ.\n\nWORKED JAMB-STYLE EXAMPLES\nExample (i): Who replaced Judas among the Twelve? \nSolution: Acts 1:26 says the lot fell on Matthias. Answer: Matthias.\nExample (ii): About how many people joined the believers at Pentecost?\nSolution: Acts 2:41 records about 3,000. Answer: Three thousand.\nExample (iii): At what hour did Peter and John go to the Temple when they healed the lame man?\nSolution: Acts 3:1 gives the ninth hour. Answer: The ninth hour.\nExample (iv): Who advised the council not to fight a work that might be from God?\nSolution: Acts 5:34–39 identifies Gamaliel. Answer: Gamaliel.\nExample (v): Why were seven men selected in Acts 6?\nSolution: Greek-speaking widows were being overlooked in the daily distribution. Answer: To address unfair food distribution and enable the apostles to focus on prayer and preaching.\nExample (vi): Who explained Isaiah's prophecy to the Ethiopian official?\nSolution: Acts 8:26–35 names Philip. Answer: Philip.\nExample (vii): Which Roman officer received Peter in Caesarea?\nSolution: Acts 10 names Cornelius. Answer: Cornelius.\nExample (viii): What question led to the Jerusalem Council?\nSolution: Acts 15 concerns whether Gentile converts had to be circumcised and observe the Mosaic law for salvation. Answer: Whether Gentiles needed circumcision to be saved.\n\nQUICK UNDERSTANDING CHECK\nExplain the difference between the 120 believers and 3,000 converts. Distinguish Acts 4 from Acts 5 hearings. Name the seven selected in Acts 6. Explain the lesson of Peter's vision. State the Jerusalem Council's decision and four requested abstentions.\n\nPRACTICE QUESTIONS\nUse the practice button for questions on Acts 1–15. Identify the event, person and chapter before selecting an answer."}},{"id":"paul-mission","revised":true,"old":{"title":"Paul's mission, generosity and Onesimus","body":"Paul's missionary work joins proclamation with the formation and support of Christian communities. At Antioch, the Holy Spirit directs the setting apart of Barnabas and Saul. The worshipping leaders fast, pray, lay hands on them and release them; Acts then describes them as sent by the Holy Spirit towards Seleucia. Divine initiative and the community's commissioning are related but should not be confused when a question quotes the account.\n\nAt Philippi, Paul expels a spirit of divination from a slave girl. Her owners lose their source of profit and bring Paul and Silas before the authorities. Their detention therefore follows a clash involving economic exploitation as well as religious opposition. The speech about an unknown God belongs to Athens, not the event that caused the Philippian imprisonment.\n\nIn Ephesus, Demetrius the silversmith stirs up craftsmen whose shrine-making trade is threatened. The crowd seizes Gaius and Aristarchus, Macedonian companions of Paul. Demetrius is the instigator, while the companions are those caught up in the riot. Alexander also appears in the wider account, so a familiar name should not replace the precise pair being asked for.\n\nPaul encourages generosity across churches. His praise of the Corinthians' readiness concerns support for needy believers, not a claim that they excel in every moral matter. He describes the Philippians' gift as a fragrant offering, an acceptable sacrifice pleasing to God. Epaphroditus delivered that support. Such giving is not a purchase of spiritual power or a guarantee of material wealth.\n\nPhilemon concerns Onesimus and reconciliation within Christian fellowship. Paul calls Onesimus his child, having become his spiritual father in imprisonment, and also describes him as a beloved brother. A question asking merely what Paul called him can therefore permit both answers. Paul's appeal challenges Philemon to receive him on a different basis from a mere property claim.\n\nCommon traps:\n• Philippi: slave girl's deliverance; Ephesus: shrine-makers' riot; Athens: unknown-God speech.\n• Distinguish the person leading opposition from Paul's travelling companions.\n• Several descriptions of the same person may all be present in one letter.\n\nAt Paphos Paul rebukes Elymas, also called Bar-Jesus, for trying to turn the proconsul away from faith. At Lystra the crowd attempts sacrifices to Barnabas and Paul after a healing, mistaking them for gods. Lydia responds to Paul's preaching when the Lord opens her heart. Philippians thanks the church that supported Paul's ministry and promises God's provision according to his riches in Christ. Second Corinthians connects generous giving with thanksgiving to God and assistance to believers. None of these passages teaches that money can buy salvation.\n\nPaul says his persecution sought to destroy the Church. Ananias initially fears meeting him because of his reputation."},"new":{"title":"Paul's mission, generosity and Onesimus","body":"PAUL'S CONVERSION, MISSIONARY JOURNEYS AND CHRISTIAN GENEROSITY — DETAILED LESSON\n\nINTRODUCTION\nPaul, also called Saul, was a Jew from Tarsus who became a leading missionary to Gentiles. Acts describes his conversion, journeys, opposition, imprisonment and preaching. His letters address the faith and conduct of Christian communities. Do not assume that every city or companion belongs to the same journey.\n\n1. SAUL'S CONVERSION (ACTS 9; 22; 26)\nSaul approved of Stephen's death and persecuted Christians. He obtained authority from the high priest to arrest followers of Jesus in Damascus. On the road a light from heaven flashed around him; he fell and heard a voice asking, “Saul, Saul, why are you persecuting me?” The voice identified itself as Jesus. Saul became blind and was led into Damascus. He remained without sight and did not eat or drink for three days. God instructed Ananias, a disciple living in Damascus, to visit Saul in the house of Judas on Straight Street. Ananias hesitated because he knew Saul's reputation, but God called Saul a chosen instrument to carry his name before Gentiles, kings and Israel. Ananias laid hands on him; something like scales fell from Saul's eyes, he regained sight, was baptised and ate. This Ananias is not the man who died with Sapphira in Acts 5.\nActs 9 says Saul escaped a plot in Damascus when disciples lowered him in a basket through an opening in the city wall. Barnabas later introduced him to the apostles in Jerusalem. Saul is called Paul in Acts 13:9; Acts does not say God changed his name at the moment of conversion.\n\n2. ANTIOCH AND THE FIRST MISSIONARY JOURNEY (ACTS 11–14)\nBarnabas went to Tarsus to bring Saul to Antioch in Syria. They taught the church there; Acts 11:26 says disciples were first called Christians in Antioch. When famine was predicted by Agabus, believers sent relief to Judea through Barnabas and Saul (Acts 11:27–30).\nIn Acts 13 the Holy Spirit directed the Antioch church to set apart Barnabas and Saul. After fasting, prayer and laying on of hands, they departed for Seleucia and sailed to Cyprus. At Paphos they encountered Elymas (Bar-Jesus), a magician opposing their message to Sergius Paulus, the proconsul. Paul rebuked Elymas, who became temporarily blind; the proconsul believed.\nThey travelled to Perga in Pamphylia, where John Mark left them and returned to Jerusalem. In Pisidian Antioch Paul preached in the synagogue, tracing God's promises to Jesus. After opposition they turned to Gentiles. In Iconium they preached despite threats. At Lystra Paul healed a man lame from birth; crowds called Barnabas Zeus and Paul Hermes because Paul was the chief speaker. They rejected worship as gods. Later opponents stirred the crowd, Paul was stoned and dragged out, but survived. At Derbe they made disciples and returned through the towns to strengthen believers and appoint elders.\n\n3. THE SECOND JOURNEY: PHILIPPI, THESSALONICA, BEREA, ATHENS, CORINTH (ACTS 15–18)\nPaul and Barnabas disagreed about taking John Mark again. Barnabas took Mark to Cyprus; Paul chose Silas and travelled through Syria and Cilicia. At Lystra Paul recruited Timothy, whose mother was a Jewish believer and father a Greek. In Troas Paul saw a vision of a Macedonian man asking for help. They crossed into Macedonia.\nAt Philippi Lydia, a seller of purple goods from Thyatira, listened to Paul and was baptised with her household (Acts 16). Paul expelled a spirit of divination from a slave girl; her owners became angry because they lost income. Paul and Silas were beaten and imprisoned. At midnight they prayed and sang; an earthquake opened the prison doors. The jailer feared the prisoners had escaped and prepared to kill himself. Paul stopped him. Asked what he must do to be saved, Paul answered, “Believe in the Lord Jesus,” and the jailer and household were baptised. Paul later insisted that the magistrates acknowledge the injustice of beating Roman citizens without proper trial.\nIn Thessalonica Paul reasoned from Scripture that the Messiah had to suffer and rise. Some opponents created unrest, so Paul went to Berea. The Bereans examined the Scriptures daily to test his message (Acts 17:11).\nIn Athens Paul spoke at the Areopagus about an altar inscribed “To an unknown god.” He proclaimed the Creator who does not live in man-made temples and called for repentance because God had appointed a judge confirmed by resurrection. Some mocked, others wanted to hear more; Dionysius the Areopagite and a woman named Damaris believed (Acts 17:34).\nAt Corinth Paul worked with Aquila and Priscilla, tentmakers who had come from Italy after Claudius expelled Jews from Rome. He remained there about 18 months (Acts 18:11). Gallio, proconsul of Achaia, refused to judge a dispute about Jewish religious law.\n\n4. THE THIRD JOURNEY AND EPHESUS (ACTS 18–21)\nApollos, a learned Jew from Alexandria, taught about Jesus but knew only John's baptism. Priscilla and Aquila explained God's way to him more accurately (Acts 18:24–26).\nPaul spent a long period in Ephesus, teaching in the synagogue and then the hall of Tyrannus. Extraordinary healings occurred. Some itinerant Jewish exorcists, including seven sons of Sceva, tried to use Jesus' name without understanding his authority and were overpowered (Acts 19:13–16). Many believers confessed their former practices; those who practised magic burned books valued at 50,000 pieces of silver (Acts 19:19).\nDemetrius, a silversmith making silver shrines of Artemis, feared loss of trade. He stirred up craftsmen and a crowd, which seized Gaius and Aristarchus, Paul's Macedonian companions. The city clerk calmed the riot (Acts 19). This economic conflict in Ephesus differs from the slave girl's owners in Philippi.\nAt Troas Paul spoke late into the night. Eutychus, sitting in a window on the third storey, fell and was taken up dead; Paul went down, embraced him and he was restored (Acts 20:7–12). At Miletus Paul called the Ephesian elders, reminded them of his ministry and warned them to guard the flock.\n\n5. PAUL'S ARREST AND JOURNEY TO ROME (ACTS 21–28)\nIn Jerusalem opponents accused Paul of defiling the Temple. Roman soldiers intervened, and Paul addressed the crowd. Before the council he spoke of the hope of resurrection, dividing Pharisees and Sadducees (Acts 23). More than 40 men plotted to kill him, but Paul's nephew learned of the plan. Paul was transferred to Caesarea under guard. He defended himself before Felix, Festus and King Agrippa II. As a Roman citizen he appealed to Caesar (Acts 25:11).\nDuring the voyage to Rome a violent storm struck the ship. Paul encouraged those aboard and assured them their lives would be spared though the ship would be lost. Acts 27:37 says 276 people were aboard. They were shipwrecked on Malta. A viper fastened to Paul's hand, but he suffered no harm; he also healed Publius' father. In Rome Paul lived under guard and continued preaching (Acts 28). Acts does not narrate Paul's death.\n\n6. GENEROSITY AND COLLECTIONS FOR BELIEVERS\nActs 11 describes Antioch sending famine relief to Judea. In 2 Corinthians 8–9 Paul encouraged generous, willing contributions for believers in need, praising the Macedonian churches for giving despite hardship. He taught that giving should be deliberate and cheerful rather than forced (2 Corinthians 9:7). In 1 Corinthians 16:1–4 he instructed believers to set aside contributions on the first day of each week. Christian generosity involves honesty and care, not competition over the largest amount.\n\n7. ONESIMUS AND PHILEMON\nPhilemon was a Christian associated with the church meeting in his house. Onesimus, an enslaved man connected with Philemon, became close to Paul during Paul's imprisonment and was converted through Paul's ministry. Paul sent him back with a letter appealing to Philemon to receive him no longer merely as a slave but as a beloved brother (Philemon 10–16). Paul offered to repay any debt or wrong charged to Onesimus (Philemon 18–19). The letter is a personal appeal for reconciliation, not a report of a missionary journey. The name Onesimus is connected with usefulness; Paul makes a wordplay about formerly being useless but now useful.\n\nWORKED JAMB-STYLE EXAMPLES\nExample (i): Who laid hands on Saul after his blindness in Damascus?\nSolution: Acts 9:17 names Ananias. Answer: Ananias.\nExample (ii): Where were disciples first called Christians?\nSolution: Acts 11:26 identifies Antioch. Answer: Antioch.\nExample (iii): Which missionary companion left Paul and Barnabas at Perga?\nSolution: Acts 13:13 identifies John Mark. Answer: John Mark.\nExample (iv): Why were Paul and Silas imprisoned at Philippi?\nSolution: Acts 16 says the slave girl's owners lost income when her spirit of divination was expelled. Answer: Her owners accused them after losing their source of profit.\nExample (v): What inscription did Paul mention in Athens?\nSolution: Acts 17:23 mentions an altar “To an unknown god.” Answer: To an unknown god.\nExample (vi): Who stirred the riot in Ephesus over silver shrines?\nSolution: Acts 19:24 names Demetrius. Answer: Demetrius.\nExample (vii): How many people were aboard Paul's storm-tossed ship?\nSolution: Acts 27:37 gives 276. Answer: Two hundred and seventy-six.\nExample (viii): How did Paul ask Philemon to receive Onesimus?\nSolution: Philemon 16 asks that he be welcomed as a beloved brother. Answer: As a beloved brother.\n\nQUICK UNDERSTANDING CHECK\nTrace Paul's first journey in order. Distinguish Philippi's slave girl from Ephesus' silversmiths. Name the people Paul met in Athens and Corinth. Explain the Jerusalem appeal to Caesar. Explain why Paul's request about Onesimus challenges ordinary social divisions.\n\nPRACTICE QUESTIONS\nAttempt mapped JAMB questions on Paul's conversion, journeys, speeches, companions, trials, generosity and Philemon."}},{"id":"faith-new-life","revised":true,"old":{"title":"Justification, grace and new life in Christ","body":"In Romans, justification is God's gracious gift declaring sinners righteous or acquitted through faith in Christ, not earned by law-keeping. Grace means God's freely given favour; redemption concerns deliverance accomplished through Christ. Do not confuse the ground of salvation with the believer's response of faith.\n\nThe law makes sin known but cannot itself justify the sinner. Galatians says Christ redeemed people from its curse by becoming a curse, using the image of one hung on a tree. Cross and curse describe related aspects of his act; a question must distinguish the intended clause.\n\nRomans contrasts Adam's trespass, bringing condemnation, with Christ's righteous act, bringing justification and life. It also says that enemies were reconciled to God through the death of his Son. Read the precise sentence before substituting another genuine Christian belief, such as resurrection or the Spirit's work, into the wrong clause.\n\nBaptism into Christ is described as baptism into his death and burial, with resurrection pointing to walking in newness of life. Colossians connects putting off the old self with refusing falsehood. In practical terms, a student cannot claim a changed life while treating cheating, lying or cruelty as unimportant.\n\nRomans says that where sin increased, grace abounded all the more. It does not say people should sin to create more grace; the next chapter rejects that conclusion. “Put on the Lord Jesus Christ” is followed by the instruction not to provide for the flesh's desires.\n\nCommon trap: salvation by grace does not mean moral conduct is irrelevant, and exposing sin is not the same function as forgiving it.\n\nGalatians 3:14 connects redemption with Abraham's blessing reaching Gentiles and receiving the promised Spirit through faith. Romans 8 describes believers as heirs with Christ and connects sharing his sufferings with sharing his glory. Romans 10 joins confessing Jesus as Lord with heartfelt belief in his resurrection; reciting words without faith is not its point. “The word is near you” concerns the word of faith proclaimed. Romans 4 portrays Abraham growing strong in faith and giving glory to God despite age and Sarah's barrenness.\n\nGalatians says the law was added because of transgressions. Romans calls presenting one's body as a living sacrifice spiritual worship; new life rejects using bodily members as instruments of wickedness.\n\nFreedom from the law's curse does not abolish accountability or suffering. Galatians' equal standing in Christ does not erase every human difference.\n\nNew life yields oneself to God as brought from death to life. Reconciled believers receive a ministry of reconciliation. First Peter's rejected cornerstone describes Christ's preciousness to believers and rejection by unbelievers.\n\nThe law and prophets bear witness to the righteousness manifested through Christ."},"new":{"title":"Justification, grace and new life in Christ","body":"JUSTIFICATION, GRACE, FAITH AND NEW LIFE IN CHRIST — DETAILED LESSON\n\nINTRODUCTION\nPaul's letters explain what Christians believe God has done through Jesus and how believers should live. The words justification, grace, redemption, reconciliation, faith, sanctification and baptism are related but not interchangeable. Learn each meaning, the passage where it appears, and the practical consequence.\n\n1. SIN AND THE NEED FOR SALVATION (ROMANS 1–3)\nSin is disobedience to God's will in thought, attitude or action. Romans 3:23 says all have sinned and fall short of God's glory. Paul argues that both Jews and Gentiles need God's mercy. Knowing the law can expose wrongdoing, but possession of the law alone does not make a person righteous (Romans 3:20). In Romans 1 Paul criticises idolatry and moral corruption; in Romans 2 he warns people who judge others while doing wrong themselves. The argument is that no group may boast of automatic superiority before God.\n\n2. JUSTIFICATION BY FAITH (ROMANS 3–5; GALATIANS 2–3)\nJustification means being counted or declared righteous before God. Paul teaches that it is God's gracious gift through Jesus Christ and received by faith, not a reward earned by performing works of the law (Romans 3:24, 28). Grace means God's undeserved favour. Faith means trusting and relying on God's promise in Christ; it is not merely knowing religious facts.\nPaul uses Abraham as an example. Genesis 15:6 says Abraham believed God, and it was counted to him as righteousness. Paul notes that this happened before Abraham received circumcision (Romans 4:9–12). Circumcision therefore cannot be the foundation of Abraham's acceptance by God. In Galatians 2:16 Paul contrasts justification through faith in Christ with reliance on works of the law. Do not answer that Paul considered good behaviour worthless; he rejected the claim that law-keeping earns justification.\n\n3. REDEMPTION, RECONCILIATION AND ATONEMENT\nRedemption means deliverance or release through the saving work of Christ. Romans 3:24 links justification with redemption in Christ Jesus. Galatians 3:13 says Christ redeemed people from the curse of the law by becoming a curse for them, citing the statement about one hanged on a tree.\nReconciliation means the restoration of a broken relationship. Romans 5:10 says that while people were enemies, they were reconciled to God through the death of his Son; verse 11 celebrates reconciliation received through Christ. Atonement is the work by which sin is dealt with and relationship with God is restored. These words emphasise different aspects: justification = righteous standing; redemption = release; reconciliation = restored relationship; grace = unearned favour.\n\n4. ADAM AND CHRIST (ROMANS 5:12–21)\nPaul compares Adam and Christ. Through Adam's trespass sin and death entered human experience; through Christ's obedience comes the gift of righteousness and life. Romans 5:19 contrasts the disobedience of one man with the obedience of one man. This is a theological comparison, not a claim that Adam and Jesus lived at the same time. Paul emphasises that God's gift in Christ overcomes the effects of sin.\n\n5. BAPTISM, DEATH TO SIN AND NEW LIFE (ROMANS 6)\nPaul asks whether believers should continue sinning so that grace may increase. His answer is emphatically no. Romans 6:3–4 describes baptism into Christ Jesus as participation in his death and burial, so believers may walk in newness of life, just as Christ was raised. The old self is described as crucified with Christ so that sin need not rule. Christians should present themselves to God as instruments of righteousness. Romans 6:23 contrasts the wages of sin—death—with God's free gift—eternal life in Christ. Baptism is not permission to continue wrongdoing.\n\n6. THE LAW, INNER STRUGGLE AND THE SPIRIT (ROMANS 7–8)\nRomans 7 describes the struggle between knowing what is right and doing what is wrong. The law reveals sin but does not by itself supply freedom from its power. Romans 8 opens by saying there is no condemnation for those in Christ Jesus. The Spirit gives life, leads believers and helps them resist the rule of sinful desires. Romans 8:15 describes believers receiving the Spirit of adoption, by whom they cry “Abba! Father!” Romans 8:26 says the Spirit helps believers in weakness and intercedes when they do not know how to pray as they ought. Romans 8:28 speaks of God working for good with those who love him and are called according to his purpose; it is not a promise that believers never face hardship.\n\n7. FAITH AND WORKS — PAUL AND JAMES\nJames 2:14–26 challenges a claim to faith that produces no compassion or action. He gives the example of someone telling a poorly clothed and hungry person to be warm and filled without providing necessities. James says faith without works is dead. He mentions Abraham offering Isaac and Rahab receiving the messengers. Paul emphasises that salvation is not earned by works of the law; James emphasises that living faith should be evident in conduct. They answer different questions. Ephesians 2:8–10 brings the ideas together: saved by grace through faith, not as a result of works, yet created in Christ for good works.\n\n8. THE FRUIT OF THE SPIRIT (GALATIANS 5)\nPaul contrasts works of the flesh with the fruit of the Spirit. Galatians 5:22–23 lists love, joy, peace, patience, kindness, goodness, faithfulness, gentleness and self-control. The list has nine qualities. “Fruit” here describes the character produced by the Spirit, not nine unrelated supernatural offices. Paul warns against using freedom as an excuse for self-indulgence and teaches believers to serve one another through love (Galatians 5:13).\n\n9. CHRISTIAN HOPE AND THE RESURRECTION (1 CORINTHIANS 15)\nPaul reminds the Corinthians that Christ died for sins, was buried and was raised on the third day, and that he appeared to witnesses including Cephas, the Twelve, more than 500 believers at once, James, all the apostles and finally Paul (1 Corinthians 15:3–8). Paul argues that if Christ has not been raised, Christian preaching and faith are empty. He describes Christ as the firstfruits of those who have died and compares Adam's death with life in Christ. The resurrection hope concerns God's victory over death, not a claim that Christians will never experience physical death.\n\n10. CHRISTIAN CONDUCT THAT FOLLOWS GRACE\nRomans 12 calls believers to offer their lives in service, avoid conformity to the world, renew their minds and use gifts humbly. Romans 12:9–21 teaches genuine love, hospitality, peace, patience and overcoming evil with good. Romans 13 discusses respect for governing authorities and the continuing duty to love one's neighbour. Philippians 2:5–11 presents Christ's humility and self-giving as a pattern for believers. Grace produces gratitude and responsible living; it is not an excuse for pride or injustice.\n\nWORKED JAMB-STYLE EXAMPLES\nExample (i): What does justification mean in Romans?\nSolution: It means being declared or counted righteous by God's grace through faith in Christ. Answer: Being declared righteous before God.\nExample (ii): Which Old Testament figure does Paul use to illustrate justification by faith?\nSolution: Romans 4 refers to Abraham's belief in Genesis 15:6. Answer: Abraham.\nExample (iii): Through what were people reconciled to God while still enemies?\nSolution: Romans 5:10 specifically says through the death of God's Son. Answer: The death of Jesus Christ.\nExample (iv): What does baptism into Christ's death signify?\nSolution: Romans 6:3–4 links it with burial of the old life and walking in newness of life. Answer: Dying to sin and living a new life.\nExample (v): What is the wage of sin according to Romans 6:23?\nSolution: The verse contrasts death with God's gift of eternal life. Answer: Death.\nExample (vi): What is James' teaching about faith without works?\nSolution: James 2:17 says faith without works is dead. Answer: Genuine faith should produce action.\nExample (vii): How many qualities are listed as the fruit of the Spirit in Galatians 5?\nSolution: Galatians 5:22–23 lists nine. Answer: Nine.\nExample (viii): What does Paul say if Christ has not been raised?\nSolution: 1 Corinthians 15:14 says preaching and faith are in vain. Answer: Christian preaching and faith are empty.\n\nQUICK UNDERSTANDING CHECK\nDefine justification, grace, redemption and reconciliation in your own words. Explain why Paul refers to Abraham before circumcision. Distinguish Romans 6 from Romans 7–8. Explain how Paul's teaching on grace and James' teaching on works fit together. Recite the nine qualities of the Spirit's fruit.\n\nPRACTICE QUESTIONS\nPractise identifying Paul's exact wording, the letters and chapters, and the difference between similar theological terms.\n\nSYLLABUS DEEP DIVE — ADOPTION AND JOINT HEIRSHIP WITH CHRIST (ROMANS 8:12–17; GALATIANS 3:26–29; 4:1–7)\nPaul teaches that believers who are led by God's Spirit are children of God. Romans 8:15 contrasts the spirit of slavery leading back to fear with the Spirit of adoption, by whom believers cry 'Abba! Father!' The Spirit bears witness with their spirit that they are God's children (Romans 8:16). Romans 8:17 then explains the consequence: if children, they are heirs of God and fellow or joint heirs with Christ, sharing in his glory while also being called to share in suffering with him. 'Joint heirs' describes belonging to God's family and sharing the inheritance promised in Christ; it does not mean believers become equal to God or are guaranteed immediate material wealth.\nGalatians 4:4–7 connects adoption with God sending his Son and the Spirit. Paul says that through God a believer is no longer a slave but a child and therefore an heir. Galatians 3:26–29 stresses belonging to Christ through faith, not ethnic or social status. Distinguish justification (being counted righteous through faith), adoption (being received as God's children), and sanctification (the ongoing life shaped by the Spirit).\nExample (additional): According to Romans 8:17, who are joint heirs with Christ? \nSolution: Those who are God's children through the Spirit are heirs of God and fellow heirs with Christ. Answer: God's children in Christ.\nQUICK UNDERSTANDING CHECK: Define adoption, explain 'Abba', and distinguish heirship from a promise of instant riches.\n"}},{"id":"gifts-love","revised":true,"old":{"title":"Spiritual gifts, service and love","body":"A spiritual gift is an ability or form of service given through God's Spirit for the good of the Christian community. First Corinthians 12 stresses variety within unity: different gifts, the same Spirit; different services, the same Lord; different workings, the same God. Questions may ask for one term in this parallel structure, so keep gifts, services and workings in their proper clauses.\n\nPaul says that no one genuinely confesses Jesus as Lord except by the Holy Spirit. This concerns spiritual allegiance rather than the bare ability to pronounce a sentence. He then lists different gifts and explains that they are given for the common good. Their purpose is not to create competition between supposedly superior and inferior believers.\n\nThe body illustration shows why different members need each other. An eye cannot dismiss a hand as unnecessary; a less prominent member still matters. Paul names apostles first, prophets second and teachers third in one ordering, followed by other ministries. That list must not be turned into permission for contempt towards people whose service attracts less attention.\n\nRomans 12 also compares many members of one body, but explicitly says they do not all have the same function. Their gifts differ according to the grace given to them. A printed question saying all Christians have the same functions has reversed Paul's point. Grace is the particular word completing that Romans sentence, even though Corinthians also speaks of the Spirit's enabling.\n\nFirst Corinthians 13 places love at the centre of Christian relationships. Love is patient and kind, not jealous, boastful or self-seeking. Without love, impressive speech and gifts do not make a person spiritually mature. Paul concludes that faith, hope and love remain, and love is the greatest. A Nigerian school example is a talented student who uses ability to help classmates instead of humiliating those who struggle.\n\nCommon traps:\n• Varieties of service belong with “the same Lord.”\n• Difference of function does not imply difference of human worth.\n• Love is not merely a passing feeling or a reward given only to people who agree with us.\n\nGifts require humility and genuine love. James describes the prayer of a righteous person as powerful in its effects.\n\nFirst Corinthians 12:8–10 ends its list with interpreting tongues. The same Spirit baptises believers into one body. In chapter 14, prophecy builds up the Church; its value concerns intelligible instruction rather than personal display.\n\nFirst Peter calls believers stewards of varied grace: they should use received gifts to serve one another, with hospitality rather than selfish display. Stewardship is responsible use of what has been entrusted.\n\nFirst Corinthians 14 says speaking in a tongue addresses God; prophecy addresses people for edification."},"new":{"title":"Spiritual gifts, service and love","body":"SPIRITUAL GIFTS, CHRISTIAN SERVICE AND LOVE — DETAILED LESSON\n\nINTRODUCTION\nThe early churches included people with different abilities, responsibilities and backgrounds. Paul taught that spiritual gifts come from God and should build up the community rather than promote rivalry. Love is the guiding principle for their use. JAMB questions may test lists of gifts, the body illustration, the order of ministries, the meaning of tongues and the qualities of love.\n\n1. ONE SPIRIT, DIFFERENT GIFTS (1 CORINTHIANS 12:1–11)\nPaul begins by teaching that no one speaking by God's Spirit says “Jesus is accursed,” and no one can truly confess Jesus as Lord except by the Holy Spirit (1 Corinthians 12:3). He then uses a three-part pattern: varieties of gifts but the same Spirit; varieties of service but the same Lord; varieties of activities or workings but the same God (12:4–6). The manifestation of the Spirit is given to each for the common good (12:7).\nPaul lists a word of wisdom, a word of knowledge, faith, gifts of healing, working of miracles, prophecy, distinguishing between spirits, various kinds of tongues and interpretation of tongues (12:8–10). The one Spirit distributes these gifts individually as the Spirit wills (12:11). Not everyone receives the same gift. A gift is for serving others, not proving personal superiority.\n\n2. THE CHURCH AS ONE BODY WITH MANY MEMBERS (1 CORINTHIANS 12:12–27)\nA human body has many parts but remains one body. Paul applies this to the church: believers are baptised by one Spirit into one body, whether Jews or Greeks, slaves or free. A foot cannot say it does not belong because it is not a hand; an ear cannot deny belonging because it is not an eye. Likewise the eye cannot tell the hand it is unnecessary, nor the head say that to the feet. Weaker or less honoured members deserve special care. When one member suffers, all suffer; when one is honoured, all rejoice. The message is mutual dependence and dignity, not that all members must perform identical functions.\n\n3. MINISTRIES AND THEIR ORDER (1 CORINTHIANS 12:28–31)\nPaul says God appointed in the church first apostles, second prophets and third teachers; then miracles, gifts of healing, helping, administration and various kinds of tongues. He asks rhetorical questions: Are all apostles? Are all prophets? Are all teachers? Do all speak in tongues? The implied answer is no. The order in verse 28 is not the same as the list of nine manifestations in verses 8–10. When a question asks which ministry is listed third, the answer is teachers.\n\n4. LOVE IS GREATER THAN DISPLAY (1 CORINTHIANS 13)\nPaul explains that speaking in human or angelic tongues without love is like a noisy gong or clanging cymbal. Prophecy, knowledge, extraordinary faith, generosity and even sacrifice achieve nothing without love (13:1–3). Love is patient and kind; it does not envy, boast or act arrogantly. It is not rude, self-seeking or easily provoked, and it does not rejoice in wrongdoing but rejoices in truth. Love bears, believes, hopes and endures. Love never ends.\nPaul contrasts present partial knowledge with future completeness. He uses the picture of seeing dimly in a mirror and later face to face. The chapter concludes that faith, hope and love remain, and the greatest is love (13:13). This does not mean faith and hope are useless; it means love has supreme importance in Christian life.\n\n5. PROPHECY, TONGUES AND ORDERLY WORSHIP (1 CORINTHIANS 14)\nPaul encourages believers to pursue love and desire gifts that build up the church, especially prophecy. Speaking in an unknown tongue without interpretation may not benefit listeners because they cannot understand. Prophecy speaks to people for their upbuilding, encouragement and consolation (14:3). Paul prefers understandable instruction in a gathering over impressive but unintelligible speech.\nIf tongues are spoken publicly, Paul limits speakers to two or at most three, each in turn, with someone interpreting; without an interpreter the speaker should keep silent in the assembly and speak to themselves and God (14:27–28). Two or three prophets may speak while others weigh what is said (14:29). God is not a God of confusion but of peace (14:33). The goal is orderly worship that strengthens the whole community.\n\n6. OTHER LISTS OF GIFTS AND SERVICES (ROMANS 12; EPHESIANS 4)\nRomans 12:3–8 describes one body with different gifts according to grace: prophecy, service, teaching, exhortation, giving, leadership and acts of mercy. Each should be practised sincerely and diligently. Ephesians 4:11–16 lists apostles, prophets, evangelists, pastors and teachers given to equip believers for ministry and build up the body of Christ. These lists overlap but are not identical. A question asking for a list from Romans must not be answered from 1 Corinthians simply because both mention prophecy.\n\n7. SERVANT LEADERSHIP (MARK 10; JOHN 13; PHILIPPIANS 2)\nWhen James and John sought places of honour, Jesus taught that greatness among his followers comes through service. The Son of Man came not to be served but to serve and give his life as a ransom for many (Mark 10:42–45). In John 13 Jesus washed his disciples' feet, giving an example of humble service. Philippians 2:5–11 calls believers to imitate Christ's humility. A church leader's authority should therefore be exercised for others' good, not personal prestige.\n\n8. CHRISTIAN LOVE IN ACTION (ROMANS 12; 1 JOHN 3–4; JAMES 2)\nRomans 12:9 says love must be genuine. Believers should honour others, help those in need, practise hospitality, bless persecutors and overcome evil with good. 1 John 3:17–18 challenges a person who has resources but refuses help to a brother or sister in need: love should be shown in deed and truth, not words alone. 1 John 4:7–12 teaches that love comes from God and that people should love one another because God first loved them. James 2 warns against giving special treatment to wealthy visitors while humiliating the poor. Christian love is not only a feeling; it involves fairness, care and practical action.\n\n9. GIFTS VERSUS FRUIT OF THE SPIRIT\nGifts are capacities or ministries for service, such as teaching, healing or prophecy (1 Corinthians 12). The fruit of the Spirit refers to qualities of character: love, joy, peace, patience, kindness, goodness, faithfulness, gentleness and self-control (Galatians 5:22–23). A person may display a gift but still need to grow in love and character. Paul makes this point forcefully in 1 Corinthians 13. Do not confuse speaking in tongues with the nine qualities of the fruit.\n\n10. EXAM TRAPS AND DISTINCTIONS\nFirst, second, third ministries in 1 Corinthians 12:28: apostles, prophets, teachers. Gifts, services and workings in 12:4–6 correspond to Spirit, Lord and God. Love is patient and kind; love does not envy or boast. Tongues in public worship should be interpreted to benefit the church. The greatest of faith, hope and love is love. The body metaphor teaches unity without uniformity. The fruit of the Spirit is a character list, not a list of church offices.\n\nWORKED JAMB-STYLE EXAMPLES\nExample (i): Why are spiritual gifts given according to 1 Corinthians 12:7?\nSolution: They are given for the common good. Answer: To benefit the Christian community.\nExample (ii): Which ministry is listed third in 1 Corinthians 12:28?\nSolution: The order is apostles, prophets, teachers. Answer: Teachers.\nExample (iii): What does the eye-and-hand illustration teach?\nSolution: 1 Corinthians 12:21 shows that no member can dismiss another as unnecessary. Answer: Mutual dependence in the church.\nExample (iv): What is the greatest of faith, hope and love?\nSolution: 1 Corinthians 13:13 names love. Answer: Love.\nExample (v): How many people at most should speak in tongues in one gathering according to 1 Corinthians 14:27?\nSolution: Paul says two or at most three, each in turn, with interpretation. Answer: Three.\nExample (vi): What should happen if there is no interpreter for tongues?\nSolution: 1 Corinthians 14:28 says the speaker should remain silent in the assembly. Answer: Remain silent publicly.\nExample (vii): What did Jesus teach about greatness in Mark 10?\nSolution: Greatness comes through serving others. Answer: Be a servant.\nExample (viii): How are gifts different from the fruit of the Spirit?\nSolution: Gifts equip people for service; fruit describes Christlike character. Answer: Gifts concern ministries and abilities, while fruit concerns character.\n\nQUICK UNDERSTANDING CHECK\nList the nine gifts in 1 Corinthians 12:8–10. Explain the three-part pattern in verses 4–6. Retell the body illustration. State five qualities of love in 1 Corinthians 13. Explain why interpretation matters when tongues are spoken in church.\n\nPRACTICE QUESTIONS\nUse the practice button to test biblical lists, quotations, order of ministries and the practical meaning of Christian love."}},{"id":"conduct-leadership","revised":true,"old":{"title":"Christian conduct, impartiality and responsible leadership","body":"Christian conduct means behaviour shaped by the teaching and example of Christ. James forbids showing partiality while professing faith in the Lord Jesus Christ. His example contrasts flattering a rich visitor with humiliating a poor one. In a classroom, favouring only wealthy pupils or treating poorer classmates as less worthy illustrates the same moral failure.\n\nPeter urges sympathy, brotherly love, tenderness and refusal to repay evil with evil. Forbearance means patiently bearing with others rather than retaliating. Paul likewise teaches sober judgement about oneself, concern for others and imitation of his example insofar as he follows Christ. A broad question that gives these as competing options may be defective unless it names a particular passage.\n\nRomans 13 describes governing authority's proper role as restraining wrongdoing and approving good conduct. The ruler bears the sword as an instrument of punishment. This does not mean every command issued by every government is automatically just, or that rulers replace conscience and religious instruction. The question should be read as Paul's account of authority's responsibility.\n\nPeter's household teaching addresses servants under both kind and harsh masters. “Overbearing” or “harsh” translates the difficult treatment meant by the passage. The instruction does not declare abuse good or justify slavery as a modern social system. Read its ancient setting carefully while recognising the wrongness of mistreatment.\n\nChurch elders should tend the flock willingly, eagerly and by example, not under compulsion, for dishonest gain or by domination. Peter promises an unfading crown of glory when the chief Shepherd appears. This particular promise addresses faithful elders; a general question about all suffering believers should not silently erase that setting.\n\nTrap: complete the specified passage, not a related teaching.\n\nJames calls partiality a transgression and identifies love of neighbour as the royal law. His criticism of wealthy oppressors is not praise of all rich people. Paul urges prayer for rulers so that believers may live peaceably, blessing persecutors, and disciplined work rather than idleness. Forgiving the repentant offender prevents crushing sorrow and denies Satan an advantage; the purpose depends on which sentence is quoted. Colossians warns fathers not to provoke children into discouragement. Peter urges aliens and exiles to abstain from fleshly passions that war against the soul.\n\nRomans 13 also names taxes, revenue, respect and honour as obligations. James says mercy triumphs over judgement; partiality contradicts neighbour-love rather than proving discernment.\n\nPaul names conscience as well as fear of punishment.\n\nRomans 13 traces authority to God. Peter permits living as free people but not using freedom as cover for evil. Addressing elders, he calls himself a witness of Christ's sufferings."},"new":{"title":"Christian conduct, impartiality and responsible leadership","body":"1. FOUNDATION: WHAT CHRISTIAN CONDUCT MEANS\nChristian conduct means behaving in ways consistent with the teaching and example of Jesus. It concerns what believers do at home, in school, in the church and in society—not only what they say during worship. The New Testament connects conduct with love, honesty, humility, justice, forgiveness, responsible work and respect for others. JAMB often asks for the precise teaching of James, Peter or Paul, so identify the writer and passage before answering.\n\n2. JAMES: PARTIALITY AND THE ROYAL LAW (JAMES 2:1–13)\nJames imagines two visitors entering an assembly. One wears a gold ring and fine clothing; the other is poor and shabbily dressed. If the wealthy visitor receives a good seat while the poor person is told to stand or sit on the floor, the assembly has become a judge with evil thoughts. James calls believers to love their neighbours as themselves, describing this as the royal law (James 2:8). Favouritism breaks that law. James also warns that someone who keeps much of the law but breaks one point becomes accountable as a lawbreaker (2:10). The practical lesson is to treat classmates, strangers and worshippers with dignity regardless of wealth or status.\n\n3. JAMES: FAITH SHOWN BY ACTIONS (JAMES 2:14–26)\nJames asks what benefit there is if a person claims to have faith but does nothing for someone who lacks clothing and daily food. Saying 'go in peace, be warm and filled' without providing help is inadequate. He concludes that faith without works is dead. He mentions Abraham's willingness to offer Isaac and Rahab's reception of the messengers. The issue is whether claimed faith is alive and visible in action; it is not permission to boast that one can earn God's grace through good deeds.\n\n4. PETER: RESPONSE TO SUFFERING AND PERSECUTION (1 PETER 2–3)\nPeter calls believers sojourners and exiles and urges them to abstain from desires that war against the soul (1 Peter 2:11). He urges honourable conduct so that others may see good deeds. In 1 Peter 2:18–25 he addresses household servants in an ancient social setting, including those under harsh masters, and presents Christ's unjust suffering as an example of not retaliating. This passage must not be used to excuse abuse or justify slavery today. In 1 Peter 3:8–9 he calls for unity of mind, sympathy, brotherly love, tenderness and humility: do not repay evil for evil or insult for insult, but bless. In 3:15 believers should be ready to explain their hope with gentleness and respect.\n\n5. PAUL: HUMILITY, PEACE AND FORGIVENESS (ROMANS 12; COLOSSIANS 3)\nRomans 12:3 tells believers not to think of themselves more highly than they ought. Romans 12:9–21 calls for sincere love, honour, hospitality, patience in suffering, prayer, blessing persecutors and refusing personal revenge. 'If possible, so far as it depends on you, live peaceably with all' (12:18) recognises that peace sometimes depends on another person's response. Paul tells believers to feed hungry enemies and overcome evil with good. Colossians 3:12–14 calls for compassion, kindness, humility, meekness, patience, bearing with one another and forgiving as the Lord has forgiven; above these is love. Colossians 3:21 warns fathers not to provoke children lest they become discouraged.\n\n6. GOVERNMENT AND RESPONSIBLE CITIZENSHIP (ROMANS 13; 1 TIMOTHY 2; ACTS 5)\nRomans 13:1–7 discusses submission to governing authorities, their duty to restrain wrongdoing, the payment of taxes and giving honour where due. Paul describes the ruler as God's servant for good and says the authority does not bear the sword in vain (13:4). First Timothy 2:1–2 calls for prayers and thanksgivings for kings and people in high positions so that believers may lead peaceful and godly lives. However, Acts 5:29 records the apostles' answer when forbidden to preach: 'We must obey God rather than men.' Therefore respect for government is not the same as calling every government order morally right. In an exam, distinguish Paul's description of authority's proper purpose from the apostles' response to an order contrary to their mission.\n\n7. CHURCH ELDERS AND RESPONSIBLE LEADERSHIP (1 PETER 5)\nPeter addresses elders as a fellow elder and witness of Christ's sufferings. He tells them to shepherd God's flock willingly, not under compulsion; eagerly, not for shameful gain; and by example, not by domineering over those entrusted to them (1 Peter 5:1–3). He promises that when the chief Shepherd appears, faithful elders will receive an unfading crown of glory (5:4). Younger believers are urged to be subject to elders, and all are to clothe themselves with humility. 'God opposes the proud but gives grace to the humble' (5:5). The crown in this passage specifically addresses shepherding elders.\n\n8. CHRISTIAN WORK AND DISCIPLINE (2 THESSALONIANS 3)\nPaul warns against disorderly idleness. He reminds the Thessalonians that he worked to avoid being an unnecessary burden and gives the rule that if anyone is unwilling to work, neither should that person eat (2 Thessalonians 3:10). The wording concerns refusal to work, not people who are unable to work because of illness, disability or lack of opportunity. Paul urges those living idly to work quietly and earn their living, while the community should not grow weary in doing good.\n\n9. FORGIVENESS AND RESTORING AN OFFENDER (2 CORINTHIANS 2)\nPaul urges the Corinthians to forgive and comfort a person who has been disciplined, so that the person is not overwhelmed by excessive sorrow (2 Corinthians 2:5–11). They should reaffirm their love. Forgiveness is intended to restore, not to pretend that wrongdoing did not occur. Paul also says they should not be outwitted by Satan. This passage concerns a disciplined offender and should not be confused with the parable of the unforgiving servant in Matthew 18.\n\n10. EXAM DISTINCTIONS AND APPLICATION\nJames 2: partiality toward rich and poor visitors, royal law, faith and works. 1 Peter 3: sympathy, blessing rather than retaliation, explaining hope respectfully. 1 Peter 5: willing and exemplary church leadership, unfading crown. Romans 12: humility, hospitality and overcoming evil with good. Romans 13: authority's responsibility and taxes. 2 Thessalonians 3: responsible work rather than deliberate idleness. Colossians 3: patience, forgiveness and not discouraging children. Always read the exact wording of the question before selecting a generally good Christian quality.\n\nDEEPER GUIDED TEACHING — CONCEPTS, CONTEXT AND MEANING\nTHE FOUNDATION: FAITH MUST CHANGE BEHAVIOUR\nChristian conduct is the practical expression of belief in everyday choices. James challenges people who profess faith but ignore someone lacking food or clothing (James 2:14–17). He does not say that good deeds purchase God's favour; he argues that living faith should be visible through care and obedience. Paul similarly urges believers to present themselves to God and be transformed in mind (Romans 12:1–2). The question for students is not merely 'What rule was given?' but 'What problem was the writer correcting, and what action would show genuine obedience?'\n\nPARTIALITY: THE RICH VISITOR AND THE POOR VISITOR\nJames 2:1–13 imagines two people entering a gathering: one wearing fine clothes and a gold ring, the other in shabby clothes. Giving the wealthy person an honoured seat while telling the poor person to stand or sit on the floor reveals prejudice. James calls this judging with evil thoughts and recalls the royal law, 'You shall love your neighbour as yourself' (2:8). Mercy triumphs over judgement (2:13). The lesson is not that fine clothes are forbidden, but that wealth must not determine human worth or treatment.\n\nFAITH AND WORKS: JAMES AND PAUL IN CONTEXT\nJames 2:14–26 asks whether a claim to faith without deeds can save, using Abraham's offering of Isaac and Rahab's protection of the messengers to show faith expressed in action. Paul argues in Romans 3–4 that people are justified through faith rather than earning acceptance through works of the law. They are addressing different errors: Paul rejects boasting that salvation is earned, while James rejects an empty profession without obedience. Students should not select a simplistic answer that either writer taught that loving action is irrelevant.\n\nROMANS 12: THE SHAPE OF A TRANSFORMED LIFE\nRomans 12:9–21 calls for sincere love, abhorrence of evil, patience in suffering, prayer, hospitality, blessing persecutors, humility and peace where possible. Paul says not to repay evil for evil or avenge oneself, and urges believers to overcome evil with good. This does not require tolerating ongoing violence; it rejects personal vengeance as a way of life. Romans 13 then discusses civil authority, taxes, honour and love as fulfilment of the law. Christians must distinguish respect for lawful order from treating every action of a ruler as morally right.\n\nLEADERSHIP AS SERVICE, NOT DOMINATION\nFirst Peter 5:1–4 tells elders to shepherd God's flock willingly, not under compulsion or for shameful gain, and not to domineer but to be examples. Jesus' teaching in Mark 10:42–45 contrasts rulers who lord authority over others with disciples who become servants; the Son of Man came not to be served but to serve and give his life as a ransom for many. Christian leadership therefore includes responsibility, example, fairness and willingness to sacrifice. A title or office alone does not establish good leadership.\n\nSUFFERING FOR RIGHTEOUSNESS VERSUS CONSEQUENCES OF WRONGDOING\nFirst Peter 3:13–17 encourages believers to answer hostile questions about their hope with gentleness and respect, keeping a clear conscience. First Peter 4:15–16 distinguishes suffering as a murderer, thief or wrongdoer from suffering as a Christian. Therefore 'suffering proves holiness' is false. The writer commends faithful endurance when people are mistreated for doing good, not a refusal to take responsibility for harmful actions.\n\nFORGIVENESS AND RESPONSIBLE SPEECH\nColossians 3:12–17 calls believers to compassion, kindness, humility, patience and forgiveness, with love binding virtues together. Ephesians 4:25–32 calls for truthfulness, anger without sin, honest work, speech that builds others up and forgiveness as God forgave in Christ. Forgiveness does not mean hiding injustice or denying a harmed person's need for protection. It means refusing vindictive retaliation while seeking truthful, responsible ways forward.\n\nHONEST WORK AND PRACTICAL HELP\nSecond Thessalonians 3:6–15 addresses deliberate idleness and interference in others' affairs. Paul's instruction that one unwilling to work should not eat must be read with the word 'unwilling', not applied to people who are unable to work. Ephesians 4:28 goes beyond earning one's living: honest labour should create the capacity to share with someone in need. Together the passages connect responsibility with generosity.\n\nWORKED JAMB-STYLE INTERPRETATION QUESTIONS\nExample (i): A church gives the best seat to a wealthy visitor and humiliates a poor visitor. Which teaching is most directly violated?\nA. James' warning against partiality\nB. Paul's instructions about tongues\nC. The decree of Cyrus\nD. Elijah's contest at Carmel\nSolution: James 2:1–9 explicitly contrasts preferential treatment of rich and poor worshippers. The other passages address different matters.\nCorrect answer: A.\n\nExample (ii): Which interpretation best relates James 2 and Romans 3?\nA. Both say good deeds buy salvation\nB. Paul rejects earned justification, while James rejects a claim of faith with no action\nC. James forbids helping the poor\nD. Paul teaches that dishonesty is acceptable\nSolution: Paul challenges boasting in works of law; James challenges inactive profession. The other answers distort both arguments.\nCorrect answer: B.\n\nExample (iii): According to 1 Peter 5, church elders should lead primarily by\nA. domineering over others\nB. pursuing dishonest gain\nC. willing service and good example\nD. demanding public praise\nSolution: First Peter 5:2–3 contrasts willing shepherding and example with domination and shameful gain.\nCorrect answer: C.\n\nExample (iv): Why does 1 Peter distinguish suffering for wrongdoing from suffering as a Christian?\nA. All punishment is persecution\nB. The moral cause of suffering matters\nC. Wrongdoing is harmless if confessed\nD. Christians cannot face consequences\nSolution: First Peter 4:15–16 explicitly separates suffering due to misconduct from suffering for Christian identity.\nCorrect answer: B.\n\nExample (v): What is the purpose of honest labour in Ephesians 4:28 beyond avoiding theft?\nA. To compete for the highest status\nB. To have something to share with those in need\nC. To avoid all charitable work\nD. To gain political power\nSolution: The verse explicitly joins honest work with sharing. The other answers are not its purpose.\nCorrect answer: B.\n\nQUICK UNDERSTANDING CHECK — SPECIFIC TO THIS LESSON\n1. Explain why favouring a wealthy visitor violates the royal law in James 2.\n2. Compare the problems James and Paul are addressing when discussing faith and works.\n3. Identify three leadership behaviours forbidden or required in 1 Peter 5.\n4. Why is unwillingness to work different from inability to work?\n\nPRACTICE JAMB PAST QUESTIONS\nUse the lesson's linked past questions, identify the exact biblical passage, and explain why the strongest answer is better than similar alternatives."}},{"id":"second-coming","revised":true,"old":{"title":"The coming of Christ, tested faith and watchfulness","body":"The Second Coming is the Christian expectation of Christ's return and the final fulfilment of God's purposes. First and Second Thessalonians address anxiety, hope and misleading claims connected with that expectation. Paul tells believers not to accept assertions that the day of the Lord has already arrived simply because a message claims spiritual authority.\n\nSecond Thessalonians links deception with refusal to love the truth and delight in unrighteousness. The passage about a strong delusion belongs to that judgement. It is not an instruction to speculate about dates, label every disagreement as deception or abandon ordinary responsibilities. The discussion of the man of lawlessness must be read within Paul's warning not to be shaken by false announcements.\n\nFirst Thessalonians calls believers children of light and urges them to keep awake and be sober. This means moral alertness and self-control, not refusing necessary sleep. The armour image names a breastplate of faith and love and a helmet of the hope of salvation. Do not swap these with phrases from another letter and assume that every similar armour expression is interchangeable.\n\nPeter connects expectation with holy and godly living. Since present things will pass away, believers should live responsibly while awaiting God's promised future. The appropriate response is not fear-driven inactivity. A student preparing honestly, keeping promises and treating others justly illustrates readiness through conduct rather than prediction.\n\nTrials also belong to the life of faith. First Peter compares tested faith with gold tested by fire; actual trials test the believer, while fire is the comparison. James says testing produces steadfastness, and steadfastness should reach its full effect so that believers become mature and complete. “Perfection” here concerns developed character, not a claim that difficulty is pleasant or that a sufferer must remain silent about abuse.\n\nCommon traps:\n• Watchfulness is alert, self-controlled living, not literal sleeplessness.\n• The breastplate is faith and love; the helmet is hope of salvation.\n• In James, trials produce steadfastness; maturity and completeness are its intended outcome.\n\nPeter's promised new heavens and new earth are described as the dwelling of righteousness. This phrase supplies the specific completion even when other options describe generally desirable conditions. First Peter 4:6 speaks of the gospel being preached even to the dead so that they might live in the spirit as God does; traditions differ about the passage's interpretation. Do not turn a short examination answer into a confident claim settling every theological question about the dead.\n\nFirst Thessalonians 4 describes the Lord's descent with a command, an archangel's voice and God's trumpet: the dead in Christ rise first, then living believers are caught up together with them.\n\nFirst Thessalonians 5 compares its arrival with a thief at night."},"new":{"title":"The coming of Christ, tested faith and watchfulness","body":"1. INTRODUCTION: CHRISTIAN HOPE AND WATCHFULNESS\nThe Second Coming refers to the expected return of Jesus Christ and the fulfilment of God's purposes. The New Testament links this hope with resurrection, judgement, comfort, holiness and responsible living. It does not encourage students to guess dates or abandon their daily responsibilities. The expression 'day of the Lord' appears in several passages; the details must be matched to the writer and chapter.\n\n2. JESUS' TEACHING ABOUT HIS RETURN (MATTHEW 24–25)\nJesus warns that wars, disasters, persecution and false messiahs should not lead disciples to careless predictions. Matthew 24:36 says no one knows the day or hour, but the Father. Jesus compares the coming of the Son of Man to the days of Noah, when people continued ordinary activities until the flood came. He tells the disciples to keep watch and be ready. In Matthew 25:1–13 five wise virgins carry extra oil while five foolish ones do not; the bridegroom is delayed and arrives at midnight. The wise enter the wedding feast, and the door is shut. Their preparedness, rather than staying physically awake all night, is the lesson. Matthew 25:14–30 teaches faithful stewardship through the talents, while 25:31–46 describes judgement pictured as the separation of sheep and goats, with attention to how people treated the hungry, thirsty, stranger, naked, sick and imprisoned.\n\n3. PAUL: THE DEAD IN CHRIST AND THE LIVING (1 THESSALONIANS 4:13–18)\nSome Thessalonian Christians were worried about believers who had died before Christ's return. Paul tells them not to grieve as people without hope. He says Jesus died and rose, and God will bring with Jesus those who have died in him. The Lord will descend from heaven with a cry of command, the voice of an archangel and the trumpet of God. The dead in Christ will rise first. Then living believers will be caught up together with them to meet the Lord, and they will be with him always. Paul ends by telling them to encourage one another with these words. 'Rise first' refers to the order described in this passage; it is not a claim that living Christians are forgotten.\n\n4. CHILDREN OF LIGHT AND THE ARMOUR OF WATCHFULNESS (1 THESSALONIANS 5)\nPaul says the day of the Lord will come like a thief in the night, unexpectedly. Believers are children of light and children of day; they should keep awake and be sober. The imagery means alert, disciplined conduct rather than literal refusal to sleep. In 1 Thessalonians 5:8 the breastplate is faith and love, while the helmet is the hope of salvation. Do not substitute the different armour descriptions in Ephesians 6. Paul continues by encouraging mutual upbuilding, respect for leaders, prayer without ceasing, thanksgiving, testing what is said and holding fast to what is good.\n\n5. FALSE REPORTS AND THE MAN OF LAWLESSNESS (2 THESSALONIANS 2)\nPaul tells believers not to be quickly shaken or alarmed by a spirit, spoken message or letter claiming that the day of the Lord has already arrived. He speaks of rebellion and the revelation of the man of lawlessness, who opposes and exalts himself against what is called divine and claims a place of worship. Paul describes deceptive signs and a strong delusion connected with refusal to love the truth and delight in unrighteousness (2 Thessalonians 2:9–12). The chapter calls for steadfastness, not for identifying a date or naming contemporary people without textual grounds.\n\n6. PETER: NEW HEAVENS AND NEW EARTH (2 PETER 3)\nSecond Peter 3 answers mockers who ask why the promised coming has not happened. It recalls God's earlier judgement and teaches that the Lord is not slow as some understand slowness but is patient, not wishing people to perish but to come to repentance (3:9). Verse 8 says that with the Lord one day is as a thousand years and a thousand years as one day; this is a comparison about God's relation to time, not a timetable for calculating the return. The day of the Lord comes like a thief. The present heavens and earth will pass through judgement, but believers await new heavens and a new earth in which righteousness dwells (3:13). Peter therefore urges holy and godly lives.\n\n7. JAMES: TRIALS, STEADFASTNESS AND MATURITY (JAMES 1)\nJames says believers should count it joy when they meet trials of various kinds because the testing of faith produces steadfastness or perseverance. Steadfastness should have its full effect so that believers may be mature and complete, lacking nothing (James 1:2–4). If someone lacks wisdom, they should ask God in faith (1:5–6). James 1:12 promises the crown of life to the person who remains steadfast under trial and loves God. James also distinguishes trials from temptation to evil: God does not tempt anyone with evil; temptation grows from a person's own desires (1:13–15). These ideas must not be collapsed into the false claim that God personally entices people to sin.\n\n8. PETER: TESTED FAITH AND CHRISTIAN SUFFERING (1 PETER 1; 4)\nFirst Peter 1:6–7 compares the genuineness of faith tested by various trials with gold tested by fire, even though faith is more precious than perishable gold. The point is the value and testing of faith, not that suffering is pleasant. First Peter 4:12–16 warns believers not to be surprised by fiery trials and distinguishes suffering as a Christian from suffering because of wrongdoing. Believers should not be ashamed of bearing Christ's name. Peter's teaching does not mean that abuse must be ignored or that injustice is good.\n\n9. PAUL: RESURRECTION AND FINAL VICTORY (1 CORINTHIANS 15)\nPaul argues that Christ's resurrection is central to Christian faith. If Christ has not been raised, preaching and faith are in vain (1 Corinthians 15:14). Christ is called the firstfruits of those who have died (15:20). As in Adam all die, so in Christ shall all be made alive (15:22), with Paul explaining the order of resurrection and the final defeat of death. He says the perishable will put on imperishability and the mortal immortality. The chapter ends by urging believers to be steadfast and active in the Lord's work because their labour is not in vain (15:58).\n\n10. EXAM DISTINCTIONS\nMatthew 24: no one knows the day or hour; watchfulness. Matthew 25: ten virgins, talents, sheep and goats. 1 Thessalonians 4: comfort about the dead in Christ; trumpet; resurrection. 1 Thessalonians 5: children of light, breastplate of faith and love, helmet of salvation's hope. 2 Thessalonians 2: false claims that the day has arrived and deception. 2 Peter 3: God's patience, new heavens and new earth where righteousness dwells. James 1: trials produce steadfastness; temptation to evil does not come from God. 1 Peter 1: faith compared with gold tested by fire.\n\nDEEPER GUIDED TEACHING — CONCEPTS, CONTEXT AND MEANING\nWHAT CHRISTIANS MEAN BY THE SECOND COMING\nThe Second Coming refers to the expected future return of Jesus Christ and God's final fulfilment of justice and renewal. The New Testament uses terms such as coming, appearing and day of the Lord. These passages are not all describing the same detail in the same literary form: Matthew 24–25 contains Jesus' warnings and parables; 1 Thessalonians 4 comforts believers grieving the dead; 2 Thessalonians 2 corrects claims that the day has already arrived; 2 Peter 3 answers scoffers and calls for holy living. Good exam answers first identify the author's purpose.\n\nWATCHFULNESS DOES NOT MEAN PREDICTING A DATE\nIn Matthew 24:36 Jesus says no one knows the day or hour, not even the angels of heaven, but the Father alone in Matthew's wording. He compares the unexpectedness to the days of Noah and to a thief coming when a householder is unprepared. The faithful and unfaithful servants show that readiness is expressed in responsible behaviour during the master's absence. A question claiming Jesus supplied a precise date contradicts the teaching. Watchfulness means faithful living, not abandoning work or repeatedly guessing dates.\n\nTEN VIRGINS: WHY THE OIL MATTERS\nMatthew 25:1–13 tells of ten virgins waiting for a bridegroom. Five are wise and take extra oil; five are foolish and fail to prepare. When the bridegroom is delayed, all become drowsy, but only the prepared enter the wedding feast. The door is shut before the others return. The passage's explicit conclusion is 'Keep awake therefore, for you know neither the day nor the hour.' The oil functions within a story about readiness; students should not assert that Jesus explicitly defines every drop of oil as a particular spiritual gift.\n\nTALENTS: FAITHFUL STEWARDSHIP DURING THE WAIT\nIn Matthew 25:14–30 a master entrusts five, two and one talent to servants according to ability. The first two put the resources to work and double them; the third hides the money and returns it unused. The master praises the first two in similar terms because both are faithful, despite receiving different starting amounts. A talent in this parable is a large monetary unit, not simply an individual's natural skill. Its application concerns responsibility for what has been entrusted while awaiting the master's return.\n\nSHEEP AND GOATS: WHAT ACTIONS REVEAL\nMatthew 25:31–46 pictures the Son of Man separating people like a shepherd separating sheep from goats. Feeding the hungry, welcoming strangers, clothing the naked, caring for the sick and visiting prisoners are central examples. Those who served were surprised that such care counted as service to the king; neglect also has significance. The passage links readiness for judgement with practical mercy, not mere verbal claims. It should be distinguished from the ten virgins and talents, which use different images within the same chapter.\n\nPAUL'S COMFORT TO THOSE WHO GRIEVE (1 THESSALONIANS 4)\nSome Thessalonian Christians feared that believers who had died would miss Christ's return. Paul says the dead in Christ will rise first, then those alive and remaining will be caught up together with them to meet the Lord. He closes by telling believers to encourage one another with these words (4:18). The purpose is comfort and hope, not a timetable that lets readers calculate the date. In 1 Thessalonians 5 Paul adds that the day comes like a thief in the night and calls believers to sobriety and watchfulness.\n\nFALSE ALARMS AND RESPONSIBLE WORK (2 THESSALONIANS 2–3)\nSecond Thessalonians 2 tells believers not to be quickly shaken by claims that the day of the Lord has already come. Paul discusses rebellion and the man of lawlessness, warning against deception. Chapter 3 addresses some people living idly and interfering with others, calling them to work quietly. These two issues should not be merged into an assertion that everyone who expects Christ's return must stop working. On the contrary, Christian hope should support faithful ordinary responsibility.\n\nPETER ON DELAY AND HOLY CONDUCT (2 PETER 3)\nScoffers ask why the promised coming has not happened. Peter explains that the Lord's timescale differs from human expectations and that God's patience allows room for repentance. The day will come unexpectedly; the chapter speaks of judgement and new heavens and a new earth in which righteousness dwells. Peter asks what sort of people believers ought to be in holiness and godliness. The lesson is ethical readiness, not a numerical prediction of the end.\n\nTRIALS AND FUTURE HOPE\nJames 1 says testing of faith can produce steadfastness, while 1 Peter 1 compares tested faith with gold refined by fire. These texts connect present endurance with future hope but do not claim every hardship is good or that God tempts people into sin. James 1:13 distinguishes temptation toward evil from the testing of faith. Students should avoid confusing 'tested faith' with a licence to cause suffering.\n\nWORKED JAMB-STYLE INTERPRETATION QUESTIONS\nExample (i): What is the main purpose of 1 Thessalonians 4:13–18?\nA. To calculate the exact day of Christ's return\nB. To comfort believers about those who have died in Christ\nC. To order everyone to stop working\nD. To explain the building of the temple\nSolution: Paul addresses grief over deceased believers and ends by saying to encourage one another. The other choices do not fit the passage.\nCorrect answer: B.\n\nExample (ii): What did the wise virgins do that the foolish virgins failed to do?\nA. They arrived after the door shut\nB. They took sufficient oil for the delay\nC. They refused to sleep at all\nD. They bought the bridegroom's house\nSolution: Matthew 25 contrasts preparation with lack of oil. All became drowsy; the issue is readiness, not never sleeping.\nCorrect answer: B.\n\nExample (iii): In the parable of the talents, why are the first two servants praised?\nA. They received equal amounts\nB. They used entrusted resources faithfully\nC. They hid the money safely\nD. They predicted the master's return\nSolution: Matthew 25:14–30 commends faithful stewardship, despite differing initial amounts.\nCorrect answer: B.\n\nExample (iv): What false claim does 2 Thessalonians 2 particularly address?\nA. The day of the Lord had already arrived\nB. The temple had never existed\nC. Paul was born in Bethlehem\nD. The resurrection of Jesus occurred in Egypt\nSolution: Paul cautions believers against reports that the day has already come. The other claims are unrelated.\nCorrect answer: A.\n\nExample (v): How does 2 Peter 3 explain the apparent delay of the promised coming?\nA. God has abandoned the promise\nB. God's patience leaves room for repentance\nC. The apostles knew the exact date\nD. Only wealthy believers will be judged\nSolution: Second Peter 3:9 stresses divine patience and repentance. The other options contradict the chapter.\nCorrect answer: B.\n\nQUICK UNDERSTANDING CHECK — SPECIFIC TO THIS LESSON\n1. Explain why watchfulness cannot mean predicting a date.\n2. Compare the lessons of the ten virgins, talents, and sheep and goats.\n3. What concern prompted Paul's teaching in 1 Thessalonians 4?\n4. How do 2 Thessalonians 3 and 2 Peter 3 connect future hope to present conduct?\n\nPRACTICE JAMB PAST QUESTIONS\nUse the lesson's linked past questions, identify the exact biblical passage, and explain why the strongest answer is better than similar alternatives."}},{"id":"wisdom-worship","revised":true,"old":{"title":"Wisdom, worship, suffering and Christian belief","body":"Biblical wisdom concerns living rightly before God, not merely storing information. Proverbs often personifies wisdom as a woman who guards those who love her. A feminine pronoun in that setting does not necessarily refer to a human wife. In the Gospels, the wise builder hears Jesus' words and acts on them; knowing a lesson without practising it misses the comparison's point.\n\nPsalms contains prayers, praise, thanksgiving, lament and other forms. David has the largest named collection of psalms attributed to him, but this is not the same as saying he wrote a numerical majority of all 150. Lament expresses distress before God and often joins complaint with trust and petition. Many laments move towards praise, but Psalm 88 warns against making that ending an exceptionless rule.\n\nJob explores undeserved suffering and the limits of easy explanations. His friends' assumption that suffering always proves a person's guilt is challenged. The book should not be reduced to a formula guaranteeing immediate wealth to every righteous person. In classroom discussion, avoid blaming a sick or grieving person merely because a simple explanation feels convenient.\n\nRevelation identifies its writer as John on Patmos. Traditional Christian identification with the apostle should be distinguished from the narrower fact supplied in Revelation 1:9 itself. Similarly, Christian teaching about the Incarnation explains the Son taking human nature; it does not say he ceased to be divine or was only pretending to be human.\n\nSome older practice questions also mention later church history. The Council of Nicaea in AD 325 addressed the controversy associated with Arius and affirmed the Son's full divinity. This is a historical council, not an event narrated in the New Testament. Keep later creeds distinct from the Jerusalem Council in Acts 15, whose dispute concerned Gentile circumcision and the Mosaic law.\n\nCommon traps:\n• Wisdom requires right action, not intelligence alone.\n• A traditional attribution should not be presented as a quotation that a biblical book does not make.\n• Claims using “all,” “first,” “majority” or “always” need particular care.\n\nProverbs 4 links attention to a father's instruction with gaining insight. Wisdom includes a willingness to receive correction, not the assumption that age, wealth or cleverness makes further learning unnecessary.\n\nPsalm 130 connects God's forgiveness with reverent fear. Fear here means reverence and accountable trust rather than terror that makes repentance pointless.\n\nSolomon acknowledges his inadequacy to govern and asks God for discernment. His wisdom is a divine gift used in judgement, not a result of employing seers. Honest dependence differs from refusing responsibility."},"new":{"title":"Wisdom, worship, suffering and Christian belief","body":"1. INTRODUCTION: WISDOM, WORSHIP AND FAITH UNDER PRESSURE\nWisdom in the Bible is the ability to live rightly before God, not merely to pass examinations or know many facts. Worship includes praise, lament, thanksgiving and obedience. The wisdom books also ask difficult questions about suffering and injustice. This lesson explains Proverbs, Psalms, Job, selected teachings of Jesus and Christian beliefs that often appear in CRS examinations.\n\n2. PROVERBS: THE FEAR OF THE LORD AND WISE LIVING\nProverbs 1:7 teaches that the fear of the LORD is the beginning of knowledge, while fools despise wisdom and instruction. 'Fear' in this setting means reverence, recognition of God's authority and willingness to obey. Proverbs 3:5–6 urges trust in the LORD rather than relying only on one's own understanding. Proverbs 4:5–9 calls on the learner to get wisdom and understanding, treasure them and not abandon them. Proverbs 4:23 urges guarding the heart because life flows from it. Wisdom is often personified as a woman calling people to choose life-giving instruction (Proverbs 8–9); the feminine pronouns in these passages do not automatically refer to a human wife.\n\n3. WISE AND FOOLISH BUILDERS (MATTHEW 7:24–27; LUKE 6:46–49)\nJesus compares someone who hears and does his words to a wise builder who constructs a house on rock. Rain falls, floods come and winds beat against the house, but it stands because of its foundation. The foolish builder hears but does not act; his house is built on sand and collapses when the storm comes. Luke describes digging deep and laying a foundation on rock. The key difference is obedience to Jesus' teaching, not that wise people never face storms.\n\n4. PSALMS: PRAISE, THANKSGIVING AND LAMENT\nThe book of Psalms contains 150 psalms in the received Hebrew Bible, arranged in five books. Many headings associate psalms with David, but it is inaccurate to claim that the text explicitly attributes a numerical majority of all 150 to him. Other named figures include Asaph, the sons of Korah, Solomon, Moses and others. Praise psalms celebrate God's character and deeds. Thanksgiving psalms recall help received. Laments express grief, danger, illness or injustice and appeal for God's help. Royal psalms address kingship; wisdom psalms reflect on righteous living. A lament may move from complaint to trust, but not every lament ends in praise: Psalm 88 closes in darkness.\n\n5. PSALM 23: THE LORD AS SHEPHERD\nPsalm 23, attributed to David in its heading, begins 'The LORD is my shepherd.' The shepherd provides green pastures and still waters, restores the soul and guides in right paths. Even in the valley of the shadow of death, the speaker fears no evil because God is present. The rod and staff comfort; a table is prepared in the presence of enemies; the cup overflows. Goodness and mercy will follow, and the psalmist hopes to dwell in the LORD's house. The imagery expresses guidance, provision and protection, not a promise that faithful people never suffer.\n\n6. PSALM 51: REPENTANCE AND A CLEAN HEART\nPsalm 51 is associated by its heading with David after Nathan confronted him concerning Bathsheba. The psalm asks for mercy, cleansing from sin and a clean heart. 'Create in me a clean heart, O God' (51:10) expresses the desire for inward renewal, not just outward appearance. Verse 17 calls a broken and contrite heart a sacrifice acceptable to God. This connects with prophetic criticism of ritual performed without genuine repentance.\n\n7. PSALM 130: FORGIVENESS AND REVERENCE\nPsalm 130 begins with a cry from the depths. The writer asks God to hear and recognises that if God kept a strict record of wrongdoing, no one could stand. Yet with God there is forgiveness, 'that you may be feared' (130:4). Here fear means reverent response to mercy, not terror that forgiveness is impossible. The psalmist waits for the LORD more than watchmen wait for morning and calls Israel to hope in God's steadfast love and redemption.\n\n8. JOB: WHY THE RIGHTEOUS MAY SUFFER (JOB 1–2)\nJob is introduced as blameless, upright, fearing God and turning away from evil. He had seven sons and three daughters, and his possessions included 7,000 sheep, 3,000 camels, 500 yoke of oxen and 500 female donkeys (Job 1:2–3). A series of disasters took his animals, servants and children. Job responded with grief yet did not curse God. Later he was afflicted with painful sores from the sole of his foot to the crown of his head. His wife urged him to curse God and die; Job rejected that response. His friends Eliphaz, Bildad and Zophar came to comfort him and initially sat in silence for seven days and seven nights (Job 2:11–13). Their later speeches often assume that suffering must prove personal sin, an explanation the book challenges.\n\n9. JOB'S DEBATE AND GOD'S RESPONSE (JOB 3–42)\nJob lamented his birth and asked why he suffered. His friends insisted on explanations that linked his suffering to wrongdoing. Elihu also spoke later in the book. God answered Job out of the whirlwind, asking questions about creation and the limits of human knowledge (Job 38–41). Job responded with humility. In Job 42 God rebuked Eliphaz and his two friends for not speaking rightly about him as Job had, and Job prayed for them. Job's fortunes were restored; he received twice his former possessions and later had seven sons and three daughters. The book does not teach that every sufferer has secretly committed a particular sin, or that all righteous people receive immediate material wealth.\n\n10. REVELATION, JOHN AND CHRISTIAN HOPE\nRevelation 1:9 identifies its writer as John, who was on the island of Patmos because of God's word and testimony to Jesus. Christian tradition often identifies him with John the apostle, but the verse itself calls him John. Revelation uses symbolic visions to encourage faithful witness amid hardship. Revelation 21 describes a new heaven and new earth, the new Jerusalem and God's dwelling with people. It promises that death, mourning, crying and pain will pass away. These visions should not be treated as ordinary descriptions of present-day geography.\n\n11. THE INCARNATION AND HISTORICAL COUNCILS\nJohn 1:1–14 teaches that the Word was with God, was God and became flesh. Incarnation means that the eternal Son truly took human nature; it does not mean he ceased to be divine or only appeared to be human. Philippians 2:5–11 presents Christ's humility, taking the form of a servant and becoming obedient to death, followed by exaltation. Later Christian councils addressed controversies about Jesus' identity. The Council of Nicaea in AD 325 opposed the teaching associated with Arius and affirmed the Son's full divinity. It is a later historical council, not the Jerusalem Council of Acts 15, which addressed Gentile circumcision.\n\n12. EXAM DISTINCTIONS\nProverbs 1:7: fear of the LORD and knowledge. Matthew 7: hearing AND doing Jesus' words is the rock foundation. Psalm 23: shepherd imagery. Psalm 51: repentance and clean heart. Psalm 130: forgiveness leads to reverence. Job: suffering does not always prove guilt; the friends' easy explanation is challenged. Revelation 1:9: John on Patmos. John 1:14: the Word became flesh. Nicaea AD 325: Christological controversy; Jerusalem Council Acts 15: Gentile circumcision.\n\nDEEPER GUIDED TEACHING — CONCEPTS, CONTEXT AND MEANING\nWISDOM IS MORE THAN KNOWING FACTS\nBiblical wisdom involves reverence for God, sound judgement and morally responsible action. Proverbs 1:7 states that the fear of the Lord is the beginning of knowledge, while Proverbs 9:10 links the fear of the Lord with wisdom. 'Fear' here conveys reverence and serious recognition of God's authority, not mere panic. Proverbs often states general patterns of wise living; they should not all be read as unconditional guarantees that every righteous person will become wealthy or avoid suffering. Job directly challenges that simplistic assumption.\n\nPROVERBS: SPEECH, DILIGENCE AND JUSTICE\nProverbs 15:1 teaches that a gentle answer turns away wrath while a harsh word stirs anger. Proverbs 6:6–11 directs the lazy person to observe the ant's diligence. Proverbs 11:1 condemns dishonest scales; honest weights delight the Lord. These sayings connect wisdom with practical conduct: speech can calm or inflame conflict, work requires responsibility, and trade must be fair. A student should identify the specific behaviour a proverb addresses rather than answer vaguely that all proverbs are about prayer.\n\nPSALMS: LAMENT, TRUST AND REPENTANCE\nPsalm 23 presents the Lord as shepherd who guides, provides and accompanies the worshipper through danger. Psalm 51 is a prayer of confession and appeal for cleansing, traditionally linked by its heading to David after Nathan confronted him. Psalm 130 begins from the depths, asks God to hear and emphasises forgiveness and waiting for the Lord. These are different forms of worship: trust, penitence and lament with hope. The Psalms do not require believers to pretend grief or guilt does not exist.\n\nJOB'S STORY AND THE PROBLEM OF SUFFERING\nJob is introduced as blameless and upright, fearing God and turning from evil (Job 1:1). He loses possessions, children and health, yet his friends repeatedly assume severe suffering must prove serious personal sin. The book challenges that automatic conclusion. Job wrestles with God and asks difficult questions; God later speaks from the whirlwind, directing attention to the limits of human knowledge. In Job 42:7 God says Job's friends have not spoken rightly about him as Job has. The lesson is not that righteous people never suffer or that all suffering is punishment.\n\nJOB'S FRIENDS AND ELIHU: DO NOT CONFUSE THE SPEAKERS\nThe three principal friends are Eliphaz the Temanite, Bildad the Shuhite and Zophar the Naamathite (Job 2:11). Elihu son of Barachel the Buzite speaks later in chapters 32–37. Job's wife tells him to curse God and die (Job 2:9); Job refuses that advice. When answering a question about who said a particular statement, identify the speaker and chapter. The friends' confident explanations should not automatically be treated as the book's final teaching simply because they appear in Scripture.\n\nREVELATION: VISION, SYMBOL AND HOPE\nRevelation identifies its writer as John, who says he was on the island of Patmos because of God's word and the testimony of Jesus (Revelation 1:9). The book addresses seven churches in Asia: Ephesus, Smyrna, Pergamum, Thyatira, Sardis, Philadelphia and Laodicea (Revelation 1–3). It uses visions and symbols to proclaim God's sovereignty, call believers to endurance and depict final judgement and renewed creation. The new heaven and new earth appear in Revelation 21, where God dwells with his people and wipes away tears. Symbols should be interpreted in context, not assigned arbitrary modern identities.\n\nTHE NEW JERUSALEM AND THE END OF SUFFERING\nRevelation 21 describes the holy city, new Jerusalem, coming down from God, prepared like a bride. The loud voice announces God's dwelling with humanity. Death, mourning, crying and pain will be no more. Revelation 22 depicts the river of the water of life and the tree of life, whose leaves are for the healing of the nations. The vision presents hope and restored fellowship with God, not a claim that present suffering is imaginary.\n\nINCARNATION, TRINITY AND EARLY CHURCH COUNCILS\nIncarnation refers to the Christian teaching that the eternal Word became flesh in Jesus Christ (John 1:14). The Trinity is the Christian doctrine of one God understood as Father, Son and Holy Spirit; it is a later doctrinal formulation drawing on biblical teaching, not a phrase explicitly defined in one verse. The Council of Nicaea in AD 325 addressed disputes about the Son's relation to the Father. The Jerusalem Council in Acts 15 concerned whether Gentile converts must be circumcised and keep Mosaic law. They are separated by centuries and deal with different questions. Distinguish doctrinal history from the biblical narrative.\n\nPUTTING WISDOM AND WORSHIP TOGETHER\nProverbs teaches wise choices in everyday life; Psalms supplies language for praise, grief and repentance; Job warns against judging sufferers without knowledge; Revelation offers hope under pressure. Together they show that faithful worship is not merely public ceremony. It includes truthful speech, justice, humility, perseverance and hope. A JAMB question may test the distinctive genre and message of each book.\n\nWORKED JAMB-STYLE INTERPRETATION QUESTIONS\nExample (i): What error do Job's friends repeatedly make?\nA. They refuse to speak to Job\nB. They assume his suffering proves personal wrongdoing\nC. They encourage him to build a temple\nD. They claim Job is a king\nSolution: The friends repeatedly link suffering with hidden sin, while the book introduces Job as upright and challenges their conclusions.\nCorrect answer: B.\n\nExample (ii): Which Psalm is especially associated with David's confession after Nathan's rebuke?\nA. Psalm 23\nB. Psalm 51\nC. Psalm 119\nD. Psalm 150\nSolution: The heading of Psalm 51 connects it with Nathan's confrontation after David's sin with Bathsheba.\nCorrect answer: B.\n\nExample (iii): Where does Revelation say John was when he received his visions?\nA. Mount Carmel\nB. Patmos\nC. Bethlehem\nD. Shiloh\nSolution: Revelation 1:9 identifies Patmos. The other locations belong to unrelated biblical events.\nCorrect answer: B.\n\nExample (iv): What does the Council of Nicaea chiefly differ from the Jerusalem Council about?\nA. Nicaea addressed Christological doctrine, while Jerusalem addressed Gentile inclusion and circumcision\nB. Both chose Saul as king\nC. Jerusalem occurred in AD 325\nD. Nicaea ordered the rebuilding of Jerusalem's walls\nSolution: Nicaea in 325 addressed the Son's relation to the Father; Acts 15 addresses requirements for Gentile believers.\nCorrect answer: A.\n\nExample (v): Which statement best reflects Proverbs and Job read together?\nA. Wise conduct guarantees no suffering\nB. Suffering always proves sin\nC. Wisdom guides conduct, but suffering cannot always be explained as personal punishment\nD. Prayer is useless\nSolution: Proverbs offers patterns of wise living, while Job challenges simplistic retribution. The other options ignore one or both books.\nCorrect answer: C.\n\nQUICK UNDERSTANDING CHECK — SPECIFIC TO THIS LESSON\n1. Explain why Proverbs' general wisdom statements are not automatic promises.\n2. Contrast Psalm 23, Psalm 51 and Psalm 130.\n3. Identify Job's three friends and explain why their argument is challenged.\n4. Distinguish the Jerusalem Council from the Council of Nicaea.\n\nPRACTICE JAMB PAST QUESTIONS\nUse the lesson's linked past questions, identify the exact biblical passage, and explain why the strongest answer is better than similar alternatives."}},{"id":"joshua-judges","revised":true,"old":{"title":"Joshua, covenant faithfulness and the judges","body":"Joshua succeeded Moses as leader of Israel. Numbers 27 describes him as a man in whom is the spirit; Moses publicly commissions him before Eleazar and the congregation. His appointment combines divine choice with recognised responsibility. Courage does not mean acting without instruction or treating leadership as personal ownership of the people.\n\nAt Shechem in Joshua 24, Joshua recalls God's past acts and calls the people to put away other gods and serve the LORD. Covenant renewal means reaffirming that binding relationship and its responsibilities. Joshua's declaration that he and his household will serve the LORD joins public leadership with personal commitment. Saying the right words without abandoning rival loyalties misses the challenge.\n\nJudges describes recurring wrongdoing, oppression, appeals for help and deliverance. A judge is a leader raised for deliverance and guidance, not a hereditary monarch or simply a modern courtroom official. Gideon's fleece belongs to his request for reassurance. God later reduces his force to three hundred so that Israel cannot claim its own strength secured victory. Immediately before the attack, Gideon hears a Midianite's dream and interpretation, worships God and returns encouraged. Keep the fleece, selection of troops and final encouragement in sequence.\n\nSamson is set apart from birth under Nazirite restrictions, including uncut hair. His strength comes from God's enabling, not magical hair. Judges 16 says he did not know that the LORD had departed from him when he expected to escape as before. The cutting of his hair belongs to the betrayal of his consecration; his later prayer acknowledges dependence on God. Across these narratives, covenant faithfulness requires obedient action, not confidence in status, numbers or outward symbols.\n\nGideon names his altar at Ophrah “The LORD is peace.” Joshua responds to Ai by tearing his clothes and praying. Rahab and her household are spared at Jericho because she protected the spies. Hiel's much later rebuilding of Jericho and loss of two sons belongs to the period of the kings, not Joshua's original conquest.\n\nSisera commands Jabin's army; Barak leads Israel's force. Samson begins deliverance from Philistine power; his exploits do not establish permanent national independence.\n\nJudges 3 names Shamgar as delivering Israel with an oxgoad against six hundred Philistines. Deuteronomy 7 names seven nations in Canaan; Joshua's later list of defeated kings is not the claim that he conquered seven nations before entering the land. Scripture does not state Joshua's age at the Exodus. Caleb, a different person, says he was forty when sent to spy out the land. Joshua 9:1 lists six peoples; Joshua 3:10 and Deuteronomy 7:1 list seven, including Girgashites."},"new":{"title":"Joshua, covenant faithfulness and the judges","body":"1. INTRODUCTION: FROM MOSES TO THE JUDGES\nJoshua led Israel after Moses' death. The book of Joshua describes crossing the Jordan, entering Canaan, allocating land and renewing covenant commitments. Judges describes the period before kingship, when Israel repeatedly turned away from God, faced oppression and received deliverance through leaders called judges. A judge in this period was not simply a modern court magistrate; many were military and community deliverers.\n\n2. JOSHUA'S COMMISSION (NUMBERS 27; DEUTERONOMY 31; JOSHUA 1)\nIn Numbers 27:18–23 God directed Moses to appoint Joshua son of Nun, a man in whom was the spirit. Moses laid hands on him before Eleazar the priest and the congregation. Deuteronomy 31 records Moses encouraging Joshua to be strong and courageous. After Moses' death God commissioned Joshua, promising to be with him as with Moses and instructing him to meditate on the book of the law day and night (Joshua 1:1–9). Courage meant faithful obedience to God's instruction, not acting recklessly or claiming that leadership belonged to Joshua personally.\n\n3. THE SPIES AND RAHAB (JOSHUA 2)\nJoshua sent two spies secretly from Shittim to inspect Jericho. They entered the house of Rahab, who hid them on her roof beneath stalks of flax. When the king's men came looking for them, she sent the pursuers away. Rahab told the spies that the people of Jericho feared Israel because they had heard of God's acts. She asked for protection for herself and her family. The spies told her to tie a scarlet cord in her window and gather her family inside. During Jericho's destruction, Rahab and those with her were spared. The scarlet cord was a sign of the agreed protection, not the instrument that caused the city walls to fall.\n\n4. CROSSING THE JORDAN (JOSHUA 3–4)\nIsrael camped beside the Jordan. The priests carrying the ark of the covenant entered the river at flood stage; when their feet touched the water, the upstream flow stopped, and the people crossed on dry ground. Joshua directed twelve men, one from each tribe, to take twelve stones from the riverbed and set them up at Gilgal as a memorial (Joshua 4). The stones were to prompt future children to ask what had happened, so their parents could explain God's power. Do not confuse the twelve memorial stones with Elijah's twelve altar stones on Mount Carmel.\n\n5. THE FALL OF JERICHO (JOSHUA 5–6)\nNear Jericho Joshua encountered the commander of the LORD's army and was told to remove his sandals because the place was holy (Joshua 5:13–15). God's instructions for Jericho required armed men and seven priests carrying seven trumpets before the ark to march around the city once each day for six days. On the seventh day they marched around seven times; after a long trumpet blast the people shouted, and the wall fell. The city was placed under the ban, while Rahab and her household were spared. Achan later took prohibited items, bringing trouble on Israel (Joshua 7). Remember: six days with one circuit each, seventh day with seven circuits, seven priests and seven trumpets.\n\n6. AI, ACHAN AND RESTORED OBEDIENCE (JOSHUA 7–8)\nIsrael's first attack on Ai failed, and about 36 Israelites were killed (Joshua 7:5). Joshua tore his clothes and prayed. God revealed that Israel had broken the ban. Achan of the tribe of Judah confessed that he had taken a beautiful cloak from Shinar, 200 shekels of silver and a bar of gold weighing 50 shekels. The stolen goods were found hidden in his tent. Achan and his household were brought to the Valley of Achor, where judgement was carried out. Israel later defeated Ai using an ambush (Joshua 8). The lesson concerns covenant obedience and the community consequences of concealed wrongdoing, not a lack of courage alone.\n\n7. LAND AND COVENANT RENEWAL AT SHECHEM (JOSHUA 23–24)\nWhen Joshua was old he urged Israel to remain faithful, avoid idolatry and remember God's promises. At Shechem he reviewed God's dealings with Abraham, the Exodus and entry into the land. He called the people to put away foreign gods and serve the LORD sincerely. Joshua declared, 'As for me and my house, we will serve the LORD' (Joshua 24:15). The people promised obedience. Joshua set up a large stone under the oak near the sanctuary as a witness to the covenant (24:26–27). Joshua died aged 110 (24:29).\n\n8. THE CYCLE IN JUDGES (JUDGES 2)\nJudges repeatedly describes a pattern: Israel turns to other gods, comes under oppression, cries to God, and God raises a deliverer. After a judge dies, the people often return to unfaithfulness. The book explains that Israel did not consistently keep covenant commitments. It is misleading to assume every judge ruled the whole nation simultaneously or that all judges were kings. Judges 21:25 sums up the period by saying there was no king in Israel and everyone did what was right in their own eyes.\n\n9. DEBORAH, BARAK, SISERA AND JAEL (JUDGES 4–5)\nDeborah was a prophetess and judge who sat under the palm of Deborah between Ramah and Bethel. She summoned Barak son of Abinoam and told him to gather 10,000 men from Naphtali and Zebulun to Mount Tabor. Jabin was the Canaanite king; Sisera commanded his army and had 900 iron chariots. Barak insisted Deborah accompany him. She said the honour of defeating Sisera would go to a woman. When Sisera's army was routed, he fled to the tent of Jael, wife of Heber the Kenite. She gave him milk, covered him and killed him with a tent peg while he slept. Judges 5 records Deborah and Barak's song of victory. Distinguish Jabin the king, Sisera the commander, Barak the Israelite military leader and Jael who killed Sisera.\n\n10. GIDEON'S CALL AND THE 300 (JUDGES 6–8)\nGideon son of Joash was threshing wheat in a winepress to hide it from Midianites when an angel addressed him as a mighty warrior. Gideon questioned why Israel was suffering and asked for signs. He prepared an offering that was consumed by fire, then built an altar at Ophrah called 'The LORD is peace' (Judges 6:24). He tore down his father's altar to Baal and received the name Jerubbaal. Gideon tested with a fleece: first the fleece was wet while the ground was dry; then the fleece was dry while the ground was wet. God reduced his army because victory should not be credited to human numbers. Of 32,000 men, 22,000 went home through fear; after the water test only 300 remained (Judges 7:3–7). Gideon heard a Midianite's dream of a barley loaf overturning a tent and its interpretation, which encouraged him. He divided his 300 into three groups with trumpets, jars and torches. They shouted 'A sword for the LORD and for Gideon!' and the Midianites fled in confusion. Gideon later refused kingship in words but made an ephod that became a snare (Judges 8:22–27).\n\n11. JEPHTHAH AND HIS VOW (JUDGES 11)\nJephthah, a Gileadite rejected by his brothers, was later called to lead against the Ammonites. He argued about Israel's historical claim to the land and then made a rash vow concerning whoever came out of his house to meet him if he returned victorious. His only child, a daughter, came out with tambourines and dancing. The text reports her grief and the fulfilment of the vow (Judges 11:30–40), with debate among interpreters about its precise outcome. The clear warning is against reckless vows; the story should not be used to imply that God commanded the vow.\n\n12. SAMSON: NAZIRITE CONSECRATION AND DELILAH (JUDGES 13–16)\nAn angel told Manoah's wife, who had been barren, that she would bear a son set apart as a Nazirite from birth. She was instructed not to drink wine or strong drink or eat unclean food; no razor was to touch the boy's head. Samson was born and the Spirit began to stir him. He married a Philistine woman from Timnah, posed a riddle about a lion and honey, and later fought Philistines. At Lehi he struck down 1,000 men with a donkey's jawbone (Judges 15:15). Delilah pressed him to reveal the source of his strength. After misleading her several times, he told her of his uncut hair, a sign of consecration. His hair was shaved while he slept; Judges 16:20 says he did not know that the LORD had left him. The Philistines blinded and imprisoned him. Later, at a festival in the temple of Dagon, he prayed for strength, pushed against two supporting pillars and brought down the building, dying with many Philistines. His strength was God's gift, not magic stored in hair.\n\n13. OTHER JUDGES AND EXAM DISTINCTIONS\nOthniel, Caleb's younger brother, delivered Israel from Cushan-rishathaim (Judges 3:7–11). Ehud, a left-handed Benjaminite, killed Eglon king of Moab (3:12–30). Shamgar struck down 600 Philistines with an oxgoad (3:31). Tola and Jair are named as judges in Judges 10; Ibzan, Elon and Abdon appear in Judges 12. These brief accounts are easy to overlook in multiple-choice questions. Keep the roles distinct: Rahab protected spies at Jericho; Deborah judged and instructed Barak; Jael killed Sisera; Gideon defeated Midian with 300; Samson fought Philistines; Joshua succeeded Moses and led the crossing of the Jordan.\n\nDEEPER GUIDED TEACHING — CONCEPTS, CONTEXT AND MEANING\nTHE BIG PICTURE: FROM PROMISE TO SETTLEMENT\nJoshua's leadership follows Moses' death and centres on crossing the Jordan, taking and allocating land, and renewing the covenant. Judges describes a later period when Israel repeatedly turns from God, suffers oppression, cries out and receives a deliverer or judge. A judge in this book is often a military or charismatic leader, not merely a modern courtroom official. Joshua 24's covenant renewal at Shechem and Judges 2's cycle of disobedience help explain why the two books belong together.\n\nCROSSING THE JORDAN: HOW IT DIFFERS FROM THE RED SEA\nAt the Red Sea Moses stretches out his hand and the waters divide (Exodus 14). At the Jordan the priests carrying the ark step into the river and the upstream waters stop, allowing Israel to cross on dry ground (Joshua 3). Twelve stones are taken from the river and set up as a memorial at Gilgal (Joshua 4), one for each tribe. These are distinct events and leaders. The memorial stones help later generations ask what God did for their ancestors.\n\nRAHAB'S CHOICE AND THE SPIES\nJoshua sends two spies to Jericho, where Rahab hides them from the king's men. She expresses belief that the Lord has given Israel the land and asks that her family be spared. The spies instruct her to tie a scarlet cord in the window and gather her family inside the house. At Jericho's fall Rahab and her household are protected. Rahab's action combines faith with risk and practical protection. The scarlet cord is a recognition sign in the story, not the cause of the city's walls collapsing.\n\nJERICHO: THE ORDER OF EVENTS\nJoshua 6 describes marching around Jericho once daily for six days, with priests carrying trumpets before the ark. On the seventh day the people march seven times; at the trumpet blast and shout, the wall falls. The number seven appears in different ways: seventh day, seven circuits that day and seven priests with seven trumpets. Students must not answer that Israel marched seven times every day. The story connects victory with obedience to the specific instructions given, rather than superior siege equipment.\n\nAI AND ACHAN: WHY THE FIRST ATTACK FAILED\nAfter Jericho, Israel's first attempt against Ai fails. Joshua 7 identifies Achan's taking of devoted things from Jericho as the cause of covenant violation. Achan confesses taking a beautiful cloak, silver and a bar of gold and hiding them in his tent. The narrative records severe communal consequences and Achan's punishment in the Valley of Achor. Joshua 8 then describes a successful attack on Ai using an ambush. The point is the link between obedience, accountability and communal responsibility, not that Ai was inherently stronger than Jericho.\n\nDEBORAH, BARAK AND JAEL: THREE DIFFERENT ROLES\nDeborah was a prophetess and judge who held court under the palm of Deborah between Ramah and Bethel (Judges 4). She summoned Barak and delivered God's command to gather ten thousand men from Naphtali and Zebulun against Sisera, commander of Jabin's army. Barak insisted Deborah accompany him. Deborah said the honour of defeating Sisera would go to a woman. Sisera fled to Jael's tent; Jael killed him with a tent peg. Deborah and Barak sing a victory song in Judges 5. The woman who killed Sisera was Jael, not Deborah.\n\nGIDEON: THE SIGNS AND THE THREE HUNDRED\nIn Judges 6 Gideon is called while threshing wheat in a winepress to hide it from Midianites. He asks for signs, including the fleece wet while the ground is dry, and then the reverse. In Judges 7 God reduces Gideon's army so victory cannot be credited to numbers. The final three hundred men carry trumpets, jars and torches, surround the Midianite camp and shout. The Midianites turn against one another in confusion. Gideon's victory emphasises dependence on God, but the story also records his later ephod becoming a snare (Judges 8:27), so his entire career is not presented as flawless.\n\nJEPHTHAH AND THE DANGER OF A RASH VOW\nJephthah, son of Gilead, leads against the Ammonites in Judges 11. He vows that whatever comes out of his house to meet him on return will belong to the Lord and be offered as a burnt offering. His daughter, his only child, comes out to greet him. The narrative is tragic, and interpreters disagree over some details of the vow's fulfilment. The text does not instruct believers to imitate the vow. A careful lesson should teach the danger of rash religious promises and avoid celebrating harm to the daughter.\n\nSAMSON: STRENGTH, VOW AND FAILURE\nSamson's birth is announced to Manoah's wife in Judges 13. He is to be a Nazirite from the womb, and no razor is to touch his head. His conflicts with Philistines include the lion, the riddle at his wedding, the jawbone of a donkey and the gates of Gaza. Delilah repeatedly asks the source of his strength; Samson eventually reveals the significance of his uncut hair, and his hair is shaved while he sleeps. Captured and blinded, he later prays for strength and pulls down the pillars of the Philistine temple, dying with his enemies (Judges 16). The story links divine calling with the consequences of compromised judgement.\n\nTHE JUDGES CYCLE AND ITS MORAL MESSAGE\nJudges 2 describes a recurring pattern: Israel turns to other gods; oppression follows; the people cry out; God raises judges; relief comes, but after a judge dies the people turn away again. Judges 21:25 ends with the statement that there was no king in Israel and everyone did what was right in their own eyes. This is a diagnosis of social and religious disorder, not proof that every action performed by a judge was morally exemplary. The book contains troubling events and demands careful moral interpretation.\n\nWORKED JAMB-STYLE INTERPRETATION QUESTIONS\nExample (i): What distinguishes the Jordan crossing from the Red Sea crossing?\nA. Joshua led the Jordan crossing with priests carrying the ark\nB. Moses led both with the same staff gesture\nC. Both occurred after the conquest of Jericho\nD. Rahab carried the ark across the Red Sea\nSolution: Joshua 3 describes priests with the ark at the Jordan; Exodus 14 describes Moses at the Red Sea.\nCorrect answer: A.\n\nExample (ii): How many times did Israel march around Jericho on the seventh day?\nA. Once\nB. Three times\nC. Seven times\nD. Forty times\nSolution: Joshua 6:15 states seven circuits on the seventh day; the first six days involved one circuit each.\nCorrect answer: C.\n\nExample (iii): Who killed Sisera after he fled the battle?\nA. Deborah\nB. Jael\nC. Delilah\nD. Rahab\nSolution: Judges 4:21 names Jael, who used a tent peg. Deborah was the prophetess and judge; the others belong to different stories.\nCorrect answer: B.\n\nExample (iv): Why was Gideon's army reduced to three hundred?\nA. Israel lacked weapons entirely\nB. So Israel could not claim victory came from its own numbers\nC. Because all the other soldiers joined Midian\nD. Because Joshua commanded it\nSolution: Judges 7:2 explains that God reduced the army lest Israel boast that its own hand saved it.\nCorrect answer: B.\n\nExample (v): Which statement best describes Jephthah's vow?\nA. A model of wise family care\nB. A tragic rash promise with serious consequences\nC. A command given to all Israelite fathers\nD. A vow made by Samson\nSolution: Judges 11 portrays the vow and its tragic consequences; it is not a general instruction to imitate.\nCorrect answer: B.\n\nExample (vi): What repeated pattern organises much of Judges?\nA. Exile → Persian decree → temple rebuilding\nB. Disobedience → oppression → cry for help → deliverance\nC. Birth → baptism → temptation\nD. Trade → census → taxation\nSolution: Judges 2 sets out the cycle repeated in the narratives. The other sequences come from unrelated books.\nCorrect answer: B.\n\nQUICK UNDERSTANDING CHECK — SPECIFIC TO THIS LESSON\n1. Explain three differences between the Red Sea and Jordan crossings.\n2. Describe Jericho's marching instructions day by day.\n3. Distinguish Deborah, Barak and Jael by their actions.\n4. Explain the judges cycle and why it does not mean every judge was morally perfect.\n\nPRACTICE JAMB PAST QUESTIONS\nUse the lesson's linked past questions, identify the exact biblical passage, and explain why the strongest answer is better than similar alternatives."}},{"id":"patriarchs-faith","revised":true,"old":{"title":"Abraham, Isaac, Jacob and Joseph: faith and responsibility","body":"The patriarchs are Abraham, Isaac and Jacob, the ancestral figures through whom Genesis describes Israel's beginnings. Their narratives connect God's promises with trust, obedience, conflict and reconciliation. Faith responds to God's direction; religious vocabulary does not guarantee immediate fulfilment of every desire.\n\nGod's promises to Abraham concern descendants, land and blessing extending to other nations. Genesis 22 connects renewed worldwide blessing with Abraham's obedience. At Moriah, sacrificing Isaac becomes a test; God stops the sacrifice and supplies a ram. This commends obedience and provision, not human sacrifice as a continuing duty. Abraham names the place “The LORD will provide.” Circumcision is the Genesis 17 covenant sign; the rainbow belongs to Noah.\n\nGenesis 24 describes Abraham's servant seeking a wife for Isaac among Abraham's relatives. He prays, and Rebekah offers water to him and his camels. He refuses food until explaining his errand: finding a wife, not purchasing animals or simply delivering a message. Her kindness answers his requested sign, but the episode does not require every marriage decision to repeat that test.\n\nIsaac prays concerning Rebekah's barrenness and the LORD answers, illustrating reliance rather than contempt for a childless woman. Jacob promises a tenth at Bethel in connection with protection and returning home.\n\nJoseph's leadership joins humility with practical wisdom. He first credits God for interpreting Pharaoh's dream, explains the plentiful and famine years, and advises grain storage. Interpretation and counsel lead to his appointment. His brothers earlier appealed to kinship when choosing sale rather than murder. Integrity and forgiving his brothers also matter. Trusting God while preparing carefully for a Nigerian examination likewise distinguishes reliance from neglect of duty.\n\nGenesis 12 places Abraham's departure at Haran; Acts 7 recalls God's appearance before he lived there. The land promise is given at Shechem. Genesis 17 dates an appearance to age ninety-nine, but several appearances are recorded, making “the second time” imprecise without a passage. Abraham expects God's angel to guide his servant; Genesis 24 does not name the servant Eliezer.\n\nSarah dies at 127. Her demand to send Hagar's son away concerns Isaac's inheritance, not merely an invented emotional motive. Abraham separates from Lot after conflict between their herdsmen over grazing resources, not a quarrel about sexual assault. Genesis 12 narrates Abraham obeying the call to leave; a question counting every divine address needs a defined event. Pharaoh appoints Joseph over Egypt, fulfilling his earlier leadership dreams when his brothers later bow before him."},"new":{"title":"Abraham, Isaac, Jacob and Joseph: faith and responsibility","body":"1. THE CALL OF ABRAM (GENESIS 12:1–9)\nGod told Abram to leave his country, kindred and father's household for a land God would show him. God promised to make him a great nation, bless him, make his name great, make him a blessing, bless those who blessed him, curse the one who dishonoured him, and bless all families of the earth through him. Abram was 75 when he departed Haran (Genesis 12:4). He travelled with Sarai and Lot to Canaan, reaching Shechem near the oak of Moreh, where God promised the land to his offspring. Abram built an altar there and later another between Bethel and Ai. The call demanded trust before Abram knew every detail of his destination.\n\n2. ABRAM, LOT AND THE PROMISE OF LAND (GENESIS 13–15)\nAbram and Lot became wealthy in livestock, creating disputes between their herdsmen. Abram allowed Lot to choose first; Lot selected the well-watered Jordan plain toward Sodom, while Abram remained in Canaan. God reaffirmed the promise of land and descendants as numerous as the dust. When Lot was captured in a regional war, Abram rescued him with 318 trained men born in his household (Genesis 14:14). Melchizedek, king of Salem and priest of God Most High, blessed Abram; Abram gave him a tenth of everything. In Genesis 15 Abram worried that Eliezer of Damascus would inherit his household. God promised an heir from Abram's own body and descendants as numerous as the stars. Abram believed God, and it was counted to him as righteousness (15:6). In the covenant ceremony God foretold that his descendants would be strangers and oppressed for 400 years (15:13).\n\n3. HAGAR, ISHMAEL AND THE COVENANT OF CIRCUMCISION (GENESIS 16–17)\nSarai, still childless, gave Hagar her Egyptian servant to Abram. Hagar conceived, conflict followed, and she fled. An angel met her by a spring in the wilderness and told her to return; her son was to be named Ishmael, meaning God hears. Abram was 86 when Ishmael was born (Genesis 16:16). In Genesis 17, when Abram was 99, God renamed him Abraham, meaning father of a multitude, and Sarai became Sarah. Circumcision of males on the eighth day was the sign of the covenant; Abraham and the males in his household were circumcised. God promised that Sarah would bear Isaac and that the covenant line would continue through Isaac, while Ishmael would also be blessed. Do not confuse Ishmael with Isaac or the age 86 with 99.\n\n4. THE VISITORS, SODOM AND ISAAC'S BIRTH (GENESIS 18–21)\nThree visitors appeared to Abraham near the oaks of Mamre. Abraham offered hospitality. Sarah, listening at the tent entrance, laughed when told she would have a son; the promise was that the child would arrive at the appointed time the following year. Abraham pleaded for Sodom, asking whether it would be spared for 50 righteous people and then successively 45, 40, 30, 20 and 10 (Genesis 18:23–32). Two angels visited Lot in Sodom. Lot and his family were urged to flee; Lot's wife looked back and became a pillar of salt (Genesis 19:26). Sarah gave birth to Isaac when Abraham was 100 (Genesis 21:5). Isaac's name is connected with laughter. Hagar and Ishmael were later sent away, but God heard the boy and showed Hagar a well.\n\n5. THE TEST OF ABRAHAM (GENESIS 22)\nGod tested Abraham by telling him to take Isaac, his beloved son, to the land of Moriah and offer him on a mountain God would indicate. Abraham rose early, took two servants, Isaac, wood and a donkey, and travelled for three days before seeing the place. Abraham left the servants and continued with Isaac, who carried the wood. When Isaac asked where the lamb was, Abraham said God would provide. Abraham built an altar, arranged the wood and bound Isaac. An angel stopped him before he harmed the boy. Abraham saw a ram caught by its horns in a thicket and offered it instead. He named the place 'The LORD will provide.' The narrative ends with God's reaffirmed promise. The sacrifice actually offered was a ram, not Isaac.\n\n6. ISAAC, REBEKAH AND THE TWINS (GENESIS 24–27)\nAbraham sent his servant to his relatives to find a wife for Isaac rather than among the Canaanites. At a well the servant prayed for a sign: the woman who offered him water and also watered his camels. Rebekah, daughter of Bethuel and sister of Laban, did so. She agreed to go and became Isaac's wife (Genesis 24). Isaac was 40 when he married Rebekah (25:20), and 60 when the twins were born (25:26). Esau emerged first, red and hairy; Jacob followed holding Esau's heel. Esau later sold his birthright to Jacob for bread and lentil stew. In Genesis 27 Rebekah helped Jacob obtain Isaac's blessing by disguising himself with Esau's clothes and goat skins. The birthright sale and the stolen blessing are separate events.\n\n7. JACOB AT BETHEL, LABAN'S HOUSE AND PENIEL (GENESIS 28–33)\nFleeing Esau, Jacob slept at Bethel with a stone for his headrest. He dreamed of a stairway reaching heaven with angels ascending and descending; God repeated the promises of land, descendants and presence. Jacob set the stone as a pillar and vowed devotion to God. In Haran he worked seven years for Rachel, but Laban gave him Leah first. Jacob then married Rachel and served another seven years for her (Genesis 29). His children became ancestors of Israel's tribes; Rachel bore Joseph and later Benjamin. Returning home, Jacob wrestled through the night with a mysterious man at the Jabbok. His hip was touched and he was renamed Israel because he had striven with God and humans (Genesis 32:22–32). He called the place Peniel or Penuel. Jacob then met Esau, who ran to embrace him. Bethel is the dream location; Peniel is the wrestling location.\n\n8. JOSEPH'S DREAMS AND SALE INTO EGYPT (GENESIS 37)\nJacob loved Joseph, the son of his old age, and gave him a special robe. Joseph dreamed that his brothers' sheaves bowed to his sheaf and that the sun, moon and eleven stars bowed to him. His brothers became jealous. When Joseph went to look for them, they plotted to kill him. Reuben persuaded them to place him in a pit, hoping to rescue him later. Judah proposed selling him rather than killing him. Joseph was sold for 20 pieces of silver to traders travelling to Egypt (Genesis 37:28). His brothers dipped the robe in goat's blood and led Jacob to believe Joseph had been killed by a wild animal. Joseph was 17 at the beginning of this account (37:2).\n\n9. JOSEPH IN POTIPHAR'S HOUSE AND PRISON (GENESIS 39–40)\nIn Egypt Joseph served Potiphar, an officer of Pharaoh and captain of the guard. God prospered Joseph's work, and Potiphar entrusted him with responsibility. Potiphar's wife repeatedly tried to seduce him. Joseph refused, saying it would be a great wickedness and sin against God. She seized his garment; he fled, leaving it behind, and she falsely accused him. Joseph was imprisoned but gained the keeper's trust. In prison Pharaoh's cupbearer dreamed of a vine with three branches and grapes pressed into Pharaoh's cup; Joseph said he would be restored in three days. The baker dreamed of three baskets of bread being eaten by birds; Joseph said he would be executed in three days. Both interpretations came true, but the restored cupbearer initially forgot Joseph.\n\n10. JOSEPH BEFORE PHARAOH AND THE FAMINE (GENESIS 41–45)\nPharaoh dreamed of seven healthy cows consumed by seven thin cows and seven full ears of grain swallowed by seven thin ears. Joseph explained that both dreams meant seven years of abundance followed by seven years of famine. He advised appointing a wise administrator and storing one-fifth of produce during prosperous years. Pharaoh appointed Joseph over Egypt, gave him his signet ring and the Egyptian name Zaphenath-paneah. Joseph was 30 when he entered Pharaoh's service (41:46). He married Asenath, daughter of Potiphera, priest of On, and had sons Manasseh and Ephraim. During famine Joseph's brothers came to buy grain and bowed before him without recognising him. Joseph tested their honesty, especially their treatment of Benjamin, then revealed himself, wept and forgave them. He said God had sent him ahead to preserve life (45:5–8). Joseph was 17 when sold and 30 at his appointment; these are different stages.\n\n11. JACOB'S FAMILY IN EGYPT AND JOSEPH'S LAST WORDS (GENESIS 46–50)\nJacob moved to Egypt with his household during the famine. Pharaoh allowed the family to settle in Goshen. Jacob was 130 when he met Pharaoh (Genesis 47:9) and died aged 147 (47:28). He blessed Joseph's sons, deliberately giving the stronger blessing to younger Ephraim rather than older Manasseh (Genesis 48). After Jacob's death, Joseph's brothers feared revenge, but Joseph reassured them: they had intended evil, while God used the events for good and the preservation of many lives (50:20). Joseph died aged 110 and requested that his bones be carried from Egypt when God visited Israel (50:25–26).\n\n12. EXAM DISTINCTIONS\nAbraham left Haran aged 75; Ishmael was born when Abraham was 86; circumcision covenant when he was 99; Isaac born when he was 100. Isaac married at 40 and became father of twins at 60. Jacob dreamed at Bethel and wrestled at Peniel. Joseph was sold for 20 pieces of silver at about 17, interpreted dreams in prison, and became Pharaoh's official at 30. Pharaoh's dreams involved seven years of abundance and seven of famine. Jacob died aged 147; Joseph died aged 110.\n\nDEEPER TEACHING: UNDERSTANDING THE EVENTS AND THEIR MEANING\nWHAT IS FAITH? WHY DOES THE COVENANT MATTER?\nFaith in these narratives is not merely believing that God exists. It is trusting God's promise sufficiently to act when the full outcome is not visible. Abram leaves Haran before possessing Canaan; he trusts the promise of descendants while Sarah remains childless. A covenant is a binding relationship with promises and obligations. God's promises to Abraham concern land, descendants and blessing to the nations; circumcision later becomes its visible sign. The test involving Isaac is not a second call to leave Haran. It tests Abraham's trust in the God who had promised Isaac. Read Genesis 12, 15, 17 and 22 as distinct episodes that develop one story.\n\nHOW THE PATRIARCHAL STORIES CONNECT\nAbraham receives the promise; Isaac is the promised child; Jacob inherits the covenant line despite family conflict; Joseph's suffering eventually places him in Egypt where he preserves the family during famine. This sequence explains why Israel's ancestors later live in Egypt before Exodus begins. Joseph's forgiveness does not make his brothers' betrayal acceptable: Genesis 50:20 distinguishes their evil intention from God's ability to bring good out of the events. The key examination skill is tracing the same promise through different generations, not memorising four unrelated biographies.\n\nJOSEPH'S TWO SETS OF DREAMS: DO NOT MIX THEM\nJoseph's youthful dreams in Genesis 37 are about sheaves and heavenly bodies bowing to him; they create tension with his brothers. In prison in Genesis 40, the cupbearer's three branches indicate restoration in three days, while the baker's three baskets indicate death in three days. Pharaoh's two dreams in Genesis 41 involve seven cows and seven ears of grain, pointing to seven prosperous years and seven famine years. Joseph does not interpret the dreams by magic; he explicitly credits God. He then proposes storing a fifth of the harvest during abundance. A question asking about three days points to the prisoners, not Pharaoh.\n\nEXAM REASONING: RIGHT ACTION VERSUS WRONG MOTIVE\nAbram's peaceful choice to let Lot select land first demonstrates conflict avoidance, whereas Lot's choice of fertile land does not automatically make his decision morally sound. Rebekah's deception to secure Jacob's blessing should be described as an event, not presented as a command to deceive. Joseph refuses Potiphar's wife because he sees adultery as wrongdoing against his master and God. His later forgiveness involves truth, testing changed behaviour and practical preservation of the family. When JAMB asks for the moral of an episode, connect the answer to the action and its stated reason rather than a vague slogan.\n\nWORKED JAMB-STYLE INTERPRETATION QUESTIONS\nExample (i): Joseph's statement that his brothers intended evil but God intended good mainly teaches that\nA. betrayal is acceptable when the victim succeeds\nB. God can bring preservation out of human wrongdoing without approving the wrongdoing\nC. brothers should never be held accountable\nD. every dream predicts wealth\nSolution: Genesis 50:20 explicitly names the brothers' evil intention and God's preserving purpose. A wrongly excuses betrayal; C denies responsibility; D has no basis in the verse.\nCorrect answer: B.\n\nExample (ii): Which sequence correctly follows the covenant family?\nA. Joseph → Abraham → Isaac → Jacob\nB. Jacob → Isaac → Abraham → Joseph\nC. Abraham → Isaac → Jacob → Joseph\nD. Abraham → Joseph → Isaac → Jacob\nSolution: Genesis follows the promise from Abraham to Isaac to Jacob and then the Joseph narrative. The other options confuse generations.\nCorrect answer: C.\n\nExample (iii): Which dream belongs to Pharaoh rather than Joseph's fellow prisoners?\nA. Three baskets of bread\nB. A vine with three branches\nC. Seven healthy cows swallowed by thin cows\nD. A ladder between earth and heaven\nSolution: Genesis 41 contains Pharaoh's seven cows and grain ears. A and B are Genesis 40 prison dreams; D is Jacob's dream in Genesis 28.\nCorrect answer: C.\n\nExample (iv): What made Abraham's obedience in Genesis 12 an act of faith?\nA. He already owned Canaan\nB. He trusted God's direction before knowing the full destination\nC. Lot promised to finance the journey\nD. He had already become a great nation\nSolution: Genesis 12:1–4 records a call to go to a land God would show him. A and D are future promises, not accomplished facts; C is absent.\nCorrect answer: B.\n\nQUICK UNDERSTANDING CHECK — SPECIFIC TO THIS LESSON\n1. Explain the difference between the promises in Genesis 12 and circumcision in Genesis 17.\n2. Trace the family from Abraham to Joseph and show why Joseph's move to Egypt matters for Exodus.\n3. Match each dream to Jacob, Joseph, the cupbearer, the baker or Pharaoh.\n4. Why is Joseph's forgiveness not the same as saying his brothers acted rightly?\n\nPRACTICE JAMB PAST QUESTIONS\nOpen the lesson's linked JAMB questions. For each question, identify the relevant passage and explain why its correct option is stronger than the alternatives."}},{"id":"prophetic-calls","revised":true,"old":{"title":"The calls of Isaiah, Jeremiah and Ezekiel","body":"A prophetic call is God's commissioning of a person to receive and communicate his message. It explains the prophet's authority and responsibility, not simply an ambition to become influential. Isaiah, Jeremiah and Ezekiel have distinct call narratives. A quotation should remain attached to its own prophet, setting and sequence rather than being assigned to any familiar religious leader.\n\nIsaiah 6 places the vision in the year King Uzziah died. Isaiah sees the Lord enthroned and hears the seraphim proclaim God's holiness. Holiness means God's unique purity and majesty, which exposes human sin. Isaiah recognises his own unclean lips and those of his people. A seraph touches his mouth with a burning coal from the altar and announces that his guilt is removed. Cleansing precedes Isaiah's willing response to the question about who will go. His commission includes warning about unresponsive hearing and devastation; it is not a promise of immediate popularity. Isaiah 61 separately speaks of good news and liberty for captives. Do not collapse those passages into one scene.\n\nJeremiah 1 says God knew him before forming him in the womb and appointed him a prophet to the nations. Jeremiah objects that he does not know how to speak because he is young. God promises his presence and puts his words in Jeremiah's mouth. The first symbolic vision is an almond rod; the boiling pot comes afterwards. His commission includes uprooting and pulling down as well as building and planting, joining judgement with restoration.\n\nEzekiel 2–3 addresses a rebellious house of Israel. After the command to stand, the Spirit enters him and sets him on his feet. He receives a scroll written with lamentation, mourning and woe, eats it, and is sent to speak God's words. The problem is Israel's unwillingness to listen, not Ezekiel's inability to speak a foreign language. The eaten scroll symbolises receiving the message before proclaiming it; it is not an ordinary prescription about food.\n\nAcross the three accounts, God equips imperfect messengers and requires faithfulness. Confidence in a calling does not excuse dishonesty, exploitation or changing an unwelcome message to please a powerful audience.\n\nJeremiah's mouth is touched by God's hand; Isaiah's lips by a coal brought by a seraph. Ezekiel's scroll represents receiving God's word, not a permanent exemption from hunger."},"new":{"title":"The calls of Isaiah, Jeremiah and Ezekiel","body":"1. INTRODUCTION: WHAT MAKES A PROPHET'S CALL IMPORTANT\nA biblical prophet is a person commissioned to speak God's message. Prophets may warn about injustice and idolatry, call people to repentance, announce judgement or offer hope. A call narrative explains who called the prophet, what the prophet saw or heard, the prophet's reaction, and the task given. Isaiah, Jeremiah and Ezekiel were called in different settings; do not merge their visions.\n\n2. ISAIAH'S VISION IN THE TEMPLE (ISAIAH 6:1–8)\nIsaiah says he saw the Lord in the year King Uzziah died, commonly dated around 740 BCE. The Lord was seated on a high and lofty throne, and the hem of his robe filled the temple. Seraphim stood above him, each with six wings: two covering the face, two covering the feet and two used for flying. They called 'Holy, holy, holy is the LORD of hosts; the whole earth is full of his glory.' The thresholds shook and the temple filled with smoke. Isaiah cried 'Woe is me! For I am lost,' confessing that he was a man of unclean lips among people of unclean lips. A seraph took a burning coal from the altar with tongs and touched Isaiah's mouth, announcing that his guilt was removed and sin atoned for. When God asked 'Whom shall I send?' Isaiah answered, 'Here am I; send me.' The coal symbolises cleansing; Isaiah did not purify himself.\n\n3. ISAIAH'S COMMISSION AND THE HARD MESSAGE (ISAIAH 6:9–13)\nIsaiah was sent to a people who would hear without understanding and see without perceiving, reflecting their resistance to God's message. He asked, 'How long, O Lord?' The answer pointed toward devastated cities, emptied houses and exile. Yet the chapter ends with the image of a stump and holy seed. Isaiah's willingness to go did not mean his audience would immediately accept the message. In exams, the call's distinctive signs are the temple, seraphim, six wings, coal on lips and voluntary response.\n\n4. ISAIAH'S MESSAGE OF HOLINESS, JUSTICE AND HOPE (ISAIAH 1; 5; 7; 9; 11)\nIsaiah criticised sacrifices offered without justice: God demanded that people cease evil, seek justice, rescue the oppressed, defend the orphan and plead for the widow (Isaiah 1:16–17). Isaiah 5's song of the vineyard pictures Israel as a vineyard that produced bad fruit despite careful cultivation, representing injustice. During the Syro-Ephraimite crisis Isaiah spoke to King Ahaz and gave the sign of Immanuel (Isaiah 7:14). Isaiah 9:6 speaks of a child with titles including Wonderful Counsellor and Prince of Peace; Isaiah 11 describes a shoot from Jesse's stump associated with righteous judgement and peace. Distinguish the prophet's initial call in chapter 6 from his later messages.\n\n5. JEREMIAH'S CALL BEFORE HIS BIRTH (JEREMIAH 1:4–10)\nGod told Jeremiah, 'Before I formed you in the womb I knew you, and before you were born I consecrated you; I appointed you a prophet to the nations.' Jeremiah protested, 'Ah, Lord GOD! Truly I do not know how to speak, for I am only a boy.' God told him not to say he was only a boy, but to go wherever he was sent and speak whatever he was commanded. God promised, 'Do not be afraid of them, for I am with you to deliver you.' God stretched out his hand and touched Jeremiah's mouth, placing divine words there. Jeremiah was appointed over nations and kingdoms to pluck up and pull down, destroy and overthrow, build and plant. Unlike Isaiah's coal from the altar, Jeremiah's mouth was touched by God's hand.\n\n6. JEREMIAH'S TWO VISIONS (JEREMIAH 1:11–19)\nJeremiah first saw a branch of an almond tree. God said he was watching over his word to perform it; the Hebrew wordplay links almond with watching. Jeremiah then saw a boiling pot tilted away from the north, pointing to disaster coming from the north against Judah. God told Jeremiah to stand, speak and not be dismayed. He would be like a fortified city, an iron pillar and bronze walls against kings, officials, priests and people. They would fight him but not prevail because God was with him. Remember: almond branch = God's watchfulness; boiling pot = approaching judgement from the north.\n\n7. JEREMIAH'S SYMBOLIC ACTIONS AND THE NEW COVENANT\nJeremiah wore and later ruined a linen waistband as a sign of Judah's spoiled pride (Jeremiah 13). He visited a potter and watched clay being reshaped, illustrating God's authority to judge or restore nations in response to their ways (Jeremiah 18). He broke an earthenware flask as a sign of impending disaster (Jeremiah 19). Jeremiah 31:31–34 promises a new covenant with Israel and Judah: God's law would be written on their hearts, God would be their God, and he would forgive their sin. This promise is distinct from the Sinai covenant, which Judah had broken. Jeremiah was persecuted for his message, including confinement and being lowered into a muddy cistern (Jeremiah 38).\n\n8. EZEKIEL'S EXILIC SETTING AND VISION (EZEKIEL 1)\nEzekiel was a priest among Judean exiles by the river Chebar in Babylon. He dates his vision to the fifth year of King Jehoiachin's exile (Ezekiel 1:2). He saw a stormy wind from the north, a great cloud and flashing fire. Four living creatures appeared, each with four faces—human, lion, ox and eagle—and four wings. Beside them were wheels with a wheel within a wheel, full of eyes around their rims. Above was an expanse and a throne-like appearance with a figure surrounded by brightness like a rainbow. Ezekiel fell on his face when he perceived the likeness of the glory of the LORD. The vision emphasises God's glory and presence even outside Jerusalem; it is not Isaiah's temple vision.\n\n9. EZEKIEL'S COMMISSION AND THE SCROLL (EZEKIEL 2–3)\nGod addressed Ezekiel as 'son of man' and sent him to a rebellious people. The Spirit raised him to his feet. He was told not to fear their words, even though he would face briers and thorns. A hand presented a scroll written on front and back with lamentations, mourning and woe. Ezekiel was told to eat it; it tasted sweet as honey in his mouth (Ezekiel 3:1–3). God made him a watchman for the house of Israel, responsible for delivering warnings faithfully. A watchman who fails to warn the wicked bears responsibility for that failure, while the hearer remains responsible for their own response (Ezekiel 3:16–21). The eaten scroll belongs to Ezekiel's call, not Jeremiah's.\n\n10. EZEKIEL'S SYMBOLIC ACTIONS AND INDIVIDUAL RESPONSIBILITY\nEzekiel drew Jerusalem on a brick and enacted a siege (Ezekiel 4). He shaved hair and divided it to illustrate the fate of Jerusalem's people (Ezekiel 5). Ezekiel 18 teaches individual responsibility, challenging the proverb about fathers eating sour grapes and children's teeth being set on edge: a person is accountable for their own conduct and can turn from wrongdoing. Ezekiel 34 condemns Israel's selfish shepherds and promises God's care for the flock. Ezekiel 36 promises a new heart and a new spirit; God will remove the heart of stone and give a heart of flesh. These passages combine warning with hope for renewal.\n\n11. THE VALLEY OF DRY BONES (EZEKIEL 37:1–14)\nGod brought Ezekiel in a vision to a valley full of very dry bones and asked whether they could live. Ezekiel replied that God knew. God commanded him to prophesy. The bones came together, tendons and flesh covered them, but there was no breath. Ezekiel then prophesied to the breath, which entered the bodies and they stood as a vast multitude. God explained that the bones represented the whole house of Israel, who said their hope was lost. The vision promised restoration from national despair and exile. It is not simply a description of Ezekiel performing an ordinary funeral resurrection.\n\n12. COMPARE THE THREE CALLS\nIsaiah: Temple, year Uzziah died, seraphim with six wings, burning coal touching lips, 'Here am I; send me.' Jeremiah: called before birth, protest about youth, God's hand touching mouth, almond branch and boiling pot. Ezekiel: exile by Chebar, four living creatures and wheels, title 'son of man,' eating a scroll sweet as honey, appointed watchman. All three faced resistant audiences and proclaimed God's message, but their visions, symbols and historical settings differ.\n\nDEEPER TEACHING: UNDERSTANDING THE EVENTS AND THEIR MEANING\nWHAT IS A PROPHETIC CALL?\nA prophet is a person commissioned to deliver God's message, whether welcome or unwelcome. A call narrative usually includes an encounter with God, a response of fear or inadequacy, reassurance and a commission. Isaiah's encounter occurs in the temple, Jeremiah hears God's word about being appointed before birth, and Ezekiel sees God's glory among exiles. These are not three versions of one event. The audience and historical circumstances explain the messages: Isaiah speaks amid Judah's national challenges, Jeremiah warns Judah before its fall, and Ezekiel addresses people already living in Babylonian exile.\n\nISAIAH'S UNCLEAN LIPS: WHY THE COAL MATTERS\nIsaiah's first reaction to God's holiness is awareness of his own uncleanness and that of his people. The seraph's coal touches his lips because lips are the instrument through which he will speak God's word. The cleansing comes before his willing answer, 'Here am I; send me.' Therefore the vision teaches that a messenger needs divine cleansing and commissioning, not that Isaiah already considered himself qualified. The seraph has six wings; it is not a six-winged animal in Ezekiel's vision.\n\nJEREMIAH'S PROTEST AND THE TWO OBJECTS\nJeremiah protests that he is young and unable to speak. God's answer is not that age is irrelevant to every human responsibility; it is that Jeremiah's authority comes from God's appointment, presence and words. God's hand touches Jeremiah's mouth, whereas a seraph touches Isaiah's mouth with a coal. The almond branch signifies God watching over his word; the boiling pot tilting from the north signals approaching disaster. A student who remembers only that both prophets' mouths were touched can easily choose the wrong option unless the agent of the touch is distinguished.\n\nEZEKIEL'S SCROLL AND WATCHMAN DUTY\nEzekiel eats the scroll because he must first receive God's message before speaking it. Its sweetness in his mouth does not mean the content is pleasant: the scroll contains lamentation, mourning and woe. The watchman image explains responsibility: Ezekiel must faithfully warn, while the people remain responsible for their response. The valley of dry bones later addresses Israel's despair and promised restoration; it is not the initial call itself. The four-faced living creatures and wheels appear in Ezekiel 1, while the eaten scroll is in chapters 2–3.\n\nHOW TO INTERPRET SYMBOLIC VISIONS\nA symbol points beyond its physical appearance. A coal signifies cleansing; an almond branch divine watchfulness; a boiling pot impending disaster; a scroll internalising God's message; dry bones a community that sees itself as without hope. The safest exam method is to identify the prophet, locate the chapter and use the interpretation given in the passage. Avoid inventing meanings simply because two objects look alike.\n\nWORKED JAMB-STYLE INTERPRETATION QUESTIONS\nExample (i): Why was Isaiah's mouth touched with a coal?\nA. To punish him for refusing to preach\nB. To signify cleansing before his prophetic commission\nC. To make him king\nD. To make him unable to speak\nSolution: Isaiah 6:6–8 links the coal with removal of guilt, followed by Isaiah's acceptance of the call. A and D contradict the narrative; C is unrelated.\nCorrect answer: B.\n\nExample (ii): Which pairing is accurate?\nA. Jeremiah — seraph with a coal\nB. Ezekiel — almond branch\nC. Isaiah — temple vision in Uzziah's death year\nD. Amos — scroll sweet as honey\nSolution: Isaiah 6:1 names Uzziah. The coal belongs to Isaiah, almond to Jeremiah and sweet scroll to Ezekiel.\nCorrect answer: C.\n\nExample (iii): Ezekiel's appointment as watchman chiefly meant he must\nA. personally punish every sinner\nB. warn people faithfully of God's message\nC. predict the weather\nD. guard Jerusalem's walls at night\nSolution: Ezekiel 3:16–21 concerns the duty to communicate warning. It does not assign Ezekiel the power to force repentance or serve as a literal night guard.\nCorrect answer: B.\n\nExample (iv): Jeremiah's vision of the boiling pot symbolised\nA. famine caused by cooking\nB. the coming judgement from the north\nC. purification of the temple altar\nD. the rebuilding of Jerusalem's walls\nSolution: Jeremiah 1:13–15 interprets the pot as disaster from the north. The other choices borrow themes from unrelated passages.\nCorrect answer: B.\n\nQUICK UNDERSTANDING CHECK — SPECIFIC TO THIS LESSON\n1. Compare who touched Isaiah's mouth and Jeremiah's mouth and explain why.\n2. Explain how Ezekiel's exilic setting differs from Jeremiah's warnings before Jerusalem fell.\n3. Identify the meanings of the almond branch, boiling pot, scroll and dry bones.\n4. Why does a prophet's duty to warn not remove the listener's responsibility?\n\nPRACTICE JAMB PAST QUESTIONS\nOpen the lesson's linked JAMB questions. For each question, identify the relevant passage and explain why its correct option is stronger than the alternatives."}},{"id":"return-rebuilding","revised":true,"old":{"title":"Return from exile: Cyrus, Ezra and Nehemiah","body":"Exile is enforced removal from one's homeland; restoration concerns return and rebuilding after that disruption. The biblical return narratives combine royal permission, material support, teaching, prayer and organised work. They should not be compressed into the claim that one leader performed every task or that rebuilding the Temple and rebuilding the city wall were the same project.\n\nEzra 1 records Cyrus of Persia authorising the rebuilding of God's house in Jerusalem. Those remaining near the returning exiles are to assist with silver, gold, goods and livestock, in addition to freewill offerings for God's house. The decree therefore involves practical support as well as permission. Opposition to Temple rebuilding later comes from the people of the land. This earlier work must be distinguished from Nehemiah's later responsibility for Jerusalem's wall.\n\nEzra is a scribe skilled in the law of Moses. Ezra 7 repeatedly connects the success of his request with the good hand of God upon him. His expertise matters, but the narrative's stated explanation is not merely friendship with the king. He sets his heart to study the law, practise it and teach its statutes in Israel. This sequence connects knowledge, personal obedience and instruction. A teacher who explains honesty while cheating pupils' families contradicts that example.\n\nNehemiah serves as the king's cupbearer. On hearing of Jerusalem's ruined wall and burnt gates, he first mourns, fasts and prays, confessing sins and appealing to God's covenant faithfulness. His prayer asks for mercy before “this man,” the king whose permission he will need. Asking to travel follows his prayer; it is not his first response to the news.\n\nSanballat and Tobiah mock the work. Nehemiah prays and continues organising the builders rather than abandoning the project. The people encourage one another to rise and build, and Nehemiah 4 explains progress by saying they have a mind to work. Prayer and willing labour belong together. A Nigerian school rebuilding a damaged library likewise needs commitment, honest use of resources and cooperation, not speeches alone.\n\nCommon examination distinction: Cyrus authorises return and Temple rebuilding; Ezra studies, practises and teaches the law; Nehemiah leads wall reconstruction.\n\nArtaxerxes grants Nehemiah permission; Cyrus authorised the earlier Temple rebuilding. Nehemiah hears that the survivors are in trouble and shame. Zerubbabel and other leaders reject the neighbouring people's offer to join the Temple work, affirming responsibility to build under Cyrus's decree.\n\nAfter their offer is rejected, opponents discourage the builders and hire advisers to frustrate the project. Distinguish the leaders' reply from the opposition it provokes."},"new":{"title":"Return from exile: Cyrus, Ezra and Nehemiah","body":"1. BACKGROUND: WHY JUDAH WENT INTO EXILE\nThe southern kingdom of Judah faced Babylonian domination. King Jehoiachin and leading citizens were deported in 597 BCE. Zedekiah later rebelled; Jerusalem was captured and the temple destroyed in 586 BCE, with further deportations (2 Kings 24–25). The exile disrupted worship at the Jerusalem temple and royal rule. Prophets such as Jeremiah and Ezekiel explained the disaster in terms of covenant unfaithfulness while also promising eventual restoration. Do not confuse the northern kingdom's fall to Assyria in 722 BCE with Judah's fall to Babylon.\n\n2. CYRUS' DECREE AND THE FIRST RETURN (EZRA 1)\nIn 539 BCE Cyrus of Persia conquered Babylon. In the first year of his reign over Babylon, traditionally dated 538 BCE, he issued a decree permitting the rebuilding of the LORD's house at Jerusalem (Ezra 1:1–4). Cyrus said the God of heaven had given him kingdoms and charged him to build a house in Jerusalem. He invited those of God's people who wished to go up and urged neighbours to support them with silver, gold, goods and offerings. Cyrus also returned temple vessels that Nebuchadnezzar had taken from Jerusalem; Mithredath the treasurer counted them out to Sheshbazzar, described as a prince of Judah (Ezra 1:7–11). The decree concerned return and temple rebuilding, not Nehemiah's later wall project.\n\n3. ZERUBBABEL, JESHUA AND THE ALTAR (EZRA 2–3)\nThe returned community included family groups, priests, Levites and others. Zerubbabel, a leader of Judah's return, and Jeshua (Joshua) son of Jozadak, the high priest, led restoration of worship. They rebuilt the altar on its foundation and offered burnt offerings, including the regular morning and evening sacrifices, even before the temple itself was completed (Ezra 3:1–6). In the second year after arrival they began laying the temple's foundation. Priests sounded trumpets and Levites, sons of Asaph, played cymbals. Some older people who had seen the former temple wept, while others shouted for joy (Ezra 3:10–13). The mixed reaction reflects memory of the former building and hope for the new one.\n\n4. OPPOSITION AND COMPLETION OF THE SECOND TEMPLE (EZRA 4–6)\nLocal opponents first offered to help build, but Zerubbabel and Jeshua rejected their participation in the temple project (Ezra 4:1–3). Opposition, intimidation and political accusations delayed the work. The prophets Haggai and Zechariah later encouraged rebuilding (Ezra 5:1–2). When the governor Tattenai questioned the builders, they explained the earlier decree. King Darius ordered a search, and the decree was found in a record at Ecbatana (Ezra 6). Darius authorised support for the work. The temple was completed on the third day of Adar in the sixth year of Darius' reign, generally dated 515 BCE (Ezra 6:15). The returned exiles dedicated it with sacrifices and celebrated Passover. The temple was completed before Ezra's and Nehemiah's later reform missions.\n\n5. HAGGAI AND ZECHARIAH'S ENCOURAGEMENT\nHaggai challenged people who lived in panelled houses while God's house lay in ruins (Haggai 1:4). He urged them to consider their ways and resume the work. Zerubbabel the governor, Joshua the high priest and the people responded. Haggai 2:9 promises that the later glory of the house will be greater than the former. Zechariah encouraged Zerubbabel with the message, 'Not by might, nor by power, but by my Spirit' (Zechariah 4:6). Zechariah's vision of a lampstand and two olive trees (Zechariah 4) reinforces divine enabling. These prophets supported temple rebuilding, not the construction of Jerusalem's later walls.\n\n6. EZRA THE SCRIBE AND THE LAW (EZRA 7–10; NEHEMIAH 8)\nEzra was a priest and scribe skilled in the law of Moses. He came from Babylon to Jerusalem during the reign of Artaxerxes, traditionally dated 458 BCE (Ezra 7). Ezra 7:10 says he set his heart to study the law, do it and teach its statutes and ordinances. He addressed problems of covenant unfaithfulness, including intermarriage with surrounding peoples (Ezra 9–10); the account reflects the community's ancient identity concerns and should not be used to justify prejudice against ethnic groups today. In Nehemiah 8 Ezra publicly read the law before men, women and all able to understand. Levites explained its meaning. The people wept, but Nehemiah, Ezra and the Levites told them the day was holy and urged celebration and sharing food. The feast of booths was then observed.\n\n7. NEHEMIAH'S REQUEST TO THE KING (NEHEMIAH 1–2)\nNehemiah was cupbearer to King Artaxerxes at Susa. His brother Hanani and others reported that Jerusalem's wall was broken down and its gates burned. Nehemiah wept, fasted, prayed and confessed the people's sins. When the king noticed his sadness, Nehemiah explained Jerusalem's condition. After a brief prayer, he asked permission to go and rebuild the city, letters to governors for safe passage, and timber from Asaph, keeper of the king's forest, for gates and other construction (Nehemiah 2:1–8). He arrived in Jerusalem and secretly inspected the walls by night before announcing his plan. Nehemiah's mission concerned the city's walls, not Cyrus' original temple decree.\n\n8. OPPOSITION TO WALL REBUILDING (NEHEMIAH 2–6)\nSanballat the Horonite and Tobiah the Ammonite mocked the project; Geshem the Arab also opposed it (Nehemiah 2:19). Workers repaired sections of the wall by families and groups. When enemies threatened attack, Nehemiah prayed and posted guards. Some worked while others held weapons; builders carried burdens with one hand and held a weapon in the other, and a trumpeter stayed near Nehemiah (Nehemiah 4). Sanballat and Geshem repeatedly invited Nehemiah to meet at Ono, but he refused, saying he was doing a great work and could not come down (Nehemiah 6:1–4). The wall was completed in 52 days, on the twenty-fifth of Elul (Nehemiah 6:15).\n\n9. NEHEMIAH'S SOCIAL AND RELIGIOUS REFORMS (NEHEMIAH 5; 8–13)\nSome poor Judeans complained that wealthy members were charging interest, taking fields and vineyards, and causing children to enter debt servitude. Nehemiah rebuked the nobles and officials, urged restoration of property and cancellation of the interest demands (Nehemiah 5). He also avoided using his governor's food allowance in a burdensome way. After the wall's completion, the people heard the law, confessed sins and renewed covenant commitments (Nehemiah 8–10). Nehemiah later addressed neglected temple support, Sabbath trading and mixed marriages (Nehemiah 13). The major lesson is that rebuilding a city also required justice and covenant reform.\n\n10. CHRONOLOGY AND COMMON EXAM TRAPS\n597 BCE: Jehoiachin deported. 586 BCE: Jerusalem and first temple destroyed. 539 BCE: Cyrus captured Babylon. About 538 BCE: Cyrus' decree and return. About 515 BCE: second temple completed in Darius' sixth year. Traditionally 458 BCE: Ezra's mission under Artaxerxes. Traditionally 445/444 BCE: Nehemiah's mission and rebuilding of walls. Zerubbabel and Jeshua: altar and temple; Haggai and Zechariah: urged temple work; Ezra: law and teaching; Nehemiah: wall, protection and social reform. Dates for Ezra's chronology are debated by scholars, so biblical king names and chapter facts are more reliable than assuming every textbook date is undisputed.\n\nDEEPER TEACHING: UNDERSTANDING THE EVENTS AND THEIR MEANING\nTHREE DIFFERENT REBUILDING TASKS\nThe restoration story involves rebuilding the altar, rebuilding the temple and rebuilding the city's walls. These are separate achievements. The altar allowed sacrifices to resume before the temple building was finished (Ezra 3). The temple was completed during Darius' reign (Ezra 6). Jerusalem's walls were rebuilt much later under Nehemiah (Nehemiah 2–6). Students often wrongly credit Cyrus with building the wall or Nehemiah with laying the temple foundation. Cyrus authorised return and temple rebuilding; Zerubbabel and Jeshua led early worship restoration; Haggai and Zechariah encouraged the temple builders; Ezra taught the law; Nehemiah organised wall reconstruction and social reforms.\n\nWHY THE TEMPLE FOUNDATION PRODUCED JOY AND TEARS\nEzra 3 describes the people praising God when the foundation was laid, while some older priests, Levites and family heads who had seen the earlier temple wept. Their response is not a contradiction: younger returnees could rejoice in a new beginning while older witnesses remembered what had been lost. This teaches that restoration can produce hope and grief together. The rebuilding was more than construction; it marked the renewal of a community's worship after displacement.\n\nTHE DIFFERENCE BETWEEN OPPOSITION AND INTERNAL INJUSTICE\nSanballat, Tobiah and Geshem threatened or mocked the wall builders. Nehemiah answered by prayer, organised guards and continued working. But Nehemiah 5 describes a different problem inside the community: wealthy Judeans exploited poor neighbours through debts, interest and seizure of property. Nehemiah demanded restoration and fairness. In an exam, 'who opposed the wall' and 'what injustice did Nehemiah correct' require different answers. Faithful leadership confronts external threats AND wrongdoing within the group.\n\nWHAT THE LAW READING ACCOMPLISHED\nIn Nehemiah 8 Ezra read the law aloud while Levites explained it so people could understand. Understanding led to an emotional response, but leaders reminded the community that the day was holy and called them to share food and celebrate. Hearing God's law was meant to shape behaviour, not merely produce tears. Later confession and covenant renewal in Nehemiah 9–10 connect understanding with action. Ezra's special contribution is teaching and applying the law, rather than directing the 52-day wall construction.\n\nREADING THE CHRONOLOGY CORRECTLY\nFirst comes Judah's exile under Babylon. Persia then defeats Babylon and Cyrus permits return. The returned community restores worship and completes the temple under Darius. Ezra's law-centred mission and Nehemiah's wall mission occur in the Persian period under Artaxerxes. Some dates and the exact relationship between Ezra and Nehemiah are debated, so anchor examination answers in the kings and events explicitly named in Ezra and Nehemiah. Never confuse the northern kingdom's Assyrian fall with Judah's Babylonian exile.\n\nWORKED JAMB-STYLE INTERPRETATION QUESTIONS\nExample (i): Why did some older people weep when the new temple foundation was laid?\nA. They had never seen a temple\nB. They remembered the former temple\nC. They opposed all worship\nD. The city wall was already complete\nSolution: Ezra 3:12 specifically says older people who had seen the first house wept. A is the opposite, and C and D are unsupported.\nCorrect answer: B.\n\nExample (ii): Which pairing is correct?\nA. Cyrus — completed Nehemiah's wall\nB. Ezra — rebuilt Jericho\nC. Nehemiah — organised rebuilding Jerusalem's wall\nD. Sanballat — taught Moses' law\nSolution: Nehemiah 2–6 describes his leadership in rebuilding the wall. Cyrus' decree concerned the temple; Ezra taught the law; Sanballat opposed the project.\nCorrect answer: C.\n\nExample (iii): Nehemiah's response to the poor people's complaints in chapter 5 showed that leadership required\nA. ignoring internal exploitation during construction\nB. addressing injustice among the community itself\nC. stopping worship permanently\nD. asking the poor to surrender more land\nSolution: Nehemiah rebuked wealthy lenders and required restitution. The other answers contradict his reforms.\nCorrect answer: B.\n\nExample (iv): What was the role of Haggai and Zechariah in the restoration?\nA. Encouraging temple rebuilding\nB. Writing the decree of Cyrus\nC. Leading the 52-day wall defence\nD. Serving as Persian kings\nSolution: Ezra 5:1–2 links both prophets to renewed temple construction. The other options confuse distinct roles.\nCorrect answer: A.\n\nQUICK UNDERSTANDING CHECK — SPECIFIC TO THIS LESSON\n1. Put altar, temple and city wall rebuilding in the correct sequence.\n2. Distinguish Cyrus, Zerubbabel, Jeshua, Haggai, Zechariah, Ezra and Nehemiah by task.\n3. Explain the difference between opposition from Sanballat and injustice addressed in Nehemiah 5.\n4. Why did Ezra's public reading require Levites to explain the law?\n\nPRACTICE JAMB PAST QUESTIONS\nOpen the lesson's linked JAMB questions. For each question, identify the relevant passage and explain why its correct option is stronger than the alternatives."}},{"id":"forgiveness-generosity","revised":true,"old":{"title":"Forgiveness, reconciliation and generous giving","body":"Christian forgiveness involves responding to wrongdoing without seeking personal revenge and making room for repentance and restored fellowship. It does not mean pretending an offence never happened or exposing another person to continuing harm. Reconciliation is the rebuilding of a relationship; forgiveness and responsible correction should therefore be considered together.\n\nIn 2 Corinthians 2, Paul asks believers to forgive and comfort a person who has already received the community's punishment. The immediate concern is that the offender should not be overwhelmed by excessive sorrow. Paul also urges reaffirming love and warns against Satan gaining an advantage. These are connected reasons within the passage, but a question quoting one sentence may require its particular completion. “Forgive” is not permission to crush a repentant person indefinitely or to abolish every necessary consequence of wrongdoing.\n\nGenerosity means willingly sharing resources to meet others' needs. In 2 Corinthians 8–9, Paul describes support for the saints. The Macedonian churches give freely despite extreme poverty; their generosity is not evidence that they were materially wealthy. Paul's agricultural picture of seed and harvest connects God's provision with resources for further good work. Assistance supplies believers' needs and produces thanksgiving to God. It should not be turned into a promise that every donor will become rich.\n\nRomans 12 distinguishes giving with liberality, leading with diligence and showing mercy with cheerfulness. The qualities belong to different clauses. Philippians thanks the church that entered partnership with Paul in giving and receiving, including support delivered by Epaphroditus. The gift is described as pleasing to God.\n\nIn a Nigerian classroom, helping a pupil who cannot afford essential materials illustrates generosity when it preserves dignity and does not demand praise or favours. The practical aim is relief and fellowship, not using another person's difficulty to advertise one's own importance.\n\nThe harvest in 2 Corinthians 9 is righteousness. Related virtues complete distinct clauses: leading requires diligence and mercy cheerfulness. Christian service meets needs rather than creating a contest for status or profit.\n\nPaul tells Philemon that the good he seeks should be voluntary, not compelled. Receiving Onesimus as a brother matters more than merely extending his working time. God's provision in Philippians is according to his riches in glory, not a calculation limited to how much a person gives.\n\nThessalonica is a city within Macedonia, not a separate region.\n\nJesus teaches that forgiving others accompanies receiving forgiveness from the Father. This challenges personal revenge rather than allowing harm to continue. Philemon's child-and-brother descriptions remain genuine terms in Paul's appeal, whose point is transformed fellowship, not purchase of forgiveness.\n\nPaul asks Philemon to receive Onesimus as he would receive Paul himself."},"new":{"title":"Forgiveness, reconciliation and generous giving","body":"1. INTRODUCTION: TWO LINKED CHRISTIAN RESPONSIBILITIES\nForgiveness means releasing resentment and seeking to restore a relationship after wrongdoing; reconciliation means rebuilding a relationship that has been damaged. Generosity means willingly sharing resources and practical help with those in need. Jesus taught forgiveness and mercy; the early church and Paul's letters show how generosity should be practised. Forgiveness does not require pretending abuse never happened, abandoning safety or refusing accountability.\n\n2. THE LORD'S PRAYER AND FORGIVENESS (MATTHEW 6:9–15)\nIn the Sermon on the Mount Jesus taught his disciples to pray to the Father, honour God's name, seek his kingdom and will, ask for daily bread, forgiveness and deliverance from evil. Matthew 6:12 asks God to forgive debts as the disciples forgive their debtors. Jesus then stresses that people who forgive others will be forgiven, while those who refuse forgiveness should not presume on God's forgiveness (6:14–15). Luke 11:2–4 gives a related form of the prayer. In JAMB questions distinguish forgiveness of others from the petition for daily bread and from the warning against showy prayer.\n\n3. PETER'S QUESTION AND THE UNFORGIVING SERVANT (MATTHEW 18:21–35)\nPeter asked Jesus whether he should forgive a brother or sister up to seven times. Jesus replied not merely seven times but seventy-seven times (some translations render the expression seventy times seven), indicating forgiveness beyond narrow counting. He then told of a king settling accounts. One servant owed 10,000 talents, an enormous debt, and begged for patience. The king released him and forgave the debt. That servant then seized a fellow servant who owed 100 denarii and had him imprisoned when he could not pay. Other servants reported the cruelty. The king condemned the first servant's refusal to show mercy and handed him over to jailers. The contrast is between an immense forgiven debt and a much smaller debt the servant refused to forgive. The story does not teach that the king owed the servant anything.\n\n4. RECONCILIATION BEFORE WORSHIP (MATTHEW 5:21–26)\nJesus deepened the command against murder by warning against destructive anger and contempt. If someone brings a gift to the altar and remembers that a brother or sister has something against them, Jesus says to leave the gift, first be reconciled, and then return to offer it (Matthew 5:23–24). He also urges settling disputes quickly. The point is that religious acts cannot replace the effort to repair wrongdoing. Reconciliation may require confession, restitution, changed behaviour and protection of those harmed.\n\n5. THE PRODIGAL SON (LUKE 15:11–32)\nA younger son asked for his share of the inheritance, travelled to a distant country and wasted it. During a famine he fed pigs and longed to eat their food. He came to himself and decided to return, confess that he had sinned against heaven and his father, and ask to be treated as a hired servant. His father saw him from afar, ran to him, embraced him and ordered the best robe, a ring and sandals. A fattened calf was killed for a feast. The older son was angry because he had served faithfully without receiving such a celebration. The father explained that they should rejoice because the lost son had been found. The parable highlights repentance, mercy and the danger of resentment; it does not say the younger son's wastefulness was good.\n\n6. ZACCHAEUS AND RESTITUTION (LUKE 19:1–10)\nZacchaeus was a chief tax collector in Jericho and was rich. Being short, he climbed a sycamore tree to see Jesus. Jesus called him down and stayed at his house. Some complained that Jesus was visiting a sinner. Zacchaeus declared he would give half his goods to the poor and restore fourfold anything he had obtained by fraud. Jesus announced that salvation had come to the house and said the Son of Man came to seek and save the lost. Restitution means making amends for wrongfully taken property; it shows that repentance can have financial consequences.\n\n7. JESUS' TEACHING ON GENEROSITY (MATTHEW 6; MARK 12; LUKE 10)\nMatthew 6:1–4 warns against giving to be praised publicly; almsgiving should not be a performance. In Mark 12:41–44 Jesus watched people placing money in the temple treasury. Rich people gave large amounts, but a poor widow put in two small copper coins, worth a penny. Jesus said she had given more than the others because she gave from poverty all she had to live on, while others gave from abundance. This passage describes costly generosity; it should not be used to pressure vulnerable people to give away necessities. In Luke 10:25–37 the Good Samaritan treated a wounded traveller, bandaged him, used oil and wine, placed him on his animal, took him to an inn and paid two denarii for his care, promising further payment if needed. Generosity includes practical service beyond one's social group.\n\n8. THE SHARING COMMUNITY IN JERUSALEM (ACTS 2; 4)\nAfter Pentecost the believers devoted themselves to apostolic teaching, fellowship, breaking of bread and prayer. Acts 2:44–45 says they held possessions in common and sold goods to distribute proceeds according to need. Acts 4:32–35 describes believers sharing so that none lacked necessities. Barnabas, a Levite from Cyprus whose name is explained as son of encouragement, sold a field and brought the money to the apostles (Acts 4:36–37). This sharing was motivated by concern for others. The contrasting story of Ananias and Sapphira in Acts 5 condemns lying about the amount donated, not the mere ownership of private property.\n\n9. MACEDONIAN GENEROSITY AND THE COLLECTION (2 CORINTHIANS 8–9)\nPaul encouraged Corinthian believers to complete a collection for Christians in need. He pointed to the Macedonian churches, which gave generously even in severe affliction and extreme poverty (2 Corinthians 8:1–5). He praised Jesus Christ, who though rich became poor for their sake (8:9). Paul sought fairness: the goal was not that others be relieved while Corinthians suffered, but that abundance could supply need (8:13–15). He arranged trustworthy messengers to handle the gift transparently and avoid criticism (8:18–21). In 2 Corinthians 9:6 he compared generous giving to sowing generously; verse 7 teaches that each should give as decided in the heart, not reluctantly or under compulsion, because God loves a cheerful giver. Generosity is willing and responsible, not a competition.\n\n10. PAUL'S INSTRUCTIONS ON SETTING ASIDE GIFTS (1 CORINTHIANS 16:1–4)\nPaul told the Corinthians to follow arrangements also given to churches in Galatia: on the first day of every week each should set something aside in proportion to prosperity, so the collection would not need to begin only when Paul arrived. Approved representatives would carry the gift to Jerusalem, and Paul might accompany them. The instruction emphasises regular planning, accountability and proportional giving. It does not set a single mandatory amount for everyone.\n\n11. PHILEMON, ONESIMUS AND RESTORED RELATIONSHIP\nOnesimus, an enslaved man connected with Philemon, came into contact with Paul during imprisonment and became a Christian. Paul appealed for Philemon to receive Onesimus not merely as a slave but as a beloved brother (Philemon 15–16). Paul offered to pay any debt or wrong charged to Onesimus (verses 18–19). The letter is an appeal for reconciliation and a transformed relationship; it should not be mistaken for a public collection or the story of the prodigal son.\n\n12. DISTINCTIONS FOR EXAM QUESTIONS\nMatthew 18: Peter's question, seventy-seven/seventy-times-seven, 10,000 talents and 100 denarii. Luke 15: lost son, compassionate father, resentful older brother. Luke 19: Zacchaeus, sycamore tree, half to poor and fourfold restitution. Mark 12: widow's two small coins. Luke 10: Samaritan, oil and wine, two denarii. Acts 4: Barnabas' honest gift; Acts 5: Ananias and Sapphira's deception. 2 Corinthians 8–9: Macedonian generosity and cheerful giving. 1 Corinthians 16: planned first-day-of-week collection. Philemon: Onesimus received as a brother.\n\nDEEPER TEACHING: UNDERSTANDING THE EVENTS AND THEIR MEANING\nFORGIVENESS, REPENTANCE, RECONCILIATION AND RESTITUTION\nThese four words are related but not interchangeable. Forgiveness means letting go of the demand for personal revenge and responding with mercy. Repentance means recognising wrongdoing and turning away from it. Reconciliation means repairing a damaged relationship; it may take time and require evidence of changed conduct. Restitution means returning what was wrongly taken or compensating the person harmed. Zacchaeus illustrates restitution through his fourfold pledge; the prodigal son illustrates repentance and the father's mercy; Philemon illustrates an appeal for restored relationship. Forgiveness never requires someone to accept further abuse or abandon safety.\n\nWHY JESUS COMPARES 10,000 TALENTS WITH 100 DENARII\nIn Matthew 18 the first servant's debt of 10,000 talents is intentionally overwhelming, while the second servant's 100 denarii is much smaller. The king cancels an unpayable obligation, yet the forgiven servant refuses mercy to a fellow servant. The point is not that the smaller debt was imaginary or that borrowing is forbidden. It is the shocking inconsistency of receiving mercy while refusing to show it. The order matters: first the king forgives; afterward the servant mistreats his debtor; finally the king holds him accountable.\n\nTHE FATHER AND BOTH SONS IN LUKE 15\nThe younger son wastes his inheritance and returns prepared to confess. The father runs to welcome him and restores him with robe, ring, sandals and celebration. But the story also examines the older son, who resents the welcome and measures his worth by service and reward. The father goes out to him as well and explains why rejoicing is appropriate. Therefore the parable addresses not only the sinner who returns but also the religiously dutiful person who refuses compassion. The fattened calf is a sign of celebration, not payment for the son's wrongdoing.\n\nWHAT MAKES GIVING GENUINELY GENEROUS?\nJesus contrasts the widow's two coins with larger gifts made from abundance: her offering represents costly giving relative to what she has, not a competition over nominal amount. In 2 Corinthians 8–9 Paul praises willingness and proportionate giving, while explicitly saying that the aim is fairness, not hardship for one group so another can be relieved. His insistence on trustworthy messengers shows that handling charitable funds transparently is part of Christian responsibility. 'God loves a cheerful giver' rules out coercion as the ideal attitude.\n\nANANIAS AND SAPPHIRA: WHAT WAS THE OFFENCE?\nActs 5 reports that Ananias and Sapphira sold property but secretly withheld part of the proceeds while presenting their gift as though it were the whole price. Peter states that the property was theirs before sale and the money remained at their disposal afterward (Acts 5:4). Their sin was deliberate deceit, not failure to donate every coin. This distinguishes their action from Barnabas' open-handed gift in Acts 4. A JAMB option saying private property itself was prohibited is therefore misleading.\n\nPAUL AND ONESIMUS: MERCY WITH RESPONSIBILITY\nIn Philemon Paul appeals to Philemon to welcome Onesimus as a beloved brother, offering to repay anything Onesimus owes. Paul does not merely say 'forget the past'; he places his own resources and relationship behind the request. The letter must also be read against the ancient institution of slavery, which it reflects rather than endorsing as a modern ideal. Its central examination theme is Christian brotherhood and reconciliation expressed in concrete action.\n\nWORKED JAMB-STYLE INTERPRETATION QUESTIONS\nExample (i): What is the main fault of the servant forgiven 10,000 talents?\nA. He borrowed money\nB. He refused to show the mercy he had received\nC. He gave money to the poor\nD. He returned to his master too early\nSolution: Matthew 18 contrasts the king's mercy with the servant's harsh treatment of a fellow servant. A is not the moral point; C and D are absent.\nCorrect answer: B.\n\nExample (ii): Zacchaeus' promise to restore fourfold illustrates\nA. restitution following repentance\nB. buying salvation from Jesus\nC. compulsory temple taxation\nD. refusing to acknowledge wrongdoing\nSolution: Luke 19:8 shows practical amends for fraud. Jesus' response does not say forgiveness was purchased; the pledge shows a changed direction.\nCorrect answer: A.\n\nExample (iii): Why were Ananias and Sapphira condemned in Acts 5?\nA. They owned a field\nB. They did not join a collection\nC. They deceived the community about their gift\nD. They gave less than Barnabas\nSolution: Peter says they were free to control their property but lied about the proceeds. A, B and D miss the issue.\nCorrect answer: C.\n\nExample (iv): What principle did Paul emphasise for the Corinthian collection?\nA. Giving must always cause poverty\nB. Giving should be willing, proportionate and responsibly administered\nC. Only wealthy people may give\nD. Donations must be hidden from all accountability\nSolution: 2 Corinthians 8–9 links willingness, fairness and transparent administration. The other options contradict Paul's stated aims.\nCorrect answer: B.\n\nQUICK UNDERSTANDING CHECK — SPECIFIC TO THIS LESSON\n1. Explain the difference between forgiving, reconciling and making restitution.\n2. Why is the older brother important to the meaning of the prodigal-son parable?\n3. Compare the widow's two coins, the Macedonian churches and the Corinthians' planned collection.\n4. What exactly did Peter say Ananias was free to do with his property?\n\nPRACTICE JAMB PAST QUESTIONS\nOpen the lesson's linked JAMB questions. For each question, identify the relevant passage and explain why its correct option is stronger than the alternatives."}},{"id":"family-work-duties","revised":true,"old":{"title":"Family responsibilities, humility and honest work","body":"Christian household teaching connects faith with everyday duties. Duties work in both directions: obedience never licenses a parent or employer to exploit someone, and religious language does not remove responsibility for wrongdoing.\n\nEphesians 6 tells children to obey their parents in the Lord and links honouring father and mother with the commandment's promise of wellbeing and long life. This is a moral instruction and promise within biblical teaching, not a guarantee that every obedient child will escape accident or illness. The same passage warns fathers against provoking their children. Colossians adds the concern that children should not become discouraged. Discipline should guide and protect rather than humiliate or crush confidence.\n\nNarratives about Eli's sons and David's family show the dangers of tolerating serious misconduct. Eli's sons treated the LORD's offerings with contempt. Amnon's assault on Tamar was a grave wrong, followed by his rejection of her; it must not be romanticised as love or blamed on the victim. Absalom's later violence and revolt also reveal the destructive effects of unresolved wrongdoing. A question about parental laxity is not an excuse for any person's deliberate abuse.\n\nHumility means a truthful, unselfish attitude that considers other people rather than asserting superiority. Philippians 2 urges concern for others' interests and presents Christ's self-humbling as the example. First Peter tells believers to humble themselves under God's mighty hand and cast their anxiety on him because he cares for them. Casting anxiety does not mean refusing appropriate help or denying that a real problem exists.\n\nSecond Thessalonians rebukes unwillingness to work and meddling, urging quiet work and earning a living. It does not despise illness or inability to find employment. Colossians calls for wholehearted service as serving the Lord. Diligence means honest work even without supervision, while authorities must treat workers justly. In a Nigerian school or workplace, both duties matter.\n\nHumility includes counting others more significant than oneself. Paul's willingness to boast of weakness challenges status-seeking, while Christ's taking human form and a servant's place provides the model of self-humbling.\n\nPhilippians presents Christ's humility: taking a servant's form and obeying to death, even death on a cross. Exaltation follows this self-humbling. Faith should produce humility and service rather than superiority.\n\nAsa removes his queen mother Maacah from her position over idolatry: family accountability, not disciplining his own child. David positively instructs Solomon to obey God; his other failures do not erase that specific parental action.\n\nMatthew's divorce sayings contain an exception concerning sexual immorality, not mere quarrelling or laziness. Translations and Christian traditions discuss its precise application differently; a broad word such as “perfidy” needs explanation rather than unrestricted permission for divorce. Paul works rather than eating others' bread without paying."},"new":{"title":"Family responsibilities, humility and honest work","body":"1. INTRODUCTION: FAITH AT HOME AND AT WORK\nChristian family responsibility means showing love, care, respect, discipline and fairness in relationships. Honest work means fulfilling duties diligently without cheating, exploitation or deliberate idleness. New Testament instructions were given to households in the ancient Roman world, so students should understand the historical setting and distinguish a passage's wording from a claim that mistreatment is acceptable today.\n\n2. CHILDREN AND PARENTS (EPHESIANS 6:1–4)\nPaul tells children to obey their parents 'in the Lord, for this is right.' He quotes the command to honour father and mother, calling it the first commandment with a promise: 'that it may go well with you and that you may live long in the land' (Ephesians 6:2–3). Honour includes respect, gratitude and care, not just following orders. Paul also addresses fathers: they must not provoke their children to anger, but bring them up in the discipline and instruction of the Lord. This is a two-sided duty: children should respect parents, while parents must avoid harsh or unjust treatment.\n\n3. CHILDREN AND FATHERS (COLOSSIANS 3:20–21)\nColossians 3:20 says children should obey their parents in everything, 'for this pleases the Lord.' The next verse warns fathers not to provoke their children, lest they become discouraged. Compare the wording: Ephesians 6:1 says 'in the Lord' and cites the commandment with a promise; Colossians 3:20 says 'this pleases the Lord.' Neither passage gives permission for abuse or commands to commit wrongdoing. Good parenting includes correction delivered with care, consistency and encouragement.\n\n4. HUSBANDS AND WIVES (EPHESIANS 5:21–33)\nThe discussion begins with believers submitting to one another out of reverence for Christ (5:21). Wives are addressed concerning their relationship with husbands; husbands receive the demanding command to love their wives as Christ loved the church and gave himself up for her (5:25). Husbands should love their wives as their own bodies; 'the two shall become one flesh' recalls Genesis 2:24. Paul's emphasis on Christ's sacrificial love opposes selfish domination. In an exam, distinguish the husband's command to love sacrificially from the instruction to children to obey their parents.\n\n5. HOUSEHOLD CONDUCT (COLOSSIANS 3:18–25; 4:1)\nPaul addresses wives, husbands, children, fathers, enslaved workers and masters in the household setting of his time. Husbands are told to love their wives and not treat them harshly (3:19). Enslaved workers are told to work sincerely, 'as for the Lord and not for men' (3:23), and masters are instructed to treat them justly and fairly, knowing they too have a Master in heaven (4:1). The text describes an ancient social system; it does not justify slavery today. The wider moral principles include sincerity, fairness and accountability.\n\n6. WORKING QUIETLY AND RESPONSIBLY (2 THESSALONIANS 3:6–15)\nSome Thessalonians were living in idleness, apparently relying on others while interfering in their affairs. Paul reminded the church that he and his companions worked night and day to avoid burdening them, even though they had a right to support. He gave the rule: 'If anyone is not willing to work, let him not eat' (3:10). This addresses unwillingness, not people unable to work because of illness, disability or unemployment. Paul urges such people to work quietly and earn their living, and tells the community not to grow weary in doing good. A persistently disobedient member should be warned as a brother, not treated as an enemy (3:15).\n\n7. WORK AND SHARING (EPHESIANS 4:25–32)\nPaul says believers should put away falsehood and speak truthfully, because they belong to one another. Anger must not become an excuse for sin or be nursed indefinitely. A former thief should stop stealing and instead do honest work with their hands, so that they have something to share with a person in need (4:28). This verse connects labour with generosity: the goal is not only personal income but helping others. Paul also forbids corrupt speech and calls for kindness, tenderheartedness and forgiveness as God forgave in Christ (4:29–32).\n\n8. HUMILITY AND CHRIST'S EXAMPLE (PHILIPPIANS 2:1–11)\nPaul urges believers to have the same love, be united in spirit and do nothing from selfish ambition or conceit. In humility they should count others significant and consider others' interests as well as their own. He presents Jesus as the supreme example: although in the form of God, Christ took the form of a servant, humbled himself and became obedient to death on a cross. Therefore God highly exalted him and bestowed the name above every name. Humility is not denying one's abilities; it means using them without pride or exploitation.\n\n9. RESPONSIBILITY FOR DEPENDANTS (1 TIMOTHY 5:3–16)\nPaul instructs the church to honour widows who are truly in need. If a widow has children or grandchildren, they should learn to show godliness by caring for their own family and repaying their parents (5:4). Verse 8 warns strongly against neglecting one's relatives, especially household members. The passage also distinguishes people whose families can help them from those who depend on the church's support. The principle is practical care for vulnerable relatives, not simply saying prayers while refusing assistance.\n\n10. JESUS' EXAMPLE OF SERVICE (JOHN 13:1–17)\nDuring a meal Jesus rose, laid aside his outer garments, wrapped a towel around himself, poured water into a basin and washed the disciples' feet. Peter initially objected; Jesus explained that Peter needed to receive this act and that the disciples should learn from it. He said, 'If I then, your Lord and Teacher, have washed your feet, you also ought to wash one another's feet' (13:14). Foot washing was a lowly service in that culture. The lesson is humble practical care rather than a competition for rank.\n\n11. EXAM DISTINCTIONS\nEphesians 6: children honour parents; fathers do not provoke. Colossians 3: children obey because it pleases the Lord; fathers avoid discouraging them. Ephesians 5: husbands love sacrificially. 2 Thessalonians 3: unwillingness to work and disorderly idleness. Ephesians 4:28: honest work provides something to share. Philippians 2: Christ's humility. 1 Timothy 5: care for relatives and widows. John 13: Jesus washed disciples' feet as an example.\n\nDEEPER GUIDED TEACHING — EXPLANATION AND INTERPRETATION\nWHY FAMILY DUTIES ARE RECIPROCAL, NOT ONE-SIDED\nA reciprocal duty is an obligation that applies to both sides of a relationship, although the duties may differ. In Ephesians 6:1–4 children are asked to honour and obey, while fathers are warned against provoking them. Colossians 3:20–21 makes the same pairing but highlights discouragement. A JAMB question may quote only the child's duty and offer a misleading answer suggesting parents have no responsibilities. The passage itself prevents that conclusion. In Ephesians 5, Paul's command to husbands is measured against Christ's sacrificial love, not against a licence to command selfishly. Read each instruction alongside the next one to see the balance.\n\nHONOUR, OBEDIENCE AND THE LIMITS OF AUTHORITY\nHonour is a lasting attitude of respect and practical care, which can continue after a child becomes an adult. Obedience is following a legitimate instruction. The wording 'in the Lord' (Ephesians 6:1) matters: loyalty to God is not suspended by another person's command. If an instruction requires wrongdoing, abuse or harm, the passage must not be used to demand compliance. Paul connects honouring parents with a commandment from Exodus 20:12 and Deuteronomy 5:16. His reference to a promise is part of his scriptural argument, not a guarantee that every obedient child will live to a fixed age.\n\nWORK AS SERVICE AND WORK AS SHARING\nThree passages develop the idea of work differently. In Colossians 3:23, working 'heartily, as for the Lord' concerns sincerity rather than pretending that employers cannot be criticised. In Ephesians 4:28, a former thief is told to work honestly so that there will be something to share: labour serves the wider community. In 2 Thessalonians 3:10–12, Paul addresses people who refuse to work while interfering in others' affairs. The phrase 'not willing' is crucial; a person who cannot find employment or cannot work is not the same as one who deliberately refuses. The church is still told not to grow weary in doing good (3:13).\n\nWHAT JESUS' FOOT-WASHING ACTUALLY TEACHES\nIn John 13 Jesus performs a task normally associated with a servant, despite being acknowledged as Lord and Teacher. Peter initially refuses because the reversal of status shocks him. Jesus insists that Peter must accept his service before learning to imitate it. The lesson has two directions: leaders must serve rather than exploit, and followers must be willing to receive humble help. A student should not answer that Jesus washed their feet merely to demonstrate physical cleanliness; the action is a visible lesson in humility and mutual care.\n\nCOMPARING FAMILY SUPPORT WITH CHURCH SUPPORT\nFirst Timothy 5 distinguishes widows who have relatives able to care for them from those who have no such support. The instruction to children and grandchildren to repay parents through care is practical religion. The church's resources can then be directed toward people who are truly alone. This does not mean families must conceal neglect or that communities should abandon those whose relatives refuse help. The passage's exam point is responsibility for dependants, especially widows, rather than a blanket rejection of charitable assistance.\n\nWORKED JAMB-STYLE INTERPRETATION QUESTIONS\nExample (i): A father who insists on obedience but constantly humiliates his child has ignored which part of Paul's teaching?\nA. Children must never ask questions\nB. Parents must not provoke or discourage children\nC. Parents are exempt from Christian conduct\nD. Children are responsible for all household conflict\nSolution: Ephesians 6:4 and Colossians 3:21 address the parent's conduct. A, C and D remove the reciprocal responsibility Paul explicitly teaches.\nCorrect answer: B.\n\nExample (ii): Why does Paul tell a former thief to work in Ephesians 4:28?\nA. To become richer than neighbours\nB. To avoid all contact with the poor\nC. To earn honestly and have something to share\nD. To prove that salvation can be purchased\nSolution: Paul gives both purposes: stop stealing and work so as to share with the needy. The other options distort or contradict the verse.\nCorrect answer: C.\n\nExample (iii): Which statement best explains 2 Thessalonians 3:10?\nA. Anyone who is sick must be denied food\nB. The church must never help anyone\nC. Deliberate refusal to work is different from inability to work\nD. Working for wages is sinful\nSolution: The verse concerns people unwilling to work, while 3:13 still calls for doing good. A and B erase that distinction; D reverses Paul's example.\nCorrect answer: C.\n\nExample (iv): The main reason Jesus washed the disciples' feet was to teach\nA. the importance of Jerusalem's water supply\nB. that only Peter could lead the disciples\nC. humble service by those who hold authority\nD. that the disciples had failed to bathe\nSolution: John 13:12–17 makes Jesus' act an example for his followers. The other answers are not the interpretation Jesus gives.\nCorrect answer: C.\n\nExample (v): According to 1 Timothy 5, why should relatives care for dependent family members?\nA. To keep all widows away from church\nB. Because practical care is part of godliness and family responsibility\nC. Because prayer is forbidden for widows\nD. Because only rich relatives are Christians\nSolution: First Timothy 5:4 and 5:8 treat care for family as a serious religious duty. The distractors misstate the chapter.\nCorrect answer: B.\n\nQUICK UNDERSTANDING CHECK — LESSON-SPECIFIC\n1. Contrast honouring a parent with obeying a particular instruction. Why does the distinction matter?\n2. Explain the difference between Colossians 3:23, Ephesians 4:28 and 2 Thessalonians 3:10.\n3. Why did Peter object when Jesus washed his feet, and what did Jesus teach afterward?\n4. Give two responsibilities that Paul places on parents or husbands rather than on children or wives.\n\nPRACTICE JAMB PAST QUESTIONS\nUse the lesson's linked past questions. Identify the exact passage for each answer and explain why close alternatives are less accurate."}},{"id":"sermon-mount","revised":true,"old":{"title":"The Sermon on the Mount: character and practical obedience","body":"The Sermon on the Mount is Matthew's extended account of Jesus' teaching in chapters 5–7. It begins with the Beatitudes, statements identifying those called blessed, and ends with the comparison of wise and foolish builders. Its teaching joins inward attitudes with outward conduct; knowing the words without practising them misses the closing lesson.\n\n“Poor in spirit” describes humble dependence on God rather than pride in one's spiritual status. The promise attached to it is the kingdom of heaven. Those who mourn are promised comfort; the meek will inherit the earth; those who hunger and thirst for righteousness will be satisfied. The merciful receive mercy, the pure in heart see God, and peacemakers are called God's children. Keep each description with its particular promise instead of exchanging familiar phrases between them.\n\nThose persecuted for righteousness are also promised the kingdom. Jesus tells followers insulted on his account to rejoice because their reward in heaven is great. This does not say suffering is pleasant or that every punishment proves innocence. Persecution for doing right must be distinguished from deserved consequences for harming others.\n\nJesus deepens the demand for right conduct by addressing anger, reconciliation, faithfulness and truthful speech. Love extends beyond friends to enemies: followers are to love them and pray for those who persecute them. Turning the other cheek concerns refusal of personal retaliation; it does not command someone to arrange further abuse or prevent lawful protection. A Nigerian classroom example is refusing to spread a humiliating rumour about a pupil who insulted you while seeking a responsible resolution.\n\nGiving, prayer and fasting must not become public performances designed to win admiration. The Lord's Prayer joins honouring God with daily needs, forgiveness and deliverance from evil. The teaching against anxious preoccupation directs attention to God's kingdom and righteousness rather than making wealth the measure of a person's worth.\n\nFinally, the wise builder hears Jesus' words and acts on them, while the foolish builder hears without practising. The contrast concerns obedience, not intelligence, expensive construction or merely attending religious meetings. Character is shown in the response when the teaching becomes demanding.\n\nJesus calls his disciples the salt of the earth. Earthly treasures are vulnerable to moth, decay and theft. His teaching against retaliation redirects the eye-for-eye formula towards non-retaliation and enemy-love rather than personal revenge.\n\n“Blessed are the wise” is not a stated Beatitude. Prayer must avoid empty repetition and public performance. Love and prayer for enemies accompany refusal of retaliation; related commands must still be distinguished when completing a particular quotation."},"new":{"title":"The Sermon on the Mount: character and practical obedience","body":"1. SETTING AND PURPOSE (MATTHEW 5–7)\nMatthew places the Sermon on the Mount after Jesus began preaching and calling disciples. Seeing the crowds, Jesus went up a mountain, sat down and taught his disciples (Matthew 5:1–2). The sermon describes the character of citizens of God's kingdom and how their obedience should go deeper than outward religious performance. Luke 6:17–49 contains a related sermon in a level place, with similarities and differences; do not assume every detail in Luke's account appears in Matthew.\n\n2. THE BEATITUDES (MATTHEW 5:3–12)\nA beatitude declares someone blessed or fortunate in God's sight. Jesus says the poor in spirit receive the kingdom of heaven; those who mourn will be comforted; the meek will inherit the earth; those who hunger and thirst for righteousness will be filled; the merciful will receive mercy; the pure in heart will see God; peacemakers will be called children of God; and those persecuted for righteousness have the kingdom of heaven. He also blesses disciples insulted or persecuted on his account, telling them to rejoice because their reward is great in heaven. 'Poor in spirit' refers to humble dependence on God, not merely a lack of money. 'Peacemakers' actively seek peace rather than simply avoiding disagreements.\n\n3. SALT AND LIGHT (MATTHEW 5:13–16)\nJesus calls his followers the salt of the earth and light of the world. Salt illustrates a distinctive beneficial influence; if it loses its saltiness it is no longer useful for that purpose. A city set on a hill cannot be hidden. People do not light a lamp and place it under a basket but on a stand to give light. Likewise disciples should let their light shine so that people see their good works and give glory to their Father in heaven. The point is not showing off for personal praise: Matthew 6 later warns against doing righteous acts simply to be seen.\n\n4. JESUS AND THE LAW (MATTHEW 5:17–20)\nJesus says he came not to abolish the Law or the Prophets but to fulfil them. He stresses the seriousness of God's commands and says his followers' righteousness must exceed that of the scribes and Pharisees. In the examples that follow, he addresses not only outward actions but also inner attitudes and motives. In JAMB, do not select an answer claiming Jesus simply rejected every command of the Old Testament.\n\n5. ANGER, RECONCILIATION AND LUST (MATTHEW 5:21–30)\nJesus recalls the command against murder, then warns against destructive anger and contempt. A worshipper who remembers that a brother or sister has something against them should first seek reconciliation before offering a gift at the altar (5:23–24). Jesus also explains that deliberately looking at someone with lust is morally serious even before an outward act occurs. His strong images about removing an eye or hand are warnings to take sin seriously, not instructions for literal self-injury.\n\n6. TRUTHFUL SPEECH AND NON-RETALIATION (MATTHEW 5:33–42)\nJesus warns against elaborate oath-making and says, 'Let your word be Yes, Yes or No, No' (5:37). He challenges personal revenge through examples such as turning the other cheek, giving a cloak, going a second mile and giving to someone in need. These teachings concern freedom from vindictiveness and generosity; they should not be used to deny protection to someone facing violence. Jesus calls disciples to a higher standard than merely doing the minimum demanded.\n\n7. LOVE YOUR ENEMIES (MATTHEW 5:43–48)\nJesus tells disciples to love enemies and pray for those who persecute them, so they may reflect the Father's goodness. God makes the sun rise on the evil and the good and sends rain on the just and unjust. Loving only those who love you is not distinctive; even tax collectors do that. Jesus concludes by calling disciples to be perfect as their heavenly Father is perfect, pointing to mature, wholehearted love rather than claiming humans never make mistakes.\n\n8. GIVING, PRAYER AND FASTING WITHOUT SHOW (MATTHEW 6:1–18)\nJesus warns against performing religious acts to receive human applause. When giving alms, disciples should not announce it with trumpets; the Father sees what is done in secret. When praying, they should avoid ostentatious public display and empty repetition. Jesus teaches the Lord's Prayer: honour God's name, seek his kingdom and will, ask for daily bread, forgiveness and deliverance from evil (6:9–13). He also warns against gloomy public displays of fasting; believers should wash their faces and appear normal. The shared principle is sincere devotion rather than religious performance.\n\n9. TREASURE, MONEY AND WORRY (MATTHEW 6:19–34)\nJesus warns against storing up treasure only on earth, where moth, rust and thieves destroy, and encourages treasure in heaven. 'Where your treasure is, there your heart will be also' (6:21). He says no one can serve two masters: one cannot serve God and money (6:24). Jesus points to birds that do not sow or reap yet are fed, and lilies that do not toil or spin yet are beautifully clothed. He urges disciples to seek first God's kingdom and righteousness (6:33) rather than live under consuming anxiety about food and clothing. This teaching encourages trust and priorities, not irresponsibility or refusal to work.\n\n10. JUDGING OTHERS AND THE GOLDEN RULE (MATTHEW 7:1–12)\nJesus says, 'Do not judge, so that you may not be judged,' warning against hypocritical condemnation. The picture of a person noticing a speck in a neighbour's eye while ignoring a log in their own teaches self-examination before correcting others. He also says to ask, seek and knock, trusting the Father's goodness. Matthew 7:12 gives the Golden Rule: do to others what you would have them do to you, summarising the Law and the Prophets. The passage does not forbid every fair assessment of conduct; it condemns hypocrisy.\n\n11. TWO WAYS, FALSE PROPHETS AND THE BUILDERS (MATTHEW 7:13–27)\nJesus contrasts a narrow gate and difficult way leading to life with a wide gate and broad way leading to destruction. He warns that false prophets may appear in sheep's clothing but be inwardly ravenous wolves. They are recognised by their fruits, just as good trees bear good fruit. Not everyone who calls him 'Lord, Lord' enters the kingdom, but those who do the Father's will. He concludes with the wise builder who hears and does his words and builds on rock; rain, floods and winds strike, but the house stands. The foolish builder hears without acting and builds on sand; the house falls with a great crash. Hearing AND obeying is the decisive distinction.\n\n12. EXAM DISTINCTIONS\nMatthew 5: Beatitudes, salt/light, anger, truthful speech, enemy-love. Matthew 6: giving, prayer, fasting, money, anxiety and seeking the kingdom first. Matthew 7: judging, Golden Rule, narrow gate, false prophets and wise/foolish builders. Beatitude pairings must be exact: merciful → mercy; pure in heart → see God; peacemakers → called children of God; meek → inherit earth; mourners → comfort.\n\nDEEPER GUIDED TEACHING — EXPLANATION AND INTERPRETATION\nHOW TO READ THE SERMON: INNER MOTIVE AND OUTWARD ACTION\nMatthew 5–7 is not a collection of unrelated sayings. Jesus moves from the character of kingdom citizens (Beatitudes), to their influence (salt and light), to righteousness that reaches the heart (anger, desire, truthfulness, revenge and enemy-love), to sincere worship (giving, prayer and fasting), to priorities (treasure and anxiety), and finally to discernment and obedient action (two ways and two builders). The repeated question is not only 'What did the person do?' but also 'Why and with what attitude?' A visible good deed done to win applause can conflict with the teaching of Matthew 6 even though Matthew 5 calls for visible good works that glorify God.\n\nWHY SALT AND LIGHT DO NOT CONTRADICT SECRET GIVING\nMatthew 5:16 encourages good works to be seen so others glorify the Father. Matthew 6:1 warns against practising righteousness in order to be noticed and praised. The difference is purpose: making God's goodness visible versus advertising one's own holiness. A student who sees the words 'seen by others' in both passages may mistakenly call them contradictory. The key is the audience's response and the worshipper's motive. Salt also suggests distinctive usefulness; light suggests an influence that should not be hidden.\n\nTHE BEATITUDES: MATCH CONDITION WITH PROMISE\nEach Beatitude pairs a condition with a promise. The poor in spirit receive the kingdom of heaven; mourners are comforted; the meek inherit the earth; those hungry for righteousness are filled; the merciful receive mercy; the pure in heart see God; peacemakers are called children of God; and the persecuted for righteousness receive the kingdom. These are not interchangeable rewards. 'Meek' does not mean cowardly: it suggests strength without arrogance. 'Poor in spirit' concerns humble dependence on God, not a command to remain materially poor. 'Hunger and thirst' are metaphors for intense longing for righteousness, not a fasting instruction.\n\nANGER, RECONCILIATION AND THE HEART OF THE LAW\nJesus' teaching on murder goes beyond the physical act to contempt and hostility. When a worshipper remembers that someone has a grievance against them, reconciliation should be sought before presenting a gift at the altar (Matthew 5:23–24). The order matters: religious ceremony cannot excuse mistreatment of others. Jesus' words about lust likewise address intentional desire, not only an outward act. His striking images of removing an eye or hand are forceful moral warnings, not instructions for literal bodily harm.\n\nENEMY-LOVE IS DIFFERENT FROM APPROVING WRONGDOING\nIn Matthew 5:43–48 Jesus teaches love for enemies and prayer for persecutors. The reason he gives is God's generosity: sun and rain reach both good and evil people. Love here means refusing vindictiveness and seeking another person's good; it does not require pretending harmful behaviour is acceptable or abandoning protection. Similarly, 'turn the other cheek' challenges personal retaliation, not the obligation to protect vulnerable people. A correct JAMB interpretation should identify the principle rather than a careless extreme.\n\nPRAYER, FORGIVENESS AND FASTING AS ONE THEME\nJesus groups almsgiving, prayer and fasting in Matthew 6 because all three can become performances for public admiration. He tells believers to give without sounding a trumpet, pray without theatrical display or empty repetition, and fast without making their appearance miserable. The Lord's Prayer then places God's name, kingdom and will before personal requests, followed by daily provision, forgiveness and deliverance. Matthew 6:14–15 highlights the connection between receiving forgiveness and forgiving others. The common theme is sincere relationship with God rather than impressive religious appearance.\n\nANXIETY, PLANNING AND TRUST\nJesus points to birds and lilies to challenge consuming worry over survival, then says to seek God's kingdom and righteousness first (Matthew 6:33). This does not forbid sensible work or planning; it challenges making material security the highest loyalty. Matthew 6:24 says one cannot serve both God and money as competing masters. The 'eye as lamp of the body' (6:22–23) occurs in this discussion of priorities. The instruction not to worry about tomorrow is an invitation to faithful daily living, not a prediction that no believer will face hardship.\n\nJUDGEMENT, DISCERNMENT AND THE FINAL WARNING\nMatthew 7 begins with the speck and log: correct your own hypocrisy before correcting another. Later Jesus warns about false prophets recognised by their fruits, so the sermon does not ban every moral judgement. The Golden Rule (7:12) directs active care for others. The narrow gate and the wise builder then demand choices and action. The rock represents hearing AND doing Jesus' teaching; sand represents hearing without obedience. Merely saying 'Lord, Lord' is insufficient when conduct contradicts God's will.\n\nWORKED JAMB-STYLE INTERPRETATION QUESTIONS\nExample (i): Why can Matthew 5:16 and Matthew 6:1 both be true?\nA. Good deeds are forbidden in public\nB. Jesus changes his mind about charity\nC. One seeks God's glory while the other warns against seeking human applause\nD. Only the disciples may give publicly\nSolution: Matthew 5 focuses on glory to the Father; Matthew 6 warns against self-advertisement. The other options ignore the stated motives.\nCorrect answer: C.\n\nExample (ii): A person who prays loudly solely to impress a crowd most directly violates\nA. the instruction to be salt\nB. Jesus' teaching against showy prayer\nC. the command to build on rock\nD. the blessing on peacemakers\nSolution: Matthew 6:5 warns against praying for public praise. The other passages concern different subjects.\nCorrect answer: B.\n\nExample (iii): Which pairing from the Beatitudes is correct?\nA. Meek — shall see God\nB. Pure in heart — shall inherit the earth\nC. Peacemakers — shall be called children of God\nD. Merciful — shall be comforted\nSolution: Matthew 5:9 pairs peacemakers with children of God. The other answers exchange promises belonging to other Beatitudes.\nCorrect answer: C.\n\nExample (iv): What is the difference between the wise and foolish builders?\nA. Only the wise heard Jesus' words\nB. The wise heard and acted, while the foolish heard without acting\nC. The foolish used expensive materials\nD. The wise avoided all storms\nSolution: Matthew 7:24–27 explicitly contrasts acting on Jesus' words with hearing without doing. Both houses encounter storms.\nCorrect answer: B.\n\nExample (v): Why does Jesus mention a log in one's own eye?\nA. To prohibit all correction of wrongdoing\nB. To teach self-examination before judging others\nC. To recommend medical treatment\nD. To say other people's faults are imaginary\nSolution: Matthew 7:3–5 condemns hypocritical judgement and requires dealing with one's own fault first. It does not deny discernment.\nCorrect answer: B.\n\nQUICK UNDERSTANDING CHECK — LESSON-SPECIFIC\n1. Explain the difference between visible good works and showy religious behaviour.\n2. Match all eight Beatitudes with their exact promises without looking.\n3. How does Jesus connect reconciliation with worship in Matthew 5?\n4. Why is hearing Jesus' teaching insufficient in the parable of the builders?\n\nPRACTICE JAMB PAST QUESTIONS\nUse the lesson's linked past questions. Identify the exact passage for each answer and explain why close alternatives are less accurate."}},{"id":"birth-preparation","revised":true,"old":{"title":"Birth narratives, John the Baptist and preparation for Jesus' ministry","body":"The Gospel birth narratives present Jesus' coming as God's initiative and connect it with earlier scriptural promises. Matthew applies the name Immanuel, meaning God with us, to Jesus' birth. Luke gives connected announcements concerning John the Baptist and Jesus, but the parents, angelic messages and responses must be distinguished.\n\nZechariah is serving as a priest at the altar of incense when Gabriel appears. He is troubled and afraid. The angel announces that Elizabeth will bear John, who will prepare people for the Lord in Elijah's spirit and power. John will be filled with the Holy Spirit from the womb. Zechariah questions the promise because he and Elizabeth are old. His inability to speak is connected with not believing the angel's words, not simply with old age or a disagreement with his wife.\n\nWhen Gabriel appears to Mary, Elizabeth is in her sixth month of pregnancy. Mary is told that her child will be called Son of the Most High. Luke later describes angels announcing good news to shepherds and praising God. Learning these connected events should not turn into exchanging the names of Mary and Elizabeth or assuming both pregnancies are announced at the same time.\n\nJohn's later ministry calls people to repentance and baptism for forgiveness of sins. Repentance is turning from wrongdoing towards God; it requires changed conduct rather than confidence in ancestry alone. John's role is preparation, not a claim to be the Messiah. Jesus' baptism in Matthew is explained as fulfilling all righteousness, rather than confessing personal wrongdoing.\n\nJesus' temptation follows his baptism. The accounts show challenges involving bread, protection and authority, with Jesus refusing to misuse sonship, test God or worship another. Matthew and Luke order the temptations differently: the Temple challenge is Matthew's second and Luke's third. The offer of the world's kingdoms therefore cannot be placed in the same numbered position in both accounts.\n\nIn an examination, identify the Gospel, speaker and immediate context before choosing a familiar religious phrase. In everyday conduct, the temptation narratives encourage faithful obedience rather than treating power, public attention or material gain as reasons to abandon what is right.\n\nLuke's promise turns fathers' hearts towards children, not the reverse. Worship and service belong to God alone. The temptation is distinct from the later ascension or public commission. Matthew's wise men follow the star; Herod is troubled by news of the child's birth. Luke links John's promised birth with Zechariah's answered prayer.\n\nThe Magi are learned eastern visitors associated with interpreting stars; Matthew does not name their number or describe modern astronomers.\n\nJesus answers the first bread challenge with Scripture: human life depends not on bread alone but on God's word."},"new":{"title":"Birth narratives, John the Baptist and preparation for Jesus' ministry","body":"1. HISTORICAL SETTING AND TWO GOSPEL ACCOUNTS\nMatthew 1–2 and Luke 1–2 tell Jesus' birth and early-life events with different emphases. Matthew focuses on Joseph, dreams, wise men, Herod and the flight to Egypt. Luke focuses on Zechariah, Elizabeth, Mary, shepherds, presentation at the temple and Jesus at age twelve. Both connect Jesus' birth with Bethlehem and his upbringing with Nazareth. Mark begins with John the Baptist's ministry, while John's Gospel opens with the Word becoming flesh rather than a detailed infancy narrative.\n\n2. GABRIEL APPEARS TO ZECHARIAH (LUKE 1:5–25)\nIn the days of Herod king of Judea, Zechariah was a priest of the division of Abijah, married to Elizabeth, a descendant of Aaron. They were righteous but had no child, and both were advanced in years. While Zechariah burned incense in the temple, the angel Gabriel appeared at the right side of the altar of incense. Gabriel announced that Elizabeth would bear a son named John. John would be great before the Lord, drink no wine or strong drink, be filled with the Holy Spirit from his mother's womb and turn many Israelites to God in the spirit and power of Elijah. Zechariah questioned how this could happen because of their age. Gabriel said he would be unable to speak until the promise was fulfilled because he did not believe the message. The people outside noticed his delay, and he emerged unable to speak.\n\n3. GABRIEL APPEARS TO MARY (LUKE 1:26–38)\nIn the sixth month of Elizabeth's pregnancy, Gabriel went to Nazareth in Galilee to Mary, a virgin engaged to Joseph of David's house. Gabriel greeted her as favoured and said she would conceive and bear a son named Jesus. He would be called Son of the Most High, receive the throne of David and reign over Jacob's house forever. Mary asked how this could be since she was a virgin. Gabriel said the Holy Spirit would come upon her and the power of the Most High overshadow her; the child would be called holy, Son of God. Gabriel mentioned Elizabeth's pregnancy as a sign and declared that nothing would be impossible with God. Mary responded, 'Here am I, the servant of the Lord; let it be with me according to your word.' Unlike Zechariah's response, Mary's question was followed by willing acceptance.\n\n4. MARY VISITS ELIZABETH AND ZECHARIAH'S SONG (LUKE 1:39–80)\nMary visited Elizabeth in a town in Judah's hill country. When Mary greeted her, the baby leaped in Elizabeth's womb, and Elizabeth, filled with the Holy Spirit, blessed Mary and the fruit of her womb. Mary responded with praise commonly called the Magnificat (Luke 1:46–55), celebrating God's mercy and his lifting of the lowly. Mary stayed about three months before returning home. When Elizabeth gave birth, neighbours expected the child to be named after his father. Elizabeth insisted on John. Zechariah wrote, 'His name is John,' and immediately regained speech. Filled with the Holy Spirit, he praised God in a song called the Benedictus (1:68–79), speaking of salvation and John's role in preparing the Lord's ways. John grew and lived in the wilderness until his public appearance.\n\n5. JOSEPH'S DREAM AND JESUS' NAME (MATTHEW 1:18–25)\nMary was found to be pregnant before she and Joseph lived together. Joseph, described as righteous, planned to dismiss her quietly rather than expose her to public disgrace. An angel of the Lord appeared to him in a dream and said not to fear taking Mary as his wife, because the child was conceived from the Holy Spirit. Joseph was instructed to name the child Jesus, 'for he will save his people from their sins' (Matthew 1:21). Matthew connects this with Isaiah's prophecy about Immanuel, meaning 'God with us.' Joseph obeyed and named the child Jesus. Matthew's angel appears to Joseph in a dream; Luke's Gabriel speaks directly to Mary.\n\n6. BETHLEHEM, SHEPHERDS AND THE MANGER (LUKE 2:1–20)\nLuke associates the birth with a decree of Caesar Augustus for registration, when Quirinius was governor of Syria. Joseph went from Nazareth to Bethlehem, David's city, with Mary. Jesus was born and laid in a manger because there was no place for them in the lodging place. Shepherds were in nearby fields watching their flock at night. An angel announced good news of great joy: a Saviour, Christ the Lord, had been born in David's city. The sign was a baby wrapped in bands of cloth and lying in a manger. A multitude of heavenly host praised God. The shepherds went to Bethlehem, found Mary, Joseph and the baby, and spread the message. Luke does not say there were three shepherds.\n\n7. CIRCUMCISION, PRESENTATION AND SIMEON (LUKE 2:21–40)\nOn the eighth day the child was circumcised and named Jesus, as the angel had instructed. Mary and Joseph later brought him to Jerusalem for the purification rites and to present him to the Lord, offering a pair of turtledoves or two young pigeons (Luke 2:24). Simeon, a righteous and devout man awaiting Israel's consolation, had been told by the Holy Spirit he would see the Lord's Messiah before death. Taking Jesus in his arms, he praised God and described him as salvation, a light for revelation to Gentiles and glory to Israel. He also warned Mary that a sword would pierce her own soul. Anna, a prophetess, daughter of Phanuel of the tribe of Asher, gave thanks and spoke about the child to those awaiting Jerusalem's redemption.\n\n8. THE WISE MEN, HEROD AND FLIGHT TO EGYPT (MATTHEW 2)\nWise men from the East came to Jerusalem asking about the one born king of the Jews because they had seen his star. King Herod was troubled and consulted chief priests and scribes, who identified Bethlehem from prophecy. Herod secretly asked when the star appeared and told the wise men to report back. The star guided them to the child, and they offered gold, frankincense and myrrh. Warned in a dream not to return to Herod, they went home another way. An angel then told Joseph in a dream to take Mary and Jesus to Egypt because Herod intended to kill the child. Herod ordered the killing of boys aged two years or under in Bethlehem and its region, according to the time learned from the wise men. After Herod's death, Joseph returned but, fearing Archelaus in Judea, settled in Nazareth of Galilee. Matthew names three gifts, not three wise men.\n\n9. JESUS AT TWELVE IN THE TEMPLE (LUKE 2:41–52)\nJesus' parents travelled yearly to Jerusalem for Passover. When he was twelve, he remained in Jerusalem after the festival while his parents began returning, assuming he was in their travelling group. After a day's journey they searched among relatives and friends, returned to Jerusalem and found him after three days in the temple, listening to teachers and asking questions. His understanding and answers amazed listeners. Mary asked why he had caused them anxiety; Jesus replied that he must be in his Father's house or about his Father's business, depending on translation. He returned to Nazareth and was obedient to them, growing in wisdom, stature and favour with God and people.\n\n10. JOHN THE BAPTIST'S MINISTRY (MATTHEW 3; MARK 1; LUKE 3)\nJohn preached in the wilderness of Judea, calling people to repent because the kingdom of heaven was near. He baptised in the Jordan River and urged people to bear fruit worthy of repentance. Matthew and Mark describe his clothing as camel's hair with a leather belt and his food as locusts and wild honey. He criticised religious hypocrisy and warned of judgement. Luke 3 records practical instructions: those with two coats should share, tax collectors should collect no more than authorised, and soldiers should not extort or accuse falsely but be satisfied with wages. John said someone mightier was coming who would baptise with the Holy Spirit and fire; John was not worthy to carry or untie his sandals, depending on the Gospel account.\n\n11. THE BAPTISM OF JESUS (MATTHEW 3:13–17; MARK 1; LUKE 3)\nJesus came from Galilee to John at the Jordan to be baptised. In Matthew John initially objected, saying he needed baptism from Jesus; Jesus replied that it was fitting to fulfil all righteousness. After Jesus' baptism, the heavens opened, the Spirit descended like a dove, and a voice from heaven declared him the beloved Son in whom God was well pleased. Mark says Jesus saw the heavens torn apart and the Spirit descending; Luke describes Jesus praying as heaven opened. The event signals divine affirmation and the beginning of Jesus' public ministry, not that Jesus had to repent of personal wrongdoing.\n\n12. THE TEMPTATION OF JESUS (MATTHEW 4:1–11; LUKE 4:1–13)\nAfter baptism Jesus was led by the Spirit into the wilderness and fasted forty days and forty nights; he became hungry. The tempter challenged him to turn stones into bread, to throw himself from the temple pinnacle so angels would save him, and to gain the world's kingdoms through worship of the devil. Jesus answered with Scripture: people do not live by bread alone (Deuteronomy 8:3); do not put the Lord your God to the test (Deuteronomy 6:16); worship and serve God alone (Deuteronomy 6:13). Matthew's order is bread, temple, kingdoms; Luke's order is bread, kingdoms, temple. In Matthew angels ministered to Jesus afterward. The forty days are the period of fasting/temptation, not the duration of Noah's flood.\n\n13. EXAM DISTINCTIONS\nZechariah and Elizabeth: John promised, Zechariah mute, John named on writing tablet. Mary: Gabriel at Nazareth, sixth month of Elizabeth's pregnancy, willing response. Joseph: dream in Matthew, name Jesus, flight to Egypt. Shepherds and manger: Luke 2. Wise men, star and three gifts: Matthew 2; number of wise men unstated. Simeon and Anna: temple presentation. Jesus at age twelve: Luke 2. John the Baptist: Jordan, repentance, camel's hair, locusts and honey. Baptism: Spirit like dove and heavenly voice. Temptations: forty days and Gospel order differences.\n\nDEEPER GUIDED TEACHING — EXPLANATION AND INTERPRETATION\nHOW MATTHEW AND LUKE ORGANISE THE BIRTH STORY\nMatthew 1–2 places emphasis on Joseph, his dreams, the genealogy linked to David and Abraham, the wise men, Herod and fulfilment of prophecy. Luke 1–2 begins earlier with Zechariah and Elizabeth, then Mary's encounter with Gabriel, songs of praise, the census, shepherds, temple presentation and Jesus at age twelve. These are distinct literary accounts. Neither Gospel says there were three wise men: Matthew lists three kinds of gifts. Luke does not place the wise men at the manger. For exams, first identify the Gospel named in the question, then recall the people and details that Gospel actually supplies.\n\nZECHARIAH AND MARY: TWO QUESTIONS, TWO RESPONSES\nBoth hear an extraordinary announcement from Gabriel, but the narratives highlight different responses. Zechariah asks how he can know the promise is true given his and Elizabeth's age, and Gabriel says he will be silent until the event occurs because he did not believe (Luke 1:18–20). Mary asks how she will conceive while a virgin; Gabriel explains the Holy Spirit's action, and she accepts God's word (Luke 1:34–38). The distinction is not that asking questions is always sinful. The passages contrast Zechariah's unbelief with Mary's willing trust. The child promised to Zechariah is John; the child promised to Mary is Jesus.\n\nTHE THREE SONGS AND WHO SPOKE THEM\nMary's Magnificat (Luke 1:46–55) praises God for mercy and lifting the humble. Zechariah's Benedictus (Luke 1:68–79) blesses God for salvation and speaks of John's role in preparing the way. Simeon's Nunc Dimittis (Luke 2:29–32) celebrates seeing God's salvation and calls Jesus a light for the Gentiles and glory for Israel. Elizabeth's blessing of Mary (Luke 1:42–45) is also important, but it is not the same song as Mary's Magnificat. JAMB may identify a speaker from a line rather than ask for a song's Latin title.\n\nSIGNS, TIMES AND LOCATIONS\nElizabeth is in her sixth month when Gabriel visits Mary (Luke 1:26, 36). Mary stays with Elizabeth about three months (1:56). John is named after birth, and Zechariah regains speech when he confirms the name (1:63–64). Jesus is circumcised on the eighth day (Luke 2:21), presented at the temple in Jerusalem and later found there at age twelve. Bethlehem is Jesus' birthplace in the narratives, Nazareth his home town, and Egypt the place of refuge in Matthew 2. Herod's order concerns boys two years old and under; that does not establish Jesus' exact age at the wise men's visit.\n\nWHY JOHN PREACHES REPENTANCE BEFORE JESUS' PUBLIC MINISTRY\nJohn's baptism of repentance calls people to turn from sin and show changed behaviour. In Luke 3, sharing clothing and food, collecting only authorised taxes and refusing extortion are examples of the 'fruit' of repentance. John's role is preparatory: he points beyond himself to someone mightier. John says he baptises with water while the coming one will baptise with the Holy Spirit and fire. This is different from Jesus' own baptism: the Spirit descends like a dove and the heavenly voice identifies Jesus as the beloved Son. John's clothing and food are described in Matthew and Mark, but not as a condition of salvation.\n\nTEMPTATIONS: IDENTIFY THE TEST AND THE SCRIPTURE ANSWER\nTurning stones into bread tests whether Jesus will misuse power to satisfy hunger; he cites Deuteronomy 8:3 about living by God's word. Jumping from the temple tests whether he will force a dramatic proof of protection; he cites Deuteronomy 6:16 against testing God. Worshipping the devil for kingdoms tests loyalty; he cites Deuteronomy 6:13 about worshipping God alone. Matthew orders them bread, temple, kingdoms; Luke orders bread, kingdoms, temple. A question about 'the second temptation' must specify the Gospel. The devil quotes Scripture in the temple scene, so quoting a verse alone does not guarantee its proper application.\n\nTHE BAPTISM AS A PUBLIC REVELATION\nAt the Jordan Jesus comes to John, who in Matthew initially protests. Jesus answers that the baptism is fitting 'to fulfil all righteousness' (Matthew 3:15). The Spirit descends like a dove and the voice identifies Jesus as the beloved Son. The event is a revelation of identity and a transition into public ministry; it does not imply that Jesus confessed personal sin. Distinguish the descent of the Spirit at baptism from Gabriel's explanation of the Holy Spirit's role in Mary's conception.\n\nWORKED JAMB-STYLE INTERPRETATION QUESTIONS\nExample (i): A question mentions three gifts and a warning dream not to return to Herod. Which Gospel supplies these details?\nA. Luke\nB. Matthew\nC. Mark\nD. John\nSolution: Matthew 2 names gold, frankincense and myrrh and the wise men's dream. Luke describes shepherds instead; Mark and John contain no comparable infancy narrative.\nCorrect answer: B.\n\nExample (ii): What most clearly distinguishes Mary's response to Gabriel from Zechariah's?\nA. Mary demanded a written guarantee\nB. Mary accepted the announcement after asking how it would happen\nC. Zechariah was told to name his child Jesus\nD. Mary was struck speechless\nSolution: Luke 1 contrasts Mary's willing acceptance with Zechariah's unbelief and temporary muteness. The other options misassign details.\nCorrect answer: B.\n\nExample (iii): Which person described Jesus as a light for revelation to the Gentiles?\nA. Simeon\nB. Herod\nC. Joseph of Arimathea\nD. John's father at the altar\nSolution: Simeon speaks these words in Luke 2:32 during the temple presentation. Zechariah's song occurs in Luke 1.\nCorrect answer: A.\n\nExample (iv): In Matthew's temptation order, what came immediately after stones-to-bread?\nA. The kingdoms of the world\nB. The temple pinnacle\nC. The baptism in Jordan\nD. The call of Peter\nSolution: Matthew 4:5–7 puts the temple test second; Luke reverses the second and third temptations. The other events are not Matthew's second temptation.\nCorrect answer: B.\n\nExample (v): Which statement about the wise men is supported by Matthew 2?\nA. Exactly three wise men visited\nB. They visited Jesus after a vision from Simeon\nC. They offered three named kinds of gifts\nD. They were shepherds from Bethlehem\nSolution: Matthew lists three gifts but does not state the number of visitors. Simeon and the shepherds belong to Luke's account.\nCorrect answer: C.\n\nQUICK UNDERSTANDING CHECK — LESSON-SPECIFIC\n1. Create separate Matthew 1–2 and Luke 1–2 timelines and explain where each event appears.\n2. Identify who spoke the Magnificat, Benedictus and Nunc Dimittis and what each celebrates.\n3. Explain the difference between John's baptismal preaching and Jesus' baptism.\n4. Write the three temptations in Matthew's order and Luke's order, with Jesus' replies.\n\nPRACTICE JAMB PAST QUESTIONS\nUse the lesson's linked past questions. Identify the exact passage for each answer and explain why close alternatives are less accurate."}},{"id":"new-covenant","revised":true,"old":{"title":"The new covenant: inward obedience, forgiveness and the Spirit","body":"A covenant is a binding relationship involving commitments. Biblical covenant promises should be identified by their participants, setting and stated responsibilities. Noah's rainbow, Abraham's circumcision and the Sinai covenant must not be treated as interchangeable signs. Prophetic promises of renewal address people's unfaithfulness and God's purpose to restore a faithful relationship.\n\nJeremiah 31:29–34 sets the new-covenant promise beside rejection of the proverb about fathers eating sour grapes and children's teeth being affected. Each person is responsible for his own wrongdoing. The promised law is written within people and on their hearts, rather than remaining an external command they continually break. God will be their God, they will be his people, they will know him, and their sins will be forgiven. The passage therefore joins personal responsibility, inward obedience and restored relationship. Describing it simply as “righteousness” or “restoration” may summarise aspects of it, but neither word replaces the fuller promise.\n\nEzekiel 18 likewise distinguishes personal guilt from inherited blame. A child does not bear a parent's moral guilt merely through birth, though wrongdoing can still harm a family or community. Ezekiel 36 describes gathering from the nations, sprinkling clean water, cleansing from idols, receiving a new heart and a new spirit, and God's Spirit enabling obedience. A heart of flesh contrasts with an unresponsive heart of stone. These are promises of renewed responsiveness, not an anatomical operation.\n\nThe New Testament applies new-covenant language to Christ. At the Last Supper, Jesus connects the cup with the new covenant in his blood. Hebrews quotes Jeremiah's promise, while 2 Corinthians 3 describes ministry of the Spirit. Christian participation is therefore connected with Christ and the gift of the Spirit; it should not be reduced to obedience to a particular church official or an outward ceremony by itself.\n\nIn a Nigerian classroom, learning an honesty rule is different from accepting responsibility for keeping it when nobody is watching. That distinction helps explain inward obedience, although the biblical covenant is more than a school agreement. The common examination trap is to confuse a sign belonging to an earlier covenant with the particular prophetic or Christian promise being asked about.\n\nBaptism also signifies participation in Christ and entry into his community. Christian traditions differ over mode; mentioning immersion does not by itself establish that other modes are excluded. Outward practice should not be presented as mechanically replacing faith or the Spirit."},"new":{"title":"The new covenant: inward obedience, forgiveness and the Spirit","body":"1. INTRODUCTION: WHAT A COVENANT IS\nA covenant is a binding relationship or agreement with obligations and promises. In the Old Testament God made covenants with Noah, Abraham and Israel, among others. The Sinai covenant called Israel to faithful worship and obedience. Prophets later spoke of a new covenant because people repeatedly broke the old one. The New Testament connects Jesus' death, forgiveness and the work of the Holy Spirit with this promised renewal.\n\n2. THE SINAI COVENANT (EXODUS 19–24)\nAfter the Exodus Israel camped at Mount Sinai. God reminded them how he had carried them 'on eagles' wings' and brought them to himself. He said that if they obeyed his voice and kept his covenant, they would be his treasured possession among all peoples, a kingdom of priests and a holy nation (Exodus 19:4–6). The people answered that they would do all the Lord had spoken. God gave the Ten Commandments in Exodus 20 and further laws governing worship, justice and community life. Moses read the book of the covenant, and the people again promised obedience. He sprinkled blood on the people, calling it 'the blood of the covenant' (Exodus 24:8). Sinai's covenant was public and national; Jeremiah's later promise stresses inward knowledge and forgiveness.\n\n3. WHY THE PROPHETS SPOKE OF RENEWAL\nIsrael's history included idolatry, oppression, injustice and disregard for covenant commands. Jeremiah warned Judah that possessing the temple did not guarantee safety while people practised wrongdoing (Jeremiah 7). Ezekiel described a people who profaned God's name among the nations (Ezekiel 36). The prophets did not say God had forgotten his character; they insisted that the people needed more than external religious signs. Genuine renewal required changed hearts, forgiveness and faithful conduct.\n\n4. JEREMIAH'S NEW COVENANT PROMISE (JEREMIAH 31:31–34)\nGod announced that days were coming when he would make a new covenant with the house of Israel and the house of Judah. It would not be like the covenant made when he took their ancestors by the hand to bring them out of Egypt, a covenant they broke. God promised: 'I will put my law within them, and I will write it on their hearts; and I will be their God, and they shall be my people.' People would know the Lord, from the least to the greatest, because God would forgive their iniquity and remember their sin no more. The central features are inward law, personal knowledge of God, restored relationship and forgiveness. Jeremiah does not say God's concern for justice disappears.\n\n5. EZEKIEL: A NEW HEART AND SPIRIT (EZEKIEL 36:25–27)\nGod promised to sprinkle clean water on his people, cleanse them from impurities and idols, give them a new heart and put a new spirit within them. He would remove the heart of stone and give a heart of flesh. God would put his Spirit within them and cause them to walk in his statutes. The stone heart symbolises stubborn resistance; the flesh heart symbolises responsiveness to God. Ezekiel's imagery complements Jeremiah's law written on the heart but uses different wording.\n\n6. EZEKIEL'S TWO STICKS AND THE COVENANT OF PEACE (EZEKIEL 37:15–28)\nGod told Ezekiel to take two sticks: one for Judah and associated Israelites, the other for Joseph, the stick of Ephraim and associated tribes. He was to join them so they became one in his hand. The sign represented God's intention to reunite his divided people under one king and cleanse them from idols. God promised a covenant of peace, described as an everlasting covenant, and said his dwelling place would be with them. This symbolic act is distinct from the dry bones vision earlier in the same chapter.\n\n7. THE LAST SUPPER AND THE NEW COVENANT (LUKE 22; 1 CORINTHIANS 11)\nAt the final meal with his disciples Jesus took bread, gave thanks, broke it and spoke of his body given for them. In Luke 22:20 he described the cup after supper as 'the new covenant in my blood, which is poured out for you.' Paul repeats the cup formula in 1 Corinthians 11:25 and says believers proclaim the Lord's death until he comes when they eat the bread and drink the cup (11:26). Matthew 26:28 and Mark 14:24 refer to covenant blood; manuscript and translation wording differs in the Gospel accounts. In exams, associate 'new covenant in my blood' especially with Luke 22:20 and 1 Corinthians 11:25.\n\n8. HEBREWS: JESUS AS MEDIATOR OF A BETTER COVENANT (HEBREWS 8–10)\nHebrews 8 calls Jesus the mediator of a better covenant founded on better promises and quotes Jeremiah 31 at length. The argument is that the former system pointed beyond itself and that God's promised inward renewal and forgiveness are fulfilled through Christ. Hebrews 9 describes Christ as entering the heavenly holy place through his own blood, not through the blood of goats and calves, securing eternal redemption. Hebrews 10:16–18 again cites the promise that God's laws will be placed in hearts and sins remembered no more. Where sins are forgiven, Hebrews says, no further offering for sin is needed. The letter uses priestly and sacrifice imagery to explain Christ's once-for-all saving work.\n\n9. THE HOLY SPIRIT AND INNER OBEDIENCE (ROMANS 8; GALATIANS 5)\nRomans 8 contrasts living according to the flesh with life directed by the Spirit. Paul teaches that the Spirit gives life, leads believers as God's children and helps them in weakness. Galatians 5:13–26 says Christian freedom should be used for loving service, not self-indulgence. The fruit of the Spirit includes love, joy, peace, patience, kindness, goodness, faithfulness, gentleness and self-control. Inner obedience is visible in behaviour; the Spirit does not make justice, truthfulness or love unnecessary.\n\n10. PAUL'S LETTER ABOUT THE SPIRIT AND THE LETTER (2 CORINTHIANS 3)\nPaul describes the Corinthians as a letter from Christ, written not with ink but with the Spirit of the living God, not on tablets of stone but on tablets of human hearts (2 Corinthians 3:3). He says God made him and his companions ministers of a new covenant, 'not of letter but of spirit; for the letter kills, but the Spirit gives life' (3:6). Paul contrasts the old covenant's fading glory, associated with Moses, with the greater and enduring glory of the Spirit's ministry. The image of hearts instead of stone tablets echoes prophetic promises of inward renewal.\n\n11. THE JERUSALEM COUNCIL AND GENTILE BELIEVERS (ACTS 15)\nSome believers insisted Gentile converts needed circumcision according to Moses' custom. Peter recalled God's acceptance of Gentiles and the gift of the Holy Spirit. James supported not placing the full Mosaic burden on Gentile converts, while asking them to abstain from things polluted by idols, sexual immorality, strangled meat and blood. The council's decision showed that Gentiles could belong to the Christian community without first becoming Jews through circumcision. This is a later apostolic meeting, not the making of the Sinai covenant.\n\n12. EXAM DISTINCTIONS\nSinai (Exodus 19–24): covenant at mountain, commandments, blood sprinkled, people promise obedience. Jeremiah 31: law written on hearts, God known, sins forgiven. Ezekiel 36: clean water, heart of flesh, new spirit. Ezekiel 37: two sticks reunited, covenant of peace. Luke 22 and 1 Corinthians 11: cup of the new covenant in Jesus' blood. Hebrews 8–10: Christ mediator, once-for-all sacrifice. 2 Corinthians 3: tablets of human hearts and Spirit gives life. Acts 15: Gentile circumcision question.\n\nDEEPER GUIDED TEACHING — EXPLANATION AND INTERPRETATION\nWHY 'OLD' AND 'NEW' COVENANT ARE NOT JUST TWO SETS OF RULES\nA covenant establishes a relationship and its obligations. The Sinai covenant includes God's rescue of Israel, the people's promise to obey, commandments and a blood ceremony (Exodus 19–24). Jeremiah's promised new covenant is not merely a replacement list of rules. It emphasises God's law internalised, people knowing God and forgiveness of sin. Ezekiel adds imagery of cleansing, a changed heart and God's Spirit enabling obedience. Hebrews and Paul then interpret these promises in relation to Jesus. Students should distinguish the historical promise in Jeremiah from its New Testament interpretation.\n\nWHY JEREMIAH MENTIONS ISRAEL AND JUDAH\nJeremiah 31:31 promises the new covenant with the house of Israel and the house of Judah. These names recall the divided people of God. The prophet is addressing a broken covenant community and announcing renewed relationship. The key phrase is not simply 'a new king' but 'I will put my law within them ... write it on their hearts' (31:33). God's forgiveness in 31:34 means the relationship is restored, not that wrongdoing no longer matters. A question asking 'where will the law be written?' points to Jeremiah; a question asking 'what heart replaces stone?' points to Ezekiel.\n\nHOW EZEKIEL'S TWO SYMBOLS DIFFER\nEzekiel 36 promises cleansing with water and replacement of the heart of stone with a heart of flesh, together with the Spirit's enabling presence. Ezekiel 37:15–28 uses two sticks labelled for Judah and Joseph/Ephraim to represent reunification. These are different images: one concerns inner transformation, the other restored unity. The dry bones vision in Ezekiel 37:1–14 symbolises hope for a people who say their bones are dried up and their hope lost. It should not be confused with the two-stick sign, even though both appear in chapter 37.\n\nTHE CUP AND THE MEANING OF JESUS' BLOOD\nAt the Last Supper Jesus associates the cup with covenant blood. Luke 22:20 and 1 Corinthians 11:25 explicitly speak of the 'new covenant in my blood'. This recalls the covenant blood in Exodus 24:8 while giving it a new meaning through Jesus' approaching death. Paul's account in 1 Corinthians 11 says the meal proclaims the Lord's death until he comes; it is therefore an act of remembrance and proclamation, not a repetition of the crucifixion. A JAMB question may ask who wrote the formula to the Corinthians: Paul, not Moses.\n\nHEBREWS: WHY JESUS IS CALLED MEDIATOR\nA mediator stands between parties in establishing or maintaining an agreement. Hebrews 8:6 calls Jesus mediator of a better covenant. Hebrews 9–10 contrasts repeated sacrifices under the old priestly system with Christ's effective, once-for-all offering. The argument is about the sufficiency of Jesus' sacrifice and access to God, not that all ancient priests were personally wicked. Hebrews quotes Jeremiah 31 to explain inward renewal and forgiveness. The phrase 'where there is forgiveness ... there is no longer any offering for sin' (Hebrews 10:18) belongs to this argument.\n\nSPIRIT, LAW AND ETHICAL BEHAVIOUR\nSecond Corinthians 3 contrasts letters on stone tablets with a letter written by the Spirit on human hearts. 'The letter kills, but the Spirit gives life' (3:6) belongs to Paul's contrast between ministries, not a general excuse to ignore every written command. Romans 8 describes life led by the Spirit, while Galatians 5 names observable fruit: love, joy, peace, patience, kindness, goodness, faithfulness, gentleness and self-control. The Spirit's work should therefore lead to changed conduct, not merely religious feelings.\n\nGENTILE INCLUSION IS A DIFFERENT QUESTION\nActs 15 addresses whether Gentile believers must be circumcised and take on the Mosaic law as a condition of salvation. Peter recalls God's acceptance of Gentiles, and James proposes a letter with four practical abstentions. This council is not where Jeremiah first announced the new covenant, nor where Jesus instituted the Last Supper. It is an apostolic decision about belonging to the Christian community. The connection is that salvation and the Spirit are not restricted to those who first adopt Israel's ethnic boundary markers.\n\nWORKED JAMB-STYLE INTERPRETATION QUESTIONS\nExample (i): Which feature most clearly identifies Jeremiah's new-covenant promise?\nA. A law written only on stone tablets\nB. A law written on hearts and sins forgiven\nC. A decree from Cyrus to rebuild the temple\nD. A return to Egyptian bondage\nSolution: Jeremiah 31:33–34 promises inward law and forgiveness. Stone tablets belong to Sinai; Cyrus and Egypt are different settings.\nCorrect answer: B.\n\nExample (ii): Which vision concerns the reunion of Judah and Joseph/Ephraim?\nA. Jeremiah's boiling pot\nB. Ezekiel's two sticks\nC. Isaiah's coal\nD. Amos' basket of summer fruit\nSolution: Ezekiel 37:15–28 explains the two sticks as reunited people. The other symbols belong to different prophetic passages.\nCorrect answer: B.\n\nExample (iii): Why does Hebrews describe Jesus' sacrifice as once for all?\nA. Because forgiveness is no longer necessary\nB. Because his offering is sufficient rather than requiring repeated sacrifices\nC. Because priests were forbidden to pray\nD. Because the Last Supper occurred only once\nSolution: Hebrews 9–10 contrasts Christ's effective offering with repeated ritual sacrifices. The other options do not express the letter's argument.\nCorrect answer: B.\n\nExample (iv): Which text calls the Corinthians a letter written on human hearts by the Spirit?\nA. 2 Corinthians 3\nB. Genesis 17\nC. Nehemiah 6\nD. Acts 15\nSolution: Paul uses the living-letter and human-heart imagery in 2 Corinthians 3:2–3. The other chapters concern different events.\nCorrect answer: A.\n\nExample (v): What did the Jerusalem Council chiefly decide about Gentile converts?\nA. They must first become priests\nB. They must be circumcised before baptism\nC. They should not be required to take on circumcision as a condition of salvation\nD. They must rebuild the temple\nSolution: Acts 15 rejects imposing circumcision as a requirement for Gentile salvation, while giving guidance for shared community life.\nCorrect answer: C.\n\nQUICK UNDERSTANDING CHECK — LESSON-SPECIFIC\n1. Compare the covenant ceremony at Sinai with Jeremiah's promise of inward law.\n2. Distinguish Ezekiel's heart of flesh, dry bones and two sticks.\n3. Explain how Luke 22:20 relates to Exodus 24:8.\n4. Why does Hebrews call Jesus a mediator, and what does 'once for all' mean?\n\nPRACTICE JAMB PAST QUESTIONS\nUse the lesson's linked past questions. Identify the exact passage for each answer and explain why close alternatives are less accurate."}},{"id":"prayer-tested-faith","revised":true,"old":{"title":"Prayer, tested faith and responsible endurance","body":"Prayer is addressing God in trust, including praise, thanksgiving, confession, petition and intercession for others. Effective prayer is not measured by loudness, elaborate language or emotional display. Biblical teaching connects prayer with faith and righteous living while leaving the outcome in God's hands rather than treating words as a technique that controls him.\n\nJames 5 directs a sick person to call the church elders, who pray and anoint in the Lord's name. The prayer of faith will save or restore the sick person, the Lord will raise him up, and sins, if committed, will be forgiven. The text joins healing, prayer and forgiveness; it does not say every illness proves a particular sin. Confession and prayer for one another accompany the statement that a righteous person's prayer is powerful in its effects. “Faith” and “righteous” belong to particular descriptions, not a competition between silent and loud prayer. Appropriate practical care is compatible with praying for someone who is ill.\n\nJames 1 tells believers to count trials as joy because testing produces steadfastness, which should lead to mature and complete character. This is an assessment of the intended outcome, not a claim that pain feels pleasant. First Peter compares tested faith to gold tested by fire. Actual trials test the believer; fire supplies the comparison. Neither passage requires a person to hide abuse or refuse lawful help.\n\nFirst Peter also distinguishes suffering for Christ from punishment for wrongdoing. Judgement beginning with God's household means believers themselves face God's searching assessment; it is not a guarantee that their persecution will be painless or that religious membership removes accountability. Peter urges hospitality and use of each received gift to serve others as stewards of God's varied grace. Stewardship means responsible use of what has been entrusted, not ownership for selfish advantage.\n\nPaul urges prayer, intercession and thanksgiving for rulers so that believers may live quiet, peaceable and godly lives. A Nigerian student may pray for public leaders while also acting honestly and respecting other people. Prayer does not replace the conduct for which one remains responsible.\n\nIn James 5, “save” can describe the sick person's restoration; it must not automatically be read as a promise of eternal salvation. Healing and forgiveness therefore do not contradict that contextual wording.\n\nBelievers insulted for Christ's name are called blessed because the Spirit of glory and of God rests on them. Peter distinguishes suffering for righteousness from deserved punishment for wrongdoing. James also calls the poor who love God heirs of the promised kingdom.\n\nJames compares the doubter to a sea wave driven and tossed by wind.\n\nThe Hebrews declare God able to deliver, yet refuse the image's worship even if he does not."},"new":{"title":"Prayer, tested faith and responsible endurance","body":"1. WHAT PRAYER MEANS\nPrayer is communication with God involving praise, thanksgiving, confession, petition and intercession for others. Petition asks God for help; intercession prays on another person's behalf. Faith means trusting God, not forcing God to grant every request. Biblical teaching connects prayer with sincerity, persistence, forgiveness and obedience. JAMB may ask which passage describes a particular prayer, who prayed, or what answer followed.\n\n2. JESUS TEACHES HOW TO PRAY (MATTHEW 6:5–15)\nJesus warns against praying for public admiration and against heaping up empty phrases as if many words automatically secure an answer. He teaches his disciples to pray to 'Our Father in heaven', hallow God's name, seek his kingdom and will, ask for daily bread, forgiveness of debts as they forgive debtors, and deliverance from evil. Matthew 6:14–15 especially stresses forgiving others. Luke 11:1–4 gives a related, shorter form of the Lord's Prayer. 'Daily bread' concerns dependence on God for needs; it is not the request for forgiveness.\n\n3. PERSISTENCE: THE FRIEND AT MIDNIGHT (LUKE 11:5–13)\nJesus tells of a man who approaches his friend at midnight requesting three loaves because a traveller has arrived and he has nothing to offer. The friend initially refuses because the door is shut and the children are in bed. Jesus says the man will rise and give what is needed because of the requester's persistence or boldness. Jesus follows with 'Ask ... seek ... knock' and explains that even imperfect human parents know how to give good gifts; the heavenly Father gives the Holy Spirit to those who ask him (Luke 11:13). The story encourages trustful persistence, not manipulation of God.\n\n4. PERSISTENCE AND JUSTICE: THE WIDOW AND JUDGE (LUKE 18:1–8)\nA widow repeatedly asked an unjust judge for justice against her opponent. The judge neither feared God nor respected people, but finally acted because her repeated appeals troubled him. Jesus contrasts the reluctant judge with God, who hears his chosen ones. The widow was seeking justice, not wealth or revenge. The passage begins by explaining that disciples should always pray and not lose heart. Do not confuse this widow with the poor widow who offered two small coins in Mark 12.\n\n5. HUMILITY: THE PHARISEE AND TAX COLLECTOR (LUKE 18:9–14)\nTwo men went to the temple to pray. The Pharisee thanked God that he was unlike other people and listed fasting twice weekly and giving a tenth of his income. The tax collector stood far away, would not lift his eyes, beat his breast and said, 'God, be merciful to me, a sinner!' Jesus said the tax collector went home justified rather than the Pharisee. The lesson is humble repentance instead of self-righteous boasting. This is different from the persistent widow parable, although both occur in Luke 18.\n\n6. HANNAH'S PRAYER AND SAMUEL (1 SAMUEL 1–2)\nHannah, wife of Elkanah, suffered because she had no child; Peninnah, Elkanah's other wife, provoked her. At Shiloh Hannah prayed bitterly and vowed that if God gave her a son she would dedicate him to the Lord for his life, and no razor would touch his head. Eli the priest saw her lips moving without audible words and mistakenly thought she was drunk. Hannah explained her distress; Eli blessed her. She later bore Samuel, whose name she associated with asking the Lord. After weaning him, she brought Samuel to Shiloh and entrusted him to the Lord's service. In 1 Samuel 2 she praised God for overturning human pride and raising the lowly. Hannah was Samuel's mother; Elizabeth was John the Baptist's mother.\n\n7. SOLOMON ASKS FOR WISDOM (1 KINGS 3:3–15)\nAt Gibeon Solomon offered sacrifices and God appeared in a dream, inviting him to ask for what he wanted. Solomon acknowledged his youth and the responsibility of governing a numerous people. He requested an understanding or discerning heart to judge God's people and distinguish good from evil. God was pleased that Solomon had not asked for long life, riches or the death of enemies. God promised exceptional wisdom, and also riches and honour; long life was conditional on obedience. Solomon's judgement between two women claiming the same living child follows in 1 Kings 3:16–28 and illustrates his discernment.\n\n8. ELIJAH'S PRAYER AT CARMEL (1 KINGS 18:20–39)\nDuring Ahab's reign Elijah challenged the prophets of Baal on Mount Carmel. The prophets of Baal called on their god from morning until noon and beyond but received no answer. Elijah repaired the Lord's altar using twelve stones, one for each tribe, arranged the sacrifice and had water poured over it three times, filling the trench. At the time of the evening offering Elijah prayed that the people would know the Lord is God and that he had acted at God's command. Fire fell and consumed the offering, wood, stones, dust and water. The people cried, 'The LORD, he is God!' Elijah's prayer is linked to covenant faithfulness, not theatrical display. Afterward Elijah prayed for rain; his servant looked toward the sea seven times before seeing a small cloud (1 Kings 18:41–46).\n\n9. DANIEL'S PRAYER DESPITE THE DECREE (DANIEL 6)\nOfficials persuaded King Darius to issue a decree forbidding petitions to any god or human other than the king for thirty days. Daniel continued praying three times a day with his windows open toward Jerusalem, as he had done before. He was arrested and thrown into the lions' den. The king spent the night fasting and returned early. Daniel reported that God sent an angel to shut the lions' mouths, so they had not harmed him. The king ordered Daniel brought out and acknowledged Daniel's God. Daniel's consistency is the main lesson; he did not begin praying only after the decree.\n\n10. JESUS IN GETHSEMANE (MATTHEW 26:36–46)\nAfter the Last Supper Jesus went to Gethsemane with his disciples and took Peter and the two sons of Zebedee farther with him. Distressed, he asked them to stay awake and watch. He prayed that, if possible, the cup might pass from him, yet submitted to the Father's will: 'not as I will, but as you will.' He returned to find the disciples sleeping and warned them to watch and pray so they would not enter temptation. Matthew records three periods of prayer. Luke 22:43–44 includes the angel and sweat like drops of blood in many manuscripts, with textual variation noted by scholars. The cup signifies approaching suffering, not an ordinary drinking cup.\n\n11. JAMES ON TRIALS, WISDOM AND PRAYER (JAMES 1; 5)\nJames 1:2–4 says testing of faith produces steadfastness, which should lead to maturity. If someone lacks wisdom, they should ask God, who gives generously, but ask in faith without divided loyalty (1:5–8). James 1:13–15 distinguishes testing from enticement to sin: God does not tempt people with evil; desire can lead to sin and death. James 5:13–18 urges the suffering to pray, the cheerful to sing praise and the sick to call elders to pray and anoint them with oil in the Lord's name. It calls for confession and mutual prayer and cites Elijah, who prayed that rain would stop and later return. James 5:17 says no rain fell for three years and six months. Do not confuse that duration with the forty days of Jesus' temptation.\n\n12. PETER ON SUFFERING AND HOPE (1 PETER 1; 3–4)\nPeter describes faith as more precious than gold tested by fire (1 Peter 1:6–7). He encourages believers to give a reason for their hope with gentleness and respect (3:15), to do good rather than retaliate, and not to be surprised by fiery trials (4:12). Suffering because of wrongdoing is not praised as though it were persecution for Christ (4:15–16). Believers should entrust themselves to a faithful Creator while doing good (4:19). Christian endurance is not passive approval of injustice or abuse.\n\n13. EXAM DISTINCTIONS\nMatthew 6: Lord's Prayer and secret sincerity. Luke 11: friend at midnight, three loaves. Luke 18: persistent widow before unjust judge; humble tax collector in temple. 1 Samuel 1: Hannah and Samuel, Eli thought she was drunk. 1 Kings 3: Solomon asked for discernment. 1 Kings 18: Elijah's altar and prayer for fire, seven searches for rain. Daniel 6: Daniel prayed three times daily. Matthew 26: Gethsemane and submission. James 1: trials develop steadfastness, God does not entice to evil. James 5: prayer, healing and Elijah's three years and six months.\n\nDEEPER GUIDED TEACHING — REASONS, CONSEQUENCES AND EXAM INTERPRETATION\nTHE FOUNDATION: PRAYER IS NOT A FORMULA FOR GETTING EVERYTHING\nPrayer includes praise, confession, thanksgiving, requests and intercession. Biblical faith means relying on God's character while acting responsibly; it is not a technique for controlling outcomes. Jesus' Gethsemane prayer is the clearest example: he expresses his desire that the cup pass but submits to the Father's will. Therefore 'pray in faith' must not be confused with 'God must grant every specific request'. In James 1, asking for wisdom requires undivided trust; in James 5, praying for the sick belongs to a community of confession, care and hope.\n\nHOW THE LORD'S PRAYER IS ORGANISED\nMatthew 6:9–13 moves from God's honour to human needs. 'Hallowed be your name' asks that God's name be treated as holy; 'your kingdom come' and 'your will be done' seek God's rule and purposes. Daily bread asks for necessary provision; forgiveness addresses wrongs committed and our duty to forgive; deliverance seeks protection from temptation and evil. Matthew 6:14–15 follows with a warning about refusing forgiveness. A question asking which request comes first should not be answered 'daily bread': God's name and kingdom come first.\n\nPERSISTENCE WITHOUT SELF-RIGHTEOUSNESS\nLuke 11's midnight friend asks for three loaves to host a visitor. Luke 18's widow repeatedly seeks justice from an unjust judge. Both stories encourage persistence, but the judge is not a model of God's reluctance; Jesus contrasts God's justice with the judge's selfishness. Immediately afterward Luke 18 gives the Pharisee and tax collector: the Pharisee lists his achievements, while the tax collector asks for mercy. This juxtaposition shows that repeated prayer and humble prayer belong together. Persistence is not boasting, and humility is not abandoning prayer.\n\nTESTING, TEMPTATION AND PERSECUTION ARE DIFFERENT\nJames 1:2–4 describes trials that test faith and can develop steadfastness. James 1:13–15 says God does not entice people into evil; temptation toward sin grows through disordered desire. First Peter distinguishes suffering for doing good from suffering as a consequence of wrongdoing (1 Peter 3:17; 4:15–16). These distinctions matter when interpreting 'rejoice in trials': believers are not commanded to celebrate harm or call their own misconduct persecution. They are encouraged to remain faithful and do good under pressure.\n\nTHE THREE PRAYERS OF LEADERS: HANNAH, SOLOMON AND ELIJAH\nHannah at Shiloh pours out distress, makes a vow and later dedicates Samuel; Eli misreads her silent lips. Solomon at Gibeon asks for a discerning heart to govern justly, rather than wealth or long life. Elijah on Carmel asks that the people know the Lord is God, and fire answers before the later rain sign. The requests differ: personal anguish and dedication, wisdom for public responsibility, and public vindication of God's identity. A student who learns only that 'all three prayed' misses the tested reason for each prayer.\n\nDANIEL AND JESUS: STEADFASTNESS WITH DIFFERENT CIRCUMSTANCES\nDaniel keeps his established pattern of prayer three times daily despite a thirty-day royal decree; he does not begin praying to provoke the king. Jesus in Gethsemane prays in deep anguish and asks disciples to watch, while accepting the Father's will. Daniel is rescued from the lions; Jesus proceeds to arrest and crucifixion. Both narratives present faithfulness, but they do not promise that every faithful person escapes suffering. That is why 'God always prevents physical harm' is an inaccurate lesson from these stories.\n\nHOW TO ANSWER A PRAYER QUESTION\nFirst identify the setting: Shiloh suggests Hannah; Gibeon suggests Solomon; Carmel suggests Elijah; Babylonian royal decree suggests Daniel; Gethsemane suggests Jesus. Next identify the request, response and result. Finally distinguish a descriptive event from a universal rule. For example, Elijah's fire is a particular sign in 1 Kings 18, not a command for every worshipper to ask for fire.\n\nWORKED JAMB-STYLE INTERPRETATION QUESTIONS\nExample (i): Which interpretation best links Jesus' Gethsemane prayer with Christian faith?\nA. Faith means never expressing fear\nB. Faith requires every request to be granted\nC. Faith can express distress while submitting to God's will\nD. Prayer removes all difficult choices\nSolution: Matthew 26:39 includes both the request and 'not as I will, but as you will'. The other options contradict Jesus' example.\nCorrect answer: C.\n\nExample (ii): Why should the unjust judge not be treated as a direct picture of God's character?\nA. Because the widow was wealthy\nB. Because Jesus contrasts the reluctant judge with God's justice\nC. Because the judge was a priest\nD. Because the story forbids repeated requests\nSolution: Luke 18 argues from an unjust human judge to God's greater willingness to do justice. A, C and D are not the parable's point.\nCorrect answer: B.\n\nExample (iii): Which passage specifically distinguishes trials from God's tempting people to evil?\nA. James 1\nB. Daniel 6\nC. 1 Kings 3\nD. Luke 2\nSolution: James 1:2–4 treats trials and 1:13–15 denies that God tempts people with evil. The other passages concern different events.\nCorrect answer: A.\n\nExample (iv): What was Solomon's reason for requesting discernment?\nA. He wanted to defeat Elijah\nB. He needed wisdom to govern God's people\nC. He wanted to interpret Pharaoh's dream\nD. He was seeking freedom from prison\nSolution: 1 Kings 3:7–9 links his request to the responsibility of governing. The other options confuse unrelated figures.\nCorrect answer: B.\n\nExample (v): Which pair correctly identifies two different kinds of persistent prayer?\nA. Hannah and Goliath\nB. Friend at midnight and widow before a judge\nC. Pilate and Herod\nD. Zacchaeus and Nicodemus\nSolution: Luke 11 and Luke 18 both teach persistence using requests to other people. The other pairs are not these prayer parables.\nCorrect answer: B.\n\nQUICK UNDERSTANDING CHECK — SPECIFIC TO THIS LESSON\n1. Explain why the Lord's Prayer begins with God's name and kingdom before daily needs.\n2. Contrast Luke 11's midnight friend with Luke 18's unjust judge.\n3. Distinguish a test of faith from temptation to sin using James 1.\n4. Why do Daniel's rescue and Jesus' suffering both illustrate faithfulness?\n\nPRACTICE JAMB PAST QUESTIONS\nOpen the lesson's linked past questions and compare each answer against the exact biblical passage. Distinguish close alternatives before selecting your answer."}},{"id":"david-leadership","revised":true,"old":{"title":"David: dependence on God, justice and accountability","body":"David's story presents courage and dependence on God alongside serious moral failure. Biblical leadership is not proved merely by winning battles or receiving public admiration. A leader remains responsible for how he treats vulnerable people and how he responds when his own wrongdoing is exposed.\n\nDavid refuses to kill Saul when Abishai proposes it because Saul is the LORD's anointed. His rebuke about failing to guard the king is addressed to Abner, Saul's commander. David's refusal of private revenge expresses recognition of God's authority over kingship; it does not mean every action of a ruler is morally right.\n\nAt Ziklag the Amalekites burn the settlement and carry away families. David seeks God's direction through the priest and ephod before pursuing them. His men find an abandoned Egyptian servant, give him food and water, and obtain his help in locating the raiders. The Egyptian asks for assurance that David will neither kill him nor hand him back to his master. Kindness and that promise belong to the sequence; his help should not be reduced to an invented desire for revenge. David recovers the captives and possessions and takes spoil. Dependence on God accompanies responsible action rather than replacing it.\n\nDavid's abuse of Uriah exposes the opposite side of his leadership. After taking Bathsheba, he sends Uriah home in an attempt to conceal the adultery. Uriah's refusal defeats that plan, and David arranges his death. Nathan confronts David, who acknowledges his sin. Forgiveness does not remove all consequences: the child dies, and continuing violence troubles the household. David's servants fear he may harm himself when the child dies. These episodes must not be excused because David is otherwise admired.\n\nAmnon's assault on Tamar and Absalom's later revolt show further damage within the family. On hearing that the people's loyalty has turned to Absalom, David immediately flees Jerusalem rather than first organising a celebratory assembly or pleading with his son. He later asks that Absalom be treated gently and mourns his death.\n\nFor a Nigerian class prefect or public leader, the practical lesson is accountability: seeking guidance and showing courage cannot compensate for dishonesty, exploitation or refusal to address abuse.\n\nDavid is anointed king over Judah at Hebron; this differs from Samuel's earlier anointing at Bethlehem."},"new":{"title":"David: dependence on God, justice and accountability","body":"1. DAVID'S BACKGROUND AND ANOINTING (1 SAMUEL 16)\nGod sent Samuel to Jesse's household in Bethlehem to anoint a new king because Saul had disobeyed. Samuel first thought Eliab looked suitable, but God said people look at outward appearance while the Lord looks at the heart (1 Samuel 16:7). Jesse presented seven sons before Samuel; none was chosen. The youngest, David, was tending sheep and was brought in. Samuel anointed David with oil in the presence of his brothers, and the Spirit of the Lord came powerfully upon him. David later played the lyre to soothe Saul when Saul was troubled. David's anointing did not mean he immediately took the throne.\n\n2. DAVID AND GOLIATH (1 SAMUEL 17)\nThe Philistine champion Goliath of Gath challenged Israel's army in the Valley of Elah. The text describes his impressive height and heavy armour; the traditional Hebrew reading is six cubits and a span, while some ancient witnesses read four cubits and a span. David came from Bethlehem bringing food for his brothers and heard the challenge. He recalled how God had helped him defend his father's sheep from a lion and a bear. David refused Saul's armour because he was unaccustomed to it, chose five smooth stones from the brook, carried his sling and staff, and declared that the battle belonged to the Lord. He struck Goliath in the forehead with a stone, then used Goliath's own sword to kill him. The story emphasises faith and courage, not that David possessed superior military equipment.\n\n3. DAVID, JONATHAN AND SAUL'S JEALOUSY (1 SAMUEL 18–20)\nJonathan, Saul's son, loved David as himself and made a covenant with him, giving him his robe and weapons (1 Samuel 18:1–4). After David's victories women sang that Saul had killed thousands and David tens of thousands. Saul became jealous and repeatedly sought to kill David. Jonathan warned David and tried to reconcile him with Saul. In 1 Samuel 20 they arranged a sign involving arrows to confirm Saul's intentions. Jonathan's loyalty is important because he remained Saul's son and potential heir yet protected David. Michal, another child of Saul, became David's wife and helped him escape through a window (1 Samuel 19).\n\n4. DAVID SPARES SAUL TWICE (1 SAMUEL 24; 26)\nAt En-gedi Saul entered a cave where David and his men were hiding. David secretly cut off a corner of Saul's robe but felt remorse and refused to harm the Lord's anointed. He later showed the piece of cloth to prove he had spared Saul (1 Samuel 24). In another episode David entered Saul's camp at night with Abishai and took Saul's spear and water jar from beside his head rather than killing him (1 Samuel 26). Distinguish the signs: cave → corner of robe; sleeping camp → spear and water jar. David's restraint did not mean he denied Saul's wrongdoing.\n\n5. DAVID BECOMES KING AND CAPTURES JERUSALEM (2 SAMUEL 2; 5)\nAfter Saul's death David was anointed king over Judah at Hebron and ruled there for seven years and six months. Ish-bosheth, Saul's son, ruled over Israel for a time, with Abner supporting him. Later the elders of Israel came to David at Hebron and anointed him king over all Israel. David was thirty years old when he began to reign and ruled forty years: seven years and six months at Hebron and thirty-three years in Jerusalem (2 Samuel 5:4–5). David captured Jerusalem from the Jebusites and made it his capital, the City of David. He brought the ark toward Jerusalem; after Uzzah died when he touched it, the ark stayed for three months at Obed-edom's house before David brought it with rejoicing (2 Samuel 6).\n\n6. GOD'S PROMISE TO DAVID (2 SAMUEL 7)\nDavid wanted to build a house for the ark, but God spoke through Nathan the prophet. God reminded David that he had taken him from following sheep to lead Israel and promised to establish his house and kingdom. God said David's offspring would succeed him and build a house for God's name; the royal throne would be established forever (2 Samuel 7:12–16). The promise uses 'house' in two senses: David wanted to build a temple for God, while God promised to establish David's royal dynasty. David responded with humility and thanksgiving. Solomon, not David, later built the temple.\n\n7. DAVID AND MEPHIBOSHETH (2 SAMUEL 9)\nDavid asked whether anyone remained of Saul's household to whom he could show kindness for Jonathan's sake. Ziba told him about Mephibosheth, Jonathan's son, who was disabled in both feet. Mephibosheth lived at Lo-debar in the house of Machir son of Ammiel. David restored Saul's land to him, instructed Ziba and his household to work it, and invited Mephibosheth to eat continually at the king's table like one of his sons. This is an example of covenant loyalty and generosity toward a former rival's family.\n\n8. DAVID, BATHSHEBA AND URIAH (2 SAMUEL 11)\nWhile his army was at war David remained in Jerusalem. From his roof he saw Bathsheba bathing; she was the wife of Uriah the Hittite. David summoned her and had sexual relations with her, abusing his royal power. When she became pregnant David tried to conceal the wrongdoing by bringing Uriah home from the battle, but Uriah refused to enjoy domestic comfort while his comrades were in the field. David then sent instructions through Joab to place Uriah where fighting was fiercest and withdraw support so he would be killed. After Uriah died David married Bathsheba. The narrative explicitly says David's act displeased the Lord. Students should not blame Bathsheba for David's decisions.\n\n9. NATHAN'S PARABLE AND DAVID'S REPENTANCE (2 SAMUEL 12)\nNathan told David about a rich man with many sheep who took a poor man's single beloved ewe lamb to feed a visitor. David angrily condemned the rich man. Nathan answered, 'You are the man!' and exposed David's treatment of Uriah and Bathsheba. David confessed, 'I have sinned against the LORD.' Nathan announced that David's sin was forgiven, yet serious consequences would follow, including the death of the child born from the relationship. David fasted and prayed while the child was ill. After the child's death, he rose, washed, worshipped and ate. Bathsheba later bore Solomon. Psalm 51's heading connects it with Nathan's confrontation of David.\n\n10. ABSALOM'S REBELLION AND DAVID'S GRIEF (2 SAMUEL 13–19)\nConflict within David's household intensified after Amnon assaulted Tamar, Absalom's sister. Absalom later arranged Amnon's death, fled and eventually returned. He gained public support by presenting himself as a better judge and declared himself king in Hebron. David fled Jerusalem to avoid bloodshed. Ahithophel advised Absalom, but Hushai, loyal to David, gave contrary advice that delayed Absalom's attack (2 Samuel 17). During battle in the forest of Ephraim, Absalom's hair or head became caught in a great oak while his mule went on. Joab killed him despite David's instruction to deal gently with him. David grieved, crying, 'O my son Absalom, my son, my son Absalom!' (2 Samuel 18:33). The rebellion illustrates the political and family consequences of unresolved wrongdoing.\n\n11. DAVID'S CENSUS AND RESPONSIBILITY (2 SAMUEL 24)\nDavid ordered a census of fighting men despite Joab's objection. Afterward David's conscience troubled him and he confessed wrongdoing. Through Gad the prophet God offered three alternatives in the account: famine, flight before enemies or pestilence; the durations vary between 2 Samuel 24 and the parallel in 1 Chronicles 21, so cite the specific passage in an exam. David chose to fall into the Lord's hands because God's mercy was great. A plague followed. David bought the threshing floor of Araunah the Jebusite and offered sacrifices, refusing to offer God a burnt offering that cost him nothing (2 Samuel 24:24). The location later becomes associated with Solomon's temple site (2 Chronicles 3:1).\n\n12. DAVID'S FINAL YEARS AND SOLOMON'S ACCESSION (1 KINGS 1–2)\nAs David aged, Adonijah tried to make himself king with support from Joab and Abiathar. Nathan and Bathsheba reminded David of his promise concerning Solomon. David ordered Zadok the priest and Nathan the prophet to anoint Solomon at Gihon, accompanied by Benaiah and the royal servants. Solomon rode David's mule, and the people rejoiced (1 Kings 1). David charged Solomon to walk in God's ways and deal wisely with unfinished matters. David died after reigning forty years. His leadership record combines genuine devotion, courage and kindness with grave failures requiring accountability.\n\n13. EXAM DISTINCTIONS\nSamuel anointed David at Bethlehem; Goliath fell to a sling stone; Jonathan protected David from Saul; cave = robe corner; camp = spear and water jar. Hebron reign = seven years six months; Jerusalem reign = thirty-three years. Nathan delivered both the dynastic promise (2 Samuel 7) and rebuke over Bathsheba/Uriah (2 Samuel 12). Mephibosheth was Jonathan's son; Absalom was David's son; Zadok and Nathan anointed Solomon.\n\nDEEPER GUIDED TEACHING — REASONS, CONSEQUENCES AND EXAM INTERPRETATION\nTHE CENTRAL QUESTION: WHAT MAKES A GOOD KING?\nDavid's life is not a simple story of a hero who never fails. Scripture presents courage and trust in God alongside serious abuse of power, family conflict and repentance. When Samuel is sent to Jesse, God's statement that he looks at the heart challenges outward appearance as the measure of leadership. But 'heart' does not mean private feelings are enough: David's later actions toward Uriah and Bathsheba are condemned. A responsible reading asks what David did, who was harmed, how God confronted him and what consequences followed.\n\nTWO ANOINTINGS AND TWO CAPITALS\nSamuel anoints David at Bethlehem while Saul still reigns (1 Samuel 16). After Saul's death David becomes king over Judah at Hebron; later the tribes of Israel anoint him king over all Israel (2 Samuel 2; 5). Jerusalem becomes his capital after its capture from the Jebusites. Thus 'Where was David first anointed?' and 'Where did David first rule as king?' have different answers: Bethlehem and Hebron respectively. David's reign is traditionally given as forty years: seven years six months in Hebron and thirty-three in Jerusalem.\n\nDAVID'S RESTRAINT TOWARD SAUL IS NOT APPROVAL OF SAUL\nSaul repeatedly attempts to kill David. David has opportunities to retaliate but refuses to strike the Lord's anointed. In the cave he cuts a robe corner; in the sleeping camp he takes a spear and water jar. The episodes demonstrate restraint and trust rather than denial of Saul's hostility. David still protects himself by fleeing. Students should avoid interpreting mercy as a duty to remain exposed to attack; the narrative shows both refusal to murder and practical self-preservation.\n\nTWO DIFFERENT MESSAGES FROM NATHAN\nIn 2 Samuel 7 Nathan delivers God's promise of a lasting dynasty after David proposes building a house for the ark. In 2 Samuel 12 Nathan confronts David through the rich man's theft of a poor man's lamb. The same prophet brings both promise and rebuke. The word 'house' in chapter 7 changes meaning: David wants a temple for God, but God promises to establish David's royal house. Solomon builds the temple. A question asking Nathan's message must be anchored to the chapter and occasion.\n\nWHY THE BATHSHEBA NARRATIVE IS ABOUT ACCOUNTABILITY\nDavid summons Bathsheba while her husband Uriah is away fighting, then arranges for Uriah's death to conceal the pregnancy. The king's greater power makes his wrongdoing especially serious. Nathan's parable causes David to condemn an injustice before recognising his own conduct. David admits sin, but forgiveness does not erase all consequences. Psalm 51 is traditionally associated with this episode through its heading. A lesson that simply says 'David committed adultery but repented' omits the abuse of authority, attempted concealment and deliberate killing of Uriah.\n\nMERCY TO MEPHIBOSHETH VERSUS THE FAMILY CRISIS\nDavid honours his covenant with Jonathan by restoring land to Mephibosheth, Jonathan's disabled son, and giving him a place at the royal table. In contrast, David's family experiences violent conflict: Amnon assaults Tamar, Absalom kills Amnon, then seeks the throne. These episodes show that kindness in one area does not remove the need for justice and wise decisions elsewhere. Mephibosheth is not David's son; Absalom is. JAMB often tests these family relationships.\n\nWHAT THE CENSUS STORY TEACHES\nIn 2 Samuel 24 David orders a military census, later acknowledges sin and chooses to fall into God's hands rather than human enemies. At Araunah's threshing floor he refuses to give an offering that costs him nothing. The narrative links leadership, responsibility and costly worship. Its parallel in 1 Chronicles 21 differs in some numerical and narrative details; avoid blending figures without noting the book. The main principle is not that counting people is always sinful, but that David's particular action and motives drew divine judgement in the narrative.\n\nDAVID'S FINAL SUCCESSION AND ITS POLITICAL CONTEXT\nAdonijah attempts to claim the throne, but Nathan and Bathsheba intervene and David orders Solomon's anointing at Gihon by Zadok and Nathan. This is not the same anointing as Samuel's earlier anointing of David. The story closes a long arc: David receives a promise about his house, fails in important ways, and must still secure an orderly succession. Leadership includes both personal character and responsible public decisions.\n\nWORKED JAMB-STYLE INTERPRETATION QUESTIONS\nExample (i): Which statement best describes David's conduct toward Saul?\nA. He approved of Saul's attempts to kill him\nB. He refused to murder Saul while taking steps to protect himself\nC. He never left Saul's palace\nD. He made Jonathan king\nSolution: 1 Samuel 24 and 26 show restraint while David remains in hiding. The other answers contradict the narrative.\nCorrect answer: B.\n\nExample (ii): Why did Nathan tell the story of the rich man and poor man's lamb?\nA. To praise David's tax policy\nB. To expose David's injustice toward Uriah and Bathsheba\nC. To explain how to build the temple\nD. To appoint Absalom\nSolution: 2 Samuel 12 uses the parable to confront David's abuse of power. The other options belong to unrelated matters.\nCorrect answer: B.\n\nExample (iii): Which distinction between David's anointing and reign is correct?\nA. First anointed at Hebron, first ruled at Bethlehem\nB. First anointed by Samuel at Bethlehem, later ruled Judah at Hebron\nC. Anointed only after building the temple\nD. First anointed by Nathan at Gihon\nSolution: 1 Samuel 16 places Samuel's anointing at Bethlehem; 2 Samuel 2 places his rule over Judah at Hebron. Gihon concerns Solomon.\nCorrect answer: B.\n\nExample (iv): The main difference between Nathan's messages in 2 Samuel 7 and 12 is that\nA. one concerns a dynastic promise and the other rebukes wrongdoing\nB. both command David to kill Saul\nC. both appoint Adonijah\nD. one forbids repentance and the other forbids worship\nSolution: Chapter 7 concerns God's promise to David's house; chapter 12 confronts the Bathsheba/Uriah wrongdoing.\nCorrect answer: A.\n\nExample (v): Which action shows David's covenant loyalty to Jonathan?\nA. Building an altar on Carmel\nB. Restoring Saul's land to Mephibosheth\nC. Appointing Absalom king\nD. Sending Uriah to battle\nSolution: 2 Samuel 9 records David's kindness to Jonathan's son Mephibosheth. The other options are unrelated or wrong.\nCorrect answer: B.\n\nQUICK UNDERSTANDING CHECK — SPECIFIC TO THIS LESSON\n1. Distinguish Bethlehem, Hebron and Jerusalem in David's life.\n2. Explain the differences between the cave and sleeping-camp episodes involving Saul.\n3. Compare Nathan's message in 2 Samuel 7 with his rebuke in 2 Samuel 12.\n4. Why does David's repentance not remove the need to discuss the harm done to Uriah and Bathsheba?\n\nPRACTICE JAMB PAST QUESTIONS\nOpen the lesson's linked past questions and compare each answer against the exact biblical passage. Distinguish close alternatives before selecting your answer."}},{"id":"trial-crucifixion","revised":true,"old":{"title":"Jesus' trials, crucifixion and burial","body":"The passion narratives describe Jesus' suffering, trials and death. Matthew, Mark, Luke and John contain related accounts with different emphases. Read each Gospel's sequence before combining details; a statement that is true in one account may not answer a question explicitly asking about another.\n\nPeter's first denial follows a servant girl's recognition of him. The Gospel accounts contain related scenes but should be read separately when a question asks for a particular sequence. In John's trial narrative, Jesus' accusers first call him an evildoer and later invoke his claim to be Son of God. A stem with no specified stage can make more than one accusation defensible.\n\nLuke narrates the crucifixion, the division of clothing by lots, the rulers' scoffing and the soldiers' mockery. Jesus' final words in Luke commit his spirit to the Father. “I thirst” and the words to his mother belong to John. Recognising a genuine saying is not enough if the question specifies a Gospel.\n\nAt the Jewish hearing, Jesus' answer combines his identity with the Son of Man seated at the right hand of Power and coming with clouds; the charge of blasphemy follows that combined declaration. Pilate marvels at Jesus' silence before accusations. \n\nMatthew takes Jesus after his arrest to Caiaphas, the high priest; John first names Annas. Luke says Pilate sends Jesus to Herod because he learns that Jesus belongs to Herod's Galilean jurisdiction. Herod is pleased because he has long wanted to see him and hopes for a sign. Neither fact means Herod's court was necessarily the final legal authority over every Roman death sentence.\n\nIn Matthew, Joseph of Arimathea is a disciple who requests Jesus' body. John records “It is finished,” understood within Christian teaching as completion of his redemptive work. The torn Temple curtain signifies access to God through Christ. Keep the speaker, audience and point in the narrative together instead of selecting any familiar name associated with the crucifixion.\n\nMark says Pilate recognises the chief priests' envy. Luke's criminal acknowledges wrongdoing and appeals to Jesus, who promises paradise. These details must not be replaced with generic claims that every observer praised him.\n\nPilate's wife warns him after suffering in a dream about Jesus. Simon of Cyrene is compelled to carry Jesus' cross; Cyrene lies in North Africa. This records one person's involvement, not proof that every African accepted or rejected Jesus. The Gospels do not describe a continent-wide response at that moment."},"new":{"title":"Jesus' trials, crucifixion and burial","body":"1. SETTING: FROM JERUSALEM ENTRY TO ARREST\nJesus entered Jerusalem riding a donkey in fulfilment of prophetic imagery (Matthew 21; Mark 11; Luke 19; John 12). Crowds welcomed him with praise. He challenged exploitation in the temple and taught publicly, intensifying conflict with religious authorities. Judas Iscariot, one of the Twelve, agreed to betray Jesus. Matthew 26:15 specifies thirty pieces of silver. The Gospels differ in their presentation of timing and details of the final meal; learn each account's wording rather than forcing every scene into one artificial chronology.\n\n2. THE LAST SUPPER (MATTHEW 26:17–30; MARK 14; LUKE 22)\nJesus shared a final meal with his disciples. In the Synoptic accounts he took bread, gave thanks or blessed it, broke it and associated it with his body. He gave a cup and connected it with covenant blood; Luke 22:20 calls it the new covenant in his blood. Jesus warned that one of the disciples would betray him. Matthew identifies the betrayer through Jesus' exchange with Judas; John 13 describes Jesus giving a morsel to Judas. Jesus also predicted Peter's denial before the cock crowed. The meal's importance is Jesus' self-giving and covenant, not a simple farewell feast.\n\n3. GETHSEMANE AND THE ARREST (MATTHEW 26:36–56)\nJesus went to Gethsemane on the Mount of Olives with disciples and took Peter, James and John farther. He prayed in anguish, submitting his will to the Father's, while the disciples repeatedly fell asleep. Judas arrived with an armed crowd and identified Jesus with a kiss in the Synoptic accounts. One follower drew a sword and cut off the ear of the high priest's servant; John 18:10 names Peter as the swordsman and Malchus as the servant. Jesus rejected violent resistance. The disciples fled. Luke 22:51 reports that Jesus healed the servant's ear.\n\n4. JESUS BEFORE THE HIGH PRIEST AND COUNCIL (MATTHEW 26:57–68; MARK 14)\nJesus was brought to Caiaphas the high priest, where religious leaders sought testimony against him. Witnesses disagreed, and statements about destroying and rebuilding the temple became part of the accusation. When asked whether he was the Messiah, Son of God, Jesus answered in language associated with the Son of Man at God's right hand and coming with the clouds. The high priest tore his garments and declared blasphemy; the council judged Jesus deserving of death. Some mocked, spat on and struck him. John's Gospel gives additional detail about Annas before Caiaphas (John 18:12–24). The Jewish council hearing is not the same as Pilate's Roman trial.\n\n5. PETER'S THREE DENIALS (MATTHEW 26:69–75; MARK 14; LUKE 22; JOHN 18)\nWhile Jesus was questioned, Peter remained nearby in the courtyard. He denied knowing Jesus three times when challenged by people present. A cock crowed, and Peter remembered Jesus' prediction and wept bitterly. Mark 14:30 specifically predicts the cock crowing twice before the three denials, while other Gospel summaries commonly refer to the cock crowing. The central fact is three denials, not three different disciples denying him. Peter's later restoration appears especially in John 21.\n\n6. JUDAS' REMORSE AND DEATH (MATTHEW 27:3–10; ACTS 1:18–19)\nMatthew reports that Judas regretted betraying innocent blood and returned the thirty silver pieces to the chief priests and elders. They refused to accept responsibility; Judas threw the money into the temple and went away and hanged himself. The priests used the money to buy the potter's field for burying foreigners, called the Field of Blood. Acts 1:18–19 presents a different description associated with Judas' death and the field. In exams, distinguish Matthew's account of returned money and hanging from Acts' description, rather than claiming both books use identical words.\n\n7. JESUS BEFORE PILATE AND HEROD (LUKE 23; JOHN 18–19)\nReligious leaders brought Jesus to Pontius Pilate, the Roman governor, accusing him of political wrongdoing and claiming kingship. Pilate questioned Jesus about being king of the Jews and found no basis for a capital charge in Luke's presentation. Learning Jesus was Galilean, Pilate sent him to Herod Antipas, who was in Jerusalem; Herod questioned him and mocked him, then returned him to Pilate (Luke 23:6–12). The crowd demanded the release of Barabbas, a prisoner associated with rebellion and murder, rather than Jesus. Pilate eventually handed Jesus over to be crucified. Matthew alone describes Pilate washing his hands before the crowd (Matthew 27:24).\n\n8. MOCKERY AND THE JOURNEY TO GOLGOTHA\nRoman soldiers clothed Jesus in a robe, placed a crown of thorns on his head, gave him a reed and mocked him as king of the Jews. He was beaten and led out to be crucified. In the Synoptic Gospels Simon of Cyrene was compelled to carry the cross; Mark 15:21 identifies him as father of Alexander and Rufus. John 19:17 describes Jesus going out bearing his own cross. The crucifixion site is called Golgotha, meaning Place of a Skull. These accounts differ in emphasis and should be cited according to the Gospel asked about.\n\n9. THE CRUCIFIXION AND KEY DETAILS (MARK 15; MATTHEW 27; LUKE 23; JOHN 19)\nJesus was crucified between two others. Mark 15:25 places crucifixion at the third hour (about 9 a.m. in the ancient hour system), while John 19:14 mentions about the sixth hour in connection with the sentencing; this is a known difference between Gospel accounts. Darkness covered the land from the sixth to ninth hour in the Synoptics. An inscription identified Jesus as king of the Jews, with wording varying by Gospel. Soldiers divided his clothing and cast lots. Luke 23 records Jesus asking forgiveness for his executioners and promising the repentant criminal that he would be with him in paradise. John 19 records Jesus entrusting his mother to the beloved disciple and saying 'I thirst' and 'It is finished.' Matthew and Mark record the cry 'My God, my God, why have you forsaken me?' drawn from Psalm 22. Do not attribute every saying to every Gospel.\n\n10. JESUS' DEATH AND THE TEMPLE CURTAIN\nJesus died on the cross. The Synoptic Gospels report that the temple curtain was torn in two; Matthew also reports an earthquake and opened tombs (Matthew 27:51–53). A Roman centurion responded to Jesus' death with a confession: Mark 15:39 says 'Truly this man was God's Son'; Luke 23:47 says 'Certainly this man was innocent' or righteous, depending on translation. These are not word-for-word identical. In John 19 a soldier pierced Jesus' side, and blood and water came out; the soldiers did not break his legs because he was already dead. The accounts present Jesus' death as central to Christian salvation.\n\n11. BURIAL BY JOSEPH OF ARIMATHEA (MATTHEW 27:57–61; MARK 15; LUKE 23; JOHN 19)\nJoseph of Arimathea, a respected council member and follower or sympathiser of Jesus, asked Pilate for Jesus' body. He wrapped it in linen and placed it in a tomb cut in rock, then rolled a stone to its entrance. John 19 adds Nicodemus, who brought a mixture of myrrh and aloes, about a hundred Roman pounds by the text's description. Women observed where Jesus was laid. Matthew reports guards posted at the tomb at the authorities' request (Matthew 27:62–66). The burial was hurried because of the approaching Sabbath.\n\n12. RESURRECTION: KEEP THE ACCOUNTS DISTINCT\nOn the first day of the week women came to the tomb and found the stone moved and Jesus' body absent. The Gospels vary in named visitors and the descriptions of angels or messengers: Matthew names Mary Magdalene and the other Mary; Mark names Mary Magdalene, Mary mother of James and Salome; Luke names Mary Magdalene, Joanna, Mary mother of James and others; John initially focuses on Mary Magdalene. Matthew describes an angel descending and rolling back the stone; Mark describes a young man inside; Luke describes two men in dazzling clothes; John later describes two angels. These differences should be learned accurately, not silently merged.\n\n13. EXAM DISTINCTIONS\nBetrayal price in Matthew: thirty pieces of silver. High priest: Caiaphas; Roman governor: Pontius Pilate; Galilean ruler in Luke: Herod Antipas. Peter denied Jesus three times. Simon of Cyrene carried the cross in the Synoptics. Golgotha means Place of a Skull. Joseph of Arimathea requested the body; John also mentions Nicodemus. Gospel-specific words from the cross and tomb visitors must be matched carefully.\n\nDEEPER GUIDED TEACHING — REASONS, CONSEQUENCES AND EXAM INTERPRETATION\nHOW TO FOLLOW THE TRIALS WITHOUT CONFUSING AUTHORITIES\nThe religious hearings and Roman proceedings answer different questions. The high priest and council examine Jesus in relation to religious claims and alleged blasphemy. Pilate, the Roman governor, considers accusations with political significance, especially kingship. Luke alone records Pilate sending Jesus to Herod Antipas because Jesus was Galilean. Caiaphas is the high priest, not the Roman governor; Herod Antipas is not Herod the Great who appears in Matthew's birth narrative. The narrative moves from arrest to religious questioning to Roman sentencing and crucifixion.\n\nJUDAS AND PETER: DIFFERENT FAILURES, DIFFERENT ACCOUNTS\nJudas betrays Jesus for thirty pieces of silver in Matthew and later returns the money in remorse. Peter denies knowing Jesus three times under pressure and weeps when he remembers Jesus' prediction. The Gospels present these events differently; Judas' death is described in Matthew 27 and Acts 1 in differing ways. Students should not assume remorse, repentance and restoration are identical terms. John's Gospel later narrates Peter's restoration (John 21), whereas the Gospels do not narrate Judas' restoration.\n\nTHE MEANING OF THE CUP AND THE CROSS\nAt the Last Supper Jesus identifies bread with his body and the cup with covenant blood; Luke and Paul explicitly use the expression 'new covenant in my blood'. In Gethsemane Jesus prays about the cup of suffering. These two 'cup' references are connected to the approaching death but occur in different settings. Crucifixion was a Roman form of execution designed to shame and punish. The Gospels interpret Jesus' death in relation to Scripture, forgiveness, covenant and obedience. A JAMB question about the cup should be answered from its precise context.\n\nGOSPEL-SPECIFIC DETAILS: DO NOT MERGE EVERYTHING\nMatthew reports Pilate washing his hands and guards at the tomb. Mark names Simon of Cyrene as father of Alexander and Rufus and records the cock crowing twice in Peter's prediction. Luke includes Jesus before Herod Antipas, the repentant criminal's promise of paradise and the centurion's statement of Jesus' innocence. John names Peter and Malchus in the ear-cutting incident, mentions Nicodemus at the burial and records 'It is finished'. These are differences in what each evangelist reports; students should not silently attribute a distinctive detail to all four books.\n\nTHE WORDS FROM THE CROSS IN THEIR SOURCES\nMatthew 27:46 and Mark 15:34 give the cry 'My God, my God, why have you forsaken me?' Luke 23:34 includes 'Father, forgive them' in many manuscripts, with an important textual variation; Luke 23:43 records the promise to the repentant criminal, and Luke 23:46 'Father, into your hands I commend my spirit'. John 19:26–27 entrusts Jesus' mother to the beloved disciple; John 19:28 records 'I thirst'; John 19:30 records 'It is finished'. The traditional 'seven last sayings' combines passages from different Gospels. It is not a seven-saying list written in one Gospel.\n\nWHY JOSEPH OF ARIMATHEA MATTERS\nAfter Jesus' death Joseph of Arimathea requests the body from Pilate and arranges burial in a rock-hewn tomb. John adds Nicodemus and the burial spices. The women observing the burial connect the burial story with their later visit to the tomb. Matthew's guards are part of Matthew's account, not a detail supplied by Luke or John. The hurried burial before Sabbath helps explain why women later come with spices in Mark and Luke. The burial is an essential link between the death and empty-tomb narratives.\n\nTHE EMPTY TOMB AND WHAT EACH ACCOUNT SAYS\nThe resurrection accounts agree on the significance of the first day of the week and the empty tomb, but their named visitors and messengers differ. Matthew names Mary Magdalene and the other Mary and describes an angel rolling away the stone. Mark names Mary Magdalene, Mary mother of James and Salome, and a young man in white. Luke names several women and describes two men in dazzling clothes. John initially focuses on Mary Magdalene and later describes two angels. In an exam asking 'according to Mark', give Mark's list, not a combined list.\n\nTEXTUAL AND CHRONOLOGICAL CAUTION\nMark 15:25 places the crucifixion at the third hour, while John 19:14 says it was about the sixth hour at a stage of the proceedings. There are scholarly proposals about different time reckoning, but the texts should not be presented as verbally identical. Similarly, Mark 16:9–20 is absent from some of the earliest manuscripts; students should know which passage a question quotes. Accurate teaching reports what each Gospel says and avoids claiming certainty where accounts or manuscripts differ.\n\nWORKED JAMB-STYLE INTERPRETATION QUESTIONS\nExample (i): Which sequence best distinguishes Jesus' proceedings in Luke?\nA. Pilate → Herod Antipas → Pilate\nB. Herod the Great → Caiaphas → Caesar Augustus\nC. Joseph of Arimathea → Pilate → Gabriel\nD. Peter → Nicodemus → Herod the Great\nSolution: Luke 23:6–12 describes Pilate sending Jesus to Herod Antipas, who returned him to Pilate. The other sequences mix unrelated figures.\nCorrect answer: A.\n\nExample (ii): Which Gospel specifically names Malchus as the high priest's servant?\nA. Matthew\nB. Mark\nC. Luke\nD. John\nSolution: John 18:10 identifies Malchus and Peter. Other Gospels describe the incident without both names.\nCorrect answer: D.\n\nExample (iii): Which saying from the cross appears in John 19:30?\nA. Father, forgive them\nB. It is finished\nC. My God, why have you forsaken me?\nD. Today you will be with me in paradise\nSolution: John 19:30 records 'It is finished'. The other sayings belong to Luke or Matthew/Mark.\nCorrect answer: B.\n\nExample (iv): Why is it inaccurate to say all four Gospels name the same women at the tomb?\nA. None names any woman\nB. Their named visitors and descriptions differ\nC. Only John mentions an empty tomb\nD. The women all arrived before Jesus died\nSolution: Matthew, Mark, Luke and John give overlapping but non-identical lists and details. The other claims are false.\nCorrect answer: B.\n\nExample (v): Who secured Jesus' body for burial from Pilate?\nA. Simon of Cyrene\nB. Joseph of Arimathea\nC. Barabbas\nD. Caiaphas\nSolution: All four Gospels associate Joseph of Arimathea with the burial. Simon carries the cross in Synoptics; Barabbas was released; Caiaphas was high priest.\nCorrect answer: B.\n\nQUICK UNDERSTANDING CHECK — SPECIFIC TO THIS LESSON\n1. Construct a sequence from Gethsemane through the religious and Roman hearings to burial.\n2. Match Caiaphas, Pilate and Herod Antipas to their roles.\n3. Identify at least three details that belong specifically to Luke or John.\n4. Explain why the traditional seven sayings from the cross cannot be cited as one Gospel passage.\n\nPRACTICE JAMB PAST QUESTIONS\nOpen the lesson's linked past questions and compare each answer against the exact biblical passage. Distinguish close alternatives before selecting your answer."}},{"id":"amos-hosea","revised":true,"old":{"title":"Amos and Hosea: justice, covenant love and true worship","body":"Amos and Hosea challenge unfaithfulness in the northern kingdom of Israel. Their messages connect worship with conduct, but their characteristic images and quotations should not be exchanged. Amos is often called a prophet of social justice and Hosea a prophet of divine love; these are summaries, not claims that either prophet speaks about only one subject.\n\nAmos says he was a herdsman and a dresser of sycamore figs when the LORD called him. Describing him simply as a professional court prophet misses his own account. His opposition to exploitation includes selling the righteous for silver, trampling the poor and enjoying wealth gained through injustice. True worship requires justice and righteousness rather than assemblies, music and sacrifices that cover wrongdoing. “Let justice roll down like waters” expresses a sustained demand, not an occasional charitable gesture.\n\nAmaziah, the priest of Bethel, reports Amos to Jeroboam as a conspirator. Accusation by a powerful official is distinct from proof that the prophet is disloyal to God. Amos announces judgement, including a famine of hearing the LORD's word, but his book also contains restoration hope. It is therefore unsafe to claim that hope after punishment belongs exclusively to another prophet.\n\nHosea uses marriage and family imagery to depict God's persistent love and Israel's covenant betrayal. Harlotry represents pursuit of other gods and misplaced loyalties. The people are destroyed for lack of knowledge: the setting concerns rejection of God's instruction, not simply absence of academic qualifications. Hosea calls for knowledge of God and steadfast love rather than burnt offerings. Ritual cannot replace faithful relationship and conduct.\n\nThe prophet also announces consequences: eating without satisfaction, harlotry without increase, and the naming of a child Not Pitied concerning the house of Israel. Such images must not become a rule blaming innocent children for adult misconduct. Hosea 2:18 promises breaking bow, sword and war, while chapter 14 pictures restored Israel beneath God's shadow.\n\nIn a Nigerian classroom, cheating another pupil and then displaying religious devotion illustrates the inconsistency these prophets expose. Justice protects a neighbour's rights; steadfast love remains faithful in relationship. Both challenge worship that leaves exploitation and idolatry untouched.\n\nHosea 1:4 names the bloodshed of Jezreel in announcing judgement on Jehu's house; Jezreel must not be replaced with Jezebel. Amos' image of the earth melting expresses God's sovereign authority, not merely the ability to touch soil."},"new":{"title":"Amos and Hosea: justice, covenant love and true worship","body":"1. HISTORICAL BACKGROUND: NORTHERN ISRAEL IN THE EIGHTH CENTURY BCE\nAmos and Hosea preached mainly to the northern kingdom of Israel during the eighth century BCE, when economic prosperity for some existed alongside injustice, idolatry and political instability. Amos 1:1 dates his ministry to the days of Uzziah king of Judah and Jeroboam II king of Israel, two years before an earthquake. Hosea 1:1 names kings of Judah—Uzziah, Jotham, Ahaz and Hezekiah—and Jeroboam II of Israel. Israel's capital was Samaria; the kingdom eventually fell to Assyria in 722/721 BCE. Amos emphasises justice and true worship; Hosea uses marital imagery to describe God's covenant love and Israel's unfaithfulness.\n\n2. AMOS' CALL AND BACKGROUND (AMOS 1; 7)\nAmos came from Tekoa in Judah, yet prophesied primarily against the northern kingdom. Amos 1:1 calls him one of the shepherds of Tekoa. When Amaziah, priest of Bethel, ordered him to leave, Amos said he was not a prophet or a prophet's son but a herdsman and dresser of sycamore figs; the Lord took him from following the flock and commanded him to prophesy to Israel (Amos 7:14–15). His authority came from God's commission, not membership in a professional prophetic group. Amaziah was priest at Bethel, not the king of Israel.\n\n3. AMOS' ORACLES AGAINST NATIONS (AMOS 1–2)\nAmos begins with judgements against neighbouring nations, including Damascus, Gaza, Tyre, Edom, Ammon and Moab, before turning to Judah and Israel. The repeated formula 'For three transgressions ... and for four' announces persistent wrongdoing. The charges include cruelty in warfare, trafficking people and violations of human dignity. Israel might initially welcome condemnation of its neighbours, but Amos then confronts Israel's own injustices. This structure shows that God's moral concern is not limited to one nation.\n\n4. INJUSTICE, BRIBERY AND EXPLOITATION (AMOS 2; 5; 8)\nAmos condemned selling the righteous for silver and the needy for a pair of sandals (2:6), trampling the poor and denying justice. Amos 5:10–12 criticises those who hate honest correction at the city gate, take bribes and push aside the needy. The gate was a public place where disputes and legal matters were heard. Amos 8:4–6 describes merchants eager for religious observances to end so they could sell grain dishonestly, making the ephah small and the shekel great, and using false balances. A small ephah means giving less grain; a large shekel means demanding more payment. The sin was deliberate commercial fraud, not trade itself.\n\n5. TRUE WORSHIP AND JUSTICE (AMOS 5:21–24)\nGod declares through Amos that he hates religious festivals and does not accept offerings or songs when worshippers practise injustice. The famous demand is: 'Let justice roll down like waters, and righteousness like an ever-flowing stream' (Amos 5:24). Justice means fair treatment and protection of rights; righteousness involves living rightly before God and others. Amos does not teach that worship is always worthless, but that ceremonies without justice and obedience are unacceptable. In an exam, connect this verse specifically to Amos.\n\n6. THE DAY OF THE LORD (AMOS 5:18–20)\nSome Israelites expected the day of the Lord to mean victory and blessing for them. Amos warned that for an unjust people it would be darkness, not light. He compared it to a person escaping a lion only to meet a bear, or entering a house and being bitten by a snake while leaning on the wall. The message reverses false confidence: belonging to Israel did not exempt people from judgement. Do not confuse Amos' warning with Paul's description of the day of the Lord coming like a thief in 1 Thessalonians 5.\n\n7. AMOS' VISIONS (AMOS 7–9)\nAmos saw locusts devouring the land and then a consuming fire; he interceded, and God relented concerning these visions (Amos 7:1–6). In the third vision he saw the Lord standing by a wall with a plumb line, symbolising a standard by which Israel would be judged (7:7–9). In Amos 8 he saw a basket of summer fruit, a wordplay in Hebrew signalling that Israel's end had come. In Amos 9 he saw the Lord beside the altar, announcing inescapable judgement. These objects must not be confused with Jeremiah's almond branch and boiling pot or Ezekiel's scroll.\n\n8. AMAZIAH'S OPPOSITION AT BETHEL (AMOS 7:10–17)\nAmaziah, priest of Bethel, sent word to Jeroboam II accusing Amos of conspiracy and warned Amos to flee to Judah, earn his bread there and prophesy no more at Bethel because it was the king's sanctuary. Amos replied that God had taken him from his ordinary work and sent him to Israel. He announced judgement against Amaziah's household and Israel's exile. The conflict concerns resistance to unwelcome prophetic truth and the misuse of religious office to defend royal power.\n\n9. HOSEA'S MARRIAGE AS A SIGN (HOSEA 1–3)\nGod instructed Hosea to marry Gomer daughter of Diblaim, and the marriage became a sign of Israel's covenant unfaithfulness. The children in Hosea 1 received symbolic names: Jezreel, pointing to judgement on the house of Jehu; Lo-ruhamah, meaning 'not pitied' or 'no mercy'; and Lo-ammi, meaning 'not my people'. Later passages promise reversal and restoration. Hosea 3 portrays Hosea's renewed commitment to an unfaithful woman as a picture of God's enduring love for Israel. Students should distinguish Gomer, Hosea's wife, from Jezebel, Ahab's wife.\n\n10. HOSEA: 'I DESIRE MERCY, NOT SACRIFICE' (HOSEA 6:6)\nHosea declares God's desire for steadfast love or mercy rather than sacrifice, and knowledge of God rather than burnt offerings. The Hebrew term hesed conveys loyal covenant love, faithfulness and mercy. Hosea criticised people who performed religious rituals but ignored faithfulness to God and neighbour. Jesus later cites Hosea 6:6 in Matthew 9:13 and 12:7. The verse does not mean every sacrifice in ancient Israel was forbidden; it places covenant love and true knowledge of God above empty ritual.\n\n11. HOSEA'S CALL TO REPENTANCE AND HOPE (HOSEA 11; 14)\nHosea 11 pictures God as a loving parent: 'When Israel was a child, I loved him, and out of Egypt I called my son.' Yet Israel turned toward idols. God describes teaching Ephraim to walk and drawing the people with cords of kindness, then expresses compassion despite judgement. Hosea 14 calls Israel to return to the Lord, bring words of repentance and reject reliance on Assyria, warhorses and handmade gods. God promises to heal their disloyalty and love them freely. Hosea combines serious warning with the possibility of restoration.\n\n12. AMOS AND HOSEA COMPARED\nAmos: from Tekoa; herdsman and dresser of sycamore figs; challenged Amaziah at Bethel; exposed bribery, dishonest scales and oppression; demanded justice like waters; visions include plumb line and summer fruit. Hosea: married Gomer; children named Jezreel, Lo-ruhamah and Lo-ammi; condemned idolatry and covenant infidelity; taught 'mercy, not sacrifice' and God's persistent love. Both challenged superficial worship and called for repentance, but they used different images and emphases.\n\nDEEPER GUIDED TEACHING — REASONS, CONSEQUENCES AND EXAM INTERPRETATION\nTHE HISTORICAL SETTING EXPLAINS BOTH PROPHETS\nAmos and Hosea ministered during the northern kingdom's eighth-century troubles, including the reign of Jeroboam II. Wealth and outward religious activity coexisted with exploitation and worship of other gods. Amos, from Tekoa in Judah, addresses Israel's injustices; Hosea, through his marriage imagery, addresses Israel's broken covenant relationship. Both insist that belonging to God's people is not a guarantee against judgement. Their emphasis differs: Amos often uses courts, markets and social oppression; Hosea often uses family love, betrayal and return.\n\nWHAT 'JUSTICE AT THE GATE' MEANS\nIn ancient Israel the city gate was a public place for hearings and disputes. Amos condemns those who take bribes, hate truthful correction and deprive the poor of justice. Therefore 'justice' in Amos 5 is not merely private kindness; it includes honest legal decisions and protection from exploitation. Amos 8's dishonest ephah and shekel show commercial injustice: shrinking the measure of grain while increasing the weight used to charge payment. The prophet condemns cheating, not the legitimate sale of food.\n\nWHY GOD REJECTS FESTIVALS IN AMOS 5\nAmos 5:21–24 places songs, sacrifices and festivals beside the demand for justice. The problem is not music itself or every religious gathering. The problem is worship offered by people who continue to oppress neighbours. 'Let justice roll down like waters' calls for consistent, public righteousness rather than occasional ceremonial performance. In a JAMB question, the strongest answer links acceptable worship to ethical conduct; an answer claiming Amos opposed all prayer or sacrifice in every circumstance overstates the passage.\n\nTHE FIVE VISIONS: ORDER AND MEANING\nAmos 7–9 contains a sequence: locusts (7:1–3), fire (7:4–6), plumb line (7:7–9), basket of summer fruit (8:1–3), and the Lord beside the altar (9:1–4). Amos intercedes after the first two visions and God relents in those scenes. The plumb line communicates judgement according to God's standard; the summer fruit uses a Hebrew wordplay with 'end'. The altar vision announces judgement that cannot be escaped. Knowing the order prevents confusion with Jeremiah's almond branch, Ezekiel's scroll and Isaiah's temple vision.\n\nAMAZIAH AND THE CONFLICT OVER PROPHETIC AUTHORITY\nAmaziah is priest at Bethel, the royal sanctuary, and reports Amos' words to King Jeroboam II. He tells Amos to leave for Judah and prophesy there. Amos answers that he was a herdsman and dresser of sycamore figs until God commissioned him. His point is not that training is evil; it is that his authority does not depend on being paid by the royal shrine. Amos' unwillingness to flatter powerful people is part of his message about justice.\n\nHOSEA'S FAMILY NAMES ARE PROPHETIC SIGNS\nHosea's wife is Gomer daughter of Diblaim. Their children are associated with names carrying messages: Jezreel recalls coming judgement; Lo-ruhamah means 'not pitied' or 'no mercy'; Lo-ammi means 'not my people'. Later promises reverse the rejection and speak of restored relationship. The names are not random labels and should be explained together with Hosea's message about Israel's infidelity. Gomer must not be confused with Jezebel, who was Ahab's wife in the Elijah narratives.\n\nWHAT 'MERCY, NOT SACRIFICE' REALLY CONTRASTS\nHosea 6:6 uses the idea of steadfast covenant love, often rendered mercy or loyalty, and knowledge of God. The contrast is with ritual divorced from faithful conduct. Amos 5 and Hosea 6 therefore converge in rejecting hypocrisy, but Amos' memorable phrase is justice like waters, whereas Hosea's is mercy rather than sacrifice. Jesus cites Hosea 6:6 in Matthew 9:13 and 12:7, so the phrase can appear in a New Testament question too.\n\nHOSEA'S GOD AS PARENT AND THE POSSIBILITY OF RETURN\nHosea 11 pictures God teaching Ephraim to walk and drawing the people with cords of kindness, while Israel turns away. Hosea 14 urges Israel to return, confess and stop relying on Assyria, horses and idols. God's promise to heal their disloyalty shows that judgement and mercy are not mutually exclusive in the book. The point is covenant renewal, not that the earlier injustice was harmless.\n\nWORKED JAMB-STYLE INTERPRETATION QUESTIONS\nExample (i): Why did Amos condemn dishonest measures in the marketplace?\nA. Trade itself was forbidden\nB. Cheating deprived buyers and harmed the vulnerable\nC. Only foreign traders could sell grain\nD. Sacrifices had to be measured in shekels\nSolution: Amos 8:4–6 criticises false measures and balances. The other options misstate the issue.\nCorrect answer: B.\n\nExample (ii): Which interpretation best explains Amos 5:24?\nA. Justice should be continual and central to genuine worship\nB. All songs must be forbidden\nC. Only priests may defend the poor\nD. Water rituals replace moral conduct\nSolution: The verse demands justice and righteousness like a flowing stream in the context of empty ritual. The other answers misread the metaphor.\nCorrect answer: A.\n\nExample (iii): What was Amos' response to Amaziah's attempt to silence him?\nA. He admitted the king had appointed him\nB. He said the Lord called him from tending flocks\nC. He claimed to be the high priest\nD. He agreed that Bethel was free from judgement\nSolution: Amos 7:14–15 describes his ordinary work and divine commission. The other choices contradict the passage.\nCorrect answer: B.\n\nExample (iv): What does the name Lo-ammi signify in Hosea?\nA. God is my strength\nB. Not my people\nC. The Lord is peace\nD. The king has returned\nSolution: Hosea 1:9 explains Lo-ammi as 'not my people'. The other meanings are not given for this name.\nCorrect answer: B.\n\nExample (v): Which comparison is accurate?\nA. Amos — marriage to Gomer; Hosea — plumb line\nB. Amos — justice like waters; Hosea — mercy rather than sacrifice\nC. Amos — rebuilding Jerusalem's wall; Hosea — Daniel's lions\nD. Amos — burning coal; Hosea — boiling pot\nSolution: Amos 5:24 and Hosea 6:6 are the respective teachings. The other options misattribute events or symbols.\nCorrect answer: B.\n\nQUICK UNDERSTANDING CHECK — SPECIFIC TO THIS LESSON\n1. Explain how dishonest ephah and shekel measures harmed ordinary buyers.\n2. List Amos' five visions in order and explain the plumb line and summer fruit.\n3. Distinguish Amaziah the priest from Jeroboam II the king.\n4. Explain why Amos 5:24 and Hosea 6:6 are similar but not identical messages.\n\nPRACTICE JAMB PAST QUESTIONS\nOpen the lesson's linked past questions and compare each answer against the exact biblical passage. Distinguish close alternatives before selecting your answer."}},{"id":"bible-text-context","revised":true,"old":{"title":"Bible structure, translation and reading in context","body":"The Bible is a collection of books with different forms, including narrative, law, prophecy, poetry, Gospels and letters. Reading responsibly means identifying the book, speaker, audience and setting before turning a sentence into a general rule. A later tradition or an examination booklet's explanation must not silently replace what the passage itself says.\n\nThe New Testament contains twenty-seven books: four Gospels, Acts, twenty-one letters and Revelation. It is centred on Jesus Christ and the Christian message, not on Moses, Peter or Paul alone. Paul's important contribution does not make the whole collection simply Paul-oriented. The Gospels describe Jesus' ministry through teaching, healing, proclamation of God's kingdom and fulfilment of Scripture. His ministry should not be reduced to becoming a celebrated political ruler.\n\nTorah means instruction or law and commonly names the first five biblical books in Jewish tradition. Judaism is the religion and developing traditions of the Jewish people, not the name of one ancient legal code. The Talmud is a much later collection of rabbinic discussion of law and tradition. It cannot accurately be described as a law code already practised by Israel before Moses received the commandments. The Code of Hammurabi belongs to ancient Babylonian royal law, not Israel's Sinai covenant. A question that confuses these periods requires a chronology note rather than memorising its mistaken premise.\n\nThe Bible Society of Nigeria is an interdenominational organisation involved in Bible translation, publication and distribution in Nigerian languages. Translation helps readers encounter Scripture in a language they understand. This does not imply that the Society is the only organisation involved in Bible translation or that one English rendering removes every uncertainty about ancient words.\n\nJude 9 names Michael the archangel disputing with the devil about Moses' body. The verse does not itself use the name Lucifer. Distinguish the passage's wording from later identifications or artistic retellings. The same principle applies to symbolic claims: one person's presence in a narrative cannot by itself prove the response of an entire continent or nation.\n\nIn a Nigerian classroom, careful comparison of the source and an answer booklet is part of learning, not disrespect for Scripture. Reliable study keeps verified facts, interpretations and unresolved questions distinct."},"new":{"title":"Bible structure, translation and reading in context","body":"1. Reading biblical genres\nThe Pentateuch (Genesis–Deuteronomy) contains narrative and law. Joshua–Esther largely recount Israel's history; Job, Psalms, Proverbs, Ecclesiastes and Song of Songs use poetry and wisdom. The prophets address particular peoples and circumstances, often with symbolic language. Matthew, Mark, Luke and John are Gospels; Acts recounts the early church; letters teach communities and individuals; Revelation uses apocalyptic visions. A parable should not be read as a historical report of a named individual, and a proverb is not necessarily a guarantee without exceptions.\n\n2. The number and arrangement of books\nThe Protestant Bible has 39 Old Testament and 27 New Testament books, totalling 66. Catholic and Orthodox Christian traditions have additional books, so this number should be identified as Protestant rather than claimed as universal. The first five books are Genesis, Exodus, Leviticus, Numbers and Deuteronomy. The four Gospels precede Acts; Revelation is the last New Testament book. Chapters and verse divisions are later reference aids rather than divisions supplied by the original authors.\n\n3. Why the four Gospels differ\nMatthew, Mark and Luke are called Synoptic because of substantial overlapping material. John uses a distinctive structure and records extended speeches and signs. The wise men are in Matthew 2, whereas the shepherds and presentation in the temple appear in Luke 2. John records Lazarus' raising in chapter 11, while Luke alone gives the parable of the Good Samaritan in chapter 10. If an examination asks 'according to Luke', answer from Luke rather than combining Gospel accounts.\n\n4. Original languages and translation\nMost Old Testament books were written in Hebrew, with some Aramaic; the New Testament was written in Koine Greek. Translators differ in how literally they reproduce grammar and idioms. English versions may choose different wording while expressing similar meaning. Some manuscript copies differ in small or larger passages; Mark 16:9–20 is absent from some early manuscripts. A sound answer identifies the version, compares the surrounding passage and avoids pretending every ancient copy is identical.\n\n5. Context, speaker and audience\nAsk who speaks, who hears, when the words are said and what the author is trying to teach. Job's friends make confident claims that the book later criticises (Job 42:7), so quoting a character does not mean Scripture endorses that character's view. James criticises claims of faith with no action; Paul rejects boasting that justification is earned through works of the law. Reading their arguments in context prevents a superficial contradiction.\n\n6. How to use Scripture references\nGenesis 12:1–3 identifies chapter 12, verses 1 to 3. John 3:16 is not 1 John 3:16. First and Second Samuel are different books, as are First, Second and Third John. When a question asks for a speaker, location or number, check the precise passage before relying on a similar story elsewhere.\n\nWORKED JAMB-STYLE INTERPRETATION QUESTIONS\nExample (i): Which five books form the Pentateuch?\nA. Genesis, Exodus, Leviticus, Numbers and Deuteronomy\nB. Joshua, Judges, Ruth, Samuel and Kings\nC. Matthew, Mark, Luke, John and Acts\nD. Psalms, Proverbs, Job, Isaiah and Jeremiah\nSolution: The Pentateuch is the five-book Torah, beginning with Genesis and ending with Deuteronomy.\nCorrect answer: A.\n\nExample (ii): Which Gospel records the wise men's gifts?\nA. Mark\nB. Matthew\nC. Luke\nD. John\nSolution: Matthew 2 names gold, frankincense and myrrh. Luke instead describes shepherds.\nCorrect answer: B.\n\nExample (iii): Why is it risky to treat every speech in Job as correct teaching?\nA. Job is a Gospel\nB. The book later challenges the friends' claims\nC. Job has no narrator\nD. All speakers are kings\nSolution: Job 42:7 rebukes the friends; reporting a speech is not the same as endorsing it.\nCorrect answer: B.\n\nExample (iv): Which language predominates in the New Testament's original texts?\nA. Latin\nB. Hebrew\nC. Koine Greek\nD. Modern English\nSolution: The New Testament books were written in Koine Greek.\nCorrect answer: C.\n\nExample (v): Why are Matthew, Mark and Luke called Synoptic?\nA. They are Paul's letters\nB. They share substantial material and similar presentation\nC. They contain no miracles\nD. They were written by Moses\nSolution: The three Gospels overlap substantially in episodes and arrangement.\nCorrect answer: B.\n\nQUICK UNDERSTANDING CHECK — SPECIFIC TO THIS LESSON\n1. Distinguish Gospel, letter, prophecy and apocalyptic writing.\n2. Why should the translation and immediate context be checked?\n3. Explain why Matthew's wise men should not be attributed to Luke.\n4. What is the difference between John and 1 John?\n\nPRACTICE JAMB PAST QUESTIONS\nUse the lesson's linked past questions and compare each answer against the relevant passage.\n\nDEEPER TEACHING — HOW TO READ BIBLE PASSAGES ACCURATELY\nThe Bible is a library of writings, not one continuous narrative by a single human writer. The Old Testament contains law, history, poetry and prophetic writing; the New Testament contains Gospels, Acts, letters and Revelation. Christian traditions differ in their biblical canons: the Protestant Old Testament has 39 books and the New Testament 27, while Catholic and Orthodox Old Testament collections include additional books. In a JAMB question, attend to the translation or canon specified. Chapter and verse numbers are later reference aids, not divisions originally written by the biblical authors.\nGenre matters. A proverb expresses wisdom rather than a mechanical guarantee that every event will always occur in one way. A parable uses a story to teach a lesson; not every detail must be treated as a separate historical claim. A Gospel narrative reports Jesus' words and deeds from its author's perspective. A prophetic oracle often addresses a particular historical audience before later readers apply its lessons. Compare immediate context, speaker, audience and occasion before interpreting a verse. For example, Jeremiah 29:11 is addressed in a letter to exiles in Babylon (Jeremiah 29:1–14); it must not be presented as a guarantee that every individual will immediately prosper. Likewise, Matthew 4:6 records Satan quoting Scripture during Jesus' temptation; the fact that a statement is quoted in the Bible does not mean its use by the speaker is approved.\nExample (additional): Does a Bible verse spoken by an unreliable character automatically express the writer's approved teaching?\nSolution: No. Identify who speaks and how the surrounding narrative evaluates the statement. Satan quotes Psalm 91 in Matthew 4:6, but Jesus rejects the temptation in Matthew 4:7.\nQUICK UNDERSTANDING CHECK: Explain the difference between a Gospel, a proverb and a parable; identify the speaker and audience in a selected passage; explain why context changes interpretation.\n"}},{"id":"holiness-worship","revised":true,"old":{"title":"Holiness, worship and accountable use of authority","body":"Holiness concerns what is set apart for God and the reverent conduct appropriate to it. Biblical accounts of worship challenge the assumption that a religious title, royal status or impressive ceremony gives someone freedom to ignore responsibility. Read each episode in its own covenant and historical setting rather than turning ancient penalties into instructions for modern crowds.\n\nLeviticus 10 names Nadab and Abihu, sons of Aaron, as offering unauthorised fire before the LORD. Fire consumes them. Their offence must not be confused with Eli's sons' misconduct or with Saul's military disobedience. The account concerns worship contrary to the command, not mockery of Moses' speech. Numbers 20 separately records Aaron's death on Mount Hor, after his priestly garments are transferred to Eleazar.\n\nSecond Chronicles 26 describes King Uzziah entering the Temple to burn incense despite the priests' resistance. He becomes leprous and lives apart. He is not Ahab, and kingship does not make every priestly duty his own. The story joins misuse of power with disregard for limits on authority.\n\nJoshua 7 connects Israel's defeat at Ai with Achan's taking goods devoted to destruction. Such goods had been set apart under the command concerning Jericho. Achan concealed a garment, silver and gold in his tent. The narrative presents this breach of the covenant as affecting the community, explaining why merely sending more soldiers would not solve the problem. Achan confesses, and the account describes stoning and burning in the Valley of Achor. This ancient judgement must not be used as permission for a modern crowd to punish suspected wrongdoing; evidence and lawful justice remain necessary.\n\nTopheth is a place associated with child sacrifice in the Valley of Hinnom. Josiah defiles it to prevent offerings of children to Molech. It is not merely the name of a dance or a synonym for every religious ceremony. Asherah is associated with fertility in ancient Canaanite religion; the biblical polemic condemns such worship rather than recommending it. Asa destroys Maacah's Asherah image at the brook Kidron.\n\nThese narratives connect worship with obedience and accountability. A Nigerian school official cannot make misuse of funds right by organising a religious service. At the same time, the ancient accounts do not authorise personal vengeance, punishment without evidence or contempt for people of another religion. The practical lesson is responsible conduct, not permission to imitate every historical penalty."},"new":{"title":"Holiness, worship and accountable use of authority","body":"1. Holiness includes conduct\nHoliness means belonging to God and living consistently with God's character, not merely taking part in ceremonies. Leviticus 19 joins the command to be holy with justice, honest dealing, care for vulnerable people and love of neighbour. Amos 5 and Isaiah 1 condemn sacrifices and songs when worshippers oppress others. The prophets are not simply forbidding every ritual: they challenge worship divorced from righteousness.\n\n2. Aaron's sons and priestly responsibility\nNadab and Abihu, sons of Aaron, offer unauthorised fire in Leviticus 10 and die. Eleazar and Ithamar are Aaron's other sons and continue priestly duties. The episode concerns responsibility in a specific priestly setting; it does not justify interpreting every modern accident as divine punishment. The important examination distinction is Nadab and Abihu versus Eli's sons Hophni and Phinehas.\n\n3. Eli, Hophni and Phinehas\nFirst Samuel 2 depicts Hophni and Phinehas abusing priestly authority and treating offerings with contempt. Eli rebukes them but does not effectively restrain their misconduct. First Samuel 3 announces judgement; First Samuel 4 reports the sons' deaths in battle, capture of the ark and Eli's death. A religious office does not excuse injustice, and a parent's position does not remove responsibility to address harm.\n\n4. Saul's two failures\nFirst Samuel 13 concerns Saul offering sacrifice instead of waiting for Samuel. First Samuel 15 concerns Saul sparing Agag and valuable livestock contrary to the command concerning Amalek, then claiming the animals were intended for sacrifice. Samuel's response that obedience is better than sacrifice identifies the central issue. Do not merge the two events or say that offering animals was the only wrongdoing.\n\n5. Uzzah, Obed-edom and the ark\nSecond Samuel 6 reports Uzzah touching the ark as the oxen stumble and dying. David becomes afraid, and the ark remains for three months with Obed-edom, whose household is blessed. David later brings the ark to Jerusalem. First Chronicles 15 discusses proper Levitical carrying of the ark. Uzzah is not Obed-edom; the former dies and the latter hosts the ark.\n\n6. Temple worship and prophetic criticism\nSolomon builds and dedicates the temple in 1 Kings 6–8, praying that God hear those who turn toward it. Jeremiah 7 later warns against trusting the temple while practising injustice. Jesus' temple action challenges abuse of a house of prayer; John 2 places it early in his Gospel, whereas the Synoptics place it near the final week. A building cannot make wrongdoing acceptable.\n\n7. Accountability of leaders\nSamuel confronts Saul, Nathan confronts David, Elijah challenges Ahab over Naboth, and Amos opposes injustice at Bethel. Each shows that kings, priests and prophets must be judged by conduct rather than status. Worship and authority are trustworthy when linked to truth, justice, obedience and care.\n\nWORKED JAMB-STYLE INTERPRETATION QUESTIONS\nExample (i): What did Samuel mean by 'obedience is better than sacrifice'?\nA. Religious excuses cannot replace obeying God's instruction\nB. Sacrifice was never practised\nC. Saul was the high priest\nD. All kings were forbidden to pray\nSolution: First Samuel 15 condemns Saul's disobedience concealed by an appeal to sacrifice.\nCorrect answer: A.\n\nExample (ii): Which sons of Eli abused their priestly office?\nA. Nadab and Abihu\nB. Hophni and Phinehas\nC. Cain and Abel\nD. Gershom and Eliezer\nSolution: First Samuel 2 identifies Hophni and Phinehas.\nCorrect answer: B.\n\nExample (iii): Who kept the ark for three months after Uzzah's death?\nA. Nathan\nB. Obed-edom\nC. Jeremiah\nD. Gehazi\nSolution: Second Samuel 6 names Obed-edom.\nCorrect answer: B.\n\nExample (iv): Why does Amos criticise religious festivals in Amos 5?\nA. All music is forbidden\nB. Worshippers neglected justice and righteousness\nC. The temple was in Babylon\nD. The priests were foreigners\nSolution: Amos 5:21–24 links rejected ceremonies with the need for justice.\nCorrect answer: B.\n\nExample (v): Which king was rebuked by Nathan over Bathsheba and Uriah?\nA. Saul\nB. David\nC. Solomon\nD. Ahab\nSolution: Second Samuel 12 records Nathan confronting David.\nCorrect answer: B.\n\nQUICK UNDERSTANDING CHECK — SPECIFIC TO THIS LESSON\n1. Compare Nadab and Abihu with Hophni and Phinehas.\n2. Distinguish Saul's actions in 1 Samuel 13 and 15.\n3. Explain why Amos and Jeremiah reject false confidence in worship.\n4. What does the ark's stay with Obed-edom teach about identifying people correctly?\n\nPRACTICE JAMB PAST QUESTIONS\nUse the lesson's linked past questions and compare each answer against the relevant passage.\n\nDEEPER TEACHING — HOLINESS, JUSTICE AND WORSHIP\nHoliness means being set apart for God and living in a way consistent with God's character. In Leviticus 19:2 Israel is told to be holy because the LORD is holy; the chapter joins worship to practical conduct, including care for the poor, fair dealings, truthfulness and love of neighbour (Leviticus 19:9–18). Holiness is therefore not simply ritual separation or public religious performance. Isaiah 1:10–17 condemns offerings and assemblies when worshippers persist in injustice; the prophet calls them to cease evil, learn to do good, seek justice, rescue the oppressed, defend the orphan and plead for the widow. Amos 5:21–24 similarly rejects festivals and songs disconnected from justice, calling for justice and righteousness to flow continuously.\nIn John 4:19–24, Jesus speaks with the Samaritan woman about worship and says true worshippers will worship the Father in spirit and truth. The point is not that place and community never matter, but that genuine worship cannot be reduced to rivalry over sacred locations. In Acts 5:1–11 Ananias and Sapphira conceal part of a property's proceeds while presenting their gift dishonestly. Peter's rebuke concerns deception, not an obligation to donate the entire sale price: Acts 5:4 explicitly says the property and its proceeds were under their control. Their story warns against seeking a reputation for generosity through lies.\nExample (additional): What did Amos demand instead of empty religious display?\nSolution: Amos 5:24 calls for justice and righteousness. Answer: A life marked by justice and righteousness.\nQUICK UNDERSTANDING CHECK: Compare Isaiah 1, Amos 5 and John 4; explain the specific wrong committed by Ananias and Sapphira.\n"}},{"id":"healing-faith","revised":true,"old":{"title":"Healing, faith and practical compassion","body":"Jesus' healings show compassion and authority and often bring out a person's trust in him. Identify the person, the Gospel and the particular action instead of treating every healing account as the same event. Faith is trust expressed in response; these stories must not become a rule blaming every sick person for insufficient faith.\n\nThe centurion trusts that Jesus can heal by a word and compares this with authority exercised over soldiers. His unworthiness explains why Jesus need not enter his house. At Nain, compassion for the bereaved mother precedes raising her son. The woman's longstanding flow of blood supplies the background to her belief that touching Jesus' garment will make her well. Different motives and circumstances belong to different people.\n\nJairus is a synagogue ruler whose daughter Jesus raises. When news of her death arrives, Jesus tells him not to fear but believe. The mourners laugh when Jesus says the child is not dead but sleeping. Their laughter reflects their conviction that she is dead, not proof that Jesus failed to arrive at all or needed their permission. Keep the report, reassurance and raising in sequence.\n\nBartimaeus persistently cries out to Jesus for help despite others rebuking him. The healing near Jericho leads the man to follow Jesus, glorifying God. His actual request must not be replaced with an imagined silent wait. Matthew's crowd says nothing like it has been seen after a speechless demoniac is healed.\n\nAfter cleansing a leper, Jesus sends him to the priest with the offering Moses prescribed. This provides the law's recognised testimony of cleansing and return to community life. He is not merely asking what offering is needed or claiming the disease will recur unless a gift is paid.\n\nIn Luke's afflicted-boy account, Jesus rebukes an unclean spirit, and the failed attempt by the disciples provides the setting for lament over a faithless generation. Matthew stresses their little faith; Mark connects the difficulty with prayer. Respect the Gospel specified. For a Nigerian student studying these accounts, the useful skill is careful comparison, while practical compassion means supporting a sick classmate rather than shaming them.\n\nIn Mark 2, teachers challenge Jesus' forgiveness of the paralytic's sins as blasphemy. His healing demonstrates the authority he claims to forgive, rather than making every illness the result of a particular sin.\n\nJesus explicitly praises the centurion’s faith."},"new":{"title":"Healing, faith and practical compassion","body":"1. Healing, faith and compassion\nJesus' healings reveal compassion and authority. Faith may be expressed by the patient, a relative or friends, but the Gospels do not teach that everyone who remains sick has insufficient faith. Examine each narrative's setting, request, action and response. Jesus' healings also restore social dignity and challenge assumptions about blame, as in John 9 where he rejects the claim that blindness must be caused by a particular person's sin.\n\n2. The paralytic and forgiveness\nMark 2:1–12 describes four people lowering a paralysed man through a roof in Capernaum. Jesus first pronounces forgiveness, provoking religious objections, then tells the man to rise, take his mat and go home. The visible healing demonstrates Jesus' authority to forgive sins; it does not mean all paralysis results from personal wrongdoing. Four carriers distinguish this episode from other healings.\n\n3. The centurion's servant\nMatthew 8:5–13 describes a Roman centurion who trusts Jesus can heal his servant without entering his home. Jesus praises his faith in the authority of Jesus' word. Luke 7 reports the request through Jewish elders and friends, a narrative detail students should not erase. The centurion is not Jairus, who is a synagogue leader asking Jesus to help his daughter.\n\n4. Twelve years and twelve years old\nMark 5 interweaves Jairus' request for his dying daughter and a woman with a flow of blood for twelve years. The woman touches Jesus' garment and is healed; Jairus' daughter dies and is raised. The daughter is twelve years old. Both details involve twelve but refer to different people. The woman's healing interrupts the journey and demonstrates Jesus' care rather than indifference to Jairus.\n\n5. Ten lepers and one thankful Samaritan\nLuke 17:11–19 describes ten people cleansed as they go to show themselves to the priests. Only one returns to thank Jesus, and he is a Samaritan. Ten healed and one returned are distinct answers. The narrative praises gratitude and the faith of an outsider, not merely compliance with an instruction.\n\n6. Bartimaeus, Siloam and Bethesda\nMark 10 names blind Bartimaeus near Jericho. John 9 describes a man blind from birth who washes in Siloam after Jesus applies mud. John 5 concerns an invalid ill for thirty-eight years near Bethesda, whose Sabbath healing provokes controversy. Siloam, Bethesda and Jericho identify different events. The thirty-eight years should not be confused with the bleeding woman's twelve years.\n\n7. Lazarus and compassionate care\nJohn 11 tells of Lazarus of Bethany, brother of Mary and Martha, in the tomb for four days before Jesus raises him. This is not the poor Lazarus in Luke 16's parable. Luke 10's Good Samaritan is a parable about practical compassion, not a narrated miracle by Jesus. The Samaritan dresses wounds and pays two denarii for further care, showing that love is expressed in practical help.\n\nWORKED JAMB-STYLE INTERPRETATION QUESTIONS\nExample (i): What did the paralytic's healing in Mark 2 demonstrate?\nA. Jesus' authority to forgive sins\nB. The sin of all disabled people\nC. The power of the roof\nD. The authority of Roman soldiers\nSolution: Jesus heals to demonstrate the Son of Man's authority to forgive.\nCorrect answer: A.\n\nExample (ii): Which two facts both involve twelve in Mark 5?\nA. The woman's years of bleeding and Jairus' daughter's age\nB. The number of centurions and disciples\nC. The age of Lazarus and the number of lepers\nD. The number of roofs and pools\nSolution: The woman suffered twelve years; the daughter was twelve years old.\nCorrect answer: A.\n\nExample (iii): How many healed lepers returned to thank Jesus?\nA. Ten\nB. Seven\nC. Three\nD. One\nSolution: Luke 17 identifies one thankful Samaritan.\nCorrect answer: D.\n\nExample (iv): Which healing involves Siloam?\nA. Blind Bartimaeus\nB. The man born blind in John 9\nC. Jairus' daughter\nD. Naaman\nSolution: John 9:7 names the pool of Siloam.\nCorrect answer: B.\n\nExample (v): How long had Lazarus been in the tomb?\nA. One day\nB. Two days\nC. Four days\nD. Twelve years\nSolution: John 11:17 says four days.\nCorrect answer: C.\n\nExample (vi): Which man had been ill for thirty-eight years?\nA. The invalid at Bethesda\nB. Bartimaeus\nC. Jairus\nD. The centurion\nSolution: John 5 describes the invalid near Bethesda.\nCorrect answer: A.\n\nQUICK UNDERSTANDING CHECK — SPECIFIC TO THIS LESSON\n1. Distinguish Siloam, Bethesda and the road near Jericho.\n2. Why does Jesus first forgive the paralytic in Mark 2?\n3. Identify the two uses of twelve in Mark 5.\n4. Why is the Good Samaritan not classified as a narrated healing miracle?\n\nPRACTICE JAMB PAST QUESTIONS\nUse the lesson's linked past questions and compare each answer against the relevant passage.\n\nDEEPER TEACHING — COMPARE THE HEALING NARRATIVES\nIn Mark 2:1–12 friends lower a paralysed man through the roof because of the crowd. Jesus first announces forgiveness of sins, provoking a dispute with scribes who believe only God can forgive sins. He then commands the man to rise, take his mat and go home, presenting the visible healing as evidence of his authority. The story must not be simplified into a claim that every illness results from personal sin. In Mark 5:21–43 Jairus, a synagogue leader, begs Jesus to heal his daughter. On the way, a woman who had suffered bleeding for twelve years touches Jesus' clothing and is healed. The interruption delays Jesus' arrival; messengers report that Jairus' daughter has died. Jesus tells Jairus not to fear but believe, takes the child's hand and raises her. The daughter was twelve years old (Mark 5:42); distinguish her age from the woman's twelve years of suffering.\nIn Luke 17:11–19 Jesus tells ten people with leprosy to show themselves to the priests; they are cleansed as they go. Only one returns to give thanks, and Luke identifies him as a Samaritan. The question may test gratitude or the identity of the returning man. In John 9 Jesus heals a man blind from birth and rejects the disciples' assumption that the man's blindness must be the result of his or his parents' sin (John 9:1–3). John 11 describes the raising of Lazarus, brother of Mary and Martha: Jesus arrives when Lazarus has been in the tomb four days (John 11:17), speaks with Martha about resurrection and life, prays, and calls Lazarus out. This is not the Lazarus in the parable of the rich man and Lazarus (Luke 16:19–31).\nExample (additional): Which of the ten cleansed lepers returned to thank Jesus?\nSolution: Luke 17:16 identifies the returning man as a Samaritan. Answer: The Samaritan.\nQUICK UNDERSTANDING CHECK: Explain the two different 'twelve' details in Mark 5, identify the four days in John 11, and explain what John 9 teaches about blaming sick people.\n"}},{"id":"elisha-naaman-gehazi","revised":true,"old":{"title":"Elisha, Naaman and Gehazi: healing and prophetic integrity","body":"Second Kings 5 tells of Naaman, an important Aramean commander whose skin disease required help beyond his military position. An Israelite servant girl directs attention to the prophet in Samaria. The account distinguishes the kings, the prophet, servants and the sick man; their different responses should not be collapsed into one event.\n\nIsrael's king tears his clothes after receiving the letter, asking whether he is God, able to kill and make alive. Elisha's immediate response is to ask that the man come to him so that he may know there is a prophet in Israel. The later instruction to wash in the Jordan is a different stage. A question asking for the response to the king's alarm must not automatically receive the most familiar washing answer.\n\nWhen Naaman arrives, Elisha sends a messenger telling him to wash seven times in the Jordan. Naaman is initially angry because he expected a dramatic personal action. His servants persuade him to obey the simple instruction. His cleansing follows obedience, not a payment purchasing prophetic power. Elisha refuses the reward offered afterwards.\n\nElisha's encounter with Naaman exposes a different abuse. Gehazi secretly pursued gifts after Elisha had refused payment. Elisha said his spirit had gone with him, revealing knowledge of the deception. The lesson concerns greed and misuse of a position of religious trust, not payment as the source of God's healing.\n\nGehazi lies about his errand and conceals what he has obtained. Elisha's question about where he has been exposes the deception. The prophet says his spirit went with him; the narrative then assigns Naaman's leprosy to Gehazi. The moral issue is covetousness, dishonesty and abuse of trust, not merely ambition or anxiety.\n\nA Nigerian classroom application is a person secretly demanding money for a service already given freely, then lying to the responsible official. Such behaviour exploits another person's need. This ancient narrative does not justify stigmatizing a modern person with a skin condition or assuming that illness proves greed.\n\nCommon traps:\n• Elisha's invitation to come precedes the Jordan instructions.\n• Naaman is the commander; Gehazi is Elisha's servant.\n• The prophet's refusal of gifts differs from Gehazi's pursuit of them.\n• A reported spiritual perception is not ordinary physical following."},"new":{"title":"Elisha, Naaman and Gehazi: healing and prophetic integrity","body":"1. Elisha succeeds Elijah\nSecond Kings 2 describes Elisha asking for a double portion of Elijah's spirit and receiving Elijah's mantle after Elijah is taken up. Elisha parts the Jordan and continues prophetic ministry. His work includes provision for a widow, help for the Shunammite woman, healing Naaman and making a borrowed axe head float. The Naaman story in 2 Kings 5 particularly contrasts humility, trust and free prophetic service with greed and deceit.\n\n2. The unnamed captive girl\nNaaman is an honoured Aramean army commander with a serious skin disease. An unnamed Israelite girl captured in a raid serves Naaman's wife and tells her of a prophet in Samaria who could help. Her testimony begins the healing journey despite her lack of power. Scripture does not name her; students should not invent one. The king of Aram sends Naaman with a letter to Israel's king, who tears his clothes in alarm.\n\n3. Naaman's gifts and expectation\nSecond Kings 5:5 records ten talents of silver, six thousand shekels of gold and ten sets of clothes as Naaman's travelling gifts. Naaman arrives with horses and chariots expecting Elisha to come out and perform a dramatic gesture. Elisha instead sends a messenger telling him to wash seven times in the Jordan. Naaman becomes angry and prefers the rivers Abana and Pharpar in Damascus. His pride and expectations nearly prevent obedience.\n\n4. Seven washings and the servants' counsel\nNaaman's servants ask why he would refuse a simple task when he would have undertaken a difficult one. He listens, dips himself seven times in the Jordan and is healed, with flesh like that of a young child. The story highlights obedience and humility, not magical properties of river water. Seven dips in the Jordan are different from Israel's seven circuits of Jericho on the seventh day.\n\n5. Naaman's confession and Elisha's refusal\nNaaman returns confessing that there is no God in all the earth except in Israel and offers Elisha a gift. Elisha refuses. Naaman requests earth to take home and discusses accompanying his king into the temple of Rimmon; Elisha tells him to go in peace. The story shows an outsider beginning to acknowledge Israel's God. The healing is not presented as something Elisha sells.\n\n6. Gehazi's request and the amount actually given\nGehazi runs after Naaman and falsely claims Elisha sent him for one talent of silver and two changes of clothes for two young prophets. Naaman gives two talents of silver and two sets of garments. Gehazi hides them and lies when Elisha questions him. Elisha exposes the deceit and declares Naaman's skin disease will cling to Gehazi. Do not confuse the one talent requested with the two talents received.\n\n7. Why Gehazi's wrongdoing matters\nGehazi uses his master's name dishonestly, exploits a healed man's gratitude, hides payment and lies again. The offence is greed and betrayal of trust, not simply that any present is always wrong. The story contrasts the unnamed girl's generosity, the servants' wisdom, Naaman's eventual humility, Elisha's refusal to profit and Gehazi's deception.\n\n8. Distinguish Elisha's miracles\nIn 2 Kings 4 a widow's oil multiplies and the Shunammite woman's son is restored to life. In 2 Kings 5 Naaman washes in the Jordan. In 2 Kings 6 a borrowed iron axe head floats. The widow's oil is not a healing ointment for Naaman, and the Shunammite woman's son is not Jairus' daughter. Linking each event to its chapter prevents mistaken answers.\n\nWORKED JAMB-STYLE INTERPRETATION QUESTIONS\nExample (i): Who told Naaman's wife about the prophet?\nA. An unnamed Israelite captive girl\nB. Gehazi\nC. Elijah\nD. Jezebel\nSolution: Second Kings 5:2–3 describes an unnamed captive girl.\nCorrect answer: A.\n\nExample (ii): Why was Naaman initially angry?\nA. He wanted a dramatic healing and preferred Damascus rivers\nB. He had washed seven times already\nC. Elisha asked for too much money\nD. The king of Israel refused him entry\nSolution: Naaman expected Elisha to appear and disliked the Jordan instruction.\nCorrect answer: A.\n\nExample (iii): How many times did Naaman wash in the Jordan?\nA. Three\nB. Five\nC. Seven\nD. Twelve\nSolution: Second Kings 5:14 specifies seven times.\nCorrect answer: C.\n\nExample (iv): What did Gehazi originally request?\nA. One talent of silver and two changes of clothes\nB. Two talents of gold\nC. Ten horses\nD. A temple\nSolution: Second Kings 5:22 records his request; Naaman actually gives two talents of silver.\nCorrect answer: A.\n\nExample (v): Why was Gehazi condemned?\nA. He refused a salary\nB. He used deception and greed to obtain gifts in Elisha's name\nC. He washed in the Jordan\nD. He gave away his possessions\nSolution: Gehazi falsely invoked Elisha, hid the gifts and lied about his actions.\nCorrect answer: B.\n\nExample (vi): Which miracle belongs to Elisha?\nA. The floating axe head\nB. The feeding of five thousand\nC. Raising Lazarus\nD. Healing Bartimaeus\nSolution: Second Kings 6 describes Elisha making a borrowed axe head float.\nCorrect answer: A.\n\nQUICK UNDERSTANDING CHECK — SPECIFIC TO THIS LESSON\n1. Identify the captive girl's role and explain why it matters.\n2. Explain why Naaman resisted a simple instruction.\n3. Distinguish Gehazi's requested and received silver amounts.\n4. Name two other Elisha miracles and their chapters.\n\nPRACTICE JAMB PAST QUESTIONS\nUse the lesson's linked past questions and compare each answer against the relevant passage.\n\nDEEPER TEACHING — NAAMAN'S JOURNEY AND GEHAZI'S DECEPTION (2 KINGS 5)\nNaaman was commander of the army of the king of Aram (Syria), respected by his master, but he suffered from a skin disease described as leprosy. A young Israelite girl taken captive and serving Naaman's wife said that the prophet in Samaria could heal him. The king of Aram sent a letter to the king of Israel with gifts, but Israel's king was distressed because he could not cure Naaman. Elisha asked that Naaman be sent to him. Instead of coming out to perform an impressive ceremony, Elisha sent a messenger instructing Naaman to wash seven times in the Jordan River. Naaman became angry: he had expected Elisha to call on God and wave his hand, and he thought the rivers Abana and Pharpar of Damascus better than Israel's waters. His servants reasoned with him that he would have done a difficult task, so he should obey this simple instruction. He washed seven times and was healed; his flesh became like that of a little child (2 Kings 5:14).\nNaaman returned to acknowledge the God of Israel and offered gifts, but Elisha refused them. Naaman requested earth to take home and raised a question about accompanying his master into the temple of Rimmon; Elisha told him to go in peace. Gehazi, Elisha's servant, secretly ran after Naaman and falsely claimed that Elisha had sent him to request a talent of silver and two changes of clothing for two visiting sons of the prophets. Naaman instead gave two talents of silver and two changes of clothing (2 Kings 5:22–23). Gehazi hid the gifts and lied to Elisha. Elisha exposed his deceit and declared that Naaman's disease would cling to Gehazi and his descendants; Gehazi left with the disease (2 Kings 5:25–27). Distinguish the amount Gehazi requested from the amount Naaman actually gave.\nExample (additional): How many times was Naaman told to wash, and in which river?\nSolution: Second Kings 5:10 says seven times in the Jordan. Answer: Seven times in the River Jordan.\nExample (additional): What was Gehazi's wrongdoing?\nSolution: He pursued gifts against Elisha's decision, lied about their purpose and then lied to Elisha. Answer: Greed and deliberate deception.\nQUICK UNDERSTANDING CHECK: Name the captive girl's role, the rivers Naaman mentioned, the requested and received gifts, and the consequences for Gehazi.\n"}}];
